window.__PYQ = [{"i":"2026-1","y":2026,"n":1,"s":10,"q":"Which one of the following Carnatic music ragas is similar to Raga Bilawal in Hindustani music?","o":["Nat Bhairavi","Kamavardhini","Hanumatodi","Dheera Shankarabharanam"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Nat Bhairavi - Incorrect\nNat Bhairavi is the 20th melakarta of Carnatic music. Its scale has komal (flat) Ga, komal Dha and komal Ni, which makes it the Carnatic equivalent of Asavari thaat in Hindustani music (similar to the Western natural minor scale). Bilawal has all notes shuddha (natural), so Nat Bhairavi does not match it.\n\n(b) Kamavardhini - Incorrect\nKamavardhini (also called Pantuvarali) is the 51st melakarta. It uses komal Re, komal Dha and tivra (sharp) Ma, which makes it the equivalent of Purvi thaat. Bilawal uses only shuddha Ma, so the presence of tivra Ma alone rules this option out.\n\n(c) Hanumatodi - Incorrect\nHanumatodi is the 8th melakarta. In it, all four notes that can be flattened (Re, Ga, Dha, Ni) are komal. Its Hindustani equivalent is Bhairavi thaat, not Bilawal. This is a common trap because Carnatic 'Todi' corresponds to Hindustani 'Bhairavi', while Hindustani 'Todi' is a different scale.\n\n(d) Dheera Shankarabharanam - Correct\nDheera Shankarabharanam is the 29th melakarta. In Hindustani terms it uses all seven shuddha swaras (Sa Re Ga Ma Pa Dha Ni), exactly like Bilawal thaat, which Pt. V.N. Bhatkhande treated as the standard scale of Hindustani music. Both correspond to the Western major scale (C D E F G A B). Hence Bilawal and Shankarabharanam are equivalent, and (d) is the answer."},{"i":"2026-2","y":2026,"n":2,"s":9,"q":"The artificially fixed rupee-sterling exchange rate prescribed by the Hilton-Young Commission (1926) was adopted by the British Government for which one of the following reasons?","o":["Aiding the flow of remittances from India and maintaining India's creditworthiness","Providing support to Indian importers","Encouraging export of cotton produce from India","Preventing depreciation of the Rupee in terms of gold"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Aiding the flow of remittances from India and maintaining India's creditworthiness - Correct\nThe Hilton-Young Commission (Royal Commission on Indian Currency and Finance, 1926) fixed the rupee at 1 shilling 6 pence, higher than the pre-war 1s 4d. An overvalued rupee meant fewer rupees were needed to buy each pound sterling. This reduced the rupee cost of the 'Home Charges' (interest on debt, pensions, salaries of British officials, army costs) and other sterling remittances from India to Britain, and kept India's credit standing in the London money market stable. This was the British Government's real motive.\n\n(b) Providing support to Indian importers - Incorrect\nA high exchange rate did make imports cheaper, but the beneficiaries were British manufacturers exporting to India, not Indian importers. Supporting Indian traders was never the stated or real aim.\n\n(c) Encouraging export of cotton produce from India - Incorrect\nAn overvalued rupee makes Indian goods more expensive for foreign buyers, so it actually hurt exports of cotton and other produce. Indian businessmen such as Purshotamdas Thakurdas strongly opposed the 1s 6d ratio for this reason.\n\n(d) Preventing depreciation of the Rupee in terms of gold - Incorrect\nThe Commission did recommend a Gold Bullion Standard, but the choice of the specific, artificially high 1s 6d sterling ratio was driven by remittance and credit considerations, not by the aim of preventing the rupee's depreciation against gold."},{"i":"2026-3","y":2026,"n":3,"s":7,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Pali texts contain the first definite references to coins, e.g., kahapana, nikkha, kamsa, and kakanika.\nII. The literary evidence from Pali texts is corroborated by archaeological evidence of punch-marked coins from many sites, most of them made of silver.\nThe above statements have been associated with which of the following?\n1. Emergence of urban life\n2. Transition to money economy\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"Statement I (first references to coins in Pali texts)\nPali Buddhist texts of the 6th-4th century BCE are the earliest literature to clearly mention coins such as kahapana (karshapana), nikkha, kamsa and kakanika, showing that metallic money was in use.\n\nStatement II (archaeological corroboration)\nPunch-marked coins, mostly silver with some copper, have been found at many sites in the Ganga valley and beyond. They confirm the literary evidence.\n\n1. Emergence of urban life - Correct\nThe appearance of coinage coincides with the 'second urbanisation' of the Mahajanapada period, when towns such as Rajagriha, Shravasti, Kaushambi and Varanasi grew. Coins were needed for trade between towns, for crafts and for markets, so the evidence is closely associated with urban growth.\n\n2. Transition to money economy - Correct\nRegular use of standardised metal coins marks the shift from barter to exchange through money. Coins were used to pay wages, taxes, loans with interest and prices of goods, which is what a money economy means.\n\nSince the evidence is associated with both developments, the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2026-4","y":2026,"n":4,"s":10,"q":"Which of the following temples has/have a Nagara-style shikhara?\n1. Malegitti Shivalaya, Badami\n2. Huchimalligudi Temple, Aihole\n3. Dashavatara Temple, Deogarh\n4. Virupaksha Temple, Pattadakal\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only","3 and 4"],"a":1,"x":"1. Malegitti Shivalaya, Badami - Incorrect\nThis early Chalukya temple (7th century) has a Dravida-style vimana: a stepped superstructure topped by a square or octagonal crowning element, not a curvilinear Nagara shikhara.\n\n2. Huchimalligudi Temple, Aihole - Correct\nThis early Chalukya temple at Aihole has a curvilinear rekha-nagara shikhara over the sanctum. It is also notable as one of the earliest temples with an antarala (vestibule) between the sanctum and the hall.\n\n3. Dashavatara Temple, Deogarh - Correct\nThis Gupta temple (early 6th century, Lalitpur district, UP) is one of the earliest examples of the Nagara style. It originally had a curvilinear shikhara, now partly ruined, over the sanctum.\n\n4. Virupaksha Temple, Pattadakal - Incorrect\nBuilt by Queen Lokamahadevi (c. 740 CE) to celebrate Vikramaditya II's victory over the Pallavas, it is a classic Dravida temple modelled on the Kailasanatha temple of Kanchipuram. Pattadakal is known for having both Nagara and Dravida temples, but Virupaksha is Dravida.\n\nHence only 2 and 3 have Nagara shikharas, and the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2026-5","y":2026,"n":5,"s":7,"q":"Among the four main forms of existence of life recognized in Jainism, which one of the following is not included?","o":["Deva (gods)","Yaksha (demi-gods)","Manushya (humans)","Tiryancha (animals and plants)"],"a":1,"x":"Jainism recognises four gatis (states or forms of existence) into which a soul (jiva) can be reborn, depending on its karma: Deva, Manushya, Tiryancha and Naraki.\n\n(a) Deva (gods) - Included\nDevas are heavenly beings who enjoy pleasures as a result of good karma, but they are still in the cycle of rebirth.\n\n(b) Yaksha (demi-gods) - Not included (correct answer)\nYakshas are not a separate gati. In Jain cosmology they are a class within the devas (vyantara devas) and appear as attendant or guardian deities of the Tirthankaras. So they are not one of the four main forms of existence.\n\n(c) Manushya (humans) - Included\nHuman birth is considered the most valuable, because only as a human can a soul attain liberation (moksha).\n\n(d) Tiryancha (animals and plants) - Included\nTiryancha covers all animals, plants and even one-sensed beings like earth, water and air bodies.\n\nThe fourth gati is Naraki (hell-beings). Hence the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2026-6","y":2026,"n":6,"s":10,"q":"The Hallisalasya painting in the Bagh Caves represents:","o":["A joyous folk dance","Buddha in a meditative pose","The depiction of Shiva and Parvati on Kailasha","Samudramanthan (Churning of the Ocean)"],"a":0,"x":"(a) A joyous folk dance - Correct\nThe Bagh caves (Dhar district, Madhya Pradesh, c. 5th-6th century CE) are famous for a painting of the Hallisaka (hallisa-lasya), a circular group dance. It shows women dancers moving in a ring around a central male figure, with musicians playing instruments. It is a secular, joyous folk-dance scene and one of the finest paintings of the period.\n\n(b) Buddha in a meditative pose - Incorrect\nAlthough the Bagh caves are Buddhist viharas, the Hallisaka panel does not depict the Buddha.\n\n(c) The depiction of Shiva and Parvati on Kailasha - Incorrect\nShiva and Parvati on Kailasha, with Ravana shaking the mountain, is a famous sculptural theme at Ellora (Kailasa temple, Cave 16), not the Bagh painting.\n\n(d) Samudramanthan (Churning of the Ocean) - Incorrect\nThe churning of the ocean is a Puranic theme found in later temple sculpture (e.g., Angkor Wat), not in this Bagh painting."},{"i":"2026-7","y":2026,"n":7,"s":10,"q":"Consider the following statements relating to the use of the place-value system in India:\n1. The earliest epigraphic use of the place-value system in India is found in the Mankani plates from Gujarat (AD 595–596).\n2. In the ninth century, place-values become general in inscriptions all over India.\n3. The place-values have been found in Sanskrit inscriptions in South-east Asia as early as the seventh century.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"1. Earliest epigraphic use in Mankani plates (AD 595-596) - Correct\nThe Mankani (Sankheda) copper plates of the Kalachuri ruler Taralasvamin, from Gujarat, are dated year 346 of the Kalachuri era, i.e., AD 595-596. The date is written using the decimal place-value system, making it the earliest known epigraphic use of this system in India.\n\n2. Place-values become general in the ninth century - Correct\nBy the 9th century, numerals written with place values are common in inscriptions across India. The Gwalior inscription of 876 CE is a well-known example and also shows a symbol for zero.\n\n3. Place-values in Sanskrit inscriptions of South-east Asia in the seventh century - Correct\nInscriptions from Cambodia and Sumatra of the late 7th century (for example records dated 683 CE, which include the earliest surviving zero symbol) use place-value numerals. This shows the system had already spread with Indian cultural influence.\n\nSince all three statements are correct, the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2026-8","y":2026,"n":8,"s":7,"q":"Consider the following statements about the archaeological findings in Harappan towns:\nI. There is wide occurrence of spindle-whorls in the houses but absence of spinning wheels.\nII. Weights and measurement scales, complete with graduations have been discovered.\nIII. There are houses built in large part with baked bricks, around relatively spacious courtyards, with their own wells, bathing platforms, and large rooms.\nWhich of the following inferences can be drawn from the above statements?\n1. Statement I suggests that spinning was a laborious activity done at home.\n2. Statement II suggests the extent of the scientific knowledge that the Harappans possessed.\n3. Statement III suggests the emergence of a common property system.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"x":"Inference 1 (drawn from Statement I) - Correct\nSpindle-whorls are small weights of clay or faience fixed to a hand spindle to twist fibres into thread. Their frequent discovery inside Harappan houses shows that spinning was done at home. No spinning wheel has been found; the spinning wheel (charkha) reached India only in the medieval period (around the 13th-14th century). Spinning with a simple hand spindle is slow and labour-intensive, so the inference that spinning was a laborious household activity follows logically from the evidence.\n\nInference 2 (drawn from Statement II) - Correct\nThe Harappans used standardised cubical weights, mostly of chert, which followed a binary series for smaller units (1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64) and a decimal system for larger ones (160, 200, 320, 640). Graduated measuring scales have also been found, such as a shell scale at Mohenjodaro and an ivory scale at Lothal with very fine divisions (about 1.7 mm). This precision in measuring weight and length reflects considerable mathematical and technical knowledge, so the inference is valid.\n\nInference 3 (drawn from Statement III) - Incorrect\nThe evidence describes large houses of baked brick built around spacious courtyards, with their own private wells, bathing platforms and big rooms. At the same sites there are also small one- or two-room dwellings. Houses with privately owned wells and facilities point to private property and to a well-off class, that is, social and economic differentiation. This is evidence against a common (collective) property system, not for it, so the inference does not follow.\n\nHence only inferences 1 and 2 are valid, and the answer is (a) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2026-9","y":2026,"n":9,"s":9,"q":"Which one of the following statements about the Eka Movement and Bardoli Satyagraha is correct?","o":["The Eka Movement was throughout supported and organized by the Congress while Bardoli Satyagraha was initially independent of Congress influence and was only in the last stages supported by the Congress.","The Eka Movement was provided leadership by the taluqdars of Awadh, whereas the Bardoli Satyagraha was a movement of the landless labourers.","The Bardoli Satyagraha was a campaign against the enhancement of land revenue, while the Eka Movement was a protest against excessive extraction of rents.","The Eka Movement was located in the Varanasi and Mirzapur districts of the present-day U.P., while the Bardoli Satyagraha took place in Saurashtra."],"a":2,"x":"(a) Incorrect\nThe Eka (Unity) movement in Awadh (1921-22, Hardoi, Bahraich, Sitapur, Barabanki) began with some Congress and Khilafat involvement, but it soon moved away under low-caste leaders like Madari Pasi and became independent of the Congress, which disowned its militancy. Bardoli (1928), on the other hand, was organised from the start by Vallabhbhai Patel with Gandhi's blessing. So the statement reverses the reality.\n\n(b) Incorrect\nEka was led by Madari Pasi and some small zamindars and was directed against the taluqdars and their exactions; taluqdars did not lead it. Bardoli was a movement of peasant landholders (mainly Patidars/Kanbis), not landless labourers.\n\n(c) Correct\nThe Bardoli Satyagraha protested against the Bombay government's enhancement of land revenue by about 22 percent (initially 30 percent) in Bardoli taluka; it ended with an inquiry (Maxwell-Broomfield) that reduced the hike to about 6 percent. The Eka movement protested against rents often 50 percent higher than the recorded rent, illegal cesses and forced labour (begar) extracted by landlords.\n\n(d) Incorrect\nEka was centred in the northern districts of Awadh, not Varanasi and Mirzapur. Bardoli is a taluka in Surat district of mainland Gujarat, not in Saurashtra."},{"i":"2026-10","y":2026,"n":10,"s":7,"q":"Consider the following statements about the Rigvedic period:\nI. Irrigation from wells allowed agriculture to expand away from flood plains and strips on river margins into the present Punjab and Haryana plains having underground water levels reasonably close to the surface.\nII. Draught-animal power was employed to draw up water out of the wells.\nWhich of the following information support/supports the above statements?\n1. There is evidence in the Rigveda of the use of ashma chakra (stone pulley wheel) and ahava (strapped wooden pails) to draw up water.\n2. Mention has been made in the Rigveda of the use of implements like parashu / kulisha (axe) and datra / sreni (sickle).\n3. There is a history of the use of ox, even before the Rigveda, for ploughing the land and pulling the carts.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3","1 and 3 only","3 only"],"a":2,"x":"1. Stone pulley-wheel (ashma chakra) and strapped wooden pails (ahava) - Correct (supports)\nThe Rigveda mentions wells (avata, kupa) from which water was drawn with a stone pulley-wheel and pails fastened with straps. This is direct evidence of well irrigation, which allowed cultivation away from river margins into areas where the water table was close to the surface (supports Statement I). Drawing water with a pulley suggests use of animal power (supports Statement II).\n\n2. Axes (parashu, kulisha) and sickles (datra, srini) - Incorrect (does not support)\nAxes relate to clearing forests and sickles to harvesting. They show agricultural activity in general but say nothing about wells, irrigation or the use of draught animals to lift water, so they do not support either statement.\n\n3. Use of the ox before the Rigveda for ploughing and carts - Correct (supports)\nOxen were used for ploughing and pulling carts even in the Harappan period (terracotta plough models, cart models). This long history of harnessing oxen makes it plausible that draught-animal power was used to lift water from wells, supporting Statement II.\n\nHence 1 and 3 support the statements, and the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2026-11","y":2026,"n":11,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following assertion:\nIn the Pleistocene period either the Yamuna once flowed into the Indus, or the Sutlej flowed into the Yamuna and one major tributary of either had shifted from the Ganga to the Indus or vice versa.\nWhich of the following is/are the basis of the above assertion?\n1. The Nadi-Sukta of the Rigveda\n2. The explorations of the Sutlej and the Yamuna by Robert Bruce Foote\n3. The presence of the same species of dolphins in both the Indus and the Ganga river systems\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","1 and 2","3"],"a":3,"x":"1. The Nadi-Sukta of the Rigveda - Incorrect\nThe Nadi-Sukta (Rigveda X.75) lists rivers from east to west (Ganga, Yamuna, Sarasvati, Sutudri and so on). It is a text of roughly the late 2nd millennium BCE and cannot be evidence for river courses in the Pleistocene epoch, which ended about 11,700 years ago.\n\n2. Explorations of the Sutlej and the Yamuna by Robert Bruce Foote - Incorrect\nRobert Bruce Foote, the 'father of Indian prehistory', is known for discovering a Palaeolithic handaxe at Pallavaram near Madras (1863) and for work in South India. He did not base any such assertion on explorations of the Sutlej and Yamuna.\n\n3. The same species of dolphin in both the Indus and the Ganga systems - Correct\nFreshwater river dolphins cannot travel through the sea. The presence of the same dolphin (then treated as one species, Platanista gangetica, with Indus and Ganga forms) in both river systems can only be explained if the two systems were once connected, for example if the Yamuna or Sutlej shifted its course between them. This zoological evidence is the basis of the assertion.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 3 only."},{"i":"2026-12","y":2026,"n":12,"s":10,"q":"What does an empty seat represent in early Buddhist iconography?","o":["The meditation of the Buddha","The Buddha's First Sermon","The Buddha's Mahaparinibbana","The Buddha's Mahabhinishkramana"],"a":0,"x":"In early Buddhist art (Sanchi, Bharhut, 2nd-1st century BCE) the Buddha was not shown in human form; symbols represented events of his life (NCERT, Themes in Indian History Part I).\n\n(a) The meditation of the Buddha - Correct\nThe empty seat (often under the Bodhi tree) was meant to indicate the meditation of the Buddha, that is, his enlightenment while seated under the tree.\n\n(b) The Buddha's First Sermon - Incorrect\nThe First Sermon at Sarnath was represented by the wheel (dharmachakra), often with deer, symbolising the 'turning of the wheel of law'.\n\n(c) The Buddha's Mahaparinibbana - Incorrect\nThe Mahaparinibbana (death and final release) was represented by the stupa.\n\n(d) The Buddha's Mahabhinishkramana - Incorrect\nThe Great Departure from the palace was represented by a riderless horse, sometimes with a parasol held over it."},{"i":"2026-13","y":2026,"n":13,"s":7,"q":"Which of the following pairs of ancient and modern names of rivers is/are correctly matched?\n1. Vitasta       :      Chenab\n2. Asikni         :      Jhelum\n3. Parushni    :      Ravi\n4. Yavyavati   :      Beas\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2","3 and 4","3 only","4 only"],"a":2,"x":"Rigvedic names of the rivers of Punjab: Sutudri (Sutlej), Vipas (Beas), Parushni (Ravi), Asikni (Chenab), Vitasta (Jhelum).\n\n1. Vitasta : Chenab - Incorrect\nVitasta is the Jhelum (the name survives in Kashmiri 'Vyeth'). The Chenab is Asikni.\n\n2. Asikni : Jhelum - Incorrect\nAsikni ('dark-coloured') is the Chenab, not the Jhelum.\n\n3. Parushni : Ravi - Correct\nParushni is the Ravi (later also called Iravati). The famous Battle of the Ten Kings (Dasarajna), won by Sudas of the Bharatas, was fought on its banks.\n\n4. Yavyavati : Beas - Incorrect\nThe Beas is Vipas (later Vipasa). Yavyavati is a separate river mentioned in the Rigveda, generally identified with the Zhob in the north-west (Balochistan region).\n\nHence only pair 3 is correct, and the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2026-14","y":2026,"n":14,"s":10,"q":"Which of the following statements on the Amaravati Stupa and its relief sculpture is/are correct?\n1. It was located in the lower Krishna valley.\n2. In India, it was next only to the Sanchi Stupa in size.\n3. The Amaravati school of sculpture made a lasting impact on the later South Indian sculpture, and its products were carried to Sri Lanka and South-east Asia.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. It was located in the lower Krishna valley - Correct\nAmaravati (Dharanikota, Guntur district, Andhra Pradesh) lies on the south bank of the Krishna in its lower course, near the old Satavahana capital Dhanyakataka.\n\n2. In India, it was next only to the Sanchi Stupa in size - Incorrect\nThe Amaravati Mahachaitya was actually larger than Sanchi. Its drum had a diameter of about 50 metres, whereas the Great Stupa at Sanchi is about 36.6 metres across. It is described as the largest stupa of South India and one of the largest in the country, so saying it was 'next only to Sanchi' is wrong.\n\n3. Lasting impact on later South Indian sculpture and spread to Sri Lanka and South-east Asia - Correct\nThe Amaravati school, using white-greenish limestone, is known for slender, dynamic figures and crowded narrative panels. It influenced later Pallava and Chola sculpture, and Amaravati-style images and the style itself spread to Sri Lanka and South-east Asia through trade and Buddhist links.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 3 only."},{"i":"2026-15","y":2026,"n":15,"s":7,"q":"Which of the following pairs of the king and his dynasty in early historical Tamilakam is/are not correctly matched?\n1. Senguttuvan : Chera\n2. Udiyanjeral : Chola\n3. Nedunjeliyan : Pandya\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2","2 only","1 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"The question asks which pairs are NOT correctly matched.\n\n1. Senguttuvan : Chera - Correctly matched\nSenguttuvan, the 'Red Chera', is the most celebrated Chera king of the Sangam age. According to the Silappadikaram he brought stone from the Himalayas and started the worship of Pattini (Kannagi).\n\n2. Udiyanjeral : Chola - Not correctly matched\nUdiyanjeral (Udiyanjeral Athan) is regarded as the earliest known Chera ruler, praised in the Padirruppattu. He was a Chera, not a Chola. So this pair is wrong.\n\n3. Nedunjeliyan : Pandya - Correctly matched\nNedunjeliyan was a famous Pandya king, known for his victory at Talaiyalanganam over a confederacy of Cheras, Cholas and chieftains, praised in Mangudi Marudan's Maduraikkanji.\n\nHence only pair 2 is not correctly matched, and the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2026-16","y":2026,"n":16,"s":9,"q":"Which of the following factors contributed to the formation of the Forward Bloc by Subhas Chandra Bose in 1939?\n1. Bose failed to win the confidence of Mahatma Gandhi.\n2. The Congress Left was disunited and failed to support Bose.\n3. The Communists did not support Bose in his endeavours.\n4. The supporters of M.N. Roy and socialist leaders like Jayaprakash Narayan preferred Congress unity to supporting Bose.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2 and 4","1, 3 and 4","2 and 4 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. Bose failed to win the confidence of Mahatma Gandhi - Correct\nBose was re-elected Congress president at Tripuri (1939) by defeating Gandhi's candidate Pattabhi Sitaramayya; Gandhi called the defeat 'more mine than his'. The Pant resolution then asked Bose to form a Working Committee in accordance with Gandhi's wishes. Without Gandhi's confidence he could not work, so he resigned and formed the Forward Bloc within the Congress.\n\n2. The Congress Left was disunited and failed to support Bose - Correct\nThe Left (Socialists, Communists, Royists, Bose's followers) did not act together. At Tripuri, the Congress Socialists stayed neutral on the Pant resolution, which therefore passed. This lack of unified Left support left Bose isolated.\n\n3. The Communists did not support Bose - Incorrect (as per key)\nThe Communists had backed Bose in the presidential contest. The key does not treat their position as a separate factor behind the Forward Bloc; the main split that isolated Bose was the Socialist and Royist stance described in statement 4.\n\n4. Royists and socialists like Jayaprakash Narayan preferred Congress unity - Correct\nM.N. Roy's followers and Congress Socialists led by Jayaprakash Narayan felt that a split with the Gandhian leadership would weaken the national movement, so they put unity first rather than backing Bose.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1, 2 and 4."},{"i":"2026-17","y":2026,"n":17,"s":9,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the British policy in Awadh immediately after its annexation in 1856:\n1. The taluqdars were dispossessed of their estates but allowed to retain their arms and forts.\n2. A Summary Revenue Settlement was made in 1856 assuming that the taluqdars were outsiders.\n3. The British believed in taking revenue directly from the peasants by removing the taluqdars.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct ?","o":["2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3","2 only"],"a":0,"x":"1. Taluqdars dispossessed but allowed to retain arms and forts - Incorrect\nAfter annexation of Awadh in 1856, the British not only took away many taluqdar estates but also disarmed them: their armed retainers were disbanded and their forts were destroyed. So they were not allowed to keep arms and forts.\n\n2. Summary Revenue Settlement of 1856 assumed the taluqdars were outsiders - Correct\nAs NCERT explains, the Summary Settlement was based on the assumption that the taluqdars were interlopers with no permanent stake in the land, who had established control by force and fraud. Many taluqdars therefore lost their villages.\n\n3. The British wanted to collect revenue directly from peasants by removing the taluqdars - Correct\nThe British hoped that by removing the taluqdars they would settle revenue directly with the actual owners (peasants), which would increase revenue for the state and win peasant loyalty. In practice, revenue demand remained high and peasants gained little, which is why many sided with the taluqdars in 1857.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2026-18","y":2026,"n":18,"s":9,"q":"Consider the following assertion:\nThe genesis of political alliances based on community lay in the very nature of the Montague-Chelmsford Reforms, 1919.\nWhich of the following statements support/supports the above assertion?\n1. Reforms retained and extended the principle of separate electorates.\n2. Separate electorates were supposed to counter Indian nationalism, which was growing stronger.\n3. Deprived classes rallied around the favours inherent in separate electorates.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"Assertion: community-based political alliances arose from the nature of the Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms (Government of India Act, 1919).\n\n1. The reforms retained and extended separate electorates - Correct (supports)\nThe 1909 Act had introduced separate electorates for Muslims. The 1919 Act kept them and extended them to Sikhs, Indian Christians, Anglo-Indians and Europeans. Politics thus came to be organised along community lines.\n\n2. Separate electorates were meant to counter growing Indian nationalism - Correct (supports)\nThe British used communal representation as a 'divide and rule' device to weaken the united nationalist demand after the Lucknow Pact and the rise of mass nationalism. This encouraged leaders to mobilise voters by community.\n\n3. Deprived classes rallied around the favours inherent in separate electorates - Correct (supports)\nNominated seats and communal representation gave various groups, including depressed classes and non-Brahmin groups, an incentive to organise as communities to claim seats and patronage (e.g., non-Brahmin politics of the Justice Party in Madras). This produced community-based alliances.\n\nSince all three support the assertion, the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2026-19","y":2026,"n":19,"s":10,"q":"Pandit Mallikarjun Mansur, the famous classical singer from Karnataka, represented the:","o":["Agra Gharana","Gwalior Gharana","Patiala Gharana","Jaipur-Atrauli Gharana"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Agra Gharana - Incorrect\nThe Agra gharana is known for a forceful, rhythmic style; its most famous exponent was Ustad Faiyaz Khan. Mansur did not belong to it.\n\n(b) Gwalior Gharana - Incorrect\nGwalior is the oldest khayal gharana; exponents include the Paluskar tradition. Mansur received some early training in the Gwalior style, but he is identified with Jaipur-Atrauli.\n\n(c) Patiala Gharana - Incorrect\nThe Patiala gharana is associated with Ustad Bade Ghulam Ali Khan and is known for its thumri and ornate taans.\n\n(d) Jaipur-Atrauli Gharana - Correct\nPt. Mallikarjun Mansur (1910-1992) of Dharwad, Karnataka, was one of the greatest exponents of the Jaipur-Atrauli gharana founded by Ustad Alladiya Khan. He learned from Alladiya Khan's sons Manji Khan and Bhurji Khan and was known for rare, complex ragas. He received the Padma Vibhushan."},{"i":"2026-20","y":2026,"n":20,"s":7,"q":"In which one among the following texts does the term kshetra-patni ('mistress of the field') originate?","o":["Rigveda","Atharvaveda","Ashtadhyayi","Arthashastra"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Rigveda - Incorrect\nThe Rigveda mentions Kshetrapati, the 'lord of the field', a male deity invoked for agricultural prosperity (Rigveda IV.57). The female term kshetra-patni does not originate here.\n\n(b) Atharvaveda - Correct\nThe Atharvaveda contains many charms and prayers for agriculture, cattle and household prosperity. The term kshetra-patni, the 'mistress of the field', a female deity of cultivated land, first appears in it.\n\n(c) Ashtadhyayi - Incorrect\nPanini's Ashtadhyayi (c. 5th-4th century BCE) is a work of Sanskrit grammar. It is not the origin of this term.\n\n(d) Arthashastra - Incorrect\nKautilya's Arthashastra, a much later work on statecraft, deals with crown lands (sita) and agricultural administration but is not where the term originates."},{"i":"2026-21","y":2026,"n":21,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements with reference to India's response to climate change:\nI. India's Long-Term Low Emission Development Strategy (LT-LEDS) is a crucial tool for achieving net-zero emissions by 2070.\nII. India's 4th Biennial Update Report (BUR-4) submitted in December, 2024 recorded around 8% decrease in Greenhouse gas emissions in 2020 over 2019.\nIII. Climate-resilient development necessarily depends on quick and short-term achievement of emission reduction targets.\nWhich of the following relationships among the above statements is/are correct?\n1. Statement I is empirically supported by statement II.\n2. Statement III contradicts the approach implicit in statement I.\n3. Statement I and statement III together establish the premise of long-term sustainability.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"Statement I is correct: India's Long-Term Low Emission Development Strategy, submitted at COP27 (2022), lays out the pathway to net-zero emissions by 2070. Statement II is correct: India's fourth Biennial Update Report (December 2024) recorded that total GHG emissions in 2020 were about 7.93 percent lower than in 2019. Statement III is flawed, because climate resilience depends on sustained long-term action, not merely quick short-term targets.\n\n1. Statement I is empirically supported by statement II - Correct\nAn actual recorded fall in emissions is real-world data supporting the credibility of India's long-term low-emission strategy.\n\n2. Statement III contradicts the approach implicit in statement I - Correct\nLT-LEDS is based on a gradual, long-term transition consistent with development needs. Statement III's claim that resilience 'necessarily depends' on quick, short-term reductions runs against this approach.\n\n3. Statement I and statement III together establish the premise of long-term sustainability - Incorrect\nSince III contradicts I, the two cannot jointly establish a premise of long-term sustainability.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 2."},{"i":"2026-22","y":2026,"n":22,"s":2,"q":"With respect to the Western Hoolock Gibbons, which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. A Sanctuary in North-east India is home to this ape species listed as Endangered in the International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN) Red List.\n2. They have specialized brachiation and can easily swing between trees.\n3. They possess a strong and heavy build like gorillas, yet are remarkably agile tree climbers.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. A sanctuary in North-east India is home to this ape, listed as Endangered by IUCN - Correct\nThe Western Hoolock Gibbon (Hoolock hoolock) is India's only ape and is listed as Endangered on the IUCN Red List (Schedule I of the Wild Life Protection Act). The Hollongapar Gibbon Sanctuary in Jorhat, Assam, is named after it and holds the largest population in India.\n\n2. They have specialised brachiation and can easily swing between trees - Correct\nGibbons move by brachiation, swinging arm over arm through the canopy using very long arms and hook-like hands. They are among the fastest and most agile arboreal mammals, covering several metres in a single swing.\n\n3. They are strongly and heavily built like gorillas - Incorrect\nGibbons are 'lesser apes'. They are slender and light, weighing only about 6-9 kg, which is what allows them to brachiate. Gorillas are great apes weighing 100-200 kg. So the comparison is wrong.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 2."},{"i":"2026-23","y":2026,"n":23,"s":2,"q":"Which of the following best explain(s) the rationale for protecting mangrove ecosystems in the context of climate resilience?\n1. Mangroves reduce tidal energy and store freshwater, making them ideal sites for paddy cultivation in saline estuarine belts.\n2. Their salt-sensitive roots filter seawater, making mangroves key to converting coastal land into freshwater aquaculture zones.\n3. By withstanding tidal surges and offering biomass resources, mangroves function both as natural bio-shields and livelihood bases for rural communities.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":3,"x":"1. Mangroves store freshwater and are ideal sites for paddy cultivation - Incorrect\nMangroves do dissipate tidal and wave energy, but they grow in saline or brackish water and do not store freshwater. Clearing mangroves for paddy destroys their protective role and is a cause of mangrove loss, not a reason to protect them.\n\n2. Salt-sensitive roots filter seawater, making mangroves key to converting land into freshwater aquaculture - Incorrect\nMangrove roots are salt-tolerant, not salt-sensitive; species exclude salt by ultrafiltration or excrete it through leaves. Moreover, conversion of mangroves into aquaculture ponds (such as shrimp farms) is one of the biggest threats to mangroves, so this cannot be a rationale for protecting them.\n\n3. Mangroves act as bio-shields and livelihood bases - Correct\nDense mangrove forests reduce the force of storm surges, cyclones and tsunamis, as seen during the 1999 Odisha super cyclone and the 2004 tsunami, where villages behind mangroves suffered less. They also provide fish, crabs, honey, fuelwood and timber, supporting coastal livelihoods (e.g., in the Sundarbans and Bhitarkanika).\n\nHence the answer is (d) 3 only."},{"i":"2026-24","y":2026,"n":24,"s":1,"q":"In what way(s) does the Vizhinjam International Seaport represent a structural shift in India's maritime trade and logistics policy?\n1. By functioning exclusively as a domestic cargo hub to reduce reliance on coastal shipping and eliminate the need for foreign collaborations.\n2. By focusing primarily on passenger cruise tourism and heritage shipping to increase Kerala's profile as a maritime heritage destination.\n3. By leveraging its natural deep draft and strategic location to reduce dependence on foreign trans-shipment ports, enhance revenue retention, and reposition India in regional maritime trade.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":3,"x":"1. Functions exclusively as a domestic cargo hub and eliminates foreign collaboration - Incorrect\nVizhinjam (Thiruvananthapuram, Kerala; commissioned in 2025) is an international deep-water container trans-shipment port. It serves global shipping lines and has been developed through a public-private partnership with Adani Ports, so it is neither exclusively domestic nor free of collaboration.\n\n2. Focuses primarily on cruise tourism and heritage shipping - Incorrect\nThe port is designed to handle very large container ships; cruise tourism and heritage shipping are not its purpose.\n\n3. Uses natural deep draft and strategic location to cut dependence on foreign trans-shipment ports - Correct\nVizhinjam has a natural depth of about 18-20 metres close to the shore and lies only about 10 nautical miles from the main East-West international shipping route. It can handle mega container ships, so cargo that was earlier trans-shipped at Colombo, Singapore or Salalah can be handled in India. This saves foreign exchange, retains port revenue in India and strengthens India's position in regional maritime trade.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 3 only."},{"i":"2026-25","y":2026,"n":25,"s":5,"q":"Identify the river of the Indian sub-continent on the basis of the following information:\n1. It has an antecedent drainage system.\n2. It flows through three countries.\n3. It originates in the Tibetan Plateau and is an important river for irrigation.\n4. It does not form distributaries.\nSelect the answer from the following:","o":["Brahmaputra","Indus","Sutlej","Teesta"],"a":2,"x":"The river must be antecedent, flow through three countries, rise on the Tibetan Plateau, be important for irrigation and have no distributaries.\n\n(a) Brahmaputra - Incorrect\nIt is antecedent, rises in Tibet (Chemayungdung glacier) and flows through China, India and Bangladesh, but it forms a huge delta with many distributaries together with the Ganga, so it fails the last condition.\n\n(b) Indus - Incorrect\nIt is antecedent, rises in Tibet near Lake Mansarovar and flows through China, India and Pakistan, but it forms a delta with distributaries where it enters the Arabian Sea.\n\n(c) Sutlej - Correct\nThe Sutlej rises near Rakas Tal in Tibet, cuts through the Himalaya at Shipki La as an antecedent river, and flows through China, India and Pakistan. It is vital for irrigation (Bhakra-Nangal dam, Indira Gandhi Canal). It does not reach the sea; it joins the Chenab to form the Panjnad, so it forms no distributaries.\n\n(d) Teesta - Incorrect\nThe Teesta rises in north Sikkim (near Tso Lhamo / Zemu glacier), not on the Tibetan Plateau, and flows through only two countries, India and Bangladesh."},{"i":"2026-26","y":2026,"n":26,"s":5,"q":"Which of the following with reference to Indian States is/are not correct?\n1. Uttar Pradesh shares its boundary with the highest number of other Indian States.\n2. Rajasthan shares the longest international border among all Indian States.\n3. Sikkim is the only State that shares its boundary with just one other Indian State.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":2,"x":"The question asks which statements are NOT correct.\n\n1. Uttar Pradesh shares its boundary with the highest number of States - Correct statement\nUttar Pradesh borders eight States (Uttarakhand, Himachal Pradesh, Haryana, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand and Bihar) and also the National Capital Territory of Delhi. No other State touches as many, so this statement is correct and is not part of the answer.\n\n2. Rajasthan shares the longest international border - Not correct\nWest Bengal has the longest international border of any Indian State, about 2,217 km with Bangladesh, plus borders with Nepal and Bhutan. Rajasthan's border with Pakistan is about 1,070 km.\n\n3. Sikkim is the only State sharing its boundary with just one other State - Not correct\nSikkim touches only West Bengal, but Meghalaya also touches only one Indian State, Assam. So Sikkim is not the only such State.\n\nHence statements 2 and 3 are not correct, and the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2026-27","y":2026,"n":27,"s":2,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to the arrival of Amur Falcons at Doyang Lake in Nagaland each year from Mongolia is/are correct?\n1. It showcases how sustained local conservation efforts can contribute to the arrival and protection of international migratory birds.\n2. It reflects the global success of advanced tracking technologies that guide migratory birds back to their stopover sites.\n3. It confirms that Amur Falcons have adapted to permanent residency in India due to favourable habitat changes.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":0,"x":"1. It shows how sustained local conservation can protect international migratory birds - Correct\nAmur falcons fly from Mongolia and north-east China to southern Africa and stop over in large numbers at Doyang reservoir (Wokha district, Nagaland) in October-November. In 2012 lakhs of birds were being trapped and killed for meat. After awareness campaigns, village council resolutions and community patrolling (Pangti village), hunting stopped almost completely, and the area is now called the 'Falcon capital of the world'. This is a model of community-led conservation.\n\n2. It reflects the success of tracking technologies that guide birds back to stopover sites - Incorrect\nScientists fit satellite transmitters to some falcons to study their routes, but tracking devices only record movement; they do not guide birds. The birds navigate on their own instinct.\n\n3. It confirms that Amur falcons have adapted to permanent residency in India - Incorrect\nAmur falcons are passage migrants. They stay in Nagaland only for a few weeks to feed before crossing the Arabian Sea to Africa; they do not reside permanently in India.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 only."},{"i":"2026-28","y":2026,"n":28,"s":6,"q":"Which among the following is/are the objective(s) of the Rainfed Area Development (RAD) initiative under the National Mission for Sustainable Agriculture (NMSA)?\n1. Encouraging monoculture in rainfed areas\n2. Increasing rice cultivation in irrigated regions\n3. Enhancing productivity and minimising climatic risks through Integrated Farming Systems (IFS)\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":3,"x":"Rainfed Area Development (RAD) is a component of the National Mission for Sustainable Agriculture that follows an area-based Integrated Farming System approach.\n\n1. Encouraging monoculture in rainfed areas - Incorrect\nRAD does the opposite. It promotes diversified farming that combines crops with horticulture, livestock, fisheries, agro-forestry, apiculture and so on, because monoculture increases risk in rainfed areas.\n\n2. Increasing rice cultivation in irrigated regions - Incorrect\nRAD is meant for rainfed areas. Expanding rice in irrigated regions is neither its focus nor consistent with sustainable water use.\n\n3. Enhancing productivity and minimising climatic risks through Integrated Farming Systems - Correct\nRAD aims to raise farm productivity and reduce the impact of drought, floods and uneven rainfall by combining several farming activities, along with soil and water conservation and value addition, so that farmers have multiple sources of income.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 3 only."},{"i":"2026-29","y":2026,"n":29,"s":1,"q":"Which of the following is/are the most significant implication(s) of obtaining Oeko-Tex certification for Eri Silk in the global textile industry?\n1. It allows Indian exporters to compete in high-end markets that prioritise chemical-free products.\n2. It confirms that Eri Silk meets international safety, environmental, and quality standards, enabling its entry into premium eco-conscious markets.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"OEKO-TEX (Standard 100) is an international label certifying that every component of a textile product has been tested for harmful substances and is safe for human health. Eri silk, the 'Ahimsa silk' of Assam and the North-east made without killing the silkworm, received this certification in 2025.\n\n1. It allows Indian exporters to compete in high-end markets that prioritise chemical-free products - Correct\nBuyers in Europe, Japan and the USA, especially sustainable-fashion brands, increasingly demand certified chemical-free textiles. Certification makes Eri silk acceptable in these premium markets.\n\n2. It confirms Eri silk meets international safety, environmental and quality standards, enabling entry into premium eco-conscious markets - Correct\nThe certificate is globally recognised proof of compliance with human-ecological safety standards, which builds buyer confidence and opens eco-conscious premium segments.\n\nBoth statements describe significant implications, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2026-30","y":2026,"n":30,"s":11,"q":"Ships from which of the following countries have to cross the Strait of Hormuz to reach out to the Indian Ocean?\n1. Bahrain\n2. Syria\n3. Qatar\n4. Egypt\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2","1 and 3","2 and 3","3 and 4"],"a":1,"x":"The Strait of Hormuz connects the Persian Gulf to the Gulf of Oman and the Arabian Sea. Any country whose only coastline is inside the Persian Gulf must pass through it to reach the Indian Ocean.\n\n1. Bahrain - Correct\nBahrain is an island country inside the Persian Gulf, so its ships must cross Hormuz.\n\n2. Syria - Incorrect\nSyria's only coastline is on the Mediterranean Sea. Its ships reach the Indian Ocean through the Suez Canal, the Red Sea and Bab-el-Mandeb, not Hormuz.\n\n3. Qatar - Correct\nQatar is a peninsula jutting into the Persian Gulf; all its sea trade, including LNG exports, passes through Hormuz.\n\n4. Egypt - Incorrect\nEgypt has coasts on the Mediterranean and the Red Sea. It reaches the Indian Ocean directly via the Red Sea and Bab-el-Mandeb.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 3."},{"i":"2026-31","y":2026,"n":31,"s":11,"q":"Tungurahua Volcano, which was declared a Global Geopark by UNESCO in 2025, is situated in which one among the following countries?","o":["Ecuador","Peru","Bolivia","Colombia"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Ecuador - Correct\nTungurahua ('throat of fire') is an active stratovolcano of about 5,000 m in the Ecuadorian Andes, near the town of Baños. It has erupted repeatedly since 1999. The Tungurahua Volcano Geopark was added to the UNESCO Global Geoparks Network in 2025 for its volcanic landscape and community-based geotourism.\n\n(b) Peru - Incorrect\nPeru's famous volcanoes include El Misti and Ubinas, but not Tungurahua.\n\n(c) Bolivia - Incorrect\nBolivia has volcanoes such as Sajama and Licancabur on its borders, but Tungurahua is not in Bolivia.\n\n(d) Colombia - Incorrect\nColombia's well-known volcanoes include Nevado del Ruiz and Galeras. Tungurahua lies south of Colombia, in Ecuador."},{"i":"2026-32","y":2026,"n":32,"s":2,"q":"With reference to Madhav National Park, which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. It was declared a Tiger Reserve in India in 2025.\n2. Sakhya Sagar, which is designated as a Ramsar Site, is situated within this National Park.\n3. Its area is shared between Madhya Pradesh and Rajasthan.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. It was declared a Tiger Reserve in 2025 - Correct\nMadhav National Park in Shivpuri district, Madhya Pradesh, was notified as India's 58th Tiger Reserve (and MP's ninth) in March 2025, after tigers were reintroduced there in 2023.\n\n2. Sakhya Sagar, a Ramsar Site, is situated within the park - Correct\nSakhya Sagar is a reservoir created on the Manier river within Madhav National Park. It was designated a Ramsar Site (wetland of international importance) in 2022. It is known for mugger crocodiles and water birds.\n\n3. Its area is shared between Madhya Pradesh and Rajasthan - Incorrect\nThe park lies entirely in Madhya Pradesh (Shivpuri district). Although it is in the Chambal region near the Rajasthan border, no part of it is in Rajasthan.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 2."},{"i":"2026-33","y":2026,"n":33,"s":5,"q":"With reference to the climate of Andaman and Nicobar Islands, which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. The climate can be defined as a humid, tropical coastal climate.\n2. It receives rainfall from both South-west monsoon and North-east monsoon.\n3. Maximum precipitation is between December and May.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. The climate can be defined as humid, tropical coastal climate - Correct\nLocated near the equator (about 6°-14° N) and surrounded by sea, the Andaman and Nicobar Islands have high temperatures (23-31°C) with little seasonal variation, very high humidity and strong maritime influence.\n\n2. It receives rainfall from both the South-west and North-east monsoons - Correct\nThe islands receive heavy rain from the South-west monsoon (May to September) and also from the North-east monsoon (October to December), with total annual rainfall of about 3,000 mm. Cyclonic storms from the Bay of Bengal add to it.\n\n3. Maximum precipitation is between December and May - Incorrect\nMost rain falls between May and December during the two monsoons. January to April is the relatively dry season, so 'December to May' is wrong.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 2."},{"i":"2026-34","y":2026,"n":34,"s":5,"q":"Which of the following geographical features or phenomena is/are associated with the Peninsular Block of India?\n1. Submergence of parts of the western coast due to tectonic activity\n2. Presence of residual mountain ranges such as the Veliconda hills and Mahendragiri hills\n3. Deep, V-shaped river valleys formed by fast-flowing rivers\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. Submergence of parts of the western coast due to tectonic activity - Correct\nNCERT (India: Physical Environment) states that the Peninsular block has undergone faulting and that some parts of the western coast were submerged under the sea following tectonic activity. The straight western coast and the Western Ghats scarp are linked to this.\n\n2. Presence of residual mountain ranges such as Veliconda and Mahendragiri hills - Correct\nThe Peninsula is an ancient landmass, and its mountains are worn-down remnants of older ranges. NCERT lists relict (residual) mountains such as the Aravalli, Nallamala, Javadi, Veliconda, Palkonda and Mahendragiri hills.\n\n3. Deep V-shaped valleys formed by fast-flowing rivers - Incorrect\nDeep V-shaped valleys and gorges are typical of young mountains like the Himalaya, where rivers are still actively cutting down. Peninsular rivers are old, have reached a graded profile and flow in broad, shallow valleys.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 2."},{"i":"2026-35","y":2026,"n":35,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements with reference to the Sagarmala Programme of the Government of India:\nI. The Sagarmala Programme seeks to achieve port-led economic growth through cost-effective and sustainable coastal infrastructure.\nII. The success of the Sagarmala Programme is reflected in significant growth in coastal and inland waterway shipping, along with improved global port rankings.\nIII. Sagarmala 2.0 aims to position India as a global maritime innovation hub aligned with Atmanirbhar Bharat and Viksit Bharat 2047 visions.\nWhich of the following relationships among the above statements is/are correct?\n1. Statement II validates the effectiveness of the strategies envisioned in statement I.\n2. Statement III extends the objectives of statement I by embedding them into a future-oriented innovation framework.\n3. Statement I contradicts statement III by focusing only on traditional infrastructure instead of modern innovation.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"Background: Sagarmala (2015) aims at port-led development through port modernisation, connectivity, port-led industrialisation and coastal community development. Statement II reflects its outcomes, such as large growth in coastal shipping and inland waterway cargo and improved rankings of Indian ports in global indices. Statement III reflects the 'Sagarmala 2.0' vision of innovation-driven maritime growth aligned with Atmanirbhar Bharat and Viksit Bharat 2047.\n\n1. Statement II validates the effectiveness of the strategies in statement I - Correct\nMeasured outcomes (more coastal and waterway traffic, better port rankings) are evidence that the port-led strategy is working.\n\n2. Statement III extends the objectives of statement I into a future-oriented innovation framework - Correct\nSagarmala 2.0 builds on the original objectives and adds innovation, shipbuilding, technology and long-term national goals.\n\n3. Statement I contradicts statement III - Incorrect\nInfrastructure-led growth and innovation are complementary; the new phase builds on the first, so there is no contradiction.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 2."},{"i":"2026-36","y":2026,"n":36,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements about Rhynchostylis retusa (Foxtail orchid):\n1. It is an epiphytic orchid.\n2. The species is endemic to North-east India.\n3. It is the State flower of Arunachal Pradesh and Assam.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","1 and 3","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. It is an epiphytic orchid - Correct\nRhynchostylis retusa (foxtail orchid) grows on the trunks and branches of trees, drawing moisture from the air, which is the definition of an epiphyte. It bears long, drooping, fox-tail-like clusters of pink and white flowers.\n\n2. The species is endemic to North-east India - Incorrect\nIt is widely distributed across tropical South and South-east Asia, including India (also the Western and Eastern Ghats), Sri Lanka, Myanmar, Thailand, Laos, Indonesia and the Philippines. It is not confined to the North-east.\n\n3. It is the State flower of Arunachal Pradesh and Assam - Correct\nIt is the State flower of Assam, where it is called 'Kopou phool' and worn by women during Bihu, and also of Arunachal Pradesh.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 3."},{"i":"2026-37","y":2026,"n":37,"s":10,"q":"Which one of the following statements with regard to the Moidams, built by the Tai-Ahom kingdom and inscribed as a World Heritage Site by UNESCO, is/are correct?\n1. They acted as army fortresses.\n2. They were recreation centres of the Royals and Nobles.\n3. They were burial grounds of the Royals and Nobles.\n4. They were battle drill centres of the Royals and Nobles.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 3","3 only","2 and 4"],"a":2,"x":"The Moidams of Charaideo, Assam, were inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2024 as India's 43rd site and the first cultural site from the North-east.\n\n1. They acted as army fortresses - Incorrect\nMoidams are burial mounds, not military structures.\n\n2. They were recreation centres of the royals and nobles - Incorrect\nRecreational structures of the Ahoms include the Rang Ghar, a royal pavilion for watching sports, but moidams were not used for recreation.\n\n3. They were burial grounds of the royals and nobles - Correct\nMoidams are earthen mounds built over vaults in which Tai-Ahom kings, queens and nobles were buried along with objects for the afterlife, between the 13th and 19th centuries. Charaideo was the first Ahom capital, founded by Sukaphaa.\n\n4. They were battle-drill centres - Incorrect\nThey had no military training function.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 3 only."},{"i":"2026-38","y":2026,"n":38,"s":2,"q":"At the United Nations Ocean Conference (UNOC) held in June, 2025 in France, the Food and Agricultural Organization (FAO) of the United Nations demonstrated its leading voice on marine and ocean issues, especially on sustainable fisheries and aquaculture for resilient livelihood and \"Blue Transformation\". \nWhich of the following combinations about the \"Four Betters\" proposed by FAO for \"Blue Transformation\" is correct?","o":["Better production, better nutrition, better environment and better ocean","Better production, better nutrition, better environment and better life","Better coral reefs, better nutrition, better environment and better life","Better estuaries, better nutrition, better environment and better mangrove vegetation"],"a":1,"x":"FAO's Strategic Framework 2022-2031 is organised around the 'Four Betters': better production, better nutrition, a better environment and a better life, leaving no one behind. 'Blue Transformation' is FAO's roadmap for aquatic food systems (fisheries and aquaculture) to deliver these four betters.\n\n(a) Better production, better nutrition, better environment and better ocean - Incorrect\n'Better ocean' is not one of the four betters; the fourth is better life.\n\n(b) Better production, better nutrition, better environment and better life - Correct\nThis is the exact set of the Four Betters.\n\n(c) Better coral reefs, better nutrition, better environment and better life - Incorrect\n'Better coral reefs' replaces 'better production', which is wrong.\n\n(d) Better estuaries, better nutrition, better environment and better mangrove vegetation - Incorrect\nNeither estuaries nor mangroves are among the Four Betters."},{"i":"2026-39","y":2026,"n":39,"s":11,"q":"Which of the following statements with reference to Lake Turkana is/are correct?\n1. It is the largest desert lake in the world.\n2. The lake is situated in South Sudan along the eastern fringe of the Sahara desert.\n3. The lake is listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site and is also referred to as the 'Jade Sea'.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. It is the largest desert lake in the world - Correct\nLake Turkana in the Kenyan Rift Valley is the world's largest permanent desert lake and also the world's largest alkaline lake, surrounded by arid volcanic landscape.\n\n2. It is situated in South Sudan along the eastern fringe of the Sahara - Incorrect\nThe lake lies mostly in north-western Kenya, with its northern tip in Ethiopia, fed mainly by the Omo river. It is not in South Sudan and not on the Sahara's fringe.\n\n3. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and is called the 'Jade Sea' - Correct\nLake Turkana National Parks were inscribed as a World Heritage Site in 1997 for their fossil sites (Koobi Fora) and wildlife. The lake's striking turquoise-green colour, caused by algae, gives it the name 'Jade Sea'.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 3 only."},{"i":"2026-40","y":2026,"n":40,"s":2,"q":"Which one of the following is the first Plan Vivo certified Reducing Emissions from Deforestation and Forest Degradation (REDD+) project in India?","o":["Uttarakhand REDD+ project","ICFRE-ICIMOD Transboundary REDD+ project in North-Eastern Himalayas","Khasi Hills Community REDD+ project","Sikkim Mamley Kamrang Community REDD+ project"],"a":2,"x":"REDD+ projects earn carbon credits for reducing deforestation and forest degradation. Plan Vivo is an international standard for community-based land-use and forestry carbon projects.\n\n(a) Uttarakhand REDD+ project - Incorrect\nUttarakhand has pilot REDD+ efforts, but it was not the first Plan Vivo-certified project in India.\n\n(b) ICFRE-ICIMOD Transboundary REDD+ project in the North-eastern Himalayas - Incorrect\nThis is a research and capacity-building initiative, not the first Plan Vivo-certified project.\n\n(c) Khasi Hills Community REDD+ project - Correct\nRun by the Ka Synjuk ki Hima Arliang Wah Umiam Mawphlang federation of indigenous Khasi governments in East Khasi Hills, Meghalaya, it became India's first Plan Vivo-certified REDD+ project (around 2012-13) and has sold carbon credits, with revenue shared by local communities who protect and restore their sacred and community forests.\n\n(d) Sikkim Mamley Kamrang Community REDD+ project - Incorrect\nThis is a later community project, not the first."},{"i":"2026-41","y":2026,"n":41,"s":3,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to genetic medicine is/are correct?\n1. Genetic medicines correct/compensate for the faulty genes responsible for disease.\n2. Engineered viruses and lipid nanoparticles are used as carriers of the genetic medicine.\n3. Genetic medicines alter the entire DNA sequence.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":2,"x":"1. Genetic medicines correct or compensate for faulty genes causing disease - Correct\nGenetic medicines include gene therapy (adding a working copy of a gene), gene editing (CRISPR to repair a mutation) and RNA-based drugs (silencing or replacing faulty messages). Their purpose is to correct or compensate for genes responsible for disease, as in spinal muscular atrophy or sickle cell disease.\n\n2. Engineered viruses and lipid nanoparticles are used as carriers - Correct\nTo deliver genetic material into cells, scientists use modified viruses (adeno-associated virus, lentivirus) that cannot cause disease, and non-viral carriers like lipid nanoparticles, which were also used in mRNA COVID vaccines.\n\n3. Genetic medicines alter the entire DNA sequence - Incorrect\nThey target specific genes or sequences only. Altering the entire genome is neither the aim nor possible with current therapies.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2026-42","y":2026,"n":42,"s":3,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to Large Language Models (LLMs) used in machine learning is/are correct?\n1. LLMs assign probabilities to the next possible words and then pick the one with the highest probability.\n2. LLMs process data through mathematical optimization to minimise prediction errors.\n3. LLMs produce unbiased outputs.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. LLMs assign probabilities to possible next words and pick the one with the highest probability - Correct (as per key)\nA large language model predicts text one token at a time. For each position it computes a probability distribution over its whole vocabulary and chooses the next token; choosing the most probable one is called greedy decoding. (In practice, sampling methods with 'temperature' are also used to add variety, but the core mechanism is probability-based next-word prediction.)\n\n2. LLMs process data through mathematical optimisation to minimise prediction errors - Correct\nDuring training, the model's billions of parameters are adjusted by gradient descent to minimise a loss function that measures the difference between predicted and actual next tokens.\n\n3. LLMs produce unbiased outputs - Incorrect\nLLMs learn from large amounts of human-generated text, which contains social, cultural and other biases. They can reproduce or even amplify these biases, which is why bias mitigation is a major area of AI safety work.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2026-43","y":2026,"n":43,"s":3,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to stealth technology is/are correct?\n1. Stealth objects have a very small radar cross-section and are coated with Radar Absorbing Material.\n2. Stealth objects can be detected using specific frequencies.\n3. Stealth objects are coated with metamaterials to increase the scattering of electromagnetic radiation.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":2,"x":"1. Stealth objects have a very small radar cross-section and are coated with Radar Absorbing Material - Correct\nStealth aircraft and ships are shaped to deflect radar waves away from the source and coated with radar-absorbing materials (RAM) that convert radar energy into heat. Together these give a very small radar cross-section, making the object appear as small as a bird on radar.\n\n2. Stealth objects can be detected using specific frequencies - Correct\nStealth design is optimised against high-frequency (X-band) fire-control radars. Low-frequency (VHF/UHF) radars, multistatic radars and passive radars that use ambient signals can still detect stealth platforms, though less precisely.\n\n3. Stealth objects are coated with metamaterials to increase scattering of radiation - Incorrect\nMetamaterials are being researched to absorb or bend radar waves around an object (cloaking) to reduce the return signal. Increasing scattering back towards the radar would make the object more visible, which is the opposite of stealth.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2026-44","y":2026,"n":44,"s":3,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to Black Boxes used in modern aircrafts is/are correct?\n1. They carry a beacon emitting red light pulses to facilitate underwater detection.\n2. They record both the cockpit voice and flight data.\n3. Their memory units are made using either stainless steel or titanium.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. They carry a beacon emitting red light pulses for underwater detection - Incorrect\nBlack boxes carry an Underwater Locator Beacon that emits acoustic (ultrasonic) pulses at 37.5 kHz for about 30-90 days after being immersed in water, detectable by sonar. Light does not travel far underwater, so red light pulses would be useless. (The boxes are actually painted bright orange.)\n\n2. They record both cockpit voice and flight data - Correct\nThe 'black box' consists of the Cockpit Voice Recorder, which records pilots' conversations and cockpit sounds, and the Flight Data Recorder, which records parameters such as altitude, speed, heading and engine performance. Some aircraft use a combined unit.\n\n3. Their memory units are made using stainless steel or titanium - Correct\nThe crash-survivable memory unit is housed in a casing of stainless steel or titanium with thermal insulation, designed to withstand huge impact forces, fire of about 1,100°C and deep-sea pressure.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2026-45","y":2026,"n":45,"s":5,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to Green Hydrogen is/are correct?\n1. It is decarbonized hydrogen obtained from natural gas reforming combined with carbon capture and storage (CCS).\n2. It is produced using electrolysis of water with electricity generated by renewable energy.\n3. National Green Hydrogen Mission of India aims for abatement of nearly 50 MMT of annual greenhouse gas emissions by 2030.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 and 3 only","2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. Hydrogen from natural gas reforming combined with carbon capture and storage - Incorrect\nHydrogen made from natural gas by steam methane reforming is called grey hydrogen; if the CO2 released is captured and stored, it is called blue hydrogen. It is not green hydrogen.\n\n2. Produced by electrolysis of water using renewable electricity - Correct\nGreen hydrogen is produced by splitting water into hydrogen and oxygen with electricity from renewable sources such as solar or wind, so there are almost no carbon emissions. India's Green Hydrogen Standard (2023) also allows biomass routes, with a limit of 2 kg CO2 per kg of hydrogen.\n\n3. The National Green Hydrogen Mission aims to abate nearly 50 MMT of annual GHG emissions by 2030 - Correct\nThe Mission (January 2023, outlay about 19,744 crore rupees) targets production of at least 5 million tonnes of green hydrogen a year by 2030, with about 125 GW of additional renewable capacity and abatement of nearly 50 million tonnes of annual GHG emissions.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2026-46","y":2026,"n":46,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements with regard to involvement of private entities in India's space programme:\n1. The Indian National Space Promotion and Authorisation Centre (IN-SPACe) is an autonomous agency formed to facilitate participation of private entities.\n2. Agnikul Cosmos launched the world's first flight using 3D-printed rocket engine.\n3. Skyroot Aerospace has developed liquid fuel for GSLV.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":2,"x":"1. IN-SPACe is an autonomous agency formed to facilitate private participation - Correct\nThe Indian National Space Promotion and Authorisation Centre (2020) is an autonomous, single-window nodal agency under the Department of Space. It promotes, authorises and supervises space activities of private companies, including use of ISRO facilities.\n\n2. Agnikul Cosmos launched the world's first flight using a 3D-printed rocket engine - Correct\nOn 30 May 2024 Agnikul's 'Agnibaan SOrTeD' lifted off from its own private launch pad at Sriharikota. It was powered by Agnilet, the world's first single-piece 3D-printed semi-cryogenic rocket engine.\n\n3. Skyroot Aerospace has developed liquid fuel for GSLV - Incorrect\nSkyroot Aerospace built Vikram-S, India's first privately developed rocket launched in November 2022, and is developing the Vikram series of small launch vehicles. It has not developed fuel for ISRO's GSLV.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2026-47","y":2026,"n":47,"s":3,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to drone swarms is/are correct?\n1. They use Terahertz band of frequency to communicate with the command centre.\n2. Individual drones in the swarm can communicate with other drones in the swarm.\n3. GPS Spoofing is a commonly used technique to counter drone swarm attack.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. They use the Terahertz band to communicate with the command centre - Incorrect\nDrone swarms usually communicate over radio frequencies such as 2.4 GHz and 5.8 GHz, or military UHF and L-band data links, and sometimes satellite links. The Terahertz band is still largely experimental because of its very short range and high absorption in the atmosphere.\n\n2. Individual drones can communicate with other drones in the swarm - Correct\nA swarm works through drone-to-drone communication (mesh networking), sharing position and task data so the group can act collectively and autonomously even if some drones are lost.\n\n3. GPS spoofing is a commonly used technique to counter drone swarms - Correct\nCounter-drone systems commonly use GPS jamming and spoofing (sending false navigation signals so drones lose their way or land), along with radio-frequency jamming, lasers and interceptor drones.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2026-48","y":2026,"n":48,"s":3,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to GenomeIndia Project is/are correct?\n1. It is a part of the Human Genome Project.\n2. The project is funded by the Department of Biotechnology (DBT), Government of India.\n3. Its primary aim is to build a catalogue of genetic diversity of the Indian population.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. It is a part of the Human Genome Project - Incorrect\nThe Human Genome Project was an international effort (1990-2003) that produced the first reference human genome. GenomeIndia, launched in 2020, is a separate Indian national project.\n\n2. It is funded by the Department of Biotechnology - Correct\nGenomeIndia is funded by the DBT, Government of India, and was coordinated by the Centre for Brain Research at IISc Bengaluru with about 20 institutions.\n\n3. Its primary aim is to build a catalogue of genetic diversity of the Indian population - Correct\nThe project sequenced 10,000 genomes from 83 diverse population groups across India, and the data was released in 2024-25 through the Indian Biological Data Centre. It helps in understanding disease risks, developing diagnostics and personalised medicine for Indians.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2026-49","y":2026,"n":49,"s":3,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to the National Quantum Mission (NQM) is/are correct?\n1. It aims at developing intermediate-scale quantum computers with 50-1000 physical qubits.\n2. Its implementation includes setting up of four Thematic Hubs (T-Hubs) in academic and national R&D institutes across India.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"1. It aims at developing intermediate-scale quantum computers with 50-1000 physical qubits - Correct\nThe National Quantum Mission (approved April 2023, about 6,003 crore rupees for 2023-24 to 2030-31) aims to develop intermediate-scale quantum computers with 50-1000 physical qubits within eight years, on platforms such as superconducting and photonic systems. It also targets satellite-based quantum communication over 2,000 km, and quantum sensors and materials.\n\n2. It includes setting up four Thematic Hubs (T-Hubs) in academic and national R&D institutes - Correct\nFour T-Hubs have been set up: quantum computing at IISc Bengaluru, quantum communication at IIT Madras (with C-DOT), quantum sensing and metrology at IIT Bombay, and quantum materials and devices at IIT Delhi.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2026-50","y":2026,"n":50,"s":3,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to India's Deep Ocean Mission is/are correct?\n1. It was launched by the Ministry of Ports, Shipping and Waterways, Government of India.\n2. Matsya-6000 has been designed to carry 3 people for deep sea exploration.\n3. Samudrayaan is a project under this mission.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. It was launched by the Ministry of Ports, Shipping and Waterways - Incorrect\nThe Deep Ocean Mission (approved 2021, about 4,077 crore rupees) is implemented by the Ministry of Earth Sciences. It covers deep-sea mining technology, a manned submersible, ocean climate advisory services, deep-sea biodiversity, ocean energy and marine biology.\n\n2. Matsya-6000 is designed to carry 3 people - Correct\nMatsya-6000, developed by the National Institute of Ocean Technology (NIOT), Chennai, is a manned submersible designed to take three people to a depth of 6,000 metres in a titanium-alloy sphere.\n\n3. Samudrayaan is a project under this mission - Correct\nSamudrayaan is India's first manned deep-ocean mission, using Matsya-6000, and is a component of the Deep Ocean Mission. It will make India one of a few countries with such capability.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2026-51","y":2026,"n":51,"s":12,"q":"Mr. X, a senior officer, was overseeing a critical vaccination programme during a pandemic. He found that a private service provider responsible for vaccine distribution was compromising on quality to make profits. Despite immense pressure to manage the issue due to vested interests, he raised his voice based on the principles of public administration which he learnt during various training programmes attended across his career. He reported the issue to the appropriate vigilance authority and halted the contract to ensure citizen welfare.\nWhich one among the following principles of public administration was most strongly demonstrated by Mr. X's actions?","o":["Esprit de corps","Equity","Accountability","Delegation"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Esprit de corps - Incorrect\nEsprit de corps is Henri Fayol's principle of team spirit and harmony among staff. Mr. X acted against a private contractor to protect citizens, not to build team spirit, so this is not the principle shown.\n\n(b) Equity - Incorrect\nEquity means fair and just treatment of employees and citizens. It is a related value, but the central issue here is holding a service provider answerable for poor quality and reporting wrongdoing.\n\n(c) Accountability - Correct\nAccountability means being answerable for the use of public resources and for results. Mr. X held the contractor accountable for compromising vaccine quality, reported the matter to the vigilance authority (the proper channel) and halted the contract to protect citizen welfare, despite pressure from vested interests. He also discharged his own responsibility as the officer in charge. This most strongly reflects accountability.\n\n(d) Delegation - Incorrect\nDelegation is the transfer of authority to subordinates. Nothing in the case concerns delegating work."},{"i":"2026-52","y":2026,"n":52,"s":12,"q":"In a multi-ethnic district where both economic competition and historical grievances frequently led to community tensions, a flashpoint has arisen with a Government decision to allocate land for a waste management facility near a tribal hamlet, sparking protests by the tribal community, which claimed that the land was sacred and critical to their cultural identity. At the same time, urban residents and local industries supported the project, citing severe solid waste challenges and health concerns due to lack of a proper disposal site. The conflict has escalated with road blockades, social media campaigns, and allegations of police excesses. \nAs a responsible Government official, you are tasked with resolving the situation through mediation, ensuring a sustainable outcome that balances environmental needs, tribal rights, and urban public health.\nConsider the following statements with reference to the above:\n1. A successful conflict resolution process must begin with acknowledging the cultural concerns of the protesting tribal community before discussing technical alternatives.\n2. The Government should move ahead with the project without delay to address urban health concerns, which outweigh the sentiments of a small group.\n3. Creating a multi-stakeholder dialogue platform — including tribal leaders, environmental experts, and municipal representatives — to build mutual understanding and help de-escalate tensions.\n4. Conducting an independent Environmental and Social Impact Assessment (ESIA) and sharing findings transparently with both sides to facilitate evidence-based decision-making.\nWhich of the statements given above would contribute to the resolution process?","o":["1, 3 and 4 only","2, 3 and 4 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"a":0,"x":"1. Begin by acknowledging the cultural concerns of the tribal community - Contributes\nConflict resolution begins with recognising the grievance of the aggrieved party. Acknowledging that the land is sacred and important to identity builds trust and brings the community to the table. It is also consistent with the rights of tribals under the Fifth Schedule, PESA and the Forest Rights Act, which require consultation and consent.\n\n2. Move ahead without delay because urban health concerns outweigh the sentiments of a small group - Does not contribute\nThis majoritarian approach dismisses tribal rights and would escalate protests and violence. It ignores the requirement of consultation and does not produce a sustainable outcome.\n\n3. Create a multi-stakeholder dialogue platform - Contributes\nBringing tribal leaders, environmental experts and municipal representatives together allows each side to understand the other's concerns, reduces mistrust and helps de-escalate tensions. It can also explore alternatives such as a different site or technology.\n\n4. Conduct an independent Environmental and Social Impact Assessment and share it transparently - Contributes\nAn independent ESIA gives objective evidence on environmental, health and cultural impacts. Sharing it openly with both sides supports evidence-based decisions and builds credibility.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1, 3 and 4 only."},{"i":"2026-53","y":2026,"n":53,"s":12,"q":"Ms. X is a mid-level civil service official working in the urban development department of a major city. Recently, she was involved in approving a contract for a public infrastructure project — a new community park. During the approval process, she received a piece of confidential information indicating that one of the shortlisted contractors had a history of poor workmanship and allegations of corruption in other cities, though nothing had been legally proven. The Head of the Department, Mr. Y, advised her not to disclose this information to the project committee or the public because it could delay the project and damage the city's reputation. However, Ms. X believed that withholding such information compromised transparency and public trust.\n\nWhat amongst the following should Ms. X do now?\n1. Immediately disclose the information to the project committee and the public\n2. Recommend removing the contractor from the shortlist to protect the project's integrity\n3. Propose a 'limited disclosure' to an oversight committee, while keeping the information confidential from the public for the time being\n\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2 only","3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. Immediately disclose the information to the project committee and the public - Not appropriate\nThe information is confidential and the allegations are not proven. Making them public immediately could be defamatory, violate confidentiality rules and prejudice a fair process, and it could unfairly damage the contractor.\n\n2. Recommend removing the contractor from the shortlist - Not appropriate\nRemoving a bidder on the basis of unverified allegations violates the principles of natural justice and fair procurement (General Financial Rules require objective criteria). The contractor must first be given the chance to respond, and the facts must be verified.\n\n3. Propose limited disclosure to an oversight committee, keeping it confidential from the public for now - Appropriate\nThis balances transparency and integrity with confidentiality and fairness. The oversight body can verify the information and take an informed decision, so the concern is not buried as Mr. Y wanted, but due process is also followed. It reflects both courage of conviction and prudence.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 3 only."},{"i":"2026-54","y":2026,"n":54,"s":0,"q":"X' was addressing a seminar on the meaning of the term 'law' as provided under Article 13, Part III of the Constitution of India. 'X' explained that the meaning of the term 'law' in the Constitution of India was very comprehensive. It included ordinances, orders and even rules and regulations. 'Y' pointed out that the term 'law' in Article 13 also included custom or usage having in the territory of India the force of law, to which 'X' was not convinced.\n\nBased on the above, select the correct conclusion from the options given below:","o":["'X' is correct in the interpretation of law, including the view on non-inclusion of custom.","The view of 'Y' that 'law' included custom is not correct.","The views of both 'X' and 'Y' are correct.","The view of only 'Y' is correct."],"a":3,"x":"Article 13(3)(a) defines 'law' to include 'any Ordinance, order, bye-law, rule, regulation, notification, custom or usage having in the territory of India the force of law'. Any such law that violates Fundamental Rights is void under Article 13.\n\n(a) X is correct, including the view that custom is not included - Incorrect\nX is right that ordinances, orders, rules and regulations are included, but wrong in rejecting custom, which is expressly included.\n\n(b) Y's view that law includes custom is not correct - Incorrect\nY's view is directly supported by the text of Article 13(3)(a).\n\n(c) The views of both X and Y are correct - Incorrect\nX's position that custom is excluded is wrong, so both cannot be correct.\n\n(d) The view of only Y is correct - Correct\nOn the point in dispute, whether custom or usage is included, only Y is right. This is why customs (for example, discriminatory customary practices) can be struck down if they violate Fundamental Rights."},{"i":"2026-55","y":2026,"n":55,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements with reference to the Constitution of India:\n1. There is no Article in the Constitution of India that specifies that the Constitution of India will be officially called the 'Constitution of India'.\n2. There is no Article in the Constitution of India that specifies that the Indian Independence Act, 1947 and the Government of India Act, 1935 stand repealed.\n3. There is no Article in the Constitution of India that mentions 26th January, 1950 as the date of the commencement of the Constitution of India.\nWhich one of the following conclusions based on the above statements is correct?","o":["All three statements are correct.","There is no correct statement.","There are two correct statements that include statement 3.","There is only one correct statement."],"a":1,"x":"1. No Article specifies that the Constitution will be called the 'Constitution of India' - Incorrect\nArticle 393 states: 'This Constitution may be called the Constitution of India.'\n\n2. No Article specifies that the Indian Independence Act, 1947 and the Government of India Act, 1935 stand repealed - Incorrect\nArticle 395 repeals the Indian Independence Act, 1947 and the Government of India Act, 1935, together with the enactments amending or supplementing the latter (except the Abolition of Privy Council Jurisdiction Act, 1949).\n\n3. No Article mentions 26 January 1950 as the date of commencement - Incorrect\nArticle 394 says that certain Articles came into force at once (26 November 1949) and that the remaining provisions shall come into force on the twenty-sixth day of January 1950, 'which day is referred to in this Constitution as the commencement of this Constitution'.\n\nAll three statements are incorrect, so the answer is (b) There is no correct statement."},{"i":"2026-56","y":2026,"n":56,"s":0,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to the persons with disabilities in India is/are correct?\n1. The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, an Act passed by the Parliament of India in 2018, mandates reservation in education and employment, places a legal duty on Governments to ensure accessibility and non-discrimination.\n2. The Sugamya Bharat Abhiyan focuses on achieving universal accessibility for Persons with Disabilities across three key domains — built infrastructure, transport systems and information and communication technology.\n3. The National Divyangjan Finance and Development Corporation (NDFDC) is a public sector organisation set up by the Ministry of Corporate Affairs as a not-for-profit company to promote entrepreneurship among Persons with Disabilities (PwDs).\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2","2 only","1 and 3","1 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act was passed in 2018 - Incorrect\nThe RPwD Act was passed in 2016 and came into force in April 2017, replacing the 1995 Act. Its content is otherwise correctly described: it raised reservation in government jobs to 4 percent and in higher education to 5 percent, increased recognised disabilities from 7 to 21, and imposes duties on governments to ensure accessibility and non-discrimination. The wrong year makes the statement incorrect.\n\n2. Sugamya Bharat Abhiyan focuses on accessibility in built infrastructure, transport and ICT - Correct\nThe Accessible India Campaign, launched in December 2015, targets three verticals: the built environment (public buildings), the transportation system (airports, railways, buses) and the information and communication technology ecosystem (websites, TV).\n\n3. NDFDC was set up by the Ministry of Corporate Affairs - Incorrect\nThe National Divyangjan Finance and Development Corporation (1997) is a not-for-profit company under the Department of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities, Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment. It provides concessional loans for self-employment of persons with disabilities.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2026-57","y":2026,"n":57,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements about the provisions pertaining to the Scheduled Castes and the Scheduled Tribes in India:\n1. Provisions regarding the administration of the Tribal Areas in the States of Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram are given in the Fifth Schedule of the Constitution of India.\n2. Some tribes of India are entitled to exemption from paying Income Tax on certain incomes.\n3. The Constitution of India provides for reservation of seats in Panchayats for women belonging to the Scheduled Castes and the Scheduled Tribes.\nWhich one of the following conclusions based on the above statements is correct?","o":["There are two correct statements, that include statement 2.","There are two correct statements, that are statements 1 and 3.","There is only one correct statement.","All three statements are correct."],"a":0,"x":"1. Administration of Tribal Areas of Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram is in the Fifth Schedule - Incorrect\nThese four States are covered by the Sixth Schedule, which provides for Autonomous District Councils. The Fifth Schedule deals with Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes in other States, such as Jharkhand, Odisha and Madhya Pradesh.\n\n2. Some tribes are exempt from paying income tax on certain incomes - Correct\nSection 10(26) of the Income-tax Act, 1961 exempts income of members of Scheduled Tribes residing in specified areas of Arunachal Pradesh, Manipur, Mizoram, Nagaland, Tripura, parts of Assam and Ladakh, if the income arises from sources in those areas.\n\n3. The Constitution provides reservation in Panchayats for SC and ST women - Correct\nArticle 243D(3) provides that not less than one-third of the seats reserved for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes shall be reserved for women belonging to those groups.\n\nSo two statements (2 and 3) are correct, and the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2026-58","y":2026,"n":58,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of questions asked by the Members in the Parliament of India:\n1. Unstarred questions are those to which a Member desires an oral answer in the House.\n2. Starred questions are those to which a Member desires a written answer.\n3. No supplementary question can be asked on an unstarred question.\nWhich one of the following conclusions based on the above statements is correct?","o":["All the three statements are correct.","There are two correct statements, that include statement 2.","There is only one correct statement.","There is no correct statement."],"a":2,"x":"1. Unstarred questions are those to which a member desires an oral answer - Incorrect\nAn unstarred question is answered in writing; the answer is laid on the table of the House and no oral answer is given.\n\n2. Starred questions are those to which a member desires a written answer - Incorrect\nA starred question, marked with an asterisk, is answered orally on the floor of the House during Question Hour.\n\n3. No supplementary question can be asked on an unstarred question - Correct\nSince unstarred questions receive only written answers, there is no occasion for supplementary questions. Supplementaries can be asked only on starred questions, after the oral answer.\n\n(Short notice questions, on matters of urgent public importance, are answered orally with less than the usual 10 days' notice.)\n\nOnly one statement is correct, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2026-59","y":2026,"n":59,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements about the Committee on the Welfare of Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes of the Parliament of India:\n1. Although members of this Committee are elected from both Houses of Parliament, the Chairperson of this Committee is appointed by the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha.\n2. Twenty members are elected by the Rajya Sabha and ten members by the Lok Sabha.\n3. No Minister, except for the Union Minister of Social Justice and Empowerment, is eligible to be a member of this Committee.\n4. Members are elected for a fixed term of two years from the date they enter their office.\nWhich one of the following conclusions based on the above statements is correct?","o":["There are four correct statements.","There is only one correct statement, that is statement 2.","There are two correct statements, that include statement 1.","There is no correct statement."],"a":3,"x":"1. The Chairperson is appointed by the Chairman of the Rajya Sabha - Incorrect\nThe Committee on the Welfare of SCs and STs (constituted in 1968) has its Chairperson appointed by the Speaker of the Lok Sabha from among the Lok Sabha members of the Committee.\n\n2. Twenty members are elected by the Rajya Sabha and ten by the Lok Sabha - Incorrect\nIt is the reverse: of 30 members, 20 are from the Lok Sabha and 10 from the Rajya Sabha, elected by proportional representation by single transferable vote.\n\n3. No Minister except the Minister of Social Justice can be a member - Incorrect\nNo Minister can be a member of the Committee at all; there is no exception.\n\n4. Members are elected for a fixed term of two years - Incorrect\nThe term of office of members does not exceed one year.\n\nAll four statements are incorrect, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2026-60","y":2026,"n":60,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements about Mission Sudarshan Chakra of India:\n1. It aims to enhance India's air defence, ballistic missile defence and aerial offensive capabilities.\n2. This Mission is being designed to enhance rapid, precise, and powerful defence responses, reinforcing India's strategic autonomy.\n3. One of the aims of this Mission is to cover all public places of India by an expanded nationwide security shield by 2035.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 only"],"a":0,"x":"Mission Sudarshan Chakra was announced by the Prime Minister in his Independence Day speech on 15 August 2025, named after Lord Krishna's discus.\n\n1. It aims to enhance air defence, ballistic missile defence and aerial offensive capabilities - Correct\nThe mission envisages an integrated, multi-layered shield that can detect and neutralise enemy aircraft, drones and missiles, and also respond with offensive capability, combining surveillance, cyber and space-based systems.\n\n2. It is designed for rapid, precise and powerful defence responses, reinforcing strategic autonomy - Correct\nThe system is to be researched, developed and manufactured in India, strengthening self-reliance in defence and allowing precise, powerful responses.\n\n3. It aims to cover all public places with an expanded nationwide security shield by 2035 - Correct\nThe PM stated that by 2035 the security shield will be expanded to cover important places across the country, including strategic, civilian and religious sites and other public places.\n\nSince all three are correct, the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2026-61","y":2026,"n":61,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements about river bridges connecting India with neighbouring countries:\n1. 'Maitri Setu', built over Feni river, connects Ramgarh in India with Sabroom in Bangladesh.\n2. Jhulaghat suspension bridge connects India with Myanmar.\n3. Mechi bridge and its approaches connect Panitanki Bypass in India with Kakarvitta in Nepal.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2","2 and 3","1 only","3 only"],"a":3,"x":"1. Maitri Setu over the Feni connects Ramgarh in India with Sabroom in Bangladesh - Incorrect\nMaitri Setu (inaugurated 2021) over the Feni river connects Sabroom in Tripura (India) with Ramgarh in Bangladesh. The statement reverses the two places. The bridge gives Tripura access to Chittagong port.\n\n2. Jhulaghat suspension bridge connects India with Myanmar - Incorrect\nJhulaghat is on the Kali (Mahakali) river in Pithoragarh district, Uttarakhand, and connects India with Nepal. India-Myanmar crossings are in Manipur and Mizoram (e.g., Moreh-Tamu).\n\n3. Mechi bridge connects Panitanki bypass in India with Kakarvitta in Nepal - Correct\nThe new Mechi bridge on the Mechi river, built with Indian assistance on Asian Highway 2, links Panitanki in Darjeeling district, West Bengal, with Kakarbhitta in Jhapa district, Nepal.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 3 only."},{"i":"2026-62","y":2026,"n":62,"s":0,"q":"Which of the following statements about a Zero First Information Report (Zero FIR) under the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita (BNSS), 2023 is/are correct?\n1. A Zero FIR can be lodged at a police station, even though the place of commission of a cognizable/non-cognizable offence is outside the territorial jurisdiction of that police station.\n2. The Officer-in-Charge of the police station where a Zero FIR has been lodged may, with the permission of the competent authority, initiate a preliminary enquiry.\n3. Under Zero FIR, it is obligatory for the informant to furnish information electronically.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2 and 3","2 and 3 only","1 only","2 only"],"a":3,"x":"A Zero FIR is an FIR registered at any police station regardless of where the offence occurred; it is later transferred to the police station having jurisdiction. The BNSS, 2023 gave it statutory basis.\n\n1. A Zero FIR can be lodged for a cognizable or non-cognizable offence outside the station's jurisdiction - Incorrect\nSection 173(1) of the BNSS applies to information relating to the commission of a cognizable offence, 'irrespective of the area where the offence is committed'. Non-cognizable offences are recorded differently (Section 174) and are not part of the Zero FIR provision.\n\n2. The officer in charge may, with permission of the competent authority, conduct a preliminary enquiry - Correct (as per key)\nSection 173(3) BNSS allows the officer in charge, with prior permission of an officer not below the rank of Deputy Superintendent of Police, to conduct a preliminary enquiry within 14 days for offences punishable with 3 to 7 years' imprisonment, to see whether a prima facie case exists.\n\n3. It is obligatory for the informant to furnish information electronically - Incorrect\nInformation may be given orally, in writing or by electronic communication (e-FIR). Electronic filing is an option, not an obligation; an e-FIR must be signed by the informant within three days.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 2 only."},{"i":"2026-63","y":2026,"n":63,"s":0,"q":"With reference to the organisations under the Government of India, consider the following details:\n| Sl. No. | Organisation | Function | Controlling Union Ministry\n| 1. | Central Economic Intelligence Bureau (CEIB) | To coordinate between various law enforcement agencies | Ministry of Home Affairs\n| 2. | Serious Fraud Investigation Office (SFIO) | To investigate complex corporate frauds | Ministry of Finance\n| 3. | Central Bureau of Investigation (CBI) | To preserve values in public life and ensure the health of the national economy | Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pension\nIn how many of the above rows are the given details correctly matched?","o":["1","2","3","None"],"a":0,"x":"Row 1: Central Economic Intelligence Bureau - Incorrect\nThe CEIB (1985) is the nodal agency for economic intelligence and coordinates among enforcement agencies such as the ED, DRI and Income Tax. However, it works under the Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance, not the Ministry of Home Affairs.\n\nRow 2: Serious Fraud Investigation Office - Incorrect\nThe SFIO investigates complex corporate frauds and has statutory status under Section 211 of the Companies Act, 2013. It works under the Ministry of Corporate Affairs, not the Ministry of Finance.\n\nRow 3: Central Bureau of Investigation - Correct\nThe CBI's stated vision is to 'preserve values in public life and to ensure the health of the national economy'. It derives power from the Delhi Special Police Establishment Act, 1946 and is administratively under the Department of Personnel and Training, Ministry of Personnel, Public Grievances and Pensions.\n\nOnly one row is correct, so the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2026-65","y":2026,"n":65,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements with respect to the AI Impact Summit, 2026 held in New Delhi:\n1. The Summit's intellectual framework was based on three foundational Sutras: People, Planning, and Progress.\n2. The Preamble of the Summit stresses Democratising AI Resources, which acknowledges the Charter for Democratic Diffusion of AI as a binding framework to support locally relevant innovation and strengthen resilient AI ecosystems while respecting national laws.\n3. The New Delhi Declaration on AI Impact was structured around seven Chakras (Pillars), which included Access for Social Empowerment, AI for Science, and Secure and Trusted AI.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","3 only"],"a":3,"x":"The India AI Impact Summit was held in New Delhi in February 2026, following the Bletchley Park (2023), Seoul (2024) and Paris (2025) summits.\n\n1. The framework was based on three Sutras: People, Planning and Progress - Incorrect\nThe three foundational Sutras were People, Planet and Progress. 'Planning' is wrong.\n\n2. The Preamble acknowledges the Charter for Democratic Diffusion of AI as a binding framework - Incorrect\nThe summit outcomes, including the charter on democratic diffusion of AI, are voluntary and non-binding. They encourage access to AI resources and local innovation while respecting national laws, but they are not binding obligations.\n\n3. The New Delhi Declaration was structured around seven Chakras, including Access for Social Empowerment, AI for Science and Secure and Trusted AI - Correct\nThe declaration was organised around seven Chakras (pillars), covering areas such as human capital, inclusion and access for social empowerment, safe and trusted AI, resilience and innovation, AI for science, democratising AI resources and AI for economic development.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 3 only."},{"i":"2026-66","y":2026,"n":66,"s":4,"q":"Which of the following connectivity projects is/are a part of cooperation between India and the ASEAN member countries?\n1. Kaladan Multi-Modal Transit Transport Project\n2. IMT Trilateral Highway\n3. Agartala-Akhaura Rail Line\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2","2 and 3","1 and 3","2 only"],"a":0,"x":"ASEAN has ten members: Brunei, Cambodia, Indonesia, Laos, Malaysia, Myanmar, Philippines, Singapore, Thailand and Vietnam (Timor-Leste joined in 2025).\n\n1. Kaladan Multi-Modal Transit Transport Project - Correct\nIt links Kolkata port to Sittwe port in Myanmar by sea, then by the Kaladan river to Paletwa and by road to Mizoram. Since Myanmar is an ASEAN member, it is part of India-ASEAN connectivity under the Act East Policy.\n\n2. India-Myanmar-Thailand Trilateral Highway - Correct\nThe highway runs from Moreh (Manipur) through Myanmar to Mae Sot (Thailand), linking India with two ASEAN members. There are plans to extend it to Laos, Cambodia and Vietnam.\n\n3. Agartala-Akhaura rail line - Incorrect\nThis rail link (inaugurated 2023) connects Tripura with Bangladesh. Bangladesh is not an ASEAN member, so it is India-Bangladesh cooperation.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 2."},{"i":"2026-67","y":2026,"n":67,"s":4,"q":"Match List I with List II and select the answer using the code given below the Lists:\n| List I (Project Supported by India) | List II (Country)\n| A. Mangdechhu Hydroelectric Project | 1. Maldives\n| B. Restoration of Stor Palace | 2. Afghanistan\n| C. District Hospital at Dickoya | 3. Bhutan\n| D. Institute of Security and Law Enforcement Studies | 4. Sri Lanka\nCode :","o":["A-1, B-4, C-2, D-3","A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1","A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1","A-1, B-2, C-4, D-3"],"a":1,"x":"A. Mangdechhu Hydroelectric Project - Bhutan (3)\nA 720 MW run-of-river project on the Mangdechhu river in central Bhutan, built with Indian assistance and inaugurated in 2019. India buys its surplus power.\n\nB. Restoration of Stor Palace - Afghanistan (2)\nThe 19th-century Stor Palace in Kabul, which housed the Afghan Foreign Office, was restored with Indian assistance and inaugurated in 2016.\n\nC. District Hospital at Dickoya - Sri Lanka (4)\nA 150-bed hospital built with Indian grant assistance at Dickoya near Hatton in Sri Lanka's central hill country, where many Indian-origin Tamil plantation workers live. It was inaugurated in 2017.\n\nD. Institute of Security and Law Enforcement Studies - Maldives (1)\nBuilt with Indian assistance to train Maldivian police and law enforcement personnel; inaugurated in 2019.\n\nHence the answer is (b) A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1."},{"i":"2026-68","y":2026,"n":68,"s":3,"q":"Which of the following items of defence hardware is/are manufactured in India?\n1. Su-30 MKI Fighter Jets\n2. T-90 MK-III Tanks\n3. Akula Class Submarine\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2","1 and 3","1 only","2 only"],"a":0,"x":"1. Su-30 MKI fighter jets - Correct\nThe Su-30 MKI is a Russian-designed air superiority fighter licence-produced by Hindustan Aeronautics Limited at Nashik, with increasing indigenous content. It is the backbone of the IAF.\n\n2. T-90 MK-III tanks - Correct\nThe T-90 'Bhishma' main battle tank is manufactured under licence at the Heavy Vehicles Factory, Avadi, Chennai (now under Armoured Vehicles Nigam Ltd).\n\n3. Akula-class submarines - Incorrect\nAkula-class nuclear-powered attack submarines are built in Russia. India has only leased them (INS Chakra, 2012-2021; another lease contracted for delivery later). They are not manufactured in India. India's indigenously built nuclear submarines are of the Arihant class.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 2."},{"i":"2026-69","y":2026,"n":69,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements about platforms for multilateral co-operation:\n1. The 'Colombo Process' is a regional consultative process in which member states take binding decisions by consensus.\n2. The 'Abu Dhabi Dialogue' is a voluntary non-binding consultative process among Asian countries of labour origin and destination to facilitate regional cooperation on contractual labour mobility.\n3. The 'Global Forum for Migration and Development', created upon the proposal of a former UN Secretary General, is a voluntary forum whose decisions are non-binding in nature.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","2 only"],"a":2,"x":"1. The Colombo Process takes binding decisions by consensus - Incorrect\nThe Colombo Process (2003) is a regional consultative process of Asian countries of origin of migrant workers, including India. It is voluntary and non-binding; it does not take binding decisions.\n\n2. The Abu Dhabi Dialogue is a voluntary, non-binding process among Asian countries of labour origin and destination - Correct\nLaunched in 2008, it brings together the Colombo Process countries and Gulf destination countries (GCC plus Malaysia) to cooperate on temporary contractual labour mobility.\n\n3. The Global Forum on Migration and Development, proposed by a former UN Secretary-General, is voluntary and non-binding - Correct\nThe GFMD grew out of Kofi Annan's proposal at the 2006 UN High-Level Dialogue on Migration and Development. It began in 2007 as a voluntary, informal, state-led process outside the UN system, with non-binding outcomes.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2026-70","y":2026,"n":70,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following UN organisations/agencies:\n1. World Food Programme\n2. United Nations Children's Fund\n3. United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees\n4. International Labour Organisation\nHow many of the above has/have been awarded the Nobel Prize twice?","o":["1","2","3","4"],"a":0,"x":"1. World Food Programme - Won once\nThe WFP received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2020 for its efforts against hunger and for preventing the use of hunger as a weapon of war.\n\n2. UNICEF - Won once\nUNICEF received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1965.\n\n3. UNHCR - Won twice\nThe Office of the UN High Commissioner for Refugees received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1954 and again in 1981 for its work for refugees.\n\n4. International Labour Organisation - Won once\nThe ILO received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1969, its 50th anniversary year.\n\n(The International Committee of the Red Cross has won three times, but it is not a UN body.)\n\nOnly one of the listed bodies (UNHCR) has won twice, so the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2026-71","y":2026,"n":71,"s":4,"q":"Match List I with List II and select the answer using the code given below the Lists:\n| List I (UN Peacekeeping Operation) | List II (Period of Operation)\n| A. UNMIL | 1. 2007 – 2010\n| B. MINURCAT | 2. 2002 – 2005\n| C. MINUSTAH | 3. 2003 – 2018\n| D. UNMISET | 4. 2004 – 2017\nCode:","o":["A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2","A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2","A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3","A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3"],"a":1,"x":"A. UNMIL - 2003 to 2018 (3)\nThe UN Mission in Liberia was set up after the second Liberian civil war and ended in 2018. India contributed the first all-women Formed Police Unit (2007).\n\nB. MINURCAT - 2007 to 2010 (1)\nThe UN Mission in the Central African Republic and Chad protected civilians affected by the Darfur crisis and ended in 2010.\n\nC. MINUSTAH - 2004 to 2017 (4)\nThe UN Stabilization Mission in Haiti was deployed after political turmoil and continued through the 2010 earthquake until 2017.\n\nD. UNMISET - 2002 to 2005 (2)\nThe UN Mission of Support in East Timor assisted the newly independent Timor-Leste from 2002 to 2005.\n\nHence the answer is (b) A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2."},{"i":"2026-72","y":2026,"n":72,"s":4,"q":"Match List I with List II and select the answer using the code given below the Lists:\n| List I (BIMSTEC Centre / Establishment) | List II (Location)\n| A. BIMSTEC Cultural Industries Observatory | 1. NOIDA\n| B. BIMSTEC Energy Centre | 2. Bengaluru\n| C. BIMSTEC Centre for Weather and Climate | 3. Colombo\n| D. BIMSTEC Technology Transfer Facility | 4. Thimphu\nCode:","o":["A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4","A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4","A-4, B-2, C-1, D-3","A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3"],"a":2,"x":"A. BIMSTEC Cultural Industries Observatory - Thimphu (4)\nIt is located in Bhutan's capital to promote cultural industries in the region.\n\nB. BIMSTEC Energy Centre - Bengaluru (2)\nIt is hosted in India, in Bengaluru, to promote energy cooperation and grid interconnection among member states.\n\nC. BIMSTEC Centre for Weather and Climate - NOIDA (1)\nIt is located at the National Centre for Medium Range Weather Forecasting (NCMRWF) in Noida, India, and provides weather and climate services to member countries.\n\nD. BIMSTEC Technology Transfer Facility - Colombo (3)\nIt is located in Sri Lanka to facilitate technology transfer among member states.\n\nHence the answer is (c) A-4, B-2, C-1, D-3."},{"i":"2026-73","y":2026,"n":73,"s":12,"q":"Which one of the following pairs is not correctly matched?\n(Indian Army Corps : Headquarters)","o":["3 Corps : Dimapur","4 Corps : Tezpur","14 Corps : Leh","33 Corps : Srinagar"],"a":3,"x":"The question asks which pair is NOT correctly matched.\n\n(a) 3 Corps : Dimapur - Correctly matched\n3 Corps (Spear Corps) is headquartered at Rangapahar near Dimapur, Nagaland, under Eastern Command.\n\n(b) 4 Corps : Tezpur - Correctly matched\n4 Corps (Gajraj Corps) is headquartered at Tezpur, Assam, responsible for much of Arunachal Pradesh.\n\n(c) 14 Corps : Leh - Correctly matched\n14 Corps (Fire and Fury Corps) is headquartered at Leh and guards Ladakh, including Siachen and the Line of Actual Control.\n\n(d) 33 Corps : Srinagar - Not correctly matched\n33 Corps (Trishakti Corps) is headquartered at Sukna near Siliguri, West Bengal, and covers Sikkim and the Siliguri corridor. Srinagar is the headquarters of 15 Corps (Chinar Corps).\n\nHence the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2026-74","y":2026,"n":74,"s":0,"q":"Which of the following statements with respect to the Revamped Rashtriya Gram Swaraj Abhiyan (RGSA) is/are correct?\n1. The period of its implementation is 1st April, 2021 to 31st March, 2026.\n2. The key objective of the Revamped RGSA is to develop the governance capabilities of the Panchayati Raj Institutions to deliver on the Sustainable Development Goals.\n3. The share of the Central funding for the Revamped RGSA is 100% for all States and Union Territories.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2","2 only","1 and 3","2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. Implementation period is 1 April 2021 to 31 March 2026 - Incorrect\nThe Revamped Rashtriya Gram Swaraj Abhiyan was approved in April 2022 for implementation from 2022-23 to 2025-26, i.e., 1 April 2022 to 31 March 2026.\n\n2. Its key objective is to develop governance capabilities of PRIs to deliver on the SDGs - Correct\nThe revamped scheme focuses on capacity building and training of elected representatives and functionaries so that Panchayats can localise and achieve the Sustainable Development Goals through nine themes (poverty-free, healthy, child-friendly, water-sufficient, clean and green village, and so on).\n\n3. Central funding is 100 percent for all States and UTs - Incorrect\nFor State components, the Centre-State sharing ratio is 60:40, and 90:10 for North-eastern, hill States and Jammu and Kashmir. Only Union Territories (and central components) are fully funded by the Centre.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2026-75","y":2026,"n":75,"s":4,"q":"Which of the following countries are members of the European Union?\n1. Belarus\n2. Poland\n3. Germany\n4. Switzerland\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2 and 4","1 and 4 only","2 and 3","2 and 4 only"],"a":2,"x":"The European Union has 27 member states.\n\n1. Belarus - Incorrect\nBelarus is not an EU member. It is closely allied with Russia and is part of the Union State with Russia and the Eurasian Economic Union.\n\n2. Poland - Correct\nPoland joined the EU in 2004 in the big eastern enlargement.\n\n3. Germany - Correct\nGermany (then West Germany) was a founding member of the European Economic Community in 1957-58.\n\n4. Switzerland - Incorrect\nSwitzerland is not an EU member. It is a member of the European Free Trade Association and is linked to the EU through bilateral agreements and the Schengen Area.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 2 and 3."},{"i":"2026-76","y":2026,"n":76,"s":4,"q":"Match List I with List II and select the answer using the code given below the Lists:\n| List I (INTERPOL Notice) | List II (Description)\n| A. Silver Notice | 1. To seek information on unidentified bodies\n| B. Blue Notice | 2. To collect additional information about a person's identity, location, or activities in relation to a criminal investigation\n| C. Black Notice | 3. To provide warning about a person's criminal activities, where the person is considered to be a possible threat to public safety\n| D. Green Notice | 4. To identify and trace criminal assets\nCode:","o":["A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4","A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4","A-4, B-2, C-1, D-3","A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3"],"a":2,"x":"A. Silver Notice - To identify and trace criminal assets (4)\nThe Silver Notice was introduced as a pilot in January 2025 to help member countries locate and recover assets linked to crime, such as property, vehicles and bank accounts. India issued its first Silver Notice in 2025.\n\nB. Blue Notice - To collect additional information about a person's identity, location or activities in relation to a criminal investigation (2)\nIt is used to locate or identify a person of interest; India has used it to track fugitives' whereabouts.\n\nC. Black Notice - To seek information on unidentified bodies (1)\nIt helps identify dead bodies found in one country that may belong to foreign nationals.\n\nD. Green Notice - To warn about a person's criminal activities where the person is a possible threat to public safety (3)\nIt alerts other countries about repeat offenders such as sex offenders or traffickers.\n\n(Other notices: Red for arrest pending extradition, Yellow for missing persons, Orange for imminent threats, Purple for criminal methods.)\n\nHence the answer is (c) A-4, B-2, C-1, D-3."},{"i":"2026-77","y":2026,"n":77,"s":2,"q":"Which of the following statements in relation to NIRANTAR (National Institute for Research and Application of Natural Resources to Transform, Adapt and Build Resilience), a platform of institutions under the Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change, is/are correct?\n1. Ecosystem Survey and Analysis is a vertical under this platform, the lead institute of which is Botanical Survey of India, Kolkata.\n2. Research and Management of Ecosystem Service is a vertical under this platform, the lead institute of which is Central Zoo Authority, New Delhi.\n3. Capacity Development Support is a vertical under this platform, the lead institute of which is Indian Institute of Forest Management, Bhopal.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 3 only","2 only","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"NIRANTAR (National Institute for Research and Application of Natural Resources to Transform, Adapt and Build Resilience) is a platform that networks MoEFCC's research and technical institutions into thematic verticals, each led by a lead institute.\n\n1. Ecosystem Survey and Analysis vertical, led by the Botanical Survey of India, Kolkata - Correct\nThe BSI (founded 1890, Kolkata) is the country's apex body for surveying and documenting plant diversity, which fits the survey and analysis vertical.\n\n2. Research and Management of Ecosystem Services vertical, led by the Central Zoo Authority - Incorrect\nThe Central Zoo Authority is a statutory body under the Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972 that recognises and regulates zoos. It is not a research institution and does not lead the ecosystem services vertical.\n\n3. Capacity Development Support vertical, led by the Indian Institute of Forest Management, Bhopal - Correct\nIIFM Bhopal is a premier training and management institute for forestry and natural resources, suited to leading capacity development.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 3 only."},{"i":"2026-78","y":2026,"n":78,"s":4,"q":"The Chancellor of the Federal Republic of Germany visited India in January, 2026. Which of the following is/are not correct in terms of outcomes of this visit?\n1. Signing of a Memorandum of Understanding between the All India Institute of Ayurveda and the University of Hamburg\n2. Signing of a Memorandum of Understanding on Youth Hockey Development between Hockey India and the German Hockey Federation\n3. Establishment of a bilateral dialogue mechanism on the Indo-Pacific\n4. Opening of an Honorary Consul of Germany in Lucknow\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["2 and 3","1 and 4","3 and 4","1 only"],"a":1,"x":"German Chancellor Friedrich Merz visited India on 12-13 January 2026 (Ahmedabad and Bengaluru). The question asks which are NOT correct outcomes.\n\n1. MoU between the All India Institute of Ayurveda and the University of Hamburg - Not correct\nThe AIIA's MoU on traditional medicine was signed with Charité - Universitätsmedizin Berlin, not with the University of Hamburg.\n\n2. MoU on Youth Hockey Development between Hockey India and the German Hockey Federation - Correct outcome\nThis MoU was among the listed outcomes of the visit.\n\n3. Establishment of a bilateral dialogue mechanism on the Indo-Pacific - Correct outcome\nBoth countries agreed to set up a dedicated dialogue on the Indo-Pacific.\n\n4. Opening of an Honorary Consul of Germany in Lucknow - Not correct\nThe honorary consul announced was in Ahmedabad, not Lucknow.\n\nHence statements 1 and 4 are not correct, and the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2026-79","y":2026,"n":79,"s":3,"q":"Which of the following statements about DHRUV64 is/are correct?\n1. It is the third chip fabricated under the DIR-V Programme with an overall aim to enable the creation of microprocessors for India.\n2. It is India's first homegrown 1.0 GHz, 64-bit dual-core microprocessor.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"1. It is the third chip under the DIR-V programme, which aims to create microprocessors for India - Correct\nDHRUV64 was unveiled by C-DAC in December 2025. It is the third processor under MeitY's Digital India RISC-V (DIR-V) programme, after THEJAS32 and THEJAS64. DIR-V aims to build indigenous processors on the open RISC-V architecture, reducing dependence on imported chips.\n\n2. It is India's first homegrown 1.0 GHz, 64-bit dual-core microprocessor - Correct\nDHRUV64 runs at 1.0 GHz and has two 64-bit cores. It is intended for applications such as 5G infrastructure, automotive systems, industrial automation, consumer electronics and IoT.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2026-80","y":2026,"n":80,"s":3,"q":"The Bureau of Indian Standard (BIS) recently introduced a national standard to test and assess bomb disposal system. Which of the following statements with regard to this system is/are correct?\n1. The new standard is known as IS 19445 : 2025.\n2. It will improve interoperability of equipment across agencies.\n3. It was developed by TBRL, DRDO in collaboration with the 30th Central Scientific Research Institute, Russia.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2 and 3","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 only"],"a":2,"x":"1. The new standard is IS 19445 : 2025 - Correct\nThe Bureau of Indian Standards notified IS 19445:2025, India's first national standard specifying test methods and performance requirements for bomb disposal systems such as bomb containment vessels and blast-suppression blankets. It was released in December 2025.\n\n2. It will improve interoperability of equipment across agencies - Correct\nWith common benchmarks, equipment bought by the NSG, CAPFs and State police forces will meet uniform performance and safety levels, making it easier to use and compare across agencies and improving procurement quality.\n\n3. It was developed by TBRL with the 30th Central Scientific Research Institute, Russia - Incorrect\nThe standard was developed by BIS together with DRDO's Terminal Ballistics Research Laboratory, Chandigarh, and Indian user agencies and experts. No Russian institute was involved.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2026-81","y":2026,"n":81,"s":3,"q":"'X', born in the UK, was conferred the Nobel Prize in 2025. He was a professor in an American university when this prize was announced. Identify X:","o":["Michel H. Devoret","Richard Robson","John Clarke","Joel Mokyr"],"a":2,"x":"The person must be born in the UK, awarded a 2025 Nobel Prize, and a professor at an American university.\n\n(a) Michel H. Devoret - Incorrect\nHe shared the 2025 Physics Nobel with John Clarke and John Martinis for demonstrating macroscopic quantum tunnelling and energy quantisation in an electric circuit. He teaches in the USA (Yale and UC Santa Barbara), but he was born in Paris, France.\n\n(b) Richard Robson - Incorrect\nHe shared the 2025 Chemistry Nobel for developing metal-organic frameworks. He was born in the UK (Yorkshire), but he is a professor at the University of Melbourne, Australia, not in America.\n\n(c) John Clarke - Correct\nHe was born in Cambridge, UK, and is a professor at the University of California, Berkeley. He shared the 2025 Physics Nobel. He satisfies all three conditions.\n\n(d) Joel Mokyr - Incorrect\nHe shared the 2025 Economics prize for explaining innovation-driven growth. He is a professor at Northwestern University, USA, but he was born in Leiden, Netherlands."},{"i":"2026-82","y":2026,"n":82,"s":12,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to the Grand Slam Tennis Tournaments is/are correct?\n1. The tournaments have a shared governance structure establishing the partnership among the four Grand Slam tournaments.\n2. They are open for entry to all internationally ranked tennis players above the age of 14.\n3. There is a limitation on the number of 'Wild Cards' a player may receive to compete in a Grand Slam Tournament.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"x":"1. The tournaments have a shared governance structure - Correct\nThe four Grand Slams (Australian Open, French Open, Wimbledon, US Open) cooperate through the Grand Slam Board, which, with the ITF, sets common rules through the Grand Slam Rulebook, including on player conduct and anti-corruption.\n\n2. They are open to all internationally ranked players above the age of 14 - Correct (as per key)\nEntry to Grand Slams is 'open' to professionals and amateurs, based on ranking plus qualifying rounds and wild cards. Players must satisfy professional tour age eligibility rules, under which the minimum age for professional competition is 14.\n\n3. There is a limit on the number of wild cards a player may receive - Incorrect\nEach Grand Slam decides its own wild cards at its discretion, often for home players or returning stars. There is no rule capping how many wild cards a player may receive over time.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2026-83","y":2026,"n":83,"s":1,"q":"Which one of the following pairs of semiconductor plants in India and their locations is not correctly matched?","o":["CG Power and Industrial Solutions Pvt. Ltd. in partnership with Renesas Electronics and STARS Microelectronics : Gujarat","Tata Semiconductor Assembly and Test Pvt. Ltd. : Assam","HCL-Foxconn Joint Venture India Chip Ltd. : Madhya Pradesh","SicSem Pvt. Ltd. : Odisha"],"a":2,"x":"The question asks which pair is NOT correctly matched.\n\n(a) CG Power with Renesas and Stars Microelectronics : Gujarat - Correctly matched\nThis OSAT (assembly and testing) unit, with Renesas (Japan) and Stars Microelectronics (Thailand), was approved in 2024 at Sanand, Gujarat.\n\n(b) Tata Semiconductor Assembly and Test : Assam - Correctly matched\nTata's assembly and test plant is being built at Jagiroad in Morigaon district, Assam, approved in 2024.\n\n(c) HCL-Foxconn joint venture : Madhya Pradesh - Not correctly matched\nThe HCL-Foxconn semiconductor unit, approved in May 2025, is located near Jewar airport in the Yamuna Expressway region of Uttar Pradesh, not Madhya Pradesh.\n\n(d) SiCSem Pvt. Ltd. : Odisha - Correctly matched\nSiCSem's silicon carbide compound semiconductor fab, India's first commercial compound semiconductor fab, was approved in 2025 at Bhubaneswar, Odisha.\n\nHence the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2026-84","y":2026,"n":84,"s":3,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to India's indigenous new high resolution weather model, the 'Bharat Forecast System', is/are correct?\n1. Its objective is to generate forecasts at the Panchayats cluster level.\n2. It was developed by IIT Delhi.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":0,"x":"1. Its objective is to generate forecasts at the Panchayat cluster level - Correct\nThe Bharat Forecast System, launched in May 2025, has a horizontal resolution of about 6 km, the highest for any operational global weather model. This allows forecasts for small areas, down to clusters of Panchayats, helping farmers and disaster management.\n\n2. It was developed by IIT Delhi - Incorrect\nBFS was developed by the Indian Institute of Tropical Meteorology, Pune, under the Ministry of Earth Sciences, and runs on the 'Arka' supercomputer. The India Meteorological Department uses it for operational forecasting. IIT Delhi was not its developer.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 only."},{"i":"2026-85","y":2026,"n":85,"s":10,"q":"Consider the following statements with regard to the film 'Boong':\n1. The film has recently won the British Academy of Film and Television Arts (BAFTA) Award in the Children's and Family Film category.\n2. The film is directed by Lakshmipriya Devi.\n3. This is the first Indian film to win a BAFTA award in the Children's and Family Film category.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","3 only"],"a":0,"x":"1. The film won the BAFTA in the Children's and Family Film category - Correct\n'Boong' won the BAFTA award in the Children's and Family Film category at the 2026 ceremony (February 2026).\n\n2. The film is directed by Lakshmipriya Devi - Correct\nBoong is a Manipuri-language film written and directed by Lakshmipriya Devi and produced by Excel Entertainment (Farhan Akhtar and Ritesh Sidhwani). It tells the story of a young boy in Manipur searching for his father.\n\n3. It is the first Indian film to win a BAFTA in this category - Correct\nBoong is the first Indian film to win in this category, a notable achievement for regional and North-eastern cinema.\n\nSince all three statements are correct, the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2026-86","y":2026,"n":86,"s":3,"q":"Which of the following statements regarding the features of blockchain technology are correct?\n1. Records stored in the database may be made visible to relevant stakeholders without risk of alteration.\n2. Copies of the entire database are stored on multiple computers on a network, syncing within seconds.\n3. Consortium blockchain is a blend of public and private blockchains allowing selective data access.\n4. Mathematical algorithms make it impossible to change or delete any data once recorded and accepted.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 3","2 and 4 only","1, 2 and 4","1 and 4 only"],"a":2,"x":"1. Records may be visible to relevant stakeholders without risk of alteration - Correct\nA blockchain ledger can be shared with permitted participants who can view records, while cryptographic linking of blocks makes any tampering evident and practically impossible.\n\n2. Copies of the entire database are stored on multiple computers and sync within seconds - Correct\nBlockchain is a distributed ledger. Each node keeps a full copy, and new transactions are propagated and agreed through consensus, so copies are synchronised quickly.\n\n3. Consortium blockchain is a blend of public and private blockchains allowing selective access - Incorrect\nA consortium (federated) blockchain is a permissioned network controlled by a group of organisations, such as a group of banks. A blend of public and private chains is called a hybrid blockchain. So the definition given belongs to a different type.\n\n4. Mathematical algorithms make it impossible to change or delete data once recorded - Correct\nEach block contains the hash of the previous block; changing one record would change all later hashes and would require control over most of the network. This makes recorded data effectively immutable.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1, 2 and 4."},{"i":"2026-87","y":2026,"n":87,"s":1,"q":"An e-commerce revenue model where the seller has control over pricing but doesn't keep products in stock and instead transfers customer orders and shipment details to a third-party supplier, who then ships the goods directly to the customer, is called:","o":["Dropshipping Model","Affiliate Revenue Model","Transaction Fee Revenue Model","Agency Revenue Model"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Dropshipping Model - Correct\nIn dropshipping, the online seller lists products and sets prices but holds no inventory. When a customer orders, the seller passes the order and shipping details to a third-party supplier, who ships directly to the customer. The seller earns the difference between the retail price and the supplier's price.\n\n(b) Affiliate Revenue Model - Incorrect\nHere a website earns a commission for referring customers to another seller's products through links (e.g., Amazon Associates). It does not handle orders or pricing.\n\n(c) Transaction Fee Revenue Model - Incorrect\nA platform earns a fee or commission on each transaction it facilitates, as in online marketplaces or payment gateways. It does not describe the seller's own pricing without stock.\n\n(d) Agency Revenue Model - Incorrect\nIn the agency model, the supplier (principal) sets the final price and the platform acts only as an agent earning a commission, as with e-books under agency pricing. Here the seller controls pricing, so it is not the agency model."},{"i":"2026-88","y":2026,"n":88,"s":1,"q":"Which one of the following correctly represents the three key sub-indices of the Financial Inclusion Index (FI-Index) of the Reserve Bank of India (RBI)?","o":["Credit access, Insurance depth, and Pension coverage","Banking access, GDP contribution, and Financial literacy","Access, Usage, and Quality","Access, Affordability, and Transparency"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Credit access, Insurance depth and Pension coverage - Incorrect\nThese are sectors covered by the index, not its sub-indices.\n\n(b) Banking access, GDP contribution and Financial literacy - Incorrect\nGDP contribution is not a sub-index; financial literacy is only one element under the Quality dimension.\n\n(c) Access, Usage and Quality - Correct\nThe RBI's Financial Inclusion Index, launched in August 2021, has three sub-indices: Access (35 percent weight), Usage (45 percent) and Quality (20 percent, covering financial literacy, consumer protection and inequalities). It covers banking, investments, insurance, postal and pension sectors, with values from 0 to 100, and is published each July.\n\n(d) Access, Affordability and Transparency - Incorrect\nAffordability and transparency are not the official sub-indices."},{"i":"2026-89","y":2026,"n":89,"s":1,"q":"Which one of the following best describes the key objective of India's 'Open Network for Digital Commerce' (ONDC) initiative?","o":["To allow government control over all digital commerce transactions","To replace private e-commerce players","To break the dominance of large e-commerce platforms by enabling interoperability across networks","To mandate UPI-based payments for all online transactions"],"a":2,"x":"(a) To allow government control over all digital commerce transactions - Incorrect\nONDC is an open protocol run by a not-for-profit company; it does not give the government control over transactions.\n\n(b) To replace private e-commerce players - Incorrect\nPrivate platforms are not replaced; they can join ONDC as buyer or seller apps.\n\n(c) To break the dominance of large platforms by enabling interoperability across networks - Correct\nThe Open Network for Digital Commerce, launched in 2022 by DPIIT as a Section 8 company, works like UPI for commerce. A buyer on one app can buy from a seller listed on another app. This 'unbundles' the platform model, lowers entry barriers for small sellers and kirana stores, and reduces the market power of large marketplaces.\n\n(d) To mandate UPI-based payments for all online transactions - Incorrect\nONDC does not mandate any single payment mode."},{"i":"2026-90","y":2026,"n":90,"s":1,"q":"Which one of the following statements about Unified Payments Interface (UPI) and Central Bank Digital Currency (Digital Rupee) is not correct?","o":["UPI is a real-time payment system but Digital Rupee is akin to sovereign paper currency.","In case of UPI, settlement for end users happens instantly as the money gets immediately debited or credited but in case of Digital Rupee, there is no settlement as the wallet balance gets transferred to another wallet.","UPI transactions are recorded by banks and reflected in bank statements but in case of Digital Rupee, no data is captured in bank statements as transactions are from one wallet to another.","In both the cases (UPI and Digital Rupee), the liability lies with the users and their respective banks."],"a":3,"x":"The question asks which statement is NOT correct.\n\n(a) UPI is a real-time payment system, while the Digital Rupee is akin to sovereign paper currency - Correct statement\nUPI moves money between bank accounts in real time. The Digital Rupee (e-Rupee, CBDC) is a digital form of currency issued by the RBI, like notes in digital form.\n\n(b) UPI settlement happens by debit and credit, while the Digital Rupee has no separate settlement - Correct statement\nRBI's FAQs explain that UPI transactions change bank balances, while in e-Rupee the token itself moves from one wallet to another and the transfer is final, like handing over cash.\n\n(c) UPI transactions appear in bank statements, while Digital Rupee wallet transactions do not - Correct statement\ne-Rupee transactions happen within wallets and are not reflected in the customer's bank account statement.\n\n(d) In both cases the liability lies with users and their banks - Not correct (answer)\nWith UPI, the money is a bank deposit, so it is the bank's liability. The Digital Rupee, however, is a direct liability of the RBI, just like currency notes. So the statement is wrong."},{"i":"2026-91","y":2026,"n":91,"s":1,"q":"Which of the following statements about Real-World Assets (RWA) Tokenization are correct?\n1. Tokenization is the process of turning real world assets into digital tokens using blockchain technology.\n2. Tokenization of real world assets offers 24x7 access, promoting financial inclusion.\n3. Tokenization of real world assets will allow the access to high growth investment opportunities for individuals in India.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2 and 3","2 and 3 only","1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only"],"a":0,"x":"1. Tokenisation turns real-world assets into digital tokens using blockchain - Correct\nReal-world asset tokenisation converts ownership rights in assets such as real estate, bonds, gold, art or invoices into digital tokens recorded on a blockchain, which can be held and transferred digitally.\n\n2. It offers 24x7 access and promotes financial inclusion - Correct\nTokens can be traded round the clock on digital platforms, and assets can be divided into small units, so people with little money can invest. This widens access to investment.\n\n3. It will allow access to high-growth investment opportunities for individuals in India - Correct\nFractional ownership lets ordinary investors take part in high-value, high-growth assets (like commercial real estate or infrastructure) that earlier were open only to large investors.\n\nSince all three statements are correct, the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2026-92","y":2026,"n":92,"s":1,"q":"A bond whose proceeds are used only to finance or refinance a combination of both environmental and social projects is called:","o":["Green Bond","Social Bond","Sustainability Bond","Sovereign Bond"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Green Bond - Incorrect\nProceeds of green bonds are used only for environmental projects, such as renewable energy, clean transport, energy efficiency or pollution control. India issues Sovereign Green Bonds.\n\n(b) Social Bond - Incorrect\nProceeds are used only for social projects, such as affordable housing, health care, education or employment generation.\n\n(c) Sustainability Bond - Correct\nAccording to the International Capital Market Association's Sustainability Bond Guidelines, a sustainability bond is one whose proceeds finance or refinance a combination of both green and social projects.\n\n(d) Sovereign Bond - Incorrect\nA sovereign bond is defined by its issuer (a national government), not by how the money is used."},{"i":"2026-93","y":2026,"n":93,"s":1,"q":"Which of the following statements about M1xchange's role in Micro, Small & Medium Enterprises (MSMEs) financing is/are correct?\n1. M1xchange provides collateral based loans to MSMEs.\n2. M1xchange facilitates discounting of invoices and Bills of Exchange for MSMEs.\n3. M1xchange functions as a credit rating agency for MSMEs.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2 and 3","2 only","2 and 3 only","1 only"],"a":1,"x":"M1xchange is one of India's RBI-licensed Trade Receivables Discounting System (TReDS) platforms.\n\n1. It provides collateral-based loans to MSMEs - Incorrect\nM1xchange is not a lender. TReDS financing is based on invoices raised on large buyers and does not require collateral from the MSME.\n\n2. It facilitates discounting of invoices and bills of exchange for MSMEs - Correct\nMSMEs upload invoices raised on corporate buyers, government departments or PSUs. Once the buyer accepts them, banks and NBFCs bid online to discount them, and the MSME gets paid quickly, easing working capital shortages.\n\n3. It functions as a credit rating agency for MSMEs - Incorrect\nIt does not rate MSMEs. Credit rating agencies are registered with and regulated by SEBI.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2026-94","y":2026,"n":94,"s":1,"q":"Which one of the following best describes the 'Crowding Out Effect' in the context of fiscal policy?","o":["A situation where private investment increases due to increased Government spending","A situation where Government borrowing leads to higher interest rates, which reduces private investment","A situation where an increase in taxes leads to increased private sector investment","A situation where Government spending has no impact on aggregate demand"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Private investment increases due to increased government spending - Incorrect\nThis describes 'crowding in', where public investment (e.g., infrastructure) encourages private investment.\n\n(b) Government borrowing leads to higher interest rates, which reduces private investment - Correct\nWhen the government runs a large deficit and borrows heavily, it competes with private firms for the limited pool of savings. Demand for loanable funds rises, interest rates go up, and private investment falls. This is the crowding-out effect, and it reduces the effectiveness of expansionary fiscal policy.\n\n(c) Higher taxes lead to increased private investment - Incorrect\nHigher taxes usually reduce disposable income and private investment; this is not crowding out.\n\n(d) Government spending has no impact on aggregate demand - Incorrect\nThis describes an extreme case of fiscal policy ineffectiveness, not the definition of crowding out."},{"i":"2026-95","y":2026,"n":95,"s":5,"q":"Which of the following statements about Rare Earth Elements (REEs) and Critical Minerals is/are correct?\n1. Modern technological innovations including Artificial Intelligence, robotics and space exploration extensively utilise Rare Earth Elements (REEs).\n2. China has the highest share in mining of REEs followed by India.\n3. The Government of India launched the National Critical Mineral Mission (NCMM) in 2025 to establish a robust framework for self-reliance in the critical mineral sector.\n4. Rare Earth Elements are a set of 13 metallic elements.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 3 only","3 only","1, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4"],"a":0,"x":"1. AI, robotics and space exploration extensively use REEs - Correct\nRare earth elements such as neodymium, praseodymium and dysprosium are used in powerful permanent magnets for motors, robots, drones, EVs and wind turbines, and REEs are used in electronics, lasers, sensors and aerospace systems.\n\n2. China has the highest share in REE mining, followed by India - Incorrect\nChina mines about 70 percent of the world's rare earths. It is followed by the USA, Myanmar and Australia. India has significant reserves (among the top five) but a very small share of mining.\n\n3. The Government launched the National Critical Mineral Mission in 2025 - Correct\nThe NCMM was approved in January 2025 with about 16,300 crore rupees of central spending plus about 18,000 crore rupees from PSUs, to build self-reliance across exploration, mining, processing, recycling and overseas acquisition of critical minerals.\n\n4. REEs are a set of 13 metallic elements - Incorrect\nRare earth elements are 17 elements: the 15 lanthanides plus scandium and yttrium.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 3 only."},{"i":"2026-96","y":2026,"n":96,"s":1,"q":"Which of the following statements about insurance in aviation sector is/are correct?\n1. 'Aviation Hull Insurance' covers the physical aircraft, including the body, engine, and on-board equipment.\n2. Under the Montreal Convention, adopted in 1999 by over 130 countries, including India, airlines are strictly liable to pay compensation to the family/nominee of every deceased passenger without requiring the family to prove fault.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"1. Aviation hull insurance covers the physical aircraft, including body, engine and on-board equipment - Correct\nHull insurance protects the owner or operator against physical loss of or damage to the aircraft itself, whether on the ground or in flight. Liability to passengers and third parties is covered by separate liability insurance.\n\n2. Under the Montreal Convention, airlines are strictly liable to pay compensation for each deceased passenger without proof of fault - Correct\nThe Montreal Convention, 1999 (India acceded in 2009; over 130 parties) created a two-tier system. In the first tier, up to a set limit (now 151,880 Special Drawing Rights per passenger), the airline is strictly liable and the family does not have to prove fault. Above that limit, the airline is liable unless it proves it was not negligent. India applies it through the Carriage by Air Act, 1972 (as amended).\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2026-97","y":2026,"n":97,"s":1,"q":"Which of the following statements about Crowdfunding is/are correct?\n1. Crowdfunding is solicitation of funds (small amount) from multiple investors through a web-based platform or social networking site for a specific project.\n2. Small and Medium Enterprises (SMEs) are able to raise funds at lower cost of capital without undergoing rigorous procedures.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"1. Crowdfunding is solicitation of small amounts from many investors through a web platform or social network for a specific project - Correct\nThis is how SEBI's 2014 consultation paper defines crowdfunding. It can be donation-based, reward-based, debt-based (peer-to-peer lending) or equity-based.\n\n2. SMEs can raise funds at lower cost of capital without rigorous procedures - Correct\nCompared with an IPO or bank loans, crowdfunding has fewer formalities and lower costs, making it attractive for start-ups and small businesses. (In India, equity crowdfunding has not yet been formally permitted by SEBI, but the question concerns its general features.)\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2026-98","y":2026,"n":98,"s":1,"q":"With reference to different Committees in India, consider the following details:\n| Sl. No. | Committee | Objective | Organization under which it was formed\n| 1. | R.N. Malhotra Committee | Comprehensive reforms of Insurance sector in India | Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India\n| 2. | L.C. Gupta Committee | Preparing a roadmap for the introduction of derivatives trading in India | Securities and Exchange Board of India\n| 3. | Urjit R. Patel Committee | Preparing a roadmap for reforming bank lending to the Housing sector | Reserve Bank of India\n| 4. | Y.H. Malegam Committee | Preparing a roadmap for reforms in Microfinance sector in India | Reserve Bank of India\nIn which of the above rows are all the details correctly matched?","o":["2 only","2 and 3","1, 3 and 4","2 and 4"],"a":3,"x":"Row 1: R.N. Malhotra Committee - Incorrect\nThe Malhotra Committee (1993) recommended comprehensive reforms of the insurance sector, including opening it to private players and creating a regulator. It was set up by the Government of India (Ministry of Finance). IRDAI did not exist then; it was created in 1999-2000 on the Committee's recommendation.\n\nRow 2: L.C. Gupta Committee - Correct\nSet up by SEBI in 1996, it recommended the regulatory framework for introducing derivatives trading in India; index futures began in 2000.\n\nRow 3: Urjit R. Patel Committee - Incorrect\nSet up by the RBI in 2013, this committee recommended revising and strengthening the monetary policy framework, leading to flexible inflation targeting. It had nothing to do with housing loans.\n\nRow 4: Y.H. Malegam Committee - Correct\nA sub-committee of the RBI Board set up in 2010, after the Andhra Pradesh microfinance crisis, to study issues in microfinance. It led to the NBFC-MFI category and caps on interest margins.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 2 and 4."},{"i":"2026-99","y":2026,"n":99,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements about the Non-Banking Financial Companies (NBFCs) in India:\n1. NBFCs cannot accept demand deposits.\n2. All the NBFCs operating in India have to be registered with the RBI.\n3. NBFCs form part of the payment and settlement system and can issue cheque drawn on itself.\n4. Deposit insurance facility of Deposit Insurance and Credit Guarantee Corporation (DICGC) is not available to the depositors of deposit taking NBFCs.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 4","1, 2 and 3","4 only","2, 3 and 4"],"a":0,"x":"1. NBFCs cannot accept demand deposits - Correct\nNBFCs are not allowed to accept demand deposits (like savings or current accounts). Only certain deposit-taking NBFCs can accept term deposits, with limits.\n\n2. All NBFCs operating in India must be registered with the RBI - Incorrect\nSeveral types of NBFCs are regulated by other regulators and are exempt from RBI registration, such as insurance companies (IRDAI), merchant bankers, stock brokers and mutual funds (SEBI), chit funds (State governments) and Nidhi companies (Ministry of Corporate Affairs).\n\n3. NBFCs are part of the payment and settlement system and can issue cheques on themselves - Incorrect\nNBFCs do not form part of the payment and settlement system and cannot issue cheques drawn on themselves; this is one of the key differences from banks.\n\n4. DICGC deposit insurance is not available to depositors of deposit-taking NBFCs - Correct\nDeposit insurance of the DICGC (up to 5 lakh rupees) covers bank deposits only, not NBFC deposits.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 4."},{"i":"2026-100","y":2026,"n":100,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements about Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI):\n1. MPI is calculated using Alkire-Foster methodology.\n2. MPI calculated by NITI Aayog has a total of twelve indicators.\n3. Maternal Health and Bank Account are common indicators in the MPI of NITI Aayog and MPI of United Nations Development Programme (UNDP).\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 3","1 and 3 only","2 only"],"a":0,"x":"1. MPI is calculated using the Alkire-Foster methodology - Correct\nBoth the global MPI (UNDP and Oxford Poverty and Human Development Initiative) and NITI Aayog's national MPI use the Alkire-Foster method, which counts overlapping deprivations of a person and identifies someone as poor if weighted deprivations exceed one-third.\n\n2. NITI Aayog's MPI has twelve indicators - Correct\nNITI Aayog's MPI has 12 indicators across three equally weighted dimensions: health (nutrition, child and adolescent mortality, maternal health), education (years of schooling, school attendance) and standard of living (cooking fuel, sanitation, drinking water, electricity, housing, assets, bank account).\n\n3. Maternal health and bank account are common to NITI Aayog's MPI and UNDP's MPI - Incorrect\nThe global MPI has 10 indicators. Maternal health and bank account are the two extra indicators added by NITI Aayog to suit Indian priorities, so they are not common to both.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2025-1","y":2025,"n":1,"s":1,"q":"With reference to investments, consider the following:\nI. Bonds\nII. Hedge Funds\nIII. Stocks\nIV. Venture Capital\nHow many of the above are treated as Alternative Investment Funds?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All the four"],"a":1,"x":"Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs) are privately pooled investment vehicles regulated by SEBI (AIF) Regulations, 2012. They are divided into Category I (venture capital, SME, social venture, infrastructure funds), Category II (private equity, debt funds) and Category III (hedge funds and funds using complex trading).\n\nI. Bonds - Not an AIF\nBonds are conventional debt instruments issued by governments or companies. They are traditional investments, not pooled alternative funds.\n\nII. Hedge Funds - AIF\nHedge funds are Category III AIFs that use leverage, derivatives and complex strategies for short-term returns.\n\nIII. Stocks - Not an AIF\nShares are traditional equity investments traded on stock exchanges.\n\nIV. Venture Capital - AIF\nVenture capital funds, which invest in start-ups and early-stage companies, are Category I AIFs.\n\nOnly two (hedge funds and venture capital) are AIFs, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2025-2","y":2025,"n":2,"s":1,"q":"Which of the following are the sources of income for the Reserve Bank of India?\nI. Buying and selling Government bonds\nII. Buying and selling foreign currency\nIII. Pension fund management\nIV. Lending to private companies\nV. Printing and distributing currency notes\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","o":["I and II only","II, III and IV","I, III, IV and V","I, II and V"],"a":0,"x":"I. Buying and selling government bonds - Correct (source of income)\nThe RBI earns interest on the government securities it holds and makes gains when it sells them through open market operations. Interest on domestic securities is one of its main income sources.\n\nII. Buying and selling foreign currency - Correct (source of income)\nThe RBI earns interest on foreign securities and deposits in which its reserves are invested, and makes gains when it sells foreign currency (for example, when defending the rupee). This is often the largest contributor to its surplus.\n\nIII. Pension fund management - Incorrect\nPension funds are managed by fund managers regulated by PFRDA (under NPS), not by the RBI.\n\nIV. Lending to private companies - Incorrect\nThe RBI is the banker to banks and governments; it does not lend to private companies.\n\nV. Printing and distributing currency notes - Incorrect\nPrinting and distributing notes is a cost for the RBI (paid to the note presses), shown as expenditure. It is not a source of income.\n\nHence the answer is (a) I and II only."},{"i":"2025-3","y":2025,"n":3,"s":0,"q":"With reference to the Government of India, consider the following information:\n| | Organization | Some of its functions | It works under\n| I. | Directorate of Enforcement | Enforcement of the Fugitive Economic Offenders Act, 2018 | Internal Security Division-I, Ministry of Home Affairs\n| II. | Directorate of Revenue Intelligence | Enforces the Provisions of the Customs Act, 1962 | Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance\n| III. | Directorate General of Systems and Data Management | Carrying out big data analytics to assist tax officers for better policy and nabbing tax evaders | Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance\nIn how many of the above rows is the information correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":0,"x":"Row I: Directorate of Enforcement - Incorrect\nThe ED enforces the Prevention of Money Laundering Act, FEMA and the Fugitive Economic Offenders Act, 2018, so the function is correct. But it works under the Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance, not the Internal Security Division of the Ministry of Home Affairs.\n\nRow II: Directorate of Revenue Intelligence - Correct\nThe DRI is India's apex anti-smuggling intelligence agency. It enforces the Customs Act, 1962 and works under the Central Board of Indirect Taxes and Customs, Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance.\n\nRow III: Directorate General of Systems and Data Management - Incorrect (as per official key)\nThe DG Systems and Data Management mainly designs, develops and maintains IT systems and data for tax administration. Big-data analytics to detect evasion and assist officers is primarily the function of the Directorate General of Analytics and Risk Management (DGARM). So the function is treated as wrongly matched.\nNote: Some sources initially treated row III as correct, since the ministry is correct and it does manage tax data. The official key accepts only row II.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Only one."},{"i":"2025-4","y":2025,"n":4,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. The Reserve Bank of India mandates all the listed companies in India to submit a Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report (BRSR).\nII. In India, a company submitting a BRSR makes disclosures in the report that are largely non-financial in nature.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"I. RBI mandates all listed companies to submit a BRSR - Incorrect\nThe Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report is mandated by SEBI, not the RBI, under its Listing Obligations and Disclosure Requirements. It applies to the top 1,000 listed companies by market capitalisation (since 2022-23), not to all listed companies.\n\nII. BRSR disclosures are largely non-financial - Correct\nThe BRSR asks companies to disclose their performance on environmental, social and governance (ESG) parameters, based on the nine principles of the National Guidelines on Responsible Business Conduct. These include energy and water use, emissions, waste, employee welfare, human rights, community engagement and ethics. These are largely non-financial disclosures.\n\nHence the answer is (b) II only."},{"i":"2025-5","y":2025,"n":5,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: In India, income from allied agricultural activities like poultry farming and wool rearing in rural areas is exempted from any tax.\nStatement II: In India, rural agricultural land is not considered a capital asset under the provisions of the Income-tax Act, 1961.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I","Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct","Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct"],"a":3,"x":"Statement I - Incorrect\nUnder Section 10(1) of the Income-tax Act, only 'agricultural income' is exempt. Section 2(1A) defines it as income from land used for agriculture, such as rent or revenue from land and income from cultivation. Courts have held that income from allied activities such as poultry farming, dairy, fisheries and rearing sheep for wool is not agricultural income, because it does not come from cultivation of land. Such income is taxable, so Statement I is wrong.\n\nStatement II - Correct\nUnder Section 2(14), rural agricultural land is excluded from the definition of 'capital asset'. Land is rural if it lies beyond specified distances from municipal limits, depending on population. Therefore, gains on its sale are not taxed as capital gains. Agricultural land within urban limits is a capital asset.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct."},{"i":"2025-6","y":2025,"n":6,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. India has joined the Minerals Security Partnership as a member.\nII. India is a resource-rich country in all the 30 critical minerals that it has identified.\nIII. The Parliament in 2023 has amended the Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act, 1957 empowering the Central Government to exclusively auction mining lease and composite license for certain critical minerals.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"x":"I. India has joined the Minerals Security Partnership as a member - Correct\nThe MSP is a US-led initiative launched in 2022 to secure supply chains for critical minerals. India joined it in June 2023.\n\nII. India is resource-rich in all 30 critical minerals it identified - Incorrect\nIn 2023 India identified 30 critical minerals. For many of them, such as lithium, cobalt, nickel, rare earth processing, germanium and gallium, India depends heavily or entirely on imports. That is why it launched the National Critical Mineral Mission and overseas acquisitions through KABIL.\n\nIII. The MMDR Act was amended in 2023 to let the Centre exclusively auction certain critical minerals - Correct\nThe Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Amendment Act, 2023 empowered the Central Government to exclusively auction mining leases and composite licences for 24 critical and strategic minerals (listed in Part D of the First Schedule), such as lithium, graphite and rare earths, though revenue goes to the States. It also removed six minerals, including lithium, from the list of atomic minerals.\n\nHence the answer is (c) I and III only."},{"i":"2025-7","y":2025,"n":7,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: As regards returns from an investment in a company, generally, bondholders are considered to be relatively at lower risk than stockholders.\nStatement II: Bondholders are lenders to a company whereas stockholders are its owners.\nStatement III: For repayment purpose, bondholders are prioritized over stockholders by a company.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement I explains Statement II","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nBondholders generally face lower risk than stockholders. They receive a fixed, contractual interest (coupon) and repayment of principal on maturity, whereas stockholders' dividends and share prices vary with the company's performance and may even fall to zero.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nBondholders are creditors who have lent money to the company, so the company is legally bound to pay them. Stockholders are owners and bear the residual risk of the business. This difference in legal position explains why bonds are less risky.\n\nStatement III - Correct, and explains Statement I\nIn repayment, especially if the company is wound up or goes bankrupt, creditors including bondholders must be paid before shareholders get anything. This priority of claim also explains the lower risk of bondholders.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both Statements II and III are correct and both explain Statement I."},{"i":"2025-8","y":2025,"n":8,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. India accounts for a very large portion of all equity option contracts traded globally thus exhibiting a great boom.\nII. India's stock market has grown rapidly in the recent past even overtaking Hong Kong's at some point of time.\nIII. There is no regulatory body either to warn the small investors about the risks of options trading or to act on unregistered financial advisors in this regard.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"x":"I. India accounts for a very large portion of equity option contracts traded globally - Correct\nAccording to data from the Futures Industry Association, India has accounted for the majority of the world's equity derivatives contracts by number, over 80 percent in recent years, driven by retail trading in index options on NSE.\n\nII. India's stock market has grown rapidly and overtook Hong Kong's at some point - Correct\nIn January 2024, the combined market capitalisation of Indian stock exchanges crossed that of Hong Kong, making India the fourth-largest stock market in the world.\n\nIII. There is no regulatory body to warn small investors or act on unregistered advisers - Incorrect\nSEBI is the regulator. Its studies showed that about 9 out of 10 individual traders lose money in futures and options, it has issued repeated warnings, tightened F&O rules in 2024, and acted against unregistered investment advisers and 'finfluencers'.\n\nHence the answer is (a) I and II only."},{"i":"2025-9","y":2025,"n":9,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Circular economy reduces the emissions of greenhouse gases.\nStatement II: Circular economy reduces the use of raw materials as inputs.\nStatement III: Circular economy reduces wastage in the production process.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nA circular economy keeps products and materials in use through reducing, reusing, repairing, remanufacturing and recycling, instead of the linear 'take-make-dispose' model. Studies show it can significantly cut greenhouse gas emissions.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nBy reusing and recycling materials, a circular economy reduces the need for fresh raw materials. Extracting and processing virgin materials (like mining, smelting, cement and steel production) is very energy- and emission-intensive, so less raw material means fewer emissions.\n\nStatement III - Correct, and explains Statement I\nIt also reduces wastage in production through better design and use of by-products. Less waste means less energy wasted in producing unused goods, and less waste in landfills, which release methane, a potent greenhouse gas.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both II and III are correct and both explain Statement I."},{"i":"2025-10","y":2025,"n":10,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Capital receipts create a liability or cause a reduction in the assets of the Government.\nII. Borrowings and disinvestment are capital receipts.\nIII. Interest received on loans creates a liability of the Government.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"x":"I. Capital receipts create a liability or reduce assets of the government - Correct\nBy definition, capital receipts either create a liability (such as market borrowings, small savings, external loans) or reduce the government's assets (such as disinvestment of shares in PSUs or recovery of loans given earlier).\n\nII. Borrowings and disinvestment are capital receipts - Correct\nBorrowings create a liability that must be repaid, and disinvestment reduces the government's assets. Both are therefore capital receipts.\n\nIII. Interest received on loans creates a liability of the government - Incorrect\nInterest received on loans given by the government to States, PSUs or others is a revenue receipt (non-tax revenue). It neither creates a liability nor reduces assets.\n\nHence the answer is (a) I and II only."},{"i":"2025-11","y":2025,"n":11,"s":9,"q":"Consider the following statements about Raja Ram Mohan Roy:\nI. He possessed great love and respect for the traditional philosophical systems of the East.\nII. He desired his countrymen to accept the rational and scientific approach and the principle of human dignity and social equality of all men and women.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"I. He possessed great love and respect for the traditional philosophical systems of the East - Correct\nRaja Ram Mohan Roy was a deep scholar of Sanskrit, Persian and Arabic. He translated the Vedas and Upanishads into Bengali and English, defended Vedantic monotheism and founded the Vedanta College (1825). He opposed idol worship and superstition, not the philosophical heritage itself.\n\nII. He wanted his countrymen to accept a rational and scientific approach and the principles of human dignity and social equality of men and women - Correct\nHe supported Western scientific education (he opposed a purely Sanskrit college in 1823), campaigned against sati (abolished in 1829), polygamy and caste rigidity, and supported women's property rights. His Brahmo Sabha (1828) promoted reason and equality.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both I and II."},{"i":"2025-12","y":2025,"n":12,"s":9,"q":"Consider the following subjects with regard to Non-Cooperation Programme:\nI. Boycott of law-courts and foreign cloth\nII. Observance of strict non-violence\nIII. Retention of titles and honours without using them in public\nIV. Establishment of Panchayats for settling disputes\nHow many of the above were parts of Non-Cooperation Programme?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All the four"],"a":2,"x":"The Non-Cooperation programme was adopted at the Calcutta special session (September 1920) and confirmed at Nagpur (December 1920).\n\nI. Boycott of law courts and foreign cloth - Part of the programme\nLawyers such as C.R. Das and Motilal Nehru gave up practice, and foreign cloth was boycotted and burnt, with promotion of khadi.\n\nII. Observance of strict non-violence - Part of the programme\nNon-violence was essential; this is why Gandhi withdrew the movement after the Chauri Chaura incident in February 1922.\n\nIII. Retention of titles and honours without using them in public - Not part of the programme\nThe programme called for surrender of titles and honorary posts, not their retention. Gandhi himself returned the Kaisar-i-Hind medal.\n\nIV. Establishment of panchayats for settling disputes - Part of the programme\nAlong with boycott of courts, people were asked to settle disputes through panchayats or arbitration courts. National schools and colleges were also set up.\n\nThree items were part of the programme, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2025-13","y":2025,"n":13,"s":10,"q":"The irrigation device called 'Araghatta' was","o":["a water bag made of leather pulled over a pulley","a large wheel with earthen pots tied to the outer ends of its spokes","a larger earthen pot driven by bullocks","a large water bucket pulled up by rope directly by hand"],"a":1,"x":"(a) A water bag made of leather pulled over a pulley - Incorrect\nThis describes the charsa (moat or kos), in which bullocks pull a large leather bag up from a well over a pulley.\n\n(b) A large wheel with earthen pots tied to the outer ends of its spokes - Correct\nThe araghatta, mentioned in early medieval texts and inscriptions, is a water-lifting wheel. A series of pots attached around a wheel (or on a chain) dips into the well and lifts water as the wheel turns, usually turned by animals through gearing. It is similar to the later 'Persian wheel'.\n\n(c) A larger earthen pot driven by bullocks - Incorrect\nThis is not a recognised description of the araghatta.\n\n(d) A large bucket pulled up by rope directly by hand - Incorrect\nThis describes the simplest manual method of drawing water, not the araghatta."},{"i":"2025-14","y":2025,"n":14,"s":7,"q":"Who among the following rulers in ancient India had assumed the titles 'Mattavilasa', 'Vichitrachitta' and 'Gunabhara'?","o":["Mahendravarman I","Simhavishnu","Narasimhavarman I","Simhavarman"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Mahendravarman I - Correct\nPallava king Mahendravarman I (c. 600-630 CE) had titles such as Mattavilasa ('given to enjoyment'), Vichitrachitta ('curious-minded'), Gunabhara and Chaityakari ('temple builder'). He wrote the Sanskrit farce Mattavilasa Prahasana and introduced rock-cut temples in the Tamil region (e.g., Mandagapattu). He was first a Jain and later became a Shaivite under the influence of Appar.\n\n(b) Simhavishnu - Incorrect\nHe was Mahendravarman's father, who established Pallava power in the Kaveri region; his title was Avanisimha.\n\n(c) Narasimhavarman I - Incorrect\nMahendravarman's son, known as Mamalla and Vatapikonda ('conqueror of Vatapi') after defeating Pulakeshin II. Mamallapuram is named after him.\n\n(d) Simhavarman - Incorrect\nAn earlier Pallava ruler, not associated with these titles."},{"i":"2025-15","y":2025,"n":15,"s":7,"q":"Fa-hien (Faxian), the Chinese pilgrim, travelled to India during the reign of","o":["Samudragupta","Chandragupta II","Kumaragupta I","Skandagupta"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Samudragupta - Incorrect\nSamudragupta ruled c. 335-375 CE, before Fa-hien's visit. The Allahabad pillar inscription by Harishena describes his conquests.\n\n(b) Chandragupta II - Correct\nFa-hien (Faxian) travelled in India from about 399 to 414 CE, collecting Buddhist texts. This was the reign of Chandragupta II Vikramaditya (c. 375-415 CE). He described a peaceful, prosperous country with mild punishments, rest houses and hospitals, and noted the practice of untouchability towards Chandalas, though he did not name the king.\n\n(c) Kumaragupta I - Incorrect\nHe ruled c. 415-455 CE, after Fa-hien had left. Nalanda is believed to have been founded in his time.\n\n(d) Skandagupta - Incorrect\nHe ruled c. 455-467 CE and is known for repelling the Hunas."},{"i":"2025-16","y":2025,"n":16,"s":7,"q":"Who among the following led a successful military campaign against the kingdom of Srivijaya, the powerful maritime State, which ruled the Malay Peninsula, Sumatra, Java and the neighbouring islands?","o":["Amoghavarsha (Rashtrakuta)","Prataparudra (Kakatiya)","Rajendra I (Chola)","Vishnuvardhana (Hoysala)"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Amoghavarsha (Rashtrakuta) - Incorrect\nAmoghavarsha I (9th century) is known as a patron of literature (he wrote Kavirajamarga in Kannada) and for peaceful rule, not for overseas naval campaigns.\n\n(b) Prataparudra (Kakatiya) - Incorrect\nHe was the last major Kakatiya ruler of Warangal and fought against the Delhi Sultanate; he had no campaign against Srivijaya.\n\n(c) Rajendra I (Chola) - Correct\nRajendra Chola I (1014-1044) launched a naval expedition around 1025 CE against the Srivijaya (Sailendra) empire, which controlled the Straits of Malacca. The Tanjore inscriptions record the capture of Kadaram (Kedah) and other ports in the Malay Peninsula and Sumatra. The campaign aimed to protect Chola trade with China. He also took the title Gangaikondachola after his northern campaign.\n\n(d) Vishnuvardhana (Hoysala) - Incorrect\nHe made the Hoysalas a major power in Karnataka in the 12th century and built the Belur temple, but had no overseas campaigns."},{"i":"2025-17","y":2025,"n":17,"s":7,"q":"With reference to ancient India (600–322 BC), consider the following pairs:\n| Territorial region | River flowing in the region\n| I. Asmaka | Godavari\n| II. Kamboja | Vipas\n| III. Avanti | Mahanadi\n| IV. Kosala | Sarayu\nHow many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All the four"],"a":1,"x":"I. Asmaka : Godavari - Correct\nAsmaka (Assaka) was the only Mahajanapada south of the Vindhyas, located on the banks of the Godavari, with its capital at Potana (Paudanya).\n\nII. Kamboja : Vipas - Incorrect\nKamboja lay in the far north-west (the Rajouri-Hazara and Kabul valley region). The Vipas (Beas) flows through Punjab and Himachal, not Kamboja.\n\nIII. Avanti : Mahanadi - Incorrect\nAvanti was in the Malwa region, with capitals at Ujjayini and Mahishmati, on the Kshipra and Narmada. The Mahanadi flows through Chhattisgarh and Odisha.\n\nIV. Kosala : Sarayu - Correct\nKosala, with capitals Shravasti and Ayodhya (Saketa), lay on the Sarayu (Ghaghara) in eastern Uttar Pradesh.\n\nTwo pairs are correctly matched, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2025-18","y":2025,"n":18,"s":10,"q":"The first Gandharva Mahavidyalaya, a music training school, was set up in 1901 by Vishnu Digambar Paluskar in","o":["Delhi","Gwalior","Ujjain","Lahore"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Delhi - Incorrect\nDelhi did not host the first Gandharva Mahavidyalaya; a branch came up there much later.\n\n(b) Gwalior - Incorrect\nPaluskar was trained in the Gwalior gharana tradition (under Balakrishnabuwa Ichalkaranjikar), but the school was not founded there.\n\n(c) Ujjain - Incorrect\nNot associated with the founding of the school.\n\n(d) Lahore - Correct\nPt. Vishnu Digambar Paluskar founded the first Gandharva Mahavidyalaya at Lahore on 5 May 1901. His aim was to free music from the exclusive control of court musicians and make it accessible to ordinary people through institutional teaching. He later opened a branch in Bombay (1908). He also composed the popular tune of 'Raghupati Raghava Raja Ram'."},{"i":"2025-19","y":2025,"n":19,"s":7,"q":"Ashokan inscriptions suggest that the 'Pradeshika', 'Rajuka' and 'Yukta' were important officers at the","o":["village-level administration","district-level administration","provincial administration","level of the central administration"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Village-level administration - Incorrect\nVillages were managed by the gramika (village headman) and local elders; the officers named were above village level.\n\n(b) District-level administration - Correct\nAshoka's Rock Edict III states that Yuktas, Rajukas and Pradeshikas should go on tours (anusamyana) every five years to spread dhamma along with their regular work. Historians regard them as district-level officers: the Pradeshika as head of district administration, the Rajuka as an officer for land measurement and justice (with wide powers, as in Pillar Edict IV), and the Yukta as a subordinate officer handling revenue and records.\n\n(c) Provincial administration - Incorrect\nProvinces were headed by kumaras or aryaputras (princes) as viceroys at places like Taxila, Ujjain, Tosali and Suvarnagiri.\n\n(d) Central administration - Incorrect\nAt the centre, the king was assisted by the mantriparishad (council of ministers) and senior mahamattas."},{"i":"2025-20","y":2025,"n":20,"s":9,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of the Non-Cooperation Movement:\nI. The Congress declared the attainment of 'Swaraj' by all legitimate and peaceful means to be its objective.\nII. It was to be implemented in stages with civil disobedience and non-payment of taxes for the next stage only if 'Swaraj' did not come within a year and the Government resorted to repression.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"I. The Congress declared Swaraj by all legitimate and peaceful means as its objective - Correct\nAt the Nagpur session (December 1920), the Congress changed its creed from attaining self-government 'by constitutional means' to attaining Swaraj 'by all peaceful and legitimate means'. This allowed extra-constitutional mass action like non-cooperation.\n\nII. It was to be implemented in stages, with civil disobedience and non-payment of taxes only if Swaraj did not come within a year and the government resorted to repression - Correct\nThe programme started with boycott of titles, councils, courts, schools and foreign cloth. The next stage, mass civil disobedience including non-payment of taxes, was to be launched only later. Gandhi promised 'Swaraj within a year' and planned no-tax campaigns such as at Bardoli (February 1922), which was called off after Chauri Chaura.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both I and II."},{"i":"2025-21","y":2025,"n":21,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following countries:\nI. Austria\nII. Bulgaria\nIII. Croatia\nIV. Serbia\nV. Sweden\nVI. North Macedonia\nHow many of the above are members of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization?","o":["Only three","Only four","Only five","All the six"],"a":1,"x":"NATO had 32 members after Sweden joined in 2024.\n\nI. Austria - Not a member\nAustria has been constitutionally neutral since 1955 and is not part of NATO, though it is in the EU.\n\nII. Bulgaria - Member\nBulgaria joined NATO in 2004 in the major eastern enlargement.\n\nIII. Croatia - Member\nCroatia joined NATO in 2009 along with Albania.\n\nIV. Serbia - Not a member\nSerbia follows a policy of military neutrality, partly due to NATO's 1999 bombing and its close ties with Russia.\n\nV. Sweden - Member\nSweden ended its long neutrality after Russia's invasion of Ukraine and joined in March 2024 as the 32nd member (Finland joined in 2023).\n\nVI. North Macedonia - Member\nIt joined in 2020 as the 30th member, after resolving its name dispute with Greece through the Prespa Agreement.\n\nFour are members, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2025-22","y":2025,"n":22,"s":11,"q":"Consider the following countries:\nI. Bolivia\nII. Brazil\nIII. Colombia\nIV. Ecuador\nV. Paraguay\nVI. Venezuela\nAndes mountains pass through how many of the above countries?","o":["Only two","Only three","Only four","Only five"],"a":2,"x":"The Andes, the world's longest continental mountain range (about 7,000 km), run along the western side of South America through seven countries: Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Bolivia, Chile and Argentina.\n\nI. Bolivia - Yes\nThe Andes form the Altiplano plateau in Bolivia, with La Paz and Lake Titicaca.\n\nII. Brazil - No\nBrazil lies east of the Andes, with the Amazon basin and the Brazilian Highlands.\n\nIII. Colombia - Yes\nThe Andes split into three ranges (cordilleras) in Colombia.\n\nIV. Ecuador - Yes\nEcuador has Andean volcanoes like Chimborazo and Cotopaxi.\n\nV. Paraguay - No\nParaguay is a landlocked lowland country (Gran Chaco and Paraná plains), east of the Andes.\n\nVI. Venezuela - Yes\nThe northern end of the Andes (Cordillera de Mérida) lies in Venezuela.\n\nFour of the listed countries have the Andes, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2025-23","y":2025,"n":23,"s":11,"q":"Consider the following water bodies:\nI. Lake Tanganyika\nII. Lake Tonlé Sap\nIII. Patos Lagoon\nThrough how many of them does the equator pass?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":3,"x":"The equator passes through Ecuador, Colombia, Brazil, Sao Tome and Principe, Gabon, Republic of Congo, DR Congo, Uganda, Kenya, Somalia, Maldives (waters), Indonesia and Kiribati. In Africa it crosses Lake Victoria.\n\nI. Lake Tanganyika - No\nIt lies in the Western Rift Valley between about 3° S and 9° S (bordering Tanzania, DR Congo, Burundi and Zambia), well south of the equator.\n\nII. Lake Tonlé Sap - No\nIt is in Cambodia at about 12°-13° N; it is known for its seasonal reversal of flow.\n\nIII. Patos Lagoon - No\nIt is in Rio Grande do Sul, southern Brazil, at about 30°-32° S.\n\nThe equator passes through none of them, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2025-24","y":2025,"n":24,"s":6,"q":"Consider the following statements about turmeric during the year 2022-23:\nI. India is the largest producer and exporter of turmeric in the world.\nII. More than 30 varieties of turmeric are grown in India.\nIII. Maharashtra, Telangana, Karnataka and Tamil Nadu are major turmeric producing States in India.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"I. India is the largest producer and exporter of turmeric in the world - Correct\nIn 2022-23 India produced over 75 percent of the world's turmeric and accounted for more than 62 percent of world turmeric trade, exporting to over 380 countries.\n\nII. More than 30 varieties of turmeric are grown in India - Correct\nOver 30 varieties are grown in more than 20 States, such as Lakadong (Meghalaya, high curcumin), Salem and Erode (Tamil Nadu), Rajapuri (Maharashtra), Alleppey Finger (Kerala) and Kandhamal (Odisha).\n\nIII. Maharashtra, Telangana, Karnataka and Tamil Nadu are major producing States - Correct\nThese are the leading turmeric-producing States. The National Turmeric Board was set up in 2025 with headquarters at Nizamabad, Telangana.\n\nHence the answer is (d) I, II and III."},{"i":"2025-25","y":2025,"n":25,"s":5,"q":"Which of the following are the evidences of the phenomenon of continental drift?\nI. The belt of ancient rocks from Brazil coast matches with those from Western Africa.\nII. The gold deposits of Ghana are derived from the Brazil plateau when the two continents lay side by side.\nIII. The Gondwana system of sediments from India is known to have its counterparts in six different landmasses of the Southern Hemisphere.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","o":["I and III only","I and II only","I, II and III","II and III only"],"a":2,"x":"Alfred Wegener's continental drift theory (1912) was supported by several lines of evidence, described in NCERT's Fundamentals of Physical Geography.\n\nI. The belt of ancient rocks of the Brazil coast matches that of Western Africa - Correct (evidence)\nRadiometric dating shows that rocks about 2,000 million years old along the Brazilian coast match those of Western Africa, suggesting the two coasts were once joined.\n\nII. Gold deposits of Ghana are derived from the Brazil plateau when the continents lay side by side - Correct (evidence)\nGhana has rich gold deposits but no source rock for them in Africa. The source rock is found in the Brazilian plateau, which shows the gold was deposited when the two landmasses were together.\n\nIII. The Gondwana sediments of India have counterparts in six Southern Hemisphere landmasses - Correct (evidence)\nThe Gondwana system of sediments in India matches those in Africa, the Falkland Islands, Madagascar, Antarctica and Australia (and South America), with similar coal-bearing rocks and fossils such as Glossopteris. This points to a single supercontinent, Gondwanaland.\n\nHence the answer is (c) I, II and III."},{"i":"2025-26","y":2025,"n":26,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: The amount of dust particles in the atmosphere is more in subtropical and temperate areas than in equatorial and polar regions.\nStatement II: Subtropical and temperate areas have less dry winds.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I","Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct","Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct"],"a":2,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nNCERT (Fundamentals of Physical Geography) notes that dust particles are concentrated more in subtropical and temperate regions than in equatorial and polar regions. Dust comes from dry soils, deserts, volcanic ash, sea salt and pollution.\n\nStatement II - Incorrect\nThe reason is the opposite. Subtropical and temperate regions have more dry winds, which lift fine particles from deserts and dry land and carry them into the atmosphere. Equatorial regions are humid with frequent rain that washes dust out of the air, and polar regions are covered with ice and snow, leaving little loose dust. So saying these areas have 'less dry winds' is wrong.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct."},{"i":"2025-27","y":2025,"n":27,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: In January, in the Northern Hemisphere, the isotherms bend equatorward while crossing the landmasses, and poleward while crossing the oceans.\nStatement II: In January, the air over the oceans is warmer than that over the landmasses in the Northern Hemisphere.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I","Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct","Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nIn January (winter in the Northern Hemisphere), isotherms bend towards the equator (southward) over the continents and towards the pole (northward) over the oceans. This pattern is clearly seen over North America, Eurasia and the North Atlantic.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nLand heats and cools quickly, while water has a high specific heat and retains heat longer. In winter the continents become very cold, while the oceans stay relatively warm, helped by warm currents such as the Gulf Stream and North Atlantic Drift. Places over the ocean are therefore warmer than places at the same latitude on land, so a given temperature line shifts poleward over the sea and equatorward over land. This directly explains the bending of isotherms described in Statement I.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both statements are correct and Statement II explains Statement I."},{"i":"2025-28","y":2025,"n":28,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: In the context of effect of water on rocks, chalk is known as a very permeable rock whereas clay is known as quite an impermeable or least permeable rock.\nStatement II: Chalk is porous and hence can absorb water.\nStatement III: Clay is not at all porous.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"a":2,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nChalk is a very permeable rock; water passes through it easily, which is why chalk areas often have few surface streams. Clay is almost impermeable and holds water at the surface, which is why clay areas are often waterlogged.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nChalk is porous and its pores and fissures are connected, so it absorbs water and lets it pass through. This explains its permeability.\n\nStatement III - Incorrect\nClay is actually highly porous; its total pore space can be 40-50 percent. But the pores are extremely tiny and poorly connected, so water is held tightly and cannot move through. Clay is therefore impermeable, not non-porous. Porosity (how much space) and permeability (how easily water moves) are different properties.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Only one of Statements II and III is correct and it explains Statement I."},{"i":"2025-29","y":2025,"n":29,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Without the atmosphere, temperature would be well below freezing point everywhere on the Earth's surface.\nII. Heat absorbed and trapped by the atmosphere maintains our planet's average temperature.\nIII. Atmosphere's gases, like carbon dioxide, are particularly good at absorbing and trapping radiation.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["I and III only","I and II only","I, II and III","II and III only"],"a":2,"x":"I. Without the atmosphere, temperatures would be well below freezing everywhere on Earth - Correct\nDue to the natural greenhouse effect, Earth's average surface temperature is about 15°C. Without an atmosphere, it would be around -18°C, below the freezing point of water, similar to the Moon, whose surface has extreme temperature swings.\n\nII. Heat absorbed and trapped by the atmosphere maintains the planet's average temperature - Correct\nThe atmosphere lets most short-wave solar radiation through but absorbs much of the long-wave radiation emitted by the Earth, and re-radiates part of it back to the surface. This keeps the planet warm and reduces the difference between day and night temperatures.\n\nIII. Gases like carbon dioxide are particularly good at absorbing and trapping radiation - Correct\nGreenhouse gases such as water vapour, carbon dioxide, methane and nitrous oxide absorb infrared (heat) radiation very efficiently. Rising concentrations of these gases are the cause of global warming.\n\nHence the answer is (c) I, II and III."},{"i":"2025-30","y":2025,"n":30,"s":6,"q":"Consider the following statements about the Rashtriya Gokul Mission:\nI. It is important for the upliftment of rural poor as majority of low producing indigenous animals are with small and marginal farmers and landless labourers.\nII. It was initiated to promote indigenous cattle and buffalo rearing and conservation in a scientific and holistic manner.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"I. It is important for the upliftment of the rural poor, as most low-producing indigenous animals are with small and marginal farmers and landless labourers - Correct\nThe Rashtriya Gokul Mission itself states this rationale. Indigenous cattle and buffaloes, which often have low milk yields, are mostly owned by small and marginal farmers and landless labourers. Improving their productivity directly raises the incomes of the rural poor.\n\nII. It was initiated to promote indigenous cattle and buffalo rearing and conservation in a scientific and holistic manner - Correct\nThe Mission was launched in December 2014 to develop and conserve indigenous bovine breeds (such as Gir, Sahiwal, Red Sindhi, Tharparkar, Murrah buffalo) through genetic improvement, artificial insemination, sex-sorted semen, IVF technology, Gokul Grams and breed multiplication farms.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both I and II."},{"i":"2025-31","y":2025,"n":31,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Studies indicate that carbon dioxide emissions from cement industry account for more than 5% of global carbon emissions.\nStatement II: Silica-bearing clay is mixed with limestone while manufacturing cement.\nStatement III: Limestone is converted into lime during clinker production for cement manufacturing.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"a":1,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nThe cement industry accounts for about 7-8 percent of global carbon dioxide emissions, well above 5 percent. If it were a country, it would be among the largest emitters.\n\nStatement II - Correct, but does not explain Statement I\nCement is made by heating limestone mixed with silica-bearing clay or shale (sources of silica, alumina and iron oxide) in a kiln to form clinker. This is true, but mixing clay does not itself release significant CO2, so it does not explain the high emissions.\n\nStatement III - Correct, and explains Statement I\nDuring clinker production, limestone (calcium carbonate) is heated to about 900°C and breaks down into lime (calcium oxide) and carbon dioxide. This process, called calcination, releases CO2 directly from the raw material and makes up about 60 percent of cement's emissions; the rest comes from burning fuel to heat kilns to about 1,450°C.\n\nHence the answer is (b) Both II and III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I."},{"i":"2025-32","y":2025,"n":32,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: At the 28th United Nations Climate Change Conference (COP28), India refrained from signing the 'Declaration on Climate and Health'.\nStatement II: The COP28 Declaration on Climate and Health is a binding declaration; and if signed, it becomes mandatory to decarbonize health sector.\nStatement III: If India's health sector is decarbonized, the resilience of its health-care system may be compromised.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"a":2,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nAt COP28 in Dubai (December 2023), about 120-plus countries endorsed the 'COP28 Declaration on Climate and Health', but India did not sign it.\n\nStatement II - Incorrect\nThe declaration is voluntary and non-binding. Signing it does not create a legal obligation to decarbonise the health sector; it only expresses commitments in principle.\n\nStatement III - Correct, and explains Statement I\nIndia's stated concern, as reported, was that commitments to cut greenhouse gas emissions from the health sector could hamper the expansion of health care, for example cooling and power needed in hospitals and cold chains, especially in rural and remote areas. It was felt that decarbonising at this stage might compromise the resilience and reach of the health-care system. This explains why India refrained from signing.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Only one of Statements II and III is correct and it explains Statement I."},{"i":"2025-33","y":2025,"n":33,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Scientific studies suggest that a shift is taking place in the Earth's rotation and axis.\nStatement II: Solar flares and associated coronal mass ejections bombarded the Earth's outermost atmosphere with tremendous amount of energy.\nStatement III: As the Earth's polar ice melts, the water tends to move towards the equator.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"a":1,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nStudies (for example by NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory and ETH Zurich, 2024) show that the Earth's rotation axis is drifting (polar motion) and that the length of the day is changing slightly.\n\nStatement II - Correct, but does not explain Statement I\nSolar flares and coronal mass ejections do bombard Earth's upper atmosphere with huge amounts of energy, causing geomagnetic storms and auroras (for example in May 2024). However, they do not change the distribution of mass on Earth, so they do not shift Earth's rotation or axis.\n\nStatement III - Correct, and explains Statement I\nAs polar ice sheets and glaciers melt due to global warming, the water spreads into the oceans and moves towards the equator. This redistribution of mass changes Earth's moment of inertia, slowing its rotation slightly (lengthening the day, like a spinning skater extending the arms) and shifting the axis. Groundwater extraction also contributes.\n\nHence the answer is (b) Both II and III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I."},{"i":"2025-34","y":2025,"n":34,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Article 6 of the Paris Agreement on climate change is frequently discussed in global discussions on sustainable development and climate change.\nStatement II: Article 6 of the Paris Agreement on climate change sets out the principles of carbon markets.\nStatement III: Article 6 of the Paris Agreement on climate change intends to promote inter-country non-market strategies to reach their climate targets.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nArticle 6 of the Paris Agreement is one of the most discussed parts of climate negotiations. Its rules were finalised at COP26 (Glasgow) and COP29 (Baku), and it is central to global carbon trading.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nArticle 6.2 allows countries to cooperate bilaterally and transfer emission reductions as 'Internationally Transferred Mitigation Outcomes' (ITMOs). Article 6.4 creates a UN-supervised carbon crediting mechanism (the Paris Agreement Crediting Mechanism, replacing the Kyoto-era CDM). Together they set out the principles of carbon markets, which is why Article 6 attracts so much attention.\n\nStatement III - Correct, and explains Statement I\nArticle 6.8 provides a framework for non-market approaches, such as cooperation through finance, technology transfer and capacity building, to help countries meet their targets. This is also widely discussed.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both II and III are correct and both explain Statement I."},{"i":"2025-35","y":2025,"n":35,"s":2,"q":"Which one of the following launched the 'Nature Solutions Finance Hub for Asia and the Pacific'?","o":["The Asian Development Bank (ADB)","The Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB)","The New Development Bank (NDB)","The International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD)"],"a":0,"x":"(a) The Asian Development Bank - Correct\nADB launched the 'Nature Solutions Finance Hub for Asia and the Pacific' in 2024. It aims to mobilise at least 2 billion dollars from public and private sources for investments in nature-based solutions, such as restoring mangroves, wetlands and forests, which help both climate adaptation and biodiversity.\n\n(b) The Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank - Incorrect\nThe Beijing-based AIIB finances infrastructure but did not launch this hub.\n\n(c) The New Development Bank - Incorrect\nThe BRICS bank, based in Shanghai, is not associated with this initiative.\n\n(d) The International Bank for Reconstruction and Development - Incorrect\nThe IBRD, part of the World Bank Group, did not launch this regional hub."},{"i":"2025-36","y":2025,"n":36,"s":2,"q":"With reference to 'Direct Air Capture', an emerging technology, which of the following statements is/are correct?\nI. It can be used as a way of carbon sequestration.\nII. It can be a valuable approach for plastic production and in food processing.\nIII. In aviation, it can be a source of carbon for combining with hydrogen to create synthetic low-carbon fuel.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","o":["I and II only","III only","I, II and III","None of the above statements is correct"],"a":2,"x":"Direct Air Capture (DAC) uses chemical sorbents or solvents to pull carbon dioxide directly out of the ambient air.\n\nI. It can be used for carbon sequestration - Correct\nThe captured CO2 can be injected deep underground into rock formations where it is permanently stored or mineralised. An example is Climeworks' Orca and Mammoth plants in Iceland.\n\nII. It can be valuable for plastic production and food processing - Correct\nCaptured CO2 is a raw material that can be converted into chemicals and polymers used to make plastics, and food-grade CO2 is used for carbonating beverages, packaging and in greenhouses.\n\nIII. In aviation, it can provide carbon to combine with hydrogen for synthetic low-carbon fuel - Correct\nCO2 from DAC can be combined with green hydrogen to produce synthetic hydrocarbon fuels (e-fuels or power-to-liquid sustainable aviation fuel). Because the carbon comes from the air, burning the fuel is close to carbon neutral.\n\nHence the answer is (c) I, II and III."},{"i":"2025-37","y":2025,"n":37,"s":2,"q":"Regarding Peacock tarantula (Gooty tarantula), consider the following statements:\nI. It is an omnivorous crustacean.\nII. Its natural habitat in India is only limited to some forest areas.\nIII. In its natural habitat, it is an arboreal species.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","I and III","II only","II and III"],"a":3,"x":"I. It is an omnivorous crustacean - Incorrect\nThe Peacock or Gooty tarantula (Poecilotheria metallica) is a spider, an arachnid, not a crustacean. It is carnivorous, feeding mainly on insects, not omnivorous.\n\nII. Its natural habitat in India is limited to some forest areas - Correct\nIt is endemic to a small area of dry deciduous forest in the Eastern Ghats of Andhra Pradesh (near Nandyal and Giddalur; it was first described from a specimen from Gooty). It is listed as Critically Endangered by the IUCN because of habitat loss and the pet trade.\n\nIII. In its natural habitat, it is an arboreal species - Correct\nIt lives in trees, sheltering in holes and crevices in tall trees, and hunts by ambushing prey on tree trunks. It is known for its striking metallic blue colour.\n\nHence the answer is (d) II and III."},{"i":"2025-38","y":2025,"n":38,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions in India are less than 0.5 t CO2/capita.\nII. In terms of CO2 emissions from fuel combustion, India ranks second in Asia-Pacific region.\nIII. Electricity and heat producers are the largest sources of CO2 emissions in India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I and III only","II only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"x":"I. India's CO2 emissions are less than 0.5 tonnes per capita - Incorrect\nIndia's per capita CO2 emissions are about 1.9 to 2 tonnes, well below the world average of about 4.7 tonnes and far below the USA (about 14 tonnes), but much higher than 0.5 tonnes.\n\nII. In CO2 emissions from fuel combustion, India ranks second in the Asia-Pacific - Correct\nIn total CO2 emissions from fuel combustion, China is first in the Asia-Pacific region and India is second (India is third in the world after China and the USA).\n\nIII. Electricity and heat producers are the largest source of CO2 emissions in India - Correct\nCoal-fired power plants make electricity and heat generation the largest source of India's CO2 emissions, accounting for around half of the total, followed by industry and transport.\n\nHence the answer is (c) II and III only."},{"i":"2025-39","y":2025,"n":39,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| Plant | Description\n| I. Cassava | Woody shrub\n| II. Ginger | Herb with pseudostem\n| III. Malabar spinach | Herbaceous climber\n| IV. Mint | Annual shrub\n| V. Papaya | Woody shrub\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["Only two","Only three","Only four","All the five"],"a":1,"x":"I. Cassava : Woody shrub - Correct\nCassava (tapioca) is a perennial woody shrub grown for its starchy tuberous roots. It is widely grown in Kerala and Tamil Nadu.\n\nII. Ginger : Herb with pseudostem - Correct\nGinger is a herbaceous perennial. The underground part is a rhizome, and the above-ground 'stem' is a pseudostem formed by rolled leaf sheaths, as in banana.\n\nIII. Malabar spinach : Herbaceous climber - Correct\nMalabar spinach (Basella alba), called 'poi' in India, is a fast-growing, soft-stemmed (herbaceous) climbing vine with thick edible leaves.\n\nIV. Mint : Annual shrub - Incorrect\nMint is a perennial herb that spreads by runners (stolons and rhizomes). It is neither annual nor a shrub.\n\nV. Papaya : Woody shrub - Incorrect\nPapaya is a large herbaceous, tree-like plant with a single soft, hollow, unbranched stem. It is not a woody shrub.\n\nThree pairs are correctly matched, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2025-40","y":2025,"n":40,"s":2,"q":"With reference to the planet Earth, consider the following statements:\nI. Rain forests produce more oxygen than that produced by oceans.\nII. Marine phytoplankton and photosynthetic bacteria produce about 50% of world's oxygen.\nIII. Well-oxygenated surface water contains several folds higher oxygen than that in atmospheric air.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I and II","II only","I and III","None of the above statements is correct"],"a":1,"x":"I. Rainforests produce more oxygen than oceans - Incorrect\nOceans produce at least half, and by some estimates up to 70-80 percent, of Earth's oxygen, mainly through phytoplankton, algae and cyanobacteria. Moreover, mature rainforests consume almost all the oxygen they produce through respiration and decay of organic matter, so their net contribution is small.\n\nII. Marine phytoplankton and photosynthetic bacteria produce about 50 percent of the world's oxygen - Correct\nAccording to NOAA and other sources, ocean phytoplankton and photosynthetic bacteria such as Prochlorococcus (the smallest photosynthetic organism) produce roughly half of the world's oxygen.\n\nIII. Well-oxygenated surface water contains several times more oxygen than air - Incorrect\nAir contains about 21 percent oxygen (about 280 mg per litre). Well-oxygenated surface water contains only about 8-10 mg of dissolved oxygen per litre. Air therefore has many times more oxygen than water, not the other way round.\n\nHence the answer is (b) II only."},{"i":"2025-41","y":2025,"n":41,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following types of vehicles:\nI. Full battery electric vehicles\nII. Hydrogen fuel cell vehicles\nIII. Fuel cell-electric hybrid vehicles\nHow many of the above are considered as alternative powertrain vehicles?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":2,"x":"Alternative powertrains are vehicle propulsion systems other than conventional internal combustion engines running on petrol or diesel.\n\nI. Full battery electric vehicles - Alternative powertrain\nBEVs run entirely on electricity stored in batteries and use electric motors, with no engine or tailpipe emissions.\n\nII. Hydrogen fuel cell vehicles - Alternative powertrain\nFCEVs generate electricity on board by combining hydrogen with oxygen in a fuel cell, emitting only water vapour.\n\nIII. Fuel cell-electric hybrid vehicles - Alternative powertrain\nThese combine a fuel cell with a battery (and sometimes plug-in charging), using the battery to capture braking energy and boost power.\n\nAll three are alternative powertrains, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2025-42","y":2025,"n":42,"s":3,"q":"With reference to Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs), consider the following statements:\nI. All types of UAVs can do vertical landing.\nII. All types of UAVs can do automated hovering.\nIII. All types of UAVs can use battery only as a source of power supply.\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":3,"x":"The key word in each statement is 'all'. UAVs come in fixed-wing, rotary-wing (multirotor or helicopter) and hybrid VTOL types, with different power sources.\n\nI. All UAVs can do vertical landing - Incorrect\nFixed-wing UAVs, such as many large military drones (e.g., Heron, Predator types), need a runway, catapult or parachute for take-off and landing; they cannot land vertically. Only rotary-wing and VTOL designs can.\n\nII. All UAVs can do automated hovering - Incorrect\nFixed-wing drones must keep moving forward to generate lift, so they cannot hover. Hovering is possible only for multirotors, helicopters and VTOL types.\n\nIII. All UAVs use only batteries as a power source - Incorrect\nMany UAVs use petrol or heavy-fuel internal combustion engines, turboprop or jet engines, hybrid systems, solar panels or hydrogen fuel cells. Batteries are mainly used in small drones.\n\nNone of the statements is correct, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2025-43","y":2025,"n":43,"s":3,"q":"In the context of electric vehicle batteries, consider the following elements:\nI. Cobalt\nII. Graphite\nIII. Lithium\nIV. Nickel\nHow many of the above usually make up battery cathodes?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All the four"],"a":2,"x":"In a lithium-ion battery, the cathode is usually a lithium metal oxide or phosphate, such as NMC (lithium nickel manganese cobalt oxide), NCA (lithium nickel cobalt aluminium oxide) or LFP (lithium iron phosphate). The anode is usually graphite.\n\nI. Cobalt - Cathode material\nCobalt stabilises the layered structure of NMC and NCA cathodes; about 70 percent of it comes from the DR Congo.\n\nII. Graphite - Not a cathode material\nGraphite is used in the anode, where lithium ions are stored during charging.\n\nIII. Lithium - Cathode material\nLithium compounds form the basis of all lithium-ion cathodes.\n\nIV. Nickel - Cathode material\nNickel increases energy density in NMC and NCA cathodes.\n\nThree of the four usually make up cathodes, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2025-44","y":2025,"n":44,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following:\nI. Cigarette butts\nII. Eyeglass lenses\nIII. Car tyres\nHow many of them contain plastic?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":2,"x":"I. Cigarette butts - Contain plastic\nCigarette filters are made of cellulose acetate, a type of plastic that takes years to break down. Cigarette butts are among the most common litter items and a source of microplastics.\n\nII. Eyeglass lenses - Contain plastic\nMost modern spectacle lenses are made of plastics such as CR-39 (allyl diglycol carbonate), polycarbonate and Trivex, which are lighter and safer than glass.\n\nIII. Car tyres - Contain plastic\nTyres are made of natural rubber blended with synthetic rubbers such as styrene-butadiene rubber (a synthetic polymer), along with nylon or polyester cords. Tyre wear is one of the largest sources of microplastics in the environment.\n\nAll three contain plastic, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2025-45","y":2025,"n":45,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following substances:\nI. Ethanol\nII. Nitroglycerine\nIII. Urea\nCoal gasification technology can be used in the production of how many of them?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":1,"x":"Coal gasification converts coal into syngas (a mixture of carbon monoxide and hydrogen) by reacting it with oxygen and steam at high temperature. Syngas can be used to make many chemicals. India's National Coal Gasification Mission aims to gasify 100 million tonnes of coal by 2030.\n\nI. Ethanol - Can be produced\nSyngas can be converted into methanol and ethanol by catalytic processes or by fermentation using special bacteria.\n\nII. Nitroglycerine - Cannot be produced (by this route)\nNitroglycerine is made by nitrating glycerol with a mixture of concentrated nitric and sulphuric acids. It is not a product of coal gasification.\n\nIII. Urea - Can be produced\nHydrogen from syngas is used to make ammonia (Haber-Bosch process), and ammonia reacts with carbon dioxide to form urea. The Talcher Fertilizers plant in Odisha uses this route.\n\nTwo can be produced, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2025-46","y":2025,"n":46,"s":3,"q":"What is the common characteristic of the chemical substances generally known as CL-20, HMX and LLM-105, which are sometimes talked about in media?","o":["These are alternatives to hydro-fluorocarbon refrigerants","These are explosives in military weapons","These are high-energy fuels for cruise missiles","These are fuels for rocket propulsion"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Alternatives to hydrofluorocarbon refrigerants - Incorrect\nAlternatives to HFCs include hydrofluoroolefins (HFOs), ammonia, CO2 and hydrocarbons, not these compounds.\n\n(b) Explosives in military weapons - Correct\nCL-20 (hexanitrohexaazaisowurtzitane) is one of the most powerful non-nuclear explosives. HMX (octogen) is a high-energy explosive used in warheads and plastic-bonded explosives. LLM-105 is an insensitive high explosive valued for its stability. All three are used in military munitions; India's HEMRL (DRDO) has worked on CL-20.\n\n(c) High-energy fuels for cruise missiles - Incorrect\nCruise missiles use jet fuels; these compounds are explosives, not fuels.\n\n(d) Fuels for rocket propulsion - Incorrect\nSome of these can be used as additives in solid propellants, but they are primarily known as explosives."},{"i":"2025-47","y":2025,"n":47,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. It is expected that Majorana 1 chip will enable quantum computing.\nII. Majorana 1 chip has been introduced by Amazon Web Services (AWS).\nIII. Deep learning is a subset of machine learning.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"x":"I. Majorana 1 chip is expected to enable quantum computing - Correct\nMajorana 1, announced in February 2025, is a quantum processor based on a new 'topological core'. It uses exotic Majorana zero modes in a special material (a topoconductor) to create topological qubits, which are expected to be more stable and easier to scale towards a million qubits.\n\nII. Majorana 1 was introduced by Amazon Web Services - Incorrect\nIt was introduced by Microsoft. AWS separately announced its own quantum chip called 'Ocelot' in 2025, and Google has its 'Willow' chip.\n\nIII. Deep learning is a subset of machine learning - Correct\nArtificial intelligence is the broadest field; machine learning is a subset where systems learn from data; deep learning is a subset of machine learning that uses multi-layered neural networks, powering image recognition and large language models.\n\nHence the answer is (c) I and III only."},{"i":"2025-48","y":2025,"n":48,"s":3,"q":"With reference to monoclonal antibodies, often mentioned in news, consider the following statements:\nI. They are man-made proteins.\nII. They stimulate immunological function due to their ability to bind to specific antigens.\nIII. They are used in treating viral infections like that of Nipah virus.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"I. They are man-made proteins - Correct\nMonoclonal antibodies are laboratory-made proteins that act like natural antibodies. They are produced from a single clone of cells (originally hybridoma technology, now recombinant methods), so all copies are identical and target one specific antigen.\n\nII. They stimulate immunological function by binding to specific antigens - Correct\nBy binding precisely to a target antigen (such as a virus protein or a cancer cell marker), they can neutralise the pathogen, mark it for destruction by immune cells, or block harmful signals, thereby enhancing the immune response.\n\nIII. They are used against viral infections such as Nipah - Correct\nMonoclonal antibodies are used to treat viral infections. During the Nipah outbreaks in Kerala (2018, 2023), India obtained the human monoclonal antibody m102.4 from Australia for emergency use. mAbs were also used against COVID-19, Ebola and RSV.\n\nHence the answer is (d) I, II and III."},{"i":"2025-49","y":2025,"n":49,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. No virus can survive in ocean waters.\nII. No virus can infect bacteria.\nIII. No virus can change the cellular transcriptional activity in host cells.\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":3,"x":"I. No virus can survive in ocean waters - Incorrect\nOceans are full of viruses; there are about 10 million viruses in every millilitre of seawater. Marine viruses are the most abundant biological entities in the oceans and play a major role in nutrient cycling by infecting bacteria and plankton.\n\nII. No virus can infect bacteria - Incorrect\nBacteriophages ('phages') are viruses that infect bacteria. They are the most numerous viruses on Earth and are being used in phage therapy against antibiotic-resistant bacteria.\n\nIII. No virus can change the transcriptional activity of host cells - Incorrect\nViruses routinely take over host cells and change their gene expression to make copies of themselves. For example, HIV integrates into host DNA, and cancer-causing viruses such as HPV alter cell cycle genes.\n\nNone of the statements is correct, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2025-50","y":2025,"n":50,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Activated carbon is a good and an attractive tool to remove pollutants from effluent streams and to remediate contaminants from various industries.\nStatement II: Activated carbon exhibits a large surface area and a strong potential for adsorbing heavy metals.\nStatement III: Activated carbon can be easily synthesized from environmental wastes with high carbon content.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I","Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I","Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nActivated carbon is widely used to treat industrial effluents and remediate contaminated water, removing dyes, pesticides, organic chemicals and heavy metals such as lead, chromium and mercury.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nActivated carbon has an extremely porous structure with a very large surface area, about 500 to 1,500 square metres per gram. Pollutants stick to this surface (adsorption), so it can remove large amounts of contaminants, including heavy metals. This explains why it is a good tool.\n\nStatement III - Correct, and explains Statement I\nIt can be produced cheaply from carbon-rich waste such as coconut shells, rice husk, sawdust, fruit peels and sewage sludge by carbonisation and activation. This low cost and use of waste make it an 'attractive' option.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both II and III are correct and both explain Statement I."},{"i":"2025-51","y":2025,"n":51,"s":0,"q":"With reference to the Indian polity, consider the following statements:\nI. An Ordinance can amend any Central Act.\nII. An Ordinance can abridge a Fundamental Right.\nIII. An Ordinance can come into effect from a back date.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"x":"I. An Ordinance can amend any Central Act - Correct\nUnder Article 123, an ordinance issued by the President has the same force and effect as an Act of Parliament. It can therefore amend or repeal any Central Act, for example the ordinances amending the Land Acquisition Act or the Insolvency Code. However, it cannot amend the Constitution.\n\nII. An Ordinance can abridge a Fundamental Right - Incorrect\nAn ordinance is 'law' under Article 13(3)(a). Article 13(2) says any law that takes away or abridges Fundamental Rights is void to that extent. So an ordinance cannot abridge Fundamental Rights (except as permitted, e.g., reasonable restrictions or during emergency under Articles 358-359).\n\nIII. An Ordinance can come into effect from a back date - Correct\nLike an Act of Parliament, an ordinance can be retrospective, that is, given effect from an earlier date, subject to constitutional limits (for example, it cannot create retrospective criminal offences, because of Article 20(1)).\n\nHence the answer is (c) I and III only."},{"i":"2025-52","y":2025,"n":52,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| State | Description\n| I. Arunachal Pradesh | The capital is named after a fort, and the State has two National Parks\n| II. Nagaland | The State came into existence on the basis of a Constitutional Amendment Act\n| III. Tripura | Initially a Part 'C' State, it became a centrally administered territory with the reorganization of States in 1956 and later attained the status of a full-fledged State\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":2,"x":"I. Arunachal Pradesh: capital named after a fort, and two National Parks - Correct\nThe capital Itanagar is named after the Ita Fort (Ita means brick), a 14th-15th century brick fort. The State has two National Parks: Namdapha (also a tiger reserve) and Mouling.\n\nII. Nagaland came into existence on the basis of a Constitutional Amendment Act - Correct\nNagaland became a State on 1 December 1963 under the State of Nagaland Act, 1962. The Constitution (Thirteenth Amendment) Act, 1962 inserted Article 371A, giving it special provisions regarding Naga customary law and land. So its formation was linked to a constitutional amendment.\n\nIII. Tripura was a Part C State, became a centrally administered territory in 1956 and later a full State - Correct\nTripura was a Part C State in 1950, became a Union Territory under the States Reorganisation Act, 1956, and became a full State in January 1972 under the North-Eastern Areas (Reorganisation) Act, 1971.\n\nAll three pairs are correctly matched, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2025-53","y":2025,"n":53,"s":0,"q":"With reference to India, consider the following:\nI. The Inter-State Council\nII. The National Security Council\nIII. Zonal Councils\nHow many of the above were established as per the provisions of the Constitution of India?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":0,"x":"I. The Inter-State Council - Constitutional\nArticle 263 empowers the President to establish an Inter-State Council to advise on disputes and coordinate policy between States and the Centre. It was set up in 1990 by a Presidential order, on the recommendation of the Sarkaria Commission. Since it is provided for in the Constitution, it counts as established under constitutional provisions.\n\nII. The National Security Council - Not constitutional\nThe NSC was set up in 1998 by an executive order of the Government of India. It is headed by the Prime Minister, with the National Security Adviser as its secretary. There is no constitutional provision for it.\n\nIII. Zonal Councils - Not constitutional\nThe five Zonal Councils (Northern, Central, Eastern, Western and Southern) are statutory bodies created under the States Reorganisation Act, 1956, chaired by the Union Home Minister. The North Eastern Council was created by a separate Act in 1971.\n\nOnly one is constitutional, so the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2025-54","y":2025,"n":54,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. The Constitution of India explicitly mentions that in certain spheres the Governor of a State acts in his/her own discretion.\nII. The President of India can, of his/her own, reserve a bill passed by a State Legislature for his/her consideration without it being forwarded by the Governor of the State concerned.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"I. The Constitution explicitly mentions that in certain spheres the Governor acts in his or her own discretion - Correct\nArticle 163(1) says the Council of Ministers aids and advises the Governor 'except in so far as he is by or under this Constitution required to exercise his functions or any of them in his discretion'. Specific discretionary powers appear in Article 371A (Nagaland, law and order), Article 371H (Arunachal Pradesh), paragraph 9 of the Sixth Schedule (royalty from mining in tribal areas) and in reserving bills for the President under Article 200. So the Constitution does explicitly mention discretion.\n\nII. The President can on his or her own reserve a State bill for consideration without it being forwarded by the Governor - Incorrect\nUnder Article 200, only the Governor can reserve a bill passed by the State legislature for the President's consideration. The President then acts under Article 201. The President cannot pick up a State bill directly.\n\nHence the answer is (a) I only."},{"i":"2025-55","y":2025,"n":55,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| Provision in the Constitution of India | Stated under\n| I. Separation of Judiciary from the Executive in the public services of the State | The Directive Principles of the State Policy\n| II. Valuing and preserving of the rich heritage of our composite culture | The Fundamental Duties\n| III. Prohibition of employment of children below the age of 14 years in factories | The Fundamental Rights\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":2,"x":"I. Separation of Judiciary from the Executive in public services of the State : Directive Principles - Correct\nArticle 50, a Directive Principle, directs the State to take steps to separate the judiciary from the executive in the public services of the State. This was largely achieved through the Code of Criminal Procedure, 1973.\n\nII. Valuing and preserving the rich heritage of our composite culture : Fundamental Duties - Correct\nArticle 51A(f), added by the 42nd Amendment (1976), makes it a Fundamental Duty of every citizen to value and preserve the rich heritage of the country's composite culture.\n\nIII. Prohibition of employment of children below 14 in factories : Fundamental Rights - Correct\nArticle 24, part of the Right against Exploitation, prohibits employment of children below 14 years in factories, mines or other hazardous employment.\n\nAll three pairs are correctly matched, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2025-56","y":2025,"n":56,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nWith reference to the Constitution of India, if an area in a State is declared as Scheduled Area under the Fifth Schedule\nI. the State Government loses its executive power in such areas and a local body assumes total administration\nII. the Union Government can take over the total administration of such areas under certain circumstances on the recommendations of the Governor\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"I. The State Government loses executive power in Scheduled Areas and a local body assumes total administration - Incorrect\nUnder the Fifth Schedule, the executive power of the State continues to extend to Scheduled Areas. The Governor has special powers: he can direct that an Act of Parliament or the State legislature shall not apply or shall apply with modifications, and can make regulations on land transfer and money-lending in consultation with the Tribes Advisory Council. There is no provision for a local body taking over total administration. (Autonomous District Councils exist only under the Sixth Schedule.)\n\nII. The Union Government can take over total administration of such areas on the Governor's recommendation - Incorrect\nParagraph 3 of the Fifth Schedule says the executive power of the Union extends only to giving directions to the State regarding the administration of Scheduled Areas. The Governor must report to the President annually. There is no provision for the Union to take over total administration.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Neither I nor II."},{"i":"2025-57","y":2025,"n":57,"s":1,"q":"With reference to India, consider the following pairs:\n| Organization | Union Ministry\n| I. The National Automotive Board | Ministry of Commerce and Industry\n| II. The Coir Board | Ministry of Heavy Industries\n| III. The National Centre for Trade Information | Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":3,"x":"I. The National Automotive Board : Ministry of Commerce and Industry - Incorrect\nThe National Automotive Board is under the Ministry of Heavy Industries. It supports implementation of schemes like FAME for electric vehicles and the automotive PLI scheme.\n\nII. The Coir Board : Ministry of Heavy Industries - Incorrect\nThe Coir Board is a statutory body set up under the Coir Industry Act, 1953, headquartered in Kochi. It works under the Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises.\n\nIII. The National Centre for Trade Information : Ministry of MSME - Incorrect\nThe NCTI was set up by the Ministry of Commerce and Industry (with ITPO and the National Informatics Centre) to provide trade information and data services.\n\nNone of the pairs is correctly matched, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2025-58","y":2025,"n":58,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following subjects under the Constitution of India:\nI. List I-Union List, in the Seventh Schedule\nII. Extent of the executive power of a State\nIII. Conditions of the Governor's office\nFor a constitutional amendment with respect to which of the above, ratification by the Legislatures of not less than one-half of the States is required before presenting the bill to the President of India for assent?","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"x":"Under the proviso to Article 368(2), certain amendments need ratification by the legislatures of at least half the States before the bill is presented to the President. These cover matters affecting the federal structure: election of the President (Articles 54, 55); extent of executive power of the Union and the States (Articles 73, 162); provisions on the Supreme Court and High Courts; distribution of legislative powers (Part XI Chapter I); any of the Lists in the Seventh Schedule; representation of States in Parliament; the GST Council (Article 279A); and Article 368 itself.\n\nI. List I - Union List in the Seventh Schedule - Requires ratification\nAny change in the Seventh Schedule lists alters the Centre-State division of powers.\n\nII. Extent of the executive power of a State - Requires ratification\nThis is Article 162, which is expressly listed.\n\nIII. Conditions of the Governor's office - Does not require ratification\nArticle 158 (conditions of Governor's office) is not in the list. Amendments to it need only a special majority of Parliament.\n\nHence the answer is (a) I and II only."},{"i":"2025-59","y":2025,"n":59,"s":0,"q":"With reference to the Indian polity, consider the following statements:\nI. The Governor of a State is not answerable to any court for the exercise and performance of the powers and duties of his/her office.\nII. No criminal proceedings shall be instituted or continued against the Governor during his/her term of office.\nIII. Members of a State Legislature are not liable to any proceedings in any court in respect of anything said within the House.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"I. The Governor is not answerable to any court for exercise of the powers and duties of office - Correct\nArticle 361(1) provides that the President and Governors are not answerable to any court for the exercise and performance of their powers and duties. (The Supreme Court has held, however, that the validity of their actions can still be examined, for example in Rameshwar Prasad, 2006.)\n\nII. No criminal proceedings can be instituted or continued against the Governor during the term of office - Correct\nArticle 361(2) gives complete immunity from criminal proceedings during the term. Article 361(3) also bars arrest or imprisonment during the term.\n\nIII. Members of a State Legislature are not liable to proceedings in any court for anything said in the House - Correct\nArticle 194(2) gives members of State Legislatures freedom of speech in the legislature: they cannot be sued or prosecuted for anything said or any vote given in the House or its committees (Article 105 gives the same protection to MPs).\n\nHence the answer is (d) I, II and III."},{"i":"2025-60","y":2025,"n":60,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following activities:\nI. Production of crude oil\nII. Refining, storage and distribution of petroleum\nIII. Marketing and sale of petroleum products\nIV. Production of natural gas\nHow many of the above activities are regulated by the Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board in our country?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All the four"],"a":1,"x":"The Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board (PNGRB), set up under the PNGRB Act, 2006, regulates the 'downstream' sector: refining, processing, storage, transportation, distribution, marketing and sale of petroleum, petroleum products and natural gas. It protects consumers and promotes competition. The 'upstream' sector (exploration and production) is overseen by the Directorate General of Hydrocarbons.\n\nI. Production of crude oil - Not regulated by PNGRB\nThis is upstream activity, under the DGH and the Ministry of Petroleum.\n\nII. Refining, storage and distribution of petroleum - Regulated by PNGRB\n\nIII. Marketing and sale of petroleum products - Regulated by PNGRB\n\nIV. Production of natural gas - Not regulated by PNGRB\nThis is also upstream activity.\n\nTwo activities are regulated by PNGRB, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2025-61","y":2025,"n":61,"s":1,"q":"Suppose the revenue expenditure is ₹80,000 crores and the revenue receipts of the Government are ₹60,000 crores. The Government budget also shows borrowings of ₹10,000 crores and interest payments of ₹6,000 crores. Which of the following statements are correct?\nI. Revenue deficit is ₹20,000 crores.\nII. Fiscal deficit is ₹10,000 crores.\nIII. Primary deficit is ₹4,000 crores.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"I. Revenue deficit is 20,000 crore rupees - Correct\nRevenue deficit = Revenue expenditure minus Revenue receipts = 80,000 minus 60,000 = 20,000 crore.\n\nII. Fiscal deficit is 10,000 crore rupees - Correct\nFiscal deficit equals the total borrowings (and other liabilities) the government needs to cover the gap between total expenditure and total non-borrowed receipts. Borrowings are given as 10,000 crore, so fiscal deficit is 10,000 crore.\n\nIII. Primary deficit is 4,000 crore rupees - Correct\nPrimary deficit = Fiscal deficit minus Interest payments = 10,000 minus 6,000 = 4,000 crore. It shows borrowing needed for purposes other than paying interest.\n\n(Note: In a real budget the fiscal deficit is normally larger than the revenue deficit; here the figures are simply to be used as given.)\n\nHence the answer is (d) I, II and III."},{"i":"2025-62","y":2025,"n":62,"s":4,"q":"India is one of the founding members of the International North-South Transport Corridor (INSTC), a multimodal transportation corridor, which will connect","o":["India to Central Asia to Europe via Iran","India to Central Asia via China","India to South-East Asia through Bangladesh and Myanmar","India to Europe through Azerbaijan"],"a":0,"x":"(a) India to Central Asia to Europe via Iran - Correct\nThe International North-South Transport Corridor was agreed in 2000 by India, Iran and Russia (founding members) and later joined by others. It is a 7,200 km multimodal network of ship, rail and road routes. Goods move by sea from Mumbai (JNPT) to Bandar Abbas or Chabahar in Iran, then by road and rail through Iran, the Caspian region, Azerbaijan or Central Asia to Russia (Moscow, St Petersburg) and onward to Europe. It cuts transport time and cost compared with the Suez route.\n\n(b) India to Central Asia via China - Incorrect\nINSTC does not pass through China.\n\n(c) India to South-East Asia through Bangladesh and Myanmar - Incorrect\nThis describes projects like the Kaladan corridor and the India-Myanmar-Thailand highway.\n\n(d) India to Europe through Azerbaijan - Incorrect\nAzerbaijan lies on the western route of INSTC, but the corridor as a whole is defined as running via Iran; this option gives an incomplete description."},{"i":"2025-63","y":2025,"n":63,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Of the two major ethanol producers in the world, i.e., Brazil and the United States of America, the former produces more ethanol than the latter.\nStatement II: Unlike in the United States of America where corn is the principal feedstock for ethanol production, sugarcane is the principal feedstock for ethanol production in Brazil.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I","Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct","Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct"],"a":3,"x":"Statement I - Incorrect\nThe United States is the world's largest producer of ethanol, producing about 15 billion gallons a year, over half of global output. Brazil is the second-largest producer. So Brazil does not produce more ethanol than the USA.\n\nStatement II - Correct\nIn the USA, ethanol is made mainly from corn (maize), about 40 percent of the US corn crop goes to ethanol. In Brazil, sugarcane is the principal feedstock, though corn ethanol is growing there. Brazil pioneered fuel ethanol through its Proalcool programme (1975) and flex-fuel vehicles. (India uses sugarcane molasses, cane juice and grains under its E20 blending programme.)\n\nHence the answer is (d) Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct."},{"i":"2025-64","y":2025,"n":64,"s":2,"q":"The World Bank warned that India could become one of the first places where wet-bulb temperatures routinely exceed 35°C. Which of the following statements best reflect(s) the implication of the above-said report?\nI. Peninsular India will most likely suffer from flooding, tropical cyclones and droughts.\nII. The survival of animals including humans will be affected as shedding of their body heat through perspiration becomes difficult.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"x":"The World Bank report 'Climate Investment Opportunities in India's Cooling Sector' (2022) warned that India could soon be one of the first places in the world where wet-bulb temperatures routinely exceed 35°C.\n\nI. Peninsular India will likely suffer from flooding, tropical cyclones and droughts - Correct (as per key)\nThe report links rising heat to more frequent and intense extreme weather. Peninsular India, surrounded by warming seas, faces increasing risks of heavy rainfall and floods, stronger cyclones and droughts, along with heatwaves.\n\nII. Survival of animals including humans will be affected, as shedding body heat through sweating becomes difficult - Correct\nWet-bulb temperature combines heat and humidity. At a wet-bulb temperature of 35°C, sweat can no longer evaporate to cool the body, so humans and animals cannot lose heat. Even healthy people in the shade cannot survive such conditions for more than a few hours. This is the main implication of the warning.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both I and II."},{"i":"2025-65","y":2025,"n":65,"s":1,"q":"A country's fiscal deficit stands at ₹50,000 crores. It is receiving ₹10,000 crores through non-debt creating capital receipts. The country's interest liabilities are ₹1,500 crores. What is the gross primary deficit?","o":["₹48,500 crores","₹51,500 crores","₹58,500 crores","None of the above"],"a":0,"x":"(a) 48,500 crore rupees - Correct\nGross primary deficit = Gross fiscal deficit minus Interest payments = 50,000 minus 1,500 = 48,500 crore. The primary deficit shows the borrowing needed to meet expenses other than interest.\n\n(b) 51,500 crore rupees - Incorrect\nThis wrongly adds interest payments to the fiscal deficit instead of subtracting them.\n\n(c) 58,500 crore rupees - Incorrect\nThis wrongly adds the non-debt capital receipts (10,000) to the fiscal deficit and then subtracts interest. Non-debt creating capital receipts (like disinvestment and recovery of loans) are already taken into account when the fiscal deficit is calculated, so they must not be used again.\n\n(d) None of the above - Incorrect\nOption (a) is the correct figure."},{"i":"2025-66","y":2025,"n":66,"s":1,"q":"Which of the following statements with regard to recommendations of the 15th Finance Commission of India are correct?\nI. It has recommended grants of ₹4,800 crores from the year 2022-23 to the year 2025-26 for incentivizing States to enhance educational outcomes.\nII. 45% of the net proceeds of Union taxes are to be shared with States.\nIII. ₹45,000 crores are to be kept as performance-based incentive for all States for carrying out agricultural reforms.\nIV. It reintroduced tax effort criteria to reward fiscal performance.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","o":["I, II and III","I, II and IV","I, III and IV","II, III and IV"],"a":2,"x":"I. It recommended grants of 4,800 crore rupees from 2022-23 to 2025-26 for incentivising States to enhance educational outcomes - Correct\nThe Fifteenth Finance Commission (chaired by N.K. Singh, covering 2021-26) recommended performance-based grants of 4,800 crore rupees for improving educational outcomes.\n\nII. 45 percent of the net proceeds of Union taxes are to be shared with States - Incorrect\nThe vertical devolution was fixed at 41 percent of the divisible pool, 1 percent less than the Fourteenth Finance Commission's 42 percent, to account for the new Union Territories of Jammu and Kashmir and Ladakh.\n\nIII. 45,000 crore rupees are kept as performance-based incentives for all States for agricultural reforms - Correct\nThe Commission recommended 45,000 crore rupees as performance-based incentives for States undertaking agricultural reforms.\n\nIV. It reintroduced tax effort criteria to reward fiscal performance - Correct\nIt used 'tax and fiscal efforts' as a criterion (2.5 percent weight) in horizontal devolution, rewarding States with higher tax collection efficiency. Other criteria were income distance (45 percent), population 2011 (15 percent), area (15 percent), forest and ecology (10 percent) and demographic performance (12.5 percent).\n\nHence the answer is (c) I, III and IV."},{"i":"2025-67","y":2025,"n":67,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD):\nI. It provides loans and guarantees to middle income countries.\nII. It works single-handedly to help developing countries to reduce poverty.\nIII. It was established to help Europe rebuild after the World War II.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"x":"I. It provides loans and guarantees to middle-income countries - Correct\nThe IBRD, the original arm of the World Bank Group, lends to governments of middle-income and creditworthy lower-income countries, and also offers guarantees and risk-management products. The International Development Association (IDA) gives concessional loans and grants to the poorest countries.\n\nII. It works single-handedly to help developing countries reduce poverty - Incorrect\nThe IBRD does not work alone. It is one of five institutions of the World Bank Group (with IDA, IFC, MIGA and ICSID) and works with governments, other development banks, UN agencies and the private sector.\n\nIII. It was established to help Europe rebuild after World War II - Correct\nThe IBRD was created at the Bretton Woods Conference in 1944, along with the IMF. Its first task was the reconstruction of war-devastated Europe; its first loan went to France in 1947. Later its focus shifted to development.\n\nHence the answer is (c) I and III only."},{"i":"2025-68","y":2025,"n":68,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of RTGS and NEFT:\nI. In RTGS, the settlement time is instantaneous while in case of NEFT, it takes some time to settle payments.\nII. In RTGS, the customer is charged for inward transactions while that is not the case for NEFT.\nIII. Operating hours for RTGS are restricted on certain days while this is not true for NEFT.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","I and II","I and III","III only"],"a":0,"x":"I. RTGS settlement is instantaneous, while NEFT takes some time - Correct\nReal Time Gross Settlement processes each transaction individually and settles it in real time, which is why it is used for large-value transfers (minimum 2 lakh rupees). National Electronic Funds Transfer settles transactions in batches every half hour, so there is a short delay.\n\nII. In RTGS the customer is charged for inward transactions, but not in NEFT - Incorrect\nThe RBI does not allow banks to charge customers for inward (incoming) RTGS or NEFT transactions. Since 2020, banks also do not charge savings account holders for online outward NEFT and RTGS transfers.\n\nIII. RTGS operating hours are restricted on certain days, but NEFT's are not - Incorrect\nNEFT became available 24x7x365 from December 2019, and RTGS became available round the clock from 14 December 2020. Neither is restricted now.\n\nHence the answer is (a) I only."},{"i":"2025-69","y":2025,"n":69,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following countries:\nI. United Arab Emirates\nII. France\nIII. Germany\nIV. Singapore\nV. Bangladesh\nHow many countries amongst the above are there other than India where international merchant payments are accepted under UPI?","o":["Only two","Only three","Only four","All the five"],"a":1,"x":"NPCI International Payments Ltd (NIPL) has tied up with partners abroad so that Indian tourists can pay merchants using UPI apps.\n\nI. United Arab Emirates - UPI accepted\nUPI payments are accepted at merchants in the UAE through tie-ups with partners such as NEOPAY (Mashreq Bank) and Network International.\n\nII. France - UPI accepted\nUPI was launched in France in 2024 through a partnership with Lyra; it can be used, for example, to buy tickets for the Eiffel Tower.\n\nIII. Germany - Not accepted (at the time)\nGermany did not have UPI merchant acceptance.\n\nIV. Singapore - UPI accepted\nUPI is accepted at many merchants in Singapore, and UPI is linked with Singapore's PayNow for person-to-person remittances (2023).\n\nV. Bangladesh - Not accepted (at the time)\nThere were announcements about RuPay card and UPI cooperation, but UPI merchant payments were not operational in Bangladesh.\n\n(UPI is also accepted in Bhutan, Nepal, Sri Lanka, Mauritius and Qatar.)\n\nThree of the listed countries accept UPI merchant payments, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2025-70","y":2025,"n":70,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements about 'PM Surya Ghar Muft Bijli Yojana':\nI. It targets installation of one crore solar rooftop panels in the residential sector.\nII. The Ministry of New and Renewable Energy aims to impart training on installation, operation, maintenance and repairs of solar rooftop systems at grassroot levels.\nIII. It aims to create more than three lakhs skilled manpower through fresh skilling, and up-skilling, under scheme component of capacity building.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["I and II only","I and III only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"PM Surya Ghar: Muft Bijli Yojana was launched in February 2024 with an outlay of about 75,021 crore rupees and is implemented by the Ministry of New and Renewable Energy.\n\nI. It targets installation of solar rooftop panels in one crore households in the residential sector - Correct\nThe scheme aims to provide rooftop solar to one crore households, giving up to 300 units of free electricity per month through subsidies of up to 78,000 rupees per household.\n\nII. MNRE aims to train people at grassroots level in installation, operation, maintenance and repair of rooftop systems - Correct\nA skilling component trains local technicians so that rooftop systems can be installed and maintained across villages and towns.\n\nIII. It aims to create more than three lakh skilled manpower through fresh skilling and up-skilling under capacity building - Correct\nThe capacity-building component targets over three lakh skilled workers, including about one lakh solar PV technicians.\n\nHence the answer is (d) I, II and III."},{"i":"2025-71","y":2025,"n":71,"s":9,"q":"\"Sedition has become my religion\" was the famous statement given by Gandhiji at the time of","o":["the Champaran Satyagraha","publicly violating Salt Law at Dandi","attending the Second Round Table Conference in London","the launch of the Quit India Movement"],"a":1,"x":"(a) The Champaran Satyagraha - Incorrect\nChamparan (1917) was Gandhi's first satyagraha in India, against the tinkathia system forced on indigo cultivators in Bihar. The statement is not linked to it.\n\n(b) Publicly violating the Salt Law at Dandi - Correct\nDuring the Salt Satyagraha of 1930, Gandhi deliberately broke the salt law at Dandi on 6 April 1930 and openly invited arrest. In this context he declared that 'sedition has become my religion', meaning that disobeying unjust British laws had become a sacred duty. The phrase reflects the defiant spirit of the Civil Disobedience Movement.\n\n(c) Attending the Second Round Table Conference in London - Incorrect\nGandhi attended the Second RTC in 1931 as the Congress's sole representative, after the Gandhi-Irwin Pact had suspended civil disobedience.\n\n(d) The launch of the Quit India Movement - Incorrect\nThe Quit India Movement (August 1942) is associated with Gandhi's slogan 'Do or Die'."},{"i":"2025-72","y":2025,"n":72,"s":7,"q":"The famous female figurine known as 'Dancing Girl', found at Mohenjo-daro, is made of","o":["carnelian","clay","bronze","gold"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Carnelian - Incorrect\nCarnelian, a red semi-precious stone, was used by Harappans to make beads, which were exported to Mesopotamia.\n\n(b) Clay - Incorrect\nMany Harappan figurines, such as the 'mother goddess' figures and toys, are of terracotta (baked clay), but the Dancing Girl is not.\n\n(c) Bronze - Correct\nThe Dancing Girl, found at Mohenjo-daro in 1926, is a small bronze statuette about 10.5 cm tall. It was made by the lost-wax (cire perdue) method, which shows advanced metal casting skill. She stands in a confident pose, with bangles covering one arm. It is now in the National Museum, New Delhi.\n\n(d) Gold - Incorrect\nGold was used for jewellery, not for this figure."},{"i":"2025-73","y":2025,"n":73,"s":9,"q":"Who provided legal defence to the people arrested in the aftermath of Chauri Chaura incident?","o":["C. R. Das","Madan Mohan Malaviya and Krishna Kant","Dr. Saifuddin Kitchlew and Khwaja Hasan Nizami","M. A. Jinnah"],"a":1,"x":"(a) C.R. Das - Incorrect\nChittaranjan Das was in prison at the time of the Chauri Chaura incident (February 1922) for his role in the Non-Cooperation Movement. Later that year he founded the Swaraj Party with Motilal Nehru.\n\n(b) Madan Mohan Malaviya and Krishna Kant - Correct\nAfter the violence at Chauri Chaura (Gorakhpur district), where 22 policemen were killed, the Sessions Court sentenced 172 people to death. Madan Mohan Malaviya, assisted by Krishna Kant Malaviya, defended the accused in the Allahabad High Court. As a result, most were saved; 19 were finally hanged and others received lesser sentences.\n\n(c) Dr. Saifuddin Kitchlew and Khwaja Hasan Nizami - Incorrect\nKitchlew was a Punjab leader associated with the Jallianwala Bagh events; Hasan Nizami was a Sufi writer. They did not defend the Chauri Chaura accused.\n\n(d) M.A. Jinnah - Incorrect\nJinnah had left the Congress in 1920 in opposition to non-cooperation and was not involved."},{"i":"2025-74","y":2025,"n":74,"s":9,"q":"Subsequent to which one of the following events, Gandhiji, who consistently opposed untouchability and appealed for its eradication from all spheres, decided to include the upliftment of 'Harijans' in his political and social programme?","o":["The Poona Pact","The Gandhi-Irwin Agreement (Delhi Pact)","Arrest of Congress leadership at the time of the Quit India Movement","Promulgation of the Government of India Act, 1935"],"a":0,"x":"(a) The Poona Pact - Correct\nIn 1932 the Communal Award gave separate electorates to the depressed classes. Gandhi, in Yerawada jail, fasted unto death against it. The Poona Pact (September 1932) between Gandhi and Dr. Ambedkar replaced separate electorates with reserved seats in joint electorates. After this, Gandhi made the removal of untouchability a central part of his programme: he founded the Harijan Sevak Sangh (1932), started the weekly 'Harijan' (1933) and undertook a nationwide Harijan tour (1933-34).\n\n(b) The Gandhi-Irwin Agreement (Delhi Pact) - Incorrect\nThe 1931 pact suspended the Civil Disobedience Movement and allowed Gandhi to attend the Second Round Table Conference.\n\n(c) Arrest of Congress leadership during the Quit India Movement - Incorrect\nThis happened in 1942, much later.\n\n(d) Promulgation of the Government of India Act, 1935 - Incorrect\nGandhi's Harijan campaign had begun years before the 1935 Act."},{"i":"2025-75","y":2025,"n":75,"s":9,"q":"Consider the following fruits:\nI. Papaya\nII. Pineapple\nIII. Guava\nHow many of the above were introduced in India by the Portuguese in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":2,"x":"The Portuguese, who came to India after Vasco da Gama's arrival in 1498, introduced many crops from the Americas (the 'New World') in the 16th and 17th centuries, including chillies, tobacco, maize, potato, tomato, groundnut, cashew and several fruits.\n\nI. Papaya - Introduced by the Portuguese\nPapaya is native to southern Mexico and Central America and was brought to India by the Portuguese.\n\nII. Pineapple - Introduced by the Portuguese\nPineapple is native to South America (Brazil and Paraguay). The Portuguese brought it to India; Akbar's court is said to have enjoyed it.\n\nIII. Guava - Introduced by the Portuguese\nGuava is native to tropical America and was introduced by the Portuguese in the 17th century. It has since become a common Indian fruit (e.g., Allahabad Safeda).\n\nAll three were introduced by the Portuguese, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2025-76","y":2025,"n":76,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following countries:\nI. United Kingdom\nII. Denmark\nIII. New Zealand\nIV. Australia\nV. Brazil\nHow many of the above countries have more than four time zones?","o":["All the five","Only four","Only three","Only two"],"a":1,"x":"Counting the time zones used across a country's overseas territories as well as its mainland:\n\nI. United Kingdom - More than four\nIncluding its overseas territories scattered across the world (such as the Falklands, British Indian Ocean Territory, Pitcairn and Caribbean islands), the UK spans 9 time zones.\n\nII. Denmark - More than four\nWith Greenland (which alone has several time zones) and the Faroe Islands, the Kingdom of Denmark uses 5 time zones.\n\nIII. New Zealand - More than four\nIncluding the Chatham Islands, Tokelau, the Cook Islands and Niue, the Realm of New Zealand has 5 time zones.\n\nIV. Australia - More than four\nMainland Australia has 3 time zones, but with external territories such as Norfolk Island, Christmas Island, Cocos (Keeling) Islands and Heard and McDonald Islands it has 9.\n\nV. Brazil - Not more than four\nBrazil has 4 time zones (UTC-2 to UTC-5), covering its offshore islands and western Amazon, so it does not have more than four.\n\nFour countries have more than four time zones, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2025-77","y":2025,"n":77,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Anadyr in Siberia and Nome in Alaska are a few kilometers from each other, but when people are waking up and getting set for breakfast in these cities, it would be different days.\nII. When it is Monday in Anadyr, it is Tuesday in Nome.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"I. Anadyr and Nome are close but on different days at breakfast time - Correct (as per key)\nAnadyr (Chukotka, Russia, UTC+12) and Nome (Alaska, USA, UTC-9) lie on either side of the Bering Strait region, separated by the International Date Line, which runs through the Bering Strait. Although geographically near each other, their clocks differ by about 21 hours, so when people wake up for breakfast in both places, the calendar dates are different.\n\nII. When it is Monday in Anadyr, it is Tuesday in Nome - Incorrect\nPlaces west of the Date Line (Asia side) are a day ahead of places east of it (America side). So when it is Monday in Anadyr, it is still Sunday in Nome. Crossing the line from Asia to America, you go back a day.\n\nHence the answer is (a) I only."},{"i":"2025-78","y":2025,"n":78,"s":9,"q":"Who among the following was the founder of the 'Self-Respect Movement'?","o":["'Periyar' E. V. Ramaswamy Naicker","Dr. B. R. Ambedkar","Bhaskarrao Jadhav","Dinkarrao Javalkar"],"a":0,"x":"(a) 'Periyar' E.V. Ramaswamy Naicker - Correct\nPeriyar launched the Self-Respect Movement in 1925 in the Madras Presidency after leaving the Congress over caste discrimination (for example at the Cheranmadevi gurukulam). The movement aimed to give non-Brahmins and lower castes a sense of self-respect, opposed caste hierarchy and Brahmanical rituals, promoted rationalism, self-respect marriages without priests, and women's rights. It later led to the Dravidar Kazhagam (1944).\n\n(b) Dr. B.R. Ambedkar - Incorrect\nAmbedkar founded the Bahishkrit Hitakarini Sabha (1924), led the Mahad Satyagraha (1927) and formed the Independent Labour Party (1936), but not the Self-Respect Movement.\n\n(c) Bhaskarrao Jadhav - Incorrect\nHe was a leader of the non-Brahmin movement in Maharashtra, linked to Jyotiba Phule's Satyashodhak tradition.\n\n(d) Dinkarrao Javalkar - Incorrect\nAlso a Maharashtrian non-Brahmin activist, known for his book 'Deshache Dushman' (Enemies of the Country)."},{"i":"2025-79","y":2025,"n":79,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| Country | Resource-rich in\n| I. Botswana | Diamond\n| II. Chile | Lithium\n| III. Indonesia | Nickel\nIn how many of the above rows is the given information correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":2,"x":"I. Botswana : Diamond - Correct\nBotswana is one of the world's largest producers of diamonds by value. Mines such as Jwaneng and Orapa, run by Debswana (a joint venture of the government and De Beers), transformed its economy.\n\nII. Chile : Lithium - Correct\nChile has the world's largest lithium reserves, mainly in the brines of the Salar de Atacama. With Argentina and Bolivia it forms the 'Lithium Triangle'. It is also the largest copper producer.\n\nIII. Indonesia : Nickel - Correct\nIndonesia has the world's largest nickel reserves and is its largest producer. It banned export of raw nickel ore (2020) to attract processing and battery industries.\n\nAll three are correctly matched, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2025-80","y":2025,"n":80,"s":11,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| Region | Country\n| I. Mallorca | Italy\n| II. Normandy | Spain\n| III. Sardinia | France\nIn how many of the above rows is the given information correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":3,"x":"I. Mallorca : Italy - Incorrect\nMallorca (Majorca) is the largest of the Balearic Islands in the Mediterranean and belongs to Spain.\n\nII. Normandy : Spain - Incorrect\nNormandy is a region in northern France on the English Channel, famous for the Allied D-Day landings in June 1944.\n\nIII. Sardinia : France - Incorrect\nSardinia is a large Mediterranean island belonging to Italy. The French island is Corsica, just north of Sardinia.\n\nNone of the pairs is correctly matched, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2025-81","y":2025,"n":81,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Some rare earth elements are used in the manufacture of flat television screens and computer monitors.\nStatement II: Some rare earth elements have phosphorescent properties.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I","Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct","Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nRare earth elements such as europium, terbium, yttrium, cerium and gadolinium are used in manufacturing flat-panel television screens, LCD and LED displays and computer monitors, as well as in energy-efficient lighting.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nThese elements have luminescent or phosphorescent properties: when excited by electrons or light, they emit light of specific colours. Europium produces red and blue light and terbium produces green; these red, green and blue phosphors create the full range of colours on screens. This property is exactly why they are used in displays.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both statements are correct and Statement II explains Statement I."},{"i":"2025-82","y":2025,"n":82,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Indian Railways have prepared a National Rail Plan (NRP) to create a 'future ready' railway system by 2028.\nII. 'Kavach' is an Automatic Train Protection system developed in collaboration with Germany.\nIII. 'Kavach' system consists of RFID tags fitted on track in station section.\nWhich of the statements given above are not correct?","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"x":"The question asks which statements are NOT correct.\n\nI. Indian Railways prepared a National Rail Plan to create a 'future ready' railway system by 2028 - Not correct\nThe National Rail Plan (2020) aims to create a 'future ready' railway system by 2030, with a vision up to 2050, including increasing railways' share in freight to 45 percent. The year 2028 is wrong.\n\nII. Kavach was developed in collaboration with Germany - Not correct\nKavach is an indigenous Automatic Train Protection system developed by the Research Designs and Standards Organisation (RDSO) with Indian industry. It automatically applies brakes if the driver fails to act, to prevent collisions and signal passing at danger.\n\nIII. Kavach consists of RFID tags fitted on the track in station sections - Correct statement\nKavach uses RFID tags placed on the tracks (at stations and near signals) to give trains their exact location and direction, along with stationary Kavach units, loco Kavach units and radio communication towers.\n\nHence statements I and II are not correct, and the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2025-83","y":2025,"n":83,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following space missions:\nI. Axiom-4\nII. SpaDeX\nIII. Gaganyaan\nHow many of the space missions given above encourage and support micro-gravity research?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":1,"x":"The question asks how many missions encourage and support microgravity research.\n\nI. Axiom-4 - Supports microgravity research\nThe Axiom-4 mission (June-July 2025) took India's Group Captain Shubhanshu Shukla to the International Space Station. The crew carried out about 60 microgravity experiments, including seven selected by ISRO on crops, tardigrades, muscle loss and microalgae.\n\nII. SpaDeX - Treated as not supporting microgravity research (as per key)\nThe Space Docking Experiment (December 2024) was mainly a technology demonstration of docking and undocking two small satellites, making India the fourth country to achieve space docking.\nNote: The PSLV-C60 stage that launched SpaDeX was used as an orbital platform (POEM-4) carrying experiments, including seeds germinated in microgravity. So some argue all three qualify, but the official key counts only two.\n\nIII. Gaganyaan - Supports microgravity research\nIndia's human spaceflight programme will carry out microgravity experiments on board and prepares the way for the Bharatiya Antariksh Station, planned for research in microgravity.\n\nHence the answer is (b) Only two."},{"i":"2025-84","y":2025,"n":84,"s":3,"q":"With reference to India's defence, consider the following pairs:\n| Aircraft type | Description\n| I. Dornier-228 | Maritime patrol aircraft\n| II. IL-76 | Supersonic combat aircraft\n| III. C-17 Globemaster III | Military transport aircraft\nHow many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All the three","None"],"a":1,"x":"I. Dornier-228 : Maritime patrol aircraft - Correct\nThe Dornier-228 is a twin-engine light aircraft built under licence by HAL at Kanpur. The Indian Navy and Coast Guard use it for maritime reconnaissance and patrol, and the IAF uses it for transport and communication.\n\nII. IL-76 : Supersonic combat aircraft - Incorrect\nThe Ilyushin IL-76 is a four-engine, subsonic heavy military transport aircraft of Soviet/Russian origin. In the IAF it carries troops and cargo, and variants serve as the platform for the Phalcon AWACS and the IL-78 aerial refueller. It is not a combat aircraft.\n\nIII. C-17 Globemaster III : Military transport aircraft - Correct\nThe C-17 is an American strategic heavy-lift transport aircraft. The IAF uses it for moving troops, tanks and supplies and for disaster relief and evacuation missions.\n\nTwo pairs are correctly matched, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2025-85","y":2025,"n":85,"s":2,"q":"Artificial way of causing rainfall to reduce air pollution makes use of","o":["silver iodide and potassium iodide","silver nitrate and potassium iodide","silver iodide and potassium nitrate","silver nitrate and potassium chloride"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Silver iodide and potassium iodide - Correct\nCloud seeding, proposed for example in Delhi to wash pollutants out of the air, involves releasing substances into clouds that act as nuclei for water droplets or ice crystals. Silver iodide is the most common agent because its crystal structure is very similar to ice. Potassium iodide is also used, along with dry ice (solid carbon dioxide), common salt and calcium chloride for warm clouds. The resulting rainfall can temporarily reduce particulate pollution.\n\n(b) Silver nitrate and potassium iodide - Incorrect\nSilver nitrate is not a cloud seeding agent.\n\n(c) Silver iodide and potassium nitrate - Incorrect\nPotassium nitrate is a fertiliser and oxidiser, not a seeding agent.\n\n(d) Silver nitrate and potassium chloride - Incorrect\nNeither is used together in this way for cloud seeding."},{"i":"2025-86","y":2025,"n":86,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements with regard to pardoning power of the President of India:\nI. The exercise of this power by the President can be subjected to limited judicial review.\nII. The President can exercise this power without the advice of the Central Government.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"I. The exercise of the pardoning power can be subject to limited judicial review - Correct\nThe Supreme Court held in Maru Ram (1980), Kehar Singh (1989) and Epuru Sudhakar (2006) that the President's power under Article 72 (and the Governor's under Article 161) can be judicially reviewed on limited grounds: if the decision was taken without application of mind, was mala fide, was based on irrelevant or extraneous considerations, or was arbitrary or discriminatory. The court does not examine the merits of the decision.\n\nII. The President can exercise this power without the advice of the Central Government - Incorrect\nUnder Article 74, the President acts on the aid and advice of the Council of Ministers. Mercy petitions are processed by the Ministry of Home Affairs, and the President decides according to the government's advice. The President has no independent discretion here.\n\nHence the answer is (a) I only."},{"i":"2025-87","y":2025,"n":87,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. On the dissolution of the House of the People, the Speaker shall not vacate his/her office until immediately before the first meeting of the House of the People after the dissolution.\nII. According to the provisions of the Constitution of India, a Member of the House of the People on being elected as Speaker shall resign from his/her political party immediately.\nIII. The Speaker of the House of the People may be removed from his/her office by a resolution of the House of the People passed by a majority of all the then Members of the House, provided that no resolution shall be moved unless at least fourteen days' notice has been given of the intention to move the resolution.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"x":"I. On dissolution of the Lok Sabha, the Speaker does not vacate office until immediately before the first meeting of the new House - Correct\nThis is the proviso to Article 94. It ensures continuity, so the outgoing Speaker continues in office until the newly elected House meets.\n\nII. On being elected Speaker, a member must resign from his or her political party immediately - Incorrect\nThere is no such provision in the Constitution. Resigning from the party is a British convention, not followed as a rule in India. The Tenth Schedule (paragraph 5) only says that if the Speaker gives up party membership on election, he or she will not be disqualified for doing so.\n\nIII. The Speaker may be removed by a resolution passed by a majority of all the then members, after at least fourteen days' notice - Correct\nArticle 94(c) provides for removal by a resolution passed by 'a majority of all the then members of the House' (effective majority), and requires at least 14 days' notice of the intention to move it.\n\nHence the answer is (c) I and III only."},{"i":"2025-88","y":2025,"n":88,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. If any question arises as to whether a Member of the House of the People has become subject to disqualification under the 10th Schedule, the President's decision in accordance with the opinion of the Council of Union Ministers shall be final.\nII. There is no mention of the word 'political party' in the Constitution of India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"x":"I. The President decides questions of disqualification under the Tenth Schedule according to the opinion of the Council of Ministers - Incorrect\nUnder paragraph 6 of the Tenth Schedule, questions of disqualification on grounds of defection are decided by the Speaker (for the Lok Sabha) or the Chairman (for the Rajya Sabha), subject to judicial review (Kihoto Hollohan, 1992). For other disqualifications (Article 102), the President decides under Article 103, acting according to the opinion of the Election Commission, not the Council of Ministers.\n\nII. There is no mention of the word 'political party' in the Constitution - Incorrect\nThe term 'political party' appears in the Tenth Schedule, added by the 52nd Amendment Act, 1985 (anti-defection law), which defines membership of a political party and disqualification for defection.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Neither I nor II."},{"i":"2025-89","y":2025,"n":89,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: In India, State Governments have no power for making rules for grant of concessions in respect of extraction of minor minerals even though such minerals are located in their territories.\nStatement II: In India, the Central Government has the power to notify minor minerals under the relevant law.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I","Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct","Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct"],"a":3,"x":"Statement I - Incorrect\nUnder Section 15 of the Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act, 1957, State Governments have the power to make rules for granting quarry leases, mining leases and other mineral concessions for minor minerals, and they collect royalty on them. Minor minerals include ordinary sand, building stone, gravel, ordinary clay and marble. So the States do have this power.\n\nStatement II - Correct\nSection 3(e) of the MMDR Act defines minor minerals and empowers the Central Government to declare any other mineral as a minor mineral by notification. For example, in 2015 the Centre notified 31 minerals as minor minerals.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct."},{"i":"2025-90","y":2025,"n":90,"s":2,"q":"Which organization has enacted the Nature Restoration Law (NRL) to tackle climate change and biodiversity loss?","o":["The European Union","The World Bank","The Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development","The Food and Agriculture Organization"],"a":0,"x":"(a) The European Union - Correct\nThe EU's Nature Restoration Law was adopted in June 2024 and entered into force in August 2024 as part of the European Green Deal and the EU Biodiversity Strategy for 2030. It requires member states to put restoration measures in place on at least 20 percent of the EU's land and sea areas by 2030, and for all ecosystems in need of restoration by 2050. It covers forests, farmland, wetlands, rivers, urban green spaces and pollinators. It is the first law of its kind on a continental scale.\n\n(b) The World Bank - Incorrect\nThe World Bank is a lending institution; it does not enact laws.\n\n(c) The OECD - Incorrect\nThe OECD issues guidelines and recommendations but does not enact binding laws.\n\n(d) The FAO - Incorrect\nThe FAO leads the UN Decade on Ecosystem Restoration (with UNEP) but does not enact laws."},{"i":"2025-91","y":2025,"n":91,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Panchayats at the intermediate level exist in all States.\nII. To be eligible to be a Member of a Panchayat at the intermediate level, a person should attain the age of thirty years.\nIII. The Chief Minister of a State constitutes a commission to review the financial position of Panchayats at the intermediate levels and to make recommendations regarding the distribution of net proceeds of taxes and duties, leviable by the State, between the State and Panchayats at the intermediate level.\nWhich of the statements given above are not correct?","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":3,"x":"The question asks which statements are NOT correct.\n\nI. Panchayats at the intermediate level exist in all States - Not correct\nArticle 243B provides for a three-tier system, but the intermediate level need not be constituted in a State with population not exceeding 20 lakh. Hence small States such as Goa, Sikkim and Manipur have only two tiers.\n\nII. To be a member of an intermediate panchayat a person must be 30 years old - Not correct\nUnder Article 243F, a person is qualified if he or she is qualified to be elected to the State legislature, except that the minimum age is 21 years (not 25 or 30).\n\nIII. The Chief Minister constitutes a commission to review panchayat finances and recommend distribution of taxes - Not correct\nUnder Article 243I, it is the Governor who constitutes the State Finance Commission every five years to review the financial position of panchayats and recommend sharing of State taxes, duties, tolls and fees and grants-in-aid.\n\nAll three statements are not correct, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2025-92","y":2025,"n":92,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of BIMSTEC:\nI. It is a regional organization consisting of seven member States till January 2025.\nII. It came into existence with the signing of the Dhaka Declaration, 1999.\nIII. Bangladesh, India, Sri Lanka, Thailand and Nepal are founding member States of BIMSTEC.\nIV. In BIMSTEC, the subsector of 'tourism' is being led by India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I and II","II and III","I and IV","I only"],"a":3,"x":"I. It is a regional organisation of seven member states (till January 2025) - Correct\nBIMSTEC (Bay of Bengal Initiative for Multi-Sectoral Technical and Economic Cooperation) has seven members: Bangladesh, Bhutan, India, Myanmar, Nepal, Sri Lanka and Thailand. Its secretariat is in Dhaka.\n\nII. It came into existence with the Dhaka Declaration, 1999 - Incorrect\nBIMSTEC was established by the Bangkok Declaration of June 1997.\n\nIII. Bangladesh, India, Sri Lanka, Thailand and Nepal are founding members - Incorrect\nThe founding members in 1997 were Bangladesh, India, Sri Lanka and Thailand (then called BIST-EC). Myanmar joined later in 1997, and Nepal and Bhutan joined in 2004.\n\nIV. The tourism sub-sector is led by India - Incorrect (as per key)\nAfter the 2022 BIMSTEC Charter, cooperation was reorganised into seven sectors, each led by a member. India leads the security sector (including counter-terrorism, disaster management and energy). Tourism is not led by India.\n\nHence the answer is (d) I only."},{"i":"2025-93","y":2025,"n":93,"s":10,"q":"Who amongst the following are members of the Jury to select the recipient of 'Gandhi Peace Prize'?\nI. The President of India\nII. The Prime Minister of India\nIII. The Chief Justice of India\nIV. The Leader of Opposition in the Lok Sabha\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","o":["II and IV only","I, II and III","II, III and IV","I and III only"],"a":2,"x":"The Gandhi Peace Prize was instituted in 1995 on Mahatma Gandhi's 125th birth anniversary. It carries 1 crore rupees and is given for social, economic and political transformation through non-violence and Gandhian methods. The jury is chaired by the Prime Minister and includes the Chief Justice of India, the Leader of the Opposition (or leader of the single largest opposition party) in the Lok Sabha, and two eminent persons.\n\nI. The President of India - Not a member\nThe President is not on the jury.\n\nII. The Prime Minister of India - Member\nThe PM chairs the jury.\n\nIII. The Chief Justice of India - Member\n\nIV. The Leader of Opposition in the Lok Sabha - Member\n\nHence the answer is (c) II, III and IV."},{"i":"2025-94","y":2025,"n":94,"s":3,"q":"GPS-Aided Geo Augmented Navigation (GAGAN) uses a system of ground stations to provide necessary augmentation. Which of the following statements is/are correct in respect of GAGAN?\nI. It is designed to provide additional accuracy and integrity.\nII. It will allow more uniform and high quality air traffic management.\nIII. It will provide benefits only in aviation but not in other modes of transportation.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","o":["I, II and III","II and III only","I only","I and II only"],"a":3,"x":"GAGAN (GPS Aided GEO Augmented Navigation) is India's Satellite-Based Augmentation System, developed by ISRO and the Airports Authority of India. Ground reference stations measure errors in GPS signals, a master control centre computes corrections, and these are broadcast via geostationary satellites (GSAT-8, 10, 15).\n\nI. It is designed to provide additional accuracy and integrity - Correct\nGAGAN improves GPS position accuracy to about 1.5 metres horizontally and gives integrity alerts when signals are unreliable, which is critical for aviation safety.\n\nII. It allows more uniform and high-quality air traffic management - Correct\nIt enables precise approach and landing guidance (APV-1) even at airports without instrument landing systems, more direct flight routes, fuel savings and seamless navigation across Indian airspace.\n\nIII. It benefits only aviation, not other modes of transport - Incorrect\nGAGAN signals are also used for railways (for example, unmanned level crossing warnings), roads, shipping, fishing (alerts to fishermen), agriculture, surveying, and disaster management.\n\nHence the answer is (d) I and II only."},{"i":"2025-95","y":2025,"n":95,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding AI Action Summit held in Grand Palais, Paris in February 2025:\nI. Co-chaired with India, the event builds on the advances made at the Bletchley Park Summit held in 2023 and the Seoul Summit held in 2024.\nII. Along with other countries, US and UK also signed the declaration on inclusive and sustainable AI.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"x":"I. Co-chaired with India, it built on the Bletchley Park (2023) and Seoul (2024) summits - Correct\nThe AI Action Summit was held at the Grand Palais, Paris, on 10-11 February 2025, co-chaired by French President Macron and Indian Prime Minister Modi. It followed the AI Safety Summit at Bletchley Park (UK, November 2023) and the AI Seoul Summit (May 2024). India announced it would host the next summit (the AI Impact Summit, New Delhi, 2026).\n\nII. The US and UK also signed the declaration on inclusive and sustainable AI - Incorrect\nThe 'Statement on Inclusive and Sustainable Artificial Intelligence for People and the Planet' was signed by about 60 countries, including India, France and China. However, the United States and the United Kingdom declined to sign it, citing concerns about over-regulation and national security.\n\nHence the answer is (a) I only."},{"i":"2025-96","y":2025,"n":96,"s":12,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\nI. International Year of the Woman Farmer : 2026\nII. International Year of Sustainable and Resilient Tourism : 2027\nIII. International Year of Peace and Trust : 2025\nIV. International Year of Asteroid Awareness and Planetary Defence : 2029\nHow many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All the four"],"a":3,"x":"All four are designations by the United Nations General Assembly.\n\nI. International Year of the Woman Farmer : 2026 - Correct\nDeclared to highlight the role of women in agriculture and food systems.\n\nII. International Year of Sustainable and Resilient Tourism : 2027 - Correct\nDeclared to promote tourism that supports sustainable development and resilience.\n\nIII. International Year of Peace and Trust : 2025 - Correct\n2025 was also the International Year of Quantum Science and Technology, of Cooperatives, and of Glaciers' Preservation.\n\nIV. International Year of Asteroid Awareness and Planetary Defence : 2029 - Correct\n2029 is the year the asteroid Apophis will pass very close to Earth, making it a focus for planetary defence awareness.\n\nAll four pairs are correct, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2025-97","y":2025,"n":97,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements with regard to BRICS:\nI. 16th BRICS Summit was held under the Chairship of Russia in Kazan.\nII. Indonesia has become a full member of BRICS.\nIII. The theme of the 16th BRICS Summit was Strengthening Multiculturalism for Just Global Development and Security.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I and II","II and III","I and III","I only"],"a":0,"x":"I. The 16th BRICS Summit was held under Russia's Chairship in Kazan - Correct\nThe 16th summit took place in Kazan, Russia, on 22-24 October 2024. It was the first summit with the expanded membership (Egypt, Ethiopia, Iran and the UAE had joined in January 2024).\n\nII. Indonesia has become a full member of BRICS - Correct\nBrazil, as the 2025 chair, announced in January 2025 that Indonesia had become a full member, the first South-east Asian nation to join.\n\nIII. The theme of the 16th Summit was 'Strengthening Multiculturalism for Just Global Development and Security' - Incorrect\nThe actual theme was 'Strengthening Multilateralism for Just Global Development and Security'. 'Multiculturalism' is wrong.\n\nHence the answer is (a) I and II."},{"i":"2025-98","y":2025,"n":98,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements about Lokpal:\nI. The power of Lokpal applies to public servants of India, but not to the Indian public servants posted outside India.\nII. The Chairperson or a Member shall not be a Member of the Parliament or a Member of the Legislature of any State or Union Territory, and only the Chief Justice of India, whether incumbent or retired, has to be its Chairperson.\nIII. The Chairperson or a Member shall not be a person of less than forty-five years of age on the date of assuming office as the Chairperson or Member, as the case may be.\nIV. Lokpal cannot inquire into the allegations of corruption against a sitting Prime Minister of India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["III only","II and III","I and IV","None of the above statements is correct"],"a":0,"x":"I. Lokpal applies to public servants in India but not to those posted outside India - Incorrect\nThe Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act, 2013 extends to public servants within and outside India.\n\nII. No MP or MLA can be Chairperson or Member, and only the Chief Justice of India, incumbent or retired, can be Chairperson - Incorrect\nThe first part is correct, but the Chairperson can be a present or former Chief Justice of India, a present or former Judge of the Supreme Court, or an eminent person with at least 25 years of expertise in anti-corruption, public administration, vigilance, finance, law or management. The first Chairperson, Justice P.C. Ghose, was a former Supreme Court judge.\n\nIII. The Chairperson or Member must be at least 45 years old on assuming office - Correct\nSection 3(4) of the Act lays down this minimum age of 45 years.\n\nIV. Lokpal cannot inquire into allegations of corruption against a sitting Prime Minister - Incorrect\nThe Lokpal can inquire into allegations against the Prime Minister, except those relating to international relations, external and internal security, public order, atomic energy and space. Such inquiries require approval of a full bench with at least two-thirds majority and are held in camera.\n\nHence the answer is (a) III only."},{"i":"2025-99","y":2025,"n":99,"s":12,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of the first Kho Kho World Cup:\nI. The event was held in Delhi, India.\nII. Indian men beat Nepal with a score of 78-40 in the final to become the World Champion in men category.\nIII. Indian women beat Nepal with a score of 54-36 in the final to become the World Champion in women category.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":0,"x":"I. The event was held in Delhi - Correct\nThe first Kho Kho World Cup was held at the Indira Gandhi Indoor Stadium, New Delhi, from 13 to 19 January 2025, with teams from over 20 countries.\n\nII. Indian men beat Nepal 78-40 in the final - Incorrect\nThe Indian men's team beat Nepal 54-36 in the final.\n\nIII. Indian women beat Nepal 54-36 in the final - Incorrect\nThe Indian women's team beat Nepal 78-40 in the final.\n\nThe scores in statements II and III have been swapped. India won both titles, but the scores given are wrong.\n\nHence the answer is (a) I only."},{"i":"2025-100","y":2025,"n":100,"s":12,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nI. In the finals of the 45th Chess Olympiad held in 2024, Gukesh Dommaraju became the world's youngest winner after defeating the Russian player Ian Nepomniachtchi.\nII. Abhimanyu Mishra, an American chess player, holds the record of becoming world's youngest ever Grandmaster.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"x":"I. At the 45th Chess Olympiad (2024), Gukesh became the youngest winner by defeating Ian Nepomniachtchi - Incorrect\nThis mixes up two events. D. Gukesh became the youngest undisputed World Chess Champion at the age of 18 by defeating Ding Liren of China in the World Championship match in Singapore in December 2024. He had earlier qualified by winning the 2024 Candidates Tournament, where Nepomniachtchi was one of the players. At the 45th Chess Olympiad in Budapest (September 2024), India won gold in both the open and women's sections, with Gukesh winning individual gold on board 1.\n\nII. Abhimanyu Mishra, an American player, holds the record of world's youngest Grandmaster - Correct\nAbhimanyu Mishra, a US player of Indian origin, became a Grandmaster in 2021 at 12 years, 4 months and 25 days, breaking Sergey Karjakin's record from 2002.\n\nHence the answer is (b) II only."},{"i":"2024-1","y":2024,"n":1,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: The atmosphere is heated more by incoming solar radiation than by terrestrial radiation.\nStatement II: Carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases in the atmosphere are good absorbers of long wave radiation.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I","Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"],"a":3,"x":"Statement I - Incorrect\nThe atmosphere is largely transparent to incoming short-wave solar radiation, which passes through and heats the Earth's surface. The heated surface then emits long-wave (infrared) terrestrial radiation, which the atmosphere absorbs. So the atmosphere is heated mainly from below by terrestrial radiation, not directly by the Sun. This is why temperature normally falls with height in the troposphere (about 6.5°C per km).\n\nStatement II - Correct\nCarbon dioxide, water vapour and other greenhouse gases are good absorbers of long-wave radiation. This is exactly why the atmosphere is heated by terrestrial radiation, and it is the basis of the greenhouse effect.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is correct."},{"i":"2024-2","y":2024,"n":2,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Thickness of the troposphere at the equator is much greater as compared to poles.\nStatement II: At the equator, heat is transported to great heights by strong convectional currents.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I","Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nThe troposphere, the lowest layer of the atmosphere where weather occurs, is about 18 km thick at the equator and about 8 km thick at the poles.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nAt the equator, strong heating of the surface produces powerful convection currents that carry heat and moisture to great heights. This vertical mixing pushes the tropopause (the upper limit of the troposphere) much higher. At the poles, the surface is cold and there is little convection, so the troposphere is shallow.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both statements are correct and Statement II explains Statement I."},{"i":"2024-3","y":2024,"n":3,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following:\n1. Pyroclastic debris\n2. Ash and dust\n3. Nitrogen compounds\n4. Sulphur compounds\nHow many of the above are products of volcanic eruptions?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":3,"x":"Volcanic eruptions release solid, liquid and gaseous materials.\n\n1. Pyroclastic debris - Product of eruptions\nPyroclastic materials are fragments of rock and lava thrown out explosively, including volcanic bombs, lapilli and pumice, and fast-moving pyroclastic flows.\n\n2. Ash and dust - Product of eruptions\nFine volcanic ash and dust are thrown high into the atmosphere and can spread over large areas, disrupting air traffic (e.g., Eyjafjallajökull, 2010).\n\n3. Nitrogen compounds - Product of eruptions\nNCERT lists nitrogen compounds among volcanic gases, along with others.\n\n4. Sulphur compounds - Product of eruptions\nSulphur dioxide and hydrogen sulphide are major volcanic gases. Sulphur dioxide forms sulphate aerosols in the stratosphere that can cool the global climate (e.g., Mount Pinatubo, 1991).\n\n(Other volcanic gases include water vapour, carbon dioxide, chlorine, hydrogen and argon.)\n\nAll four are products, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2024-4","y":2024,"n":4,"s":5,"q":"Which of the following is/are correct inference/inferences from isothermal maps in the month of January?\n1. The isotherms deviate to the north over the ocean and to the south over the continent.\n2. The presence of cold ocean currents, Gulf Stream and North Atlantic Drift make the North Atlantic Ocean colder and the isotherms bend towards the north.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":0,"x":"1. In January the isotherms deviate to the north over the ocean and to the south over the continent - Correct\nIn January, the Northern Hemisphere winter, land cools much more than the sea. So the oceans are relatively warm and isotherms bend northward (poleward) over them, while over the cold continents they bend southward (equatorward).\n\n2. Cold ocean currents, the Gulf Stream and North Atlantic Drift, make the North Atlantic colder and bend isotherms north - Incorrect\nThe Gulf Stream and North Atlantic Drift are warm currents, not cold ones. They make the North Atlantic warmer than other areas at the same latitude, which is why isotherms bend sharply northward over it and why western Europe has mild winters. The statement's reasoning is wrong.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 only."},{"i":"2024-5","y":2024,"n":5,"s":6,"q":"Which of the following countries are well known as the two largest cocoa producers in the world?","o":["Algeria and Morocco","Botswana and Namibia","Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana","Madagascar and Mozambique"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Algeria and Morocco - Incorrect\nThese North African countries have dry Mediterranean and desert climates, unsuitable for cocoa. They are known for phosphates, oil and gas, dates and citrus.\n\n(b) Botswana and Namibia - Incorrect\nThese southern African countries are largely semi-arid; they are known for diamonds and uranium, not cocoa.\n\n(c) Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana - Correct\nCocoa needs a hot, humid equatorial climate with evenly distributed rainfall. Côte d'Ivoire (about 40 percent of world production) and Ghana (about 15-20 percent) on the Gulf of Guinea together produce well over half of the world's cocoa. Cocoa is vital to both economies.\n\n(d) Madagascar and Mozambique - Incorrect\nMadagascar is famous for vanilla and cloves; both countries produce very little cocoa."},{"i":"2024-6","y":2024,"n":6,"s":5,"q":"With reference to the Himalayan rivers joining the Ganga downstream of Prayagraj from West to East, which one of the following sequences is correct?","o":["Ghaghara – Gomati – Gandak – Kosi","Gomati – Ghaghara – Gandak – Kosi","Ghaghara – Gomati – Kosi – Gandak","Gomati – Ghaghara – Kosi – Gandak"],"a":1,"x":"Downstream of Prayagraj (where the Yamuna joins), the major left-bank tributaries join the Ganga in this order from west to east:\n\nGomati - joins near Saidpur/Kaithi in Ghazipur district, Uttar Pradesh. It rises in the Terai near Pilibhit and flows past Lucknow and Jaunpur.\n\nGhaghara - joins near Chhapra (Revelganj) in Bihar. It rises in Tibet (as the Karnali) and flows through Nepal.\n\nGandak - joins opposite Patna at Hajipur-Sonpur. It rises in Nepal (Kali Gandaki) and enters India in Bihar.\n\nKosi - joins near Kursela in Katihar district, Bihar. It is known as the 'Sorrow of Bihar' because of frequent floods and course changes.\n\n(a) Ghaghara-Gomati-Gandak-Kosi - Incorrect, Gomati joins before Ghaghara.\n(b) Gomati-Ghaghara-Gandak-Kosi - Correct.\n(c) Ghaghara-Gomati-Kosi-Gandak - Incorrect, two positions are wrong.\n(d) Gomati-Ghaghara-Kosi-Gandak - Incorrect, Gandak joins before Kosi.\n\n(Strictly, the Gomati rises in the plains rather than the Himalaya, but the sequence is as given in (b).)"},{"i":"2024-7","y":2024,"n":7,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Rainfall is one of the reasons for weathering of rocks.\nStatement II: Rain water contains carbon dioxide in solution.\nStatement III: Rain water contains atmospheric oxygen.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-II and Statement-III are correct and both of them explain Statement-I","Both Statement-II and Statement-III are correct, but only one of them explains Statement-I","Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement-I","Neither Statement-II nor Statement-III is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nRainfall is a major agent of weathering. Water physically breaks rocks (for example through freeze-thaw) and, more importantly, drives chemical weathering.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nRainwater dissolves carbon dioxide from the air and soil to form weak carbonic acid. This acid reacts with rocks such as limestone and dissolves them (carbonation), forming caves and karst landscapes.\n\nStatement III - Correct, and explains Statement I\nRainwater also contains dissolved oxygen, which reacts with iron-bearing minerals in rocks (oxidation). This produces reddish-brown iron oxides, weakening the rock, as in 'rusting'.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both II and III are correct and both explain Statement I."},{"i":"2024-8","y":2024,"n":8,"s":11,"q":"Consider the following countries:\n1. Finland\n2. Germany\n3. Norway\n4. Russia\nHow many of the above countries have a border with the North Sea?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":1,"x":"The North Sea is bordered by the United Kingdom, Norway, Denmark, Germany, the Netherlands, Belgium and France.\n\n1. Finland - No\nFinland borders the Baltic Sea, through the Gulf of Bothnia and the Gulf of Finland.\n\n2. Germany - Yes\nGermany has a North Sea coast in the north-west (Heligoland Bight, ports of Hamburg via the Elbe and Bremen) and a Baltic coast in the north-east.\n\n3. Norway - Yes\nNorway's south-western coast faces the North Sea, where its major oil and gas fields lie.\n\n4. Russia - No\nRussia borders the Baltic Sea, Black Sea, Barents Sea and others, but not the North Sea.\n\nTwo countries border the North Sea, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2024-9","y":2024,"n":9,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following information:\n| | Waterfall | Region | River\n| 1. | Dhuandhar | Malwa | Narmada\n| 2. | Hundru | Chota Nagpur | Subarnarekha\n| 3. | Gersoppa | Western Ghats | Netravati\nIn how many of the above rows is the given information correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":0,"x":"Row 1: Dhuandhar - Malwa - Narmada - Incorrect\nDhuandhar falls are on the Narmada at Bhedaghat near Jabalpur, known for its marble rocks. Jabalpur lies in the Mahakoshal region, not Malwa, so the region is wrong.\n\nRow 2: Hundru - Chota Nagpur - Subarnarekha - Correct\nHundru falls, near Ranchi in Jharkhand, are formed where the Subarnarekha river drops about 98 metres from the edge of the Ranchi (Chota Nagpur) plateau.\n\nRow 3: Gersoppa - Western Ghats - Netravati - Incorrect\nGersoppa (Jog) Falls in Shimoga district, Karnataka, are in the Western Ghats but are formed by the Sharavati river, not the Netravati.\n\nOnly one row is correctly matched, so the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2024-10","y":2024,"n":10,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following information:\n| | Region | Name of the mountain range | Type of mountain\n| 1. | Central Asia | Vosges | Fold mountain\n| 2. | Europe | Alps | Block mountain\n| 3. | North America | Appalachians | Fold mountain\n| 4. | South America | Andes | Fold mountain\nIn how many of the above rows is the given information correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":1,"x":"Row 1: Central Asia - Vosges - Fold mountain - Incorrect\nThe Vosges are in Europe (north-eastern France), not Central Asia. They are a classic example of a block mountain (horst), facing the Black Forest across the Rhine rift valley.\n\nRow 2: Europe - Alps - Block mountain - Incorrect\nThe Alps are in Europe, but they are young fold mountains formed by the collision of the African and Eurasian plates, not block mountains.\n\nRow 3: North America - Appalachians - Fold mountain - Correct\nThe Appalachians in eastern North America are old fold mountains formed about 480-250 million years ago, now heavily eroded.\n\nRow 4: South America - Andes - Fold mountain - Correct\nThe Andes are young fold mountains formed by subduction of the Nazca Plate under the South American Plate.\n\nTwo rows are correctly matched, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2024-11","y":2024,"n":11,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following airports:\n1. Donyi Polo Airport\n2. Kushinagar International Airport\n3. Vijayawada International Airport\nIn the recent past, which of the above have been constructed as Greenfield projects?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"x":"A greenfield airport is built on a new site where there was no existing airport; a brownfield project upgrades an existing one.\n\n1. Donyi Polo Airport - Greenfield\nLocated at Hollongi near Itanagar, it was built from scratch and inaugurated in November 2022 as Arunachal Pradesh's first greenfield airport.\n\n2. Kushinagar International Airport - Greenfield\nIn Uttar Pradesh, it was developed as a new international airport to connect the Buddhist circuit and was inaugurated in October 2021.\n\n3. Vijayawada International Airport - Not greenfield\nThe airport at Gannavaram has existed for decades and was expanded and upgraded to international status, making it a brownfield development.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2024-12","y":2024,"n":12,"s":5,"q":"With reference to \"water vapour\", which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. It is a gas, the amount of which decreases with altitude.\n2. Its percentage is maximum at the poles.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":0,"x":"1. Water vapour is a gas whose amount decreases with altitude - Correct\nWater vapour comes from evaporation at the surface, and its amount depends on temperature. As temperature falls with height, the air can hold less moisture. About 90 percent of atmospheric water vapour is found below about 6 km.\n\n2. Its percentage is maximum at the poles - Incorrect\nWater vapour can be up to 4 percent by volume in warm, humid tropical areas, and is less than 1 percent in cold, dry polar regions and in deserts. So it is maximum in the tropics, not at the poles.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 only."},{"i":"2024-13","y":2024,"n":13,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following description:\n1. Annual and daily range of temperatures is low.\n2. Precipitation occurs throughout the year.\n3. Precipitation varies between 50 cm – 250 cm.\nWhat is this type of climate?","o":["Equatorial climate","China type climate","Humid subtropical climate","Marine West coast climate"],"a":3,"x":"The description: low annual and daily temperature range, rainfall throughout the year, and rainfall between 50 and 250 cm.\n\n(a) Equatorial climate - Incorrect\nEquatorial regions have rain throughout the year, but rainfall is usually very high (over 200 cm, often convectional thunderstorms) and the daily temperature range is greater than the annual range.\n\n(b) China type climate - Incorrect\nThis humid subtropical climate of eastern margins has hot summers and marked summer rainfall maximum, with larger temperature ranges.\n\n(c) Humid subtropical climate - Incorrect\nSame as the China type; it has hot summers, cooler winters and a larger annual range.\n\n(d) Marine West Coast climate - Correct\nFound on western margins between about 40° and 65° latitude (British Isles, north-west Europe, British Columbia, southern Chile, New Zealand). The moderating influence of the sea and warm currents gives mild winters, cool summers and low temperature ranges. Westerly winds bring rain throughout the year, from 50 cm to 250 cm (NCERT)."},{"i":"2024-14","y":2024,"n":14,"s":5,"q":"With reference to \"Coriolis force\", which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. It increases with increase in wind velocity.\n2. It is maximum at the poles and is absent at the equator.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"The Coriolis force is an apparent force caused by the Earth's rotation that deflects moving air to the right in the Northern Hemisphere and to the left in the Southern Hemisphere. Its magnitude is 2vΩ sin φ, where v is wind velocity, Ω is the Earth's angular velocity and φ is latitude.\n\n1. It increases with increase in wind velocity - Correct\nThe force is directly proportional to wind speed, so faster winds are deflected more strongly.\n\n2. It is maximum at the poles and absent at the equator - Correct\nSince sin 90° equals 1 and sin 0° equals 0, the force is greatest at the poles and zero at the equator. This is why tropical cyclones do not form within about 5° of the equator, as there is not enough Coriolis force to create rotation.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2024-15","y":2024,"n":15,"s":5,"q":"On June 21 every year, which of the following latitude(s) experience(s) a sunlight of more than 12 hours?\n1. Equator\n2. Tropic of Cancer\n3. Tropic of Capricorn\n4. Arctic Circle\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","3 and 4","2 and 4"],"a":3,"x":"On 21 June, the summer solstice, the Sun is overhead at the Tropic of Cancer and the Northern Hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun.\n\n1. Equator - Not more than 12 hours\nAt the equator day and night are always about 12 hours each, throughout the year.\n\n2. Tropic of Cancer - More than 12 hours\nIt has about 13.5 hours of daylight on 21 June.\n\n3. Tropic of Capricorn - Less than 12 hours\nIt is winter in the Southern Hemisphere, so days are short (about 10.5 hours).\n\n4. Arctic Circle - More than 12 hours\nOn this day the Arctic Circle has 24 hours of continuous daylight (the 'midnight sun').\n\nHence the answer is (d) 2 and 4."},{"i":"2024-16","y":2024,"n":16,"s":2,"q":"One of the following regions has the world's largest tropical peatland, which holds about three years worth of global carbon emissions from fossil fuels; and the possible destruction of which can exert detrimental effect on the global climate. Which one of the following denotes that region?","o":["Amazon Basin","Congo Basin","Kikori Basin","Rio de la Plata Basin"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Amazon Basin - Incorrect\nThe Amazon has large peatlands (such as in the Pastaza-Marañón basin of Peru), but not the world's largest tropical peatland.\n\n(b) Congo Basin - Correct\nThe Cuvette Centrale peatland in the central Congo Basin (Republic of Congo and DR Congo), mapped by scientists in 2017, is the world's largest tropical peatland, covering about 1.45 lakh sq km. It stores about 29-30 billion tonnes of carbon, roughly equal to three years of global fossil-fuel emissions. Drying, drainage or logging could release this carbon and worsen climate change.\n\n(c) Kikori Basin - Incorrect\nThe Kikori is in Papua New Guinea; it has swamps and peat but is much smaller.\n\n(d) Rio de la Plata Basin - Incorrect\nThis is the basin of the Paraná, Paraguay and Uruguay rivers in South America, not known for the largest tropical peatland."},{"i":"2024-17","y":2024,"n":17,"s":2,"q":"With reference to perfluoroalkyl and polyfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS) that are used in making many consumer products, consider the following statements:\n1. PFAS are found to be widespread in drinking water, food and food packaging materials.\n2. PFAS are not easily degraded in the environment.\n3. Persistent exposure to PFAS can lead to bioaccumulation in animal bodies.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"PFAS (per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances) are a group of thousands of man-made chemicals used in non-stick cookware, water-resistant clothing, food packaging, firefighting foams and cosmetics.\n\n1. PFAS are widespread in drinking water, food and food packaging - Correct\nStudies worldwide, including in India, have found PFAS in drinking water, groundwater, soil, fish, food and grease-resistant food packaging.\n\n2. PFAS are not easily degraded in the environment - Correct\nPFAS contain very strong carbon-fluorine bonds, among the strongest in chemistry, so they do not break down naturally. That is why they are called 'forever chemicals'.\n\n3. Persistent exposure can lead to bioaccumulation in animal bodies - Correct\nPFAS build up in the blood and organs of animals and humans and move up food chains. They are linked to cancers, liver damage, thyroid disease, reduced immunity and developmental problems. Some PFAS (PFOS, PFOA) are restricted under the Stockholm Convention.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2024-18","y":2024,"n":18,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following:\n1. Carabid beetles\n2. Centipedes\n3. Flies\n4. Termites\n5. Wasps\nParasitoid species are found in how many of the above kind of organisms?","o":["Only two","Only three","Only four","All five"],"a":1,"x":"A parasitoid is an organism, usually an insect, whose larvae live on or inside a single host and eventually kill it. Unlike a parasite, it kills its host; unlike a predator, it uses only one host. Parasitoids are widely used in biological pest control.\n\n1. Carabid beetles - Parasitoid species found\nMost ground beetles are predators, but some genera (such as Lebia and Brachinus) have larvae that are parasitoids of other insects' pupae.\n\n2. Centipedes - No parasitoids\nCentipedes are predators that kill prey with venomous claws.\n\n3. Flies - Parasitoid species found\nFamilies such as Tachinidae, and some Sarcophagidae and Phoridae, lay eggs on or in other insects, and their larvae kill the hosts.\n\n4. Termites - No parasitoids\nTermites are social insects that feed on wood and plant matter.\n\n5. Wasps - Parasitoid species found\nParasitoid wasps (Ichneumonidae, Braconidae, Trichogramma) are the largest group of parasitoids and are used to control crop pests.\n\nThree groups have parasitoid species, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2024-19","y":2024,"n":19,"s":6,"q":"Consider the following plants:\n1. Groundnut\n2. Horse-gram\n3. Soybean\nHow many of the above belong to the pea family?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":2,"x":"The pea family is Fabaceae (also called Leguminosae), whose members bear pods and usually have root nodules with nitrogen-fixing Rhizobium bacteria.\n\n1. Groundnut (Arachis hypogaea) - Pea family\nGroundnut is a legume that develops its pods underground after the flower stalk (peg) grows into the soil.\n\n2. Horse-gram (Macrotyloma uniflorum) - Pea family\nHorse-gram is a drought-tolerant pulse grown in South India and used for food and fodder.\n\n3. Soybean (Glycine max) - Pea family\nSoybean is a leguminous oilseed and protein-rich crop, grown mainly in Madhya Pradesh and Maharashtra.\n\nAll three belong to the pea family, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2024-21","y":2024,"n":21,"s":2,"q":"The organisms \"Cicada, Froghopper and Pond skater\" are:","o":["Birds","Fish","Insects","Reptiles"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Birds - Incorrect\nNone of these is a bird.\n\n(b) Fish - Incorrect\nAlthough the pond skater lives on water, none of them is a fish.\n\n(c) Insects - Correct\nAll three are insects belonging to the order Hemiptera (true bugs), with piercing and sucking mouthparts:\nThe cicada is known for the loud buzzing sound made by males; some species spend years underground as nymphs.\nThe froghopper (spittlebug) is named for its frog-like jumping; its nymphs hide in a frothy 'spittle' mass on plants.\nThe pond skater (water strider) walks on the surface of ponds using water-repellent hairs on its legs and the surface tension of water.\n\n(d) Reptiles - Incorrect\nNone of them is a reptile."},{"i":"2024-22","y":2024,"n":22,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Many chewing gums found in the market are considered a source of environmental pollution.\nStatement II: Many chewing gums contain plastic as gum base.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I","Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nDiscarded chewing gum is a widespread pollution problem. It sticks to pavements, streets and public places, is costly to remove, and does not decompose, which is why some countries (like Singapore) have restricted its sale.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nMost modern chewing gums use a synthetic gum base made of polymers such as polyvinyl acetate and polyisobutylene (also used in glue and tyres), instead of natural chicle. These are plastics that are not biodegradable, and gum can also shed microplastics. This is why discarded gum causes long-lasting pollution.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both statements are correct and Statement II explains Statement I."},{"i":"2024-23","y":2024,"n":23,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Country | Animal found in its natural habitat\n| 1. | Brazil | Indri\n| 2. | Indonesia | Elk\n| 3. | Madagascar | Bonobo\nHow many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":3,"x":"1. Brazil : Indri - Incorrect\nThe indri is the largest living lemur and is found only in the rainforests of Madagascar. Brazil has no lemurs.\n\n2. Indonesia : Elk - Incorrect\nElk (wapiti, and the moose called 'elk' in Europe) live in cold and temperate forests of North America and northern Eurasia, not tropical Indonesia.\n\n3. Madagascar : Bonobo - Incorrect\nThe bonobo, a great ape closely related to the chimpanzee, is found only in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, south of the Congo River.\n\nNone of the pairs is correctly matched, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2024-24","y":2024,"n":24,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding World Toilet Organization:\n1. It is one of the agencies of the United Nations.\n2. World Toilet Summit, World Toilet Day and World Toilet College are the initiatives of this organization, to inspire action to tackle the global sanitation crisis.\n3. The main focus of its function is to grant funds to the least developed countries and developing countries to achieve the end of open defecation.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["2 only","3 only","1 and 2","2 and 3"],"a":0,"x":"1. It is one of the agencies of the United Nations - Incorrect\nThe World Toilet Organization is a global non-profit organisation founded in 2001 in Singapore by Jack Sim. It is not a UN agency, although the UN General Assembly adopted 19 November, the date of its founding, as the official World Toilet Day in 2013.\n\n2. World Toilet Summit, World Toilet Day and World Toilet College are its initiatives - Correct\nThe organisation started World Toilet Day and the World Toilet Summit, and runs the World Toilet College, which trains people in sanitation and hygiene, to inspire action on the global sanitation crisis.\n\n3. Its main function is to grant funds to least developed and developing countries to end open defecation - Incorrect\nIt is not a funding agency. It works through advocacy, awareness campaigns, capacity building, training and market-based sanitation solutions.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 2 only."},{"i":"2024-25","y":2024,"n":25,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Lions do not have a particular breeding season.\n2. Unlike most other big cats, cheetahs do not roar.\n3. Unlike male lions, male leopards do not proclaim their territory by scent marking.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"x":"1. Lions do not have a particular breeding season - Correct\nLions can mate and breed at any time of the year. Lionesses in a pride often come into oestrus at the same time, but there is no fixed breeding season.\n\n2. Unlike most other big cats, cheetahs do not roar - Correct\nRoaring big cats (lion, tiger, leopard, jaguar) belong to the genus Panthera and have a specially adapted larynx and flexible hyoid bone. Cheetahs belong to a different genus (Acinonyx) and cannot roar; instead they purr, chirp, hiss and growl.\n\n3. Unlike male lions, male leopards do not proclaim their territory by scent marking - Incorrect\nMale leopards are territorial and actively mark their territory by spraying urine, leaving scrape marks and scratching trees, just as lions do. They also use a rasping call.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2024-26","y":2024,"n":26,"s":6,"q":"Which one of the following is the correct description of \"100 Million Farmers\"?","o":["It is a platform for accelerating the transition towards food and water systems that are net-zero (carbon), nature-positive and that aims to increase farmer resilience.","It is an international alliance and a network of individuals and farming organisations interested in supporting and strengthening the development of the organic animal husbandry.","It is a digital platform fully integrated with service providers and built on blockchain that lets buyers, sellers and third parties trade fertilizers quickly and securely.","It is a platform with the mission of encouraging the farmers to form Farmer Product Organisations or Agribusiness Consortiums, thus facilitating the access to global open markets to sell their products."],"a":0,"x":"(a) A platform for accelerating the transition to food and water systems that are net-zero, nature-positive and increase farmer resilience - Correct\n'100 Million Farmers' is a multi-stakeholder platform launched by the World Economic Forum in 2023. It brings together companies, governments, farmer organisations and financiers to help 100 million farmers adopt climate-smart, regenerative practices by 2030, making food and water systems net-zero and nature-positive while improving farmers' incomes and resilience.\n\n(b) An international alliance supporting organic animal husbandry - Incorrect\nThis describes organic livestock networks, not this initiative.\n\n(c) A blockchain-based digital platform for trading fertilisers - Incorrect\nThis describes a commercial trading platform, unrelated to the initiative.\n\n(d) A platform encouraging farmers to form FPOs or agribusiness consortiums for global market access - Incorrect\nThis resembles schemes such as India's programme to form 10,000 Farmer Producer Organisations, not '100 Million Farmers'."},{"i":"2024-27","y":2024,"n":27,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following:\n1. Battery storage\n2. Biomass generators\n3. Fuel cells\n4. Rooftop solar photovoltaic units\nHow many of the above are considered \"Distributed Energy Resources\"?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":3,"x":"Distributed Energy Resources (DERs) are small-scale units that generate or store electricity close to where it is used, often connected to the local distribution network or 'behind the meter', rather than large central power stations.\n\n1. Battery storage - A DER\nHome, commercial or community batteries store electricity locally and help balance supply and demand.\n\n2. Biomass generators - A DER\nSmall biomass-based power units in villages or industries generate electricity locally.\n\n3. Fuel cells - A DER\nStationary fuel cells produce electricity on site for buildings, telecom towers or data centres.\n\n4. Rooftop solar photovoltaic units - A DER\nRooftop solar is the most common DER, generating power on the consumer's own premises.\n\nAll four are DERs, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2024-28","y":2024,"n":28,"s":2,"q":"Which one of the following shows a unique relationship with an insect that has coevolved with it and that is the only insect that can pollinate this tree?","o":["Fig","Mahua","Sandalwood","Silk cotton"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Fig - Correct\nFig trees (genus Ficus, including banyan and peepal) have one of nature's most famous mutualisms with fig wasps. The fig's flowers are hidden inside a closed structure (the syconium), and only a tiny female fig wasp can enter to pollinate them and lay eggs. Most fig species have their own specific wasp species. The wasp cannot breed without the fig, and the fig cannot be pollinated without the wasp, so they have co-evolved over about 80 million years.\n\n(b) Mahua - Incorrect\nMahua flowers are pollinated mainly by bats and birds, and are also visited by many insects; there is no single co-evolved pollinator.\n\n(c) Sandalwood - Incorrect\nSandalwood is pollinated by various generalist insects such as bees and flies.\n\n(d) Silk cotton - Incorrect\nThe red silk cotton tree (Bombax ceiba) is pollinated mainly by birds and bats attracted to its large red flowers."},{"i":"2024-29","y":2024,"n":29,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following:\n1. Butterflies\n2. Fish\n3. Frogs\nHow many of the above have poisonous species among them?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":2,"x":"1. Butterflies - Have poisonous species\nSome butterflies are poisonous to predators. For example, the monarch butterfly and the common crow and tiger butterflies of India store toxins from plants (like milkweed) eaten as caterpillars. Their bright colours warn predators.\n\n2. Fish - Have poisonous species\nPufferfish contain tetrodotoxin, a deadly nerve poison; boxfish also secrete toxins. (Some fish like stonefish are venomous, injecting toxin through spines.)\n\n3. Frogs - Have poisonous species\nPoison dart frogs of Central and South America secrete powerful toxins (such as batrachotoxin) through their skin; indigenous people used them to poison arrow tips.\n\nAll three groups include poisonous species, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2024-30","y":2024,"n":30,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following:\n1. Cashew\n2. Papaya\n3. Red sanders\nHow many of the above trees are actually native to India?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":0,"x":"1. Cashew - Not native\nCashew is native to north-eastern Brazil and was brought to India by the Portuguese in the 16th century, initially to check soil erosion on the Goa coast. India is now a major producer and processor.\n\n2. Papaya - Not native\nPapaya originated in southern Mexico and Central America and was introduced by the Portuguese.\n\n3. Red sanders - Native\nRed sanders (Pterocarpus santalinus) is endemic to the southern Eastern Ghats of Andhra Pradesh, mainly the Seshachalam hills around Tirupati. It is valued for its red heartwood and is heavily smuggled; it is listed as Endangered by the IUCN.\n\nOnly one tree is native to India, so the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2024-31","y":2024,"n":31,"s":3,"q":"With reference to radioisotope thermoelectric generators (RTGs), consider the following statements:\n1. RTGs are miniature fission reactors.\n2. RTGs are used for powering the onboard systems of spacecrafts.\n3. RTGs can use Plutonium-238, which is a by-product of weapons development.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. RTGs are miniature fission reactors - Incorrect\nA radioisotope thermoelectric generator does not have a nuclear chain reaction and is not a reactor. It uses the heat released naturally by the radioactive decay of an isotope and converts it into electricity using thermocouples (based on the Seebeck effect). It has no moving parts.\n\n2. RTGs are used for powering the onboard systems of spacecraft - Correct\nRTGs are used where solar power is too weak or unavailable, such as deep-space missions and planetary rovers. Examples include Voyager 1 and 2 (still working after about 48 years), Cassini, New Horizons, and the Curiosity and Perseverance Mars rovers.\n\n3. RTGs can use Plutonium-238, a by-product of weapons development - Correct\nPlutonium-238 is the preferred fuel because it gives steady heat for decades and emits mainly alpha radiation, which is easily shielded. It was historically produced in reactors of the US nuclear weapons complex (such as at Savannah River).\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2024-32","y":2024,"n":32,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Giant stars live much longer than dwarf stars.\nStatement II: Compared to dwarf stars, giant stars have a greater rate of nuclear reactions.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I","Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"],"a":3,"x":"Statement I - Incorrect\nGiant (massive) stars live much shorter lives than dwarf stars. A star 10-20 times the Sun's mass may live only a few million to tens of millions of years, while the Sun (a yellow dwarf) lives about 10 billion years and red dwarfs may live for trillions of years.\n\nStatement II - Correct\nMassive stars have much higher pressure and temperature in their cores, so nuclear fusion proceeds at a far greater rate. Although they have more fuel, they burn it disproportionately faster. (This is in fact the reason why they die sooner, often ending in supernovae and forming neutron stars or black holes.)\n\nHence the answer is (d) Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is correct."},{"i":"2024-33","y":2024,"n":33,"s":3,"q":"Which one of the following is synthesised in human body that dilates blood vessels and increases blood flow?","o":["Nitric oxide","Nitrous oxide","Nitrogen dioxide","Nitrogen pentoxide"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Nitric oxide - Correct\nNitric oxide (NO) is produced in the inner lining (endothelium) of blood vessels from the amino acid L-arginine by the enzyme nitric oxide synthase. It diffuses into the smooth muscle of vessel walls and relaxes it, widening (dilating) the vessels and increasing blood flow. Its discovery as a signalling molecule won the 1998 Nobel Prize in Medicine. Drugs like nitroglycerine for angina work by releasing NO.\n\n(b) Nitrous oxide - Incorrect\nNitrous oxide (N2O), 'laughing gas', is used as an anaesthetic and is a greenhouse gas; it is not synthesised by the body to dilate vessels.\n\n(c) Nitrogen dioxide - Incorrect\nNO2 is a toxic air pollutant from vehicle exhaust that irritates the lungs.\n\n(d) Nitrogen pentoxide - Incorrect\nN2O5 is an unstable, acidic compound with no role in the human body."},{"i":"2024-34","y":2024,"n":34,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following activities:\n1. Identification of narcotics on passengers at airports or in aircraft\n2. Monitoring of precipitation\n3. Tracking the migration of animals\nIn how many of the above activities can the radars be used?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":1,"x":"1. Identification of narcotics on passengers at airports or in aircraft - Radar not used (as per key)\nDrugs on passengers are detected using body scanners (millimetre-wave or X-ray backscatter imaging), X-ray baggage scanners, trace detectors and sniffer dogs. Conventional radar is not used for this purpose.\n\n2. Monitoring of precipitation - Radar used\nDoppler Weather Radars send out microwave pulses that bounce off raindrops, snow and hail. They measure rainfall intensity and track thunderstorms and cyclones. The India Meteorological Department operates a network of such radars.\n\n3. Tracking the migration of animals - Radar used\nWeather radars and special bird radars detect large movements of birds, bats and insects in the air. This field, called radar ornithology or aeroecology, is used to study migration and prevent bird strikes near airports.\n\nRadar is used in two of the activities, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2024-35","y":2024,"n":35,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following aircraft:\n1. Rafael\n2. MiG-29\n3. Tejas MK-1\nHow many of the above are considered fifth generation fighter aircraft?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":3,"x":"Fifth-generation fighter aircraft have all-aspect stealth, supercruise (supersonic flight without afterburners), advanced integrated avionics and sensor fusion, and internal weapons bays. Examples are the American F-22 and F-35, China's J-20 and Russia's Su-57.\n\n1. Rafale - Not fifth generation\nThe French Rafale, inducted by the IAF in 2020, is a 4.5-generation multirole fighter.\n\n2. MiG-29 - Not fifth generation\nThe Russian MiG-29 is a fourth-generation fighter (upgraded in Indian service).\n\n3. Tejas Mk-1 - Not fifth generation\nIndia's indigenous Light Combat Aircraft Tejas is a fourth or 4.5-generation fighter. India's fifth-generation project is the Advanced Medium Combat Aircraft (AMCA), still under development.\n\nNone of them is fifth generation, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2024-36","y":2024,"n":36,"s":3,"q":"In which of the following are hydrogels used?\n1. Controlled drug delivery in patients\n2. Mobile air-conditioning systems\n3. Preparation of industrial lubricants\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"x":"Hydrogels are three-dimensional networks of water-loving polymers that can absorb and hold large amounts of water while keeping their shape.\n\n1. Controlled drug delivery in patients - Used\nHydrogels can be loaded with drugs and release them slowly in response to conditions such as temperature or pH. They are also used in contact lenses, wound dressings, tissue engineering and disposable diapers.\n\n2. Mobile air-conditioning systems - Not used (as per key)\nHydrogels are not an established component of vehicle or mobile air-conditioning systems, which use refrigerants, compressors and heat exchangers.\n\n3. Preparation of industrial lubricants - Not used (as per key)\nIndustrial lubricants are mainly oils and greases; hydrogels are not an established material for them.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 only."},{"i":"2024-37","y":2024,"n":37,"s":3,"q":"Which one of the following is the exhaust pipe emission from Fuel Cell Electric Vehicles, powered by hydrogen?","o":["Hydrogen peroxide","Hydronium","Oxygen","Water vapour"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Hydrogen peroxide - Incorrect\nHydrogen peroxide is not produced in a fuel cell.\n\n(b) Hydronium - Incorrect\nHydronium (H3O+) is an ion found in acidic water solutions; it is not an exhaust gas.\n\n(c) Oxygen - Incorrect\nOxygen from the air is consumed in the fuel cell, not emitted.\n\n(d) Water vapour - Correct\nIn a hydrogen fuel cell, hydrogen at the anode splits into protons and electrons. The electrons flow through an external circuit as electricity, while the protons pass through a membrane to the cathode, where they combine with oxygen and the electrons to form water: 2H2 + O2 gives 2H2O. The only tailpipe emission is water, mostly as vapour, which is why fuel-cell vehicles are called zero-emission vehicles."},{"i":"2024-38","y":2024,"n":38,"s":5,"q":"Recently, the term \"pumped-storage hydropower\" is actually and appropriately discussed in the context of which one of the following?","o":["Irrigation of terraced crop fields","Lift irrigation of cereal crops","Long duration energy storage","Rainwater harvesting system"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Irrigation of terraced crop fields - Incorrect\nTerraced fields are irrigated by gravity-fed channels, not by pumped-storage hydropower.\n\n(b) Lift irrigation of cereal crops - Incorrect\nLift irrigation pumps water up to fields, but pumped-storage hydropower is about generating and storing electricity.\n\n(c) Long duration energy storage - Correct\nA pumped-storage plant has two reservoirs at different heights. When electricity is cheap or surplus (for example, midday solar power), water is pumped to the upper reservoir. When demand is high, water is released through turbines to generate electricity. It can store energy for many hours and is the largest form of grid-scale storage in the world. India issued guidelines in 2023 to promote it for integrating renewable energy.\n\n(d) Rainwater harvesting system - Incorrect\nRainwater harvesting collects rain for water supply and groundwater recharge."},{"i":"2024-39","y":2024,"n":39,"s":2,"q":"\"Membrane Bioreactors\" are often discussed in the context of:","o":["Assisted reproductive technologies","Drug delivery nanotechnologies","Vaccine production technologies","Wastewater treatment technologies"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Assisted reproductive technologies - Incorrect\nTechniques like IVF use culture media and incubators, not membrane bioreactors.\n\n(b) Drug delivery nanotechnologies - Incorrect\nNanoparticle drug delivery is unrelated.\n\n(c) Vaccine production technologies - Incorrect\nVaccines are produced in bioreactors (fermenters), but the term 'membrane bioreactor' refers specifically to water treatment technology.\n\n(d) Wastewater treatment technologies - Correct\nA membrane bioreactor combines a biological treatment process (activated sludge, where microbes break down organic matter) with membrane filtration (microfiltration or ultrafiltration) that separates the treated water from the sludge. It produces very high-quality treated water suitable for reuse, needs less space than conventional plants, and is used for municipal sewage and industrial effluents."},{"i":"2024-40","y":2024,"n":40,"s":1,"q":"With reference to the Indian economy, \"Collateral Borrowing and Lending Obligations\" are the instruments of:","o":["Bond market","Forex market","Money market","Stock market"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Bond market - Incorrect\nThe bond market deals in long-term debt such as government and corporate bonds.\n\n(b) Forex market - Incorrect\nThe forex market deals in buying and selling of currencies.\n\n(c) Money market - Correct\nCollateralised Borrowing and Lending Obligation (CBLO) was a money market instrument introduced by the Clearing Corporation of India (CCIL) in 2003. It allowed banks, mutual funds, insurance companies and others to borrow and lend for short periods (one day to one year) against government securities as collateral. In November 2018 it was replaced by the Tri-party Repo (TREPS).\n\n(d) Stock market - Incorrect\nThe stock market deals in equity shares."},{"i":"2024-41","y":2024,"n":41,"s":5,"q":"The total fertility rate in an economy is defined as:","o":["the number of children born per 1000 people in the population in a year.","the number of children born to a couple in their lifetime in a given population.","the birth rate minus death rate.","the average number of live births a woman would have by the end of her child-bearing age."],"a":3,"x":"(a) The number of children born per 1000 people in a year - Incorrect\nThis is the Crude Birth Rate. India's CBR is about 19 per 1,000.\n\n(b) The number of children born to a couple in their lifetime - Incorrect\nFertility rate is measured per woman, not per couple, and TFR is a hypothetical measure based on current age-specific rates.\n\n(c) Birth rate minus death rate - Incorrect\nThis is the rate of natural increase of population.\n\n(d) The average number of live births a woman would have by the end of her child-bearing age - Correct\nTotal Fertility Rate is the average number of children a woman would have during her reproductive years (15-49) if she experienced the current age-specific fertility rates. A TFR of about 2.1 is called replacement level. India's TFR fell to 2.0 in NFHS-5 (2019-21), below replacement level, though it varies widely between States (e.g., about 3 in Bihar and 1.6 in Kerala)."},{"i":"2024-42","y":2024,"n":42,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. In India, Non-Banking Financial Companies can access the Liquidity Adjustment Facility window of the Reserve Bank of India.\n2. In India, Foreign Institutional Investors can hold the Government Securities (G-Secs).\n3. In India, Stock Exchanges can offer separate trading platforms for debts.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","3 only","1, 2 and 3","2 and 3 only"],"a":2,"x":"1. NBFCs can access the Liquidity Adjustment Facility of the RBI - Correct (as per key)\nThe Liquidity Adjustment Facility (repo and reverse repo, now the Standing Deposit Facility) is mainly for scheduled commercial banks. However, standalone Primary Dealers, which are registered as NBFCs, are also eligible to participate in LAF operations. On this basis the statement is treated as correct.\n\n2. Foreign Institutional Investors can hold Government Securities - Correct\nForeign Portfolio Investors (earlier called FIIs) can invest in G-Secs within limits set by the RBI, through the general route and the Voluntary Retention Route, and without limits in specified bonds under the Fully Accessible Route (2020). This is why Indian bonds were included in global indices like the JP Morgan index.\n\n3. Stock exchanges can offer separate trading platforms for debt - Correct\nSEBI allows stock exchanges to operate separate debt segments and request-for-quote platforms for trading corporate bonds and government securities (for example, NSE and BSE debt segments).\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2024-43","y":2024,"n":43,"s":1,"q":"In India, which of the following can trade in Corporate Bonds and Government Securities?\n1. Insurance Companies\n2. Pension Funds\n3. Retail Investors\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"1. Insurance companies - Can trade\nInsurance companies are among the largest investors in government securities and corporate bonds. IRDAI regulations require them to invest a large part of their funds in government securities.\n\n2. Pension funds - Can trade\nPension funds such as the EPFO and NPS fund managers hold large amounts of government and corporate bonds because they need safe, long-term returns.\n\n3. Retail investors - Can trade\nRetail investors can buy government securities directly through the RBI Retail Direct scheme (launched 2021) and through non-competitive bidding in auctions, and can buy corporate bonds on stock exchanges and SEBI-registered Online Bond Platforms.\n\nAll three can trade, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2024-44","y":2024,"n":44,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following:\n1. Exchange-Traded Funds (ETF)\n2. Motor vehicles\n3. Currency swap\nWhich of the above is/are considered financial instruments?","o":["1 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3","1 and 3 only"],"a":3,"x":"A financial instrument is a contract that gives rise to a financial asset for one party and a financial liability or equity for another, such as shares, bonds, derivatives and fund units.\n\n1. Exchange-Traded Funds - Financial instrument\nETFs are investment funds whose units are traded on stock exchanges like shares. They usually track an index (like Nifty 50) or a commodity (like gold ETFs).\n\n2. Motor vehicles - Not a financial instrument\nVehicles are physical (real) assets. A loan taken to buy a vehicle is a financial instrument, but the vehicle itself is not.\n\n3. Currency swap - Financial instrument\nA currency swap is a derivative contract in which two parties exchange principal and interest in different currencies. The RBI has currency swap arrangements with other central banks (for example, the SAARC swap facility).\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1 and 3 only."},{"i":"2024-45","y":2024,"n":45,"s":1,"q":"With reference to the sectors of the Indian economy, consider the following pairs:\n| | Economic activity | Sector\n| 1. | Storage of agricultural produce | Secondary\n| 2. | Dairy farm | Primary\n| 3. | Mineral exploration | Tertiary\n| 4. | Weaving cloth | Secondary\nHow many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":1,"x":"The primary sector uses natural resources directly (agriculture, animal husbandry, fishing, mining); the secondary sector transforms raw materials into goods (manufacturing); the tertiary sector provides services (transport, storage, trade, banking).\n\n1. Storage of agricultural produce : Secondary - Incorrect\nStorage (warehousing, cold storage) is a service, so it belongs to the tertiary sector.\n\n2. Dairy farm : Primary - Correct\nDairy farming is animal husbandry, a primary activity.\n\n3. Mineral exploration : Tertiary - Incorrect\nMineral exploration and mining deal with natural resources, so they belong to the primary sector.\n\n4. Weaving cloth : Secondary - Correct\nWeaving converts yarn into cloth, a manufacturing activity in the secondary sector.\n\nTwo pairs are correctly matched, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2024-46","y":2024,"n":46,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following materials:\n1. Agricultural residues\n2. Corn grain\n3. Wastewater treatment sludge\n4. Wood mill waste\nWhich of the above can be used as feedstock for producing Sustainable Aviation Fuel?","o":["1 and 2 only","3 and 4 only","1, 2, 3 and 4","1, 3 and 4 only"],"a":2,"x":"Sustainable Aviation Fuel (SAF) is jet fuel made from renewable or waste sources that can cut lifecycle emissions by up to about 80 percent. It can be produced through several approved technological pathways.\n\n1. Agricultural residues - Usable\nCrop residues like rice straw and corn stover are lignocellulosic biomass that can be converted into fuel through gasification (Fischer-Tropsch) or into alcohol and then jet fuel.\n\n2. Corn grain - Usable\nCorn can be fermented into ethanol, which can be converted into jet fuel through the alcohol-to-jet pathway.\n\n3. Wastewater treatment sludge - Usable\nSewage sludge can be converted into fuel through hydrothermal liquefaction or gasification.\n\n4. Wood mill waste - Usable\nSawdust and other forestry residues are woody biomass suitable for SAF production.\n\nThe US Department of Energy lists all of these as SAF feedstocks, along with used cooking oil, animal fats and municipal waste. India has set targets for 1 percent SAF blending in international flights by 2027.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1, 2, 3 and 4."},{"i":"2024-47","y":2024,"n":47,"s":1,"q":"With reference to physical capital in Indian economy, consider the following pairs:\n| | Items | Category\n| 1. | Farmer's plough | Working capital\n| 2. | Computer | Fixed capital\n| 3. | Yarn used by the weaver | Fixed capital\n| 4. | Petrol | Working capital\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":1,"x":"Physical capital is of two kinds (NCERT Class 9, 'The Story of Village Palampur'). Fixed capital includes tools, machines and buildings that are used over many years of production. Working capital includes raw materials and money that are used up in one production cycle.\n\n1. Farmer's plough : Working capital - Incorrect\nA plough is used season after season, so it is fixed capital.\n\n2. Computer : Fixed capital - Correct\nA computer is used for several years, so it is fixed capital.\n\n3. Yarn used by the weaver : Fixed capital - Incorrect\nYarn is a raw material consumed in making cloth, so it is working capital.\n\n4. Petrol : Working capital - Correct\nPetrol is used up in production (for example, running a pump or vehicle), so it is working capital.\n\nTwo pairs are correctly matched, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2024-48","y":2024,"n":48,"s":3,"q":"Which one of the following words/phrases is most appropriately used to denote \"an interoperable network of 3D virtual worlds that can be accessed simultaneously by millions of users, who can exert property rights over virtual items\"?","o":["Big data analytics","Cryptography","Metaverse","Virtual matrix"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Big data analytics - Incorrect\nThis is the process of examining very large datasets to find patterns and insights; it is not a virtual world.\n\n(b) Cryptography - Incorrect\nCryptography is the science of securing information through encryption; it underlies blockchain but does not describe virtual worlds.\n\n(c) Metaverse - Correct\nThe metaverse is described (for example by Matthew Ball) as a massively scaled and interoperable network of real-time 3D virtual worlds that can be experienced simultaneously by an unlimited number of users, each with their own identity, history, ownership rights over virtual items (often through NFTs), communication and payments. Users can move their avatars and assets across worlds.\n\n(d) Virtual matrix - Incorrect\nThis is not a standard term for such a network."},{"i":"2024-49","y":2024,"n":49,"s":1,"q":"With reference to the rule/rules imposed by the Reserve Bank of India while treating foreign banks, consider the following statements:\n1. There is no minimum capital requirement for wholly owned banking subsidiaries in India.\n2. For wholly owned banking subsidiaries in India, at least 50% of the board members should be Indian nationals.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":3,"x":"The RBI's 'Framework for setting up of Wholly Owned Subsidiaries by Foreign Banks in India' (November 2013) encourages foreign banks to operate as local subsidiaries rather than branches.\n\n1. There is no minimum capital requirement for wholly owned subsidiaries - Incorrect\nA WOS must have a minimum initial paid-up voting equity capital (or net worth) of 500 crore rupees, brought in as fresh capital.\n\n2. At least 50 percent of the board members of a WOS should be Indian nationals - Incorrect\nThe actual rule is that not less than 50 percent of the directors should be Indian nationals, NRIs or PIOs, with at least one-third of the directors being Indian nationals resident in India. Limiting the 50 percent requirement to 'Indian nationals' alone makes the statement inaccurate. (The rules also require at least 50 percent non-executive directors and at least one-third independent directors.)\n\nHence the answer is (d) Neither 1 nor 2."},{"i":"2024-50","y":2024,"n":50,"s":1,"q":"With reference to Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) rules in India, consider the following statements:\n1. CSR rules specify that expenditures that benefit the company directly or its employees will not be considered as CSR activities.\n2. CSR rules do not specify minimum spending on CSR activities.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":0,"x":"1. CSR rules specify that expenditure benefiting the company directly or its employees will not count as CSR - Correct\nThe Companies (Corporate Social Responsibility Policy) Rules, 2014 exclude from CSR activities undertaken in the normal course of business, activities benefiting only the employees of the company and their families, contributions to political parties, and activities for marketing or promotion of products or services.\n\n2. CSR rules do not specify minimum spending - Incorrect\nSection 135 of the Companies Act, 2013 requires every company with net worth of 500 crore rupees or more, or turnover of 1,000 crore rupees or more, or net profit of 5 crore rupees or more, to spend at least 2 percent of its average net profits of the preceding three years on CSR activities listed in Schedule VII. Unspent amounts must be transferred to specified funds.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 only."},{"i":"2024-51","y":2024,"n":51,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: If the United States of America (USA) were to default on its debt, holders of US Treasury Bonds will not be able to exercise their claims to receive payment.\nStatement II: The USA Government debt is not backed by any hard assets, but only by the faith of the Government.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I","Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct (as per key)\nIf a sovereign government like the USA defaults, bondholders have no specific assets they can claim or seize. Unlike creditors of a company, they cannot take the government to bankruptcy proceedings or attach its property to recover their money. They can only wait for the government to pay or negotiate a restructuring.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nUS Treasury securities are backed only by the 'full faith and credit' of the US Government, meaning its power to tax and its credibility, not by any hard asset such as gold or property. Since the US left the gold standard (1971), no physical asset stands behind its debt.\n\nBecause there are no hard assets backing the debt, bondholders would have no assets to claim in a default. So Statement II explains Statement I.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both statements are correct and Statement II explains Statement I."},{"i":"2024-53","y":2024,"n":53,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of the digital rupee:\n1. It is a sovereign currency issued by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) in alignment with its monetary policy.\n2. It appears as a liability on the RBI's balance sheet.\n3. It is insured against inflation by its very design.\n4. It is freely convertible against commercial bank money and cash.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 4 only","1, 2 and 4"],"a":3,"x":"The Digital Rupee (e-Rupee) is India's Central Bank Digital Currency, piloted by the RBI from November 2022 (wholesale) and December 2022 (retail).\n\n1. It is sovereign currency issued by the RBI in alignment with its monetary policy - Correct\nThe e-Rupee is legal tender issued by the RBI, just like banknotes, and its issue is consistent with the RBI's monetary policy framework.\n\n2. It appears as a liability on the RBI's balance sheet - Correct\nLike currency notes in circulation, the e-Rupee is a direct liability of the RBI.\n\n3. It is insured against inflation by its very design - Incorrect\nThe e-Rupee is simply a digital form of the rupee. Its purchasing power falls with inflation exactly like cash. It does not pay interest and has no in-built inflation protection.\n\n4. It is freely convertible against commercial bank money and cash - Correct\nUsers can convert e-Rupee to bank deposits or cash and vice versa at par (one to one).\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1, 2 and 4."},{"i":"2024-54","y":2024,"n":54,"s":7,"q":"With reference to ancient India, Gautama Buddha was generally known by which of the following epithets?\n1. Nayaputta\n2. Shakyamuni\n3. Tathagata\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3","None of the above are epithets of Gautama Buddha"],"a":1,"x":"1. Nayaputta - Not an epithet of the Buddha\nNayaputta (Nataputta, 'son of the Naya or Jnatri clan') refers to Vardhamana Mahavira, the 24th Jain Tirthankara. Buddhist texts call him Nigantha Nataputta.\n\n2. Shakyamuni - Epithet of the Buddha\nIt means 'sage of the Shakyas', since Gautama was born into the Shakya clan of Kapilavastu.\n\n3. Tathagata - Epithet of the Buddha\nIt means 'one who has thus gone' or 'thus come'. The Buddha used this term to refer to himself in Pali texts.\n\n(Other epithets include Siddhartha, Sugata, Bhagavat and Jina.)\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2024-55","y":2024,"n":55,"s":7,"q":"Consider the following information:\n| | Archaeological Site | State | Description\n| 1. | Chandraketugarh | Odisha | Trading Port town\n| 2. | Inamgaon | Maharashtra | Chalcolithic site\n| 3. | Mangadu | Kerala | Megalithic site\n| 4. | Salihundam | Andhra Pradesh | Rock-cut cave shrines\nIn which of the above rows is the given information correctly matched?","o":["1 and 2","2 and 3","3 and 4","1 and 4"],"a":1,"x":"Row 1: Chandraketugarh - Odisha - Trading port town - Incorrect\nChandraketugarh is an important early historic (Maurya to Gupta period) trading and port town known for terracotta plaques, but it is in North 24 Parganas district of West Bengal, not Odisha.\n\nRow 2: Inamgaon - Maharashtra - Chalcolithic site - Correct\nInamgaon, on the Ghod river in Pune district, is a large Chalcolithic settlement (Malwa and Jorwe cultures, c. 1600-700 BCE), known for evidence of irrigation, granaries and a chief's house.\n\nRow 3: Mangadu - Kerala - Megalithic site - Correct\nMangadu, in Kollam district, is an Iron Age megalithic burial and habitation site excavated in Kerala.\n\nRow 4: Salihundam - Andhra Pradesh - Rock-cut cave shrines - Incorrect\nSalihundam in Srikakulam district is a Buddhist site with brick stupas, chaityas and monasteries on a hill beside the Vamsadhara river, not rock-cut cave shrines.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3."},{"i":"2024-56","y":2024,"n":56,"s":8,"q":"Who of the following rulers of medieval India gave permission to the Portuguese to build a fort at Bhatkal?","o":["Krishnadevaraya","Narasimha Saluva","Muhammad Shah III","Yusuf Adil Shah"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Krishnadevaraya - Correct\nKrishnadevaraya of Vijayanagara (1509-1529) maintained friendly relations with the Portuguese. He needed the Arabian and Persian horses they supplied through the western ports and saw them as allies against the Bijapur Sultanate. After Albuquerque captured Goa (1510), Krishnadevaraya permitted the Portuguese to build a fort at Bhatkal on the Kanara coast.\n\n(b) Narasimha Saluva - Incorrect\nHe founded the Saluva dynasty of Vijayanagara in the late 15th century, before the Portuguese arrived in India (1498) and set up forts.\n\n(c) Muhammad Shah III - Incorrect\nHe was a Bahmani Sultan (1463-1482), known for the execution of his minister Mahmud Gawan.\n\n(d) Yusuf Adil Shah - Incorrect\nHe founded the Bijapur Sultanate; Goa was captured from him by the Portuguese. He did not give them permission for a fort at Bhatkal."},{"i":"2024-58","y":2024,"n":58,"s":7,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. There are no parables in Upanishads.\n2. Upanishads were composed earlier than the Puranas.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":1,"x":"1. There are no parables in the Upanishads - Incorrect\nThe Upanishads use many parables, stories and dialogues to teach philosophical ideas. Examples include the story of Nachiketa and Yama (Katha Upanishad), Satyakama Jabala and the teachings of Uddalaka Aruni to his son Svetaketu with the salt-in-water parable (Chandogya Upanishad), and the two birds on one tree (Mundaka Upanishad).\n\n2. The Upanishads were composed earlier than the Puranas - Correct\nThe principal Upanishads were composed roughly between 800 and 300 BCE, at the end of the Vedic period (hence 'Vedanta'). The Puranas were compiled in their present form much later, mostly during the Gupta and post-Gupta period (about 300-1000 CE), although they contain older traditions.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2024-59","y":2024,"n":59,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. India is a member of the International Grains Council.\n2. A country needs to be a member of the International Grains Council for exporting or importing rice and wheat.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":0,"x":"1. India is a member of the International Grains Council - Correct\nThe International Grains Council, based in London, is an intergovernmental organisation that administers the Grains Trade Convention, 1995. It promotes cooperation in trade of wheat, maize, barley, rice, oilseeds and pulses, and publishes market information. India is a member.\n\n2. A country must be a member of the IGC to export or import rice and wheat - Incorrect\nIGC membership is voluntary and is not a condition for international grain trade. Many trading countries are not members, and trade rules are governed by the WTO and bilateral agreements, not by the IGC.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 only."},{"i":"2024-60","y":2024,"n":60,"s":10,"q":"Which one of the following was the latest inclusion in the Intangible Cultural Heritage List of UNESCO?","o":["Chhau dance","Durga puja","Garba dance","Kumbh mela"],"a":2,"x":"The question asks for the latest inclusion among the options in UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity.\n\n(a) Chhau dance - Incorrect\nThe Chhau dance of Odisha, Jharkhand and West Bengal was inscribed in 2010.\n\n(b) Durga Puja - Incorrect\nDurga Puja in Kolkata was inscribed in December 2021.\n\n(c) Garba dance - Correct\nThe Garba of Gujarat was inscribed in December 2023, making it India's 15th element on the list and the latest among the options.\n\n(d) Kumbh Mela - Incorrect\nThe Kumbh Mela was inscribed in 2017."},{"i":"2024-61","y":2024,"n":61,"s":0,"q":"Who was the Provisional President of the Constituent Assembly before Dr. Rajendra Prasad took over?","o":["C. Rajagopalachari","Dr. B.R. Ambedkar","T.T. Krishnamachari","Dr. Sachchidananda Sinha"],"a":3,"x":"(a) C. Rajagopalachari - Incorrect\nRajagopalachari was a member of the Constituent Assembly and later became the first (and last) Indian Governor-General (1948-1950), but he was not the provisional President of the Assembly.\n\n(b) Dr. B.R. Ambedkar - Incorrect\nDr. Ambedkar was the Chairman of the Drafting Committee.\n\n(c) T.T. Krishnamachari - Incorrect\nHe was a member of the Drafting Committee and later Finance Minister.\n\n(d) Dr. Sachchidananda Sinha - Correct\nAt the first meeting of the Constituent Assembly on 9 December 1946, Dr. Sachchidananda Sinha, the oldest member, was elected as the temporary (provisional) President, following the French practice. Dr. Rajendra Prasad was elected the permanent President on 11 December 1946."},{"i":"2024-62","y":2024,"n":62,"s":9,"q":"With reference to the Government of India Act, 1935, consider the following statements:\n1. It provided for the establishment of an All India Federation based on the union of the British Indian Provinces and Princely States.\n2. Defence and Foreign Affairs were kept under the control of the federal legislature.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":0,"x":"1. It provided for an All-India Federation of British Indian Provinces and Princely States - Correct\nThe Government of India Act, 1935 provided for an All-India Federation consisting of the provinces of British India and the princely states that chose to join. The federation never came into being because the required number of princes did not accede. Provincial autonomy, however, was implemented in 1937.\n\n2. Defence and Foreign Affairs were kept under the control of the federal legislature - Incorrect\nThe Act introduced dyarchy at the Centre. Federal subjects were divided into 'reserved' and 'transferred' subjects. Defence, foreign affairs, ecclesiastical affairs and tribal areas were reserved subjects, administered by the Governor-General with the help of counsellors, who were not responsible to the federal legislature.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 only."},{"i":"2024-63","y":2024,"n":63,"s":10,"q":"Which one of the following is a work attributed to playwright Bhasa?","o":["Kavyalankara","Natyashastra","Madhyama-vyayoga","Mahabhashya"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Kavyalankara - Incorrect\nKavyalankara is a work on poetics (figures of speech) by Bhamaha, 6th-7th century CE.\n\n(b) Natyashastra - Incorrect\nThe Natyashastra, the classic treatise on drama, dance and music, is attributed to Bharata Muni.\n\n(c) Madhyama-vyayoga - Correct\nMadhyama-vyayoga is a one-act play by Bhasa, based on an episode from the Mahabharata involving Bhima and Ghatotkacha. Bhasa's thirteen plays were rediscovered in Kerala by T. Ganapati Sastri in 1912 and include Svapnavasavadatta, Pratijna Yaugandharayana, Urubhanga, Karnabhara, Dutavakya and Charudatta.\n\n(d) Mahabhashya - Incorrect\nThe Mahabhashya is Patanjali's great commentary (2nd century BCE) on Panini's grammar."},{"i":"2024-64","y":2024,"n":64,"s":7,"q":"Sanghabhuti, an Indian Buddhist monk, who travelled to China at the end of the fourth century AD, was the author of a commentary on:","o":["Prajnaparamita Sutra","Visuddhimagga","Sarvastivada Vinaya","Lalitavistara"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Prajnaparamita Sutra - Incorrect\nThe Prajnaparamita ('Perfection of Wisdom') sutras are early Mahayana texts; commentaries are attributed to Nagarjuna and others, not Sanghabhuti.\n\n(b) Visuddhimagga - Incorrect\nThe Visuddhimagga ('Path of Purification') is a Theravada text written by Buddhaghosa in Sri Lanka in the 5th century CE; it is not a commentary by Sanghabhuti.\n\n(c) Sarvastivada Vinaya - Correct\nSanghabhuti was a Buddhist monk from Kashmir who reached China (Chang'an) around 381-383 CE. According to Upinder Singh's history of ancient India, he wrote a commentary on the Sarvastivada Vinaya, the monastic rules of the Sarvastivada school that was strong in Kashmir, and helped translate Buddhist texts into Chinese.\n\n(d) Lalitavistara - Incorrect\nThe Lalitavistara is a Mahayana biography of the Buddha, not the subject of Sanghabhuti's commentary."},{"i":"2024-65","y":2024,"n":65,"s":10,"q":"Consider the following properties included in the World Heritage List released by UNESCO:\n1. Shantiniketan\n2. Rani-ki-Vav\n3. Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas\n4. Mahabodhi Temple Complex at Bodhgaya\nHow many of the above properties were included in 2023?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":1,"x":"1. Shantiniketan - Included in 2023\nSantiniketan in West Bengal, founded by Rabindranath Tagore's father and developed by Tagore into Visva-Bharati University, was inscribed in September 2023 at the 45th session of the World Heritage Committee in Riyadh.\n\n2. Rani-ki-Vav - Not included in 2023\nThe Rani-ki-Vav stepwell at Patan, Gujarat, built in the 11th century in memory of the Solanki king Bhimdev I, was inscribed in 2014.\n\n3. Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas - Included in 2023\nThe Hoysala temples at Belur, Halebidu and Somanathapura in Karnataka were inscribed in September 2023, making India's total 42 at that time.\n\n4. Mahabodhi Temple Complex at Bodhgaya - Not included in 2023\nIt was inscribed in 2002.\n\nTwo were inscribed in 2023, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2024-66","y":2024,"n":66,"s":0,"q":"As per Article 368 of the Constitution of India, the Parliament may amend any provision of the Constitution by way of:\n1. Addition\n2. Variation\n3. Repeal\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"Article 368(1), inserted by the 24th Amendment Act (1971), states: 'Notwithstanding anything in this Constitution, Parliament may in exercise of its constituent power amend by way of addition, variation or repeal any provision of this Constitution in accordance with the procedure laid down in this article.'\n\n1. Addition - Correct\nParliament can add new provisions, such as new Articles or Schedules (e.g., Article 21A, the Tenth Schedule).\n\n2. Variation - Correct\nParliament can change existing provisions (e.g., the change in voting age from 21 to 18 by the 61st Amendment).\n\n3. Repeal - Correct\nParliament can delete provisions (e.g., the Right to Property, Article 31, was repealed by the 44th Amendment).\n\nThis power is subject to the basic structure doctrine laid down in Kesavananda Bharati (1973).\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2024-67","y":2024,"n":67,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following countries:\n1. Italy\n2. Japan\n3. Nigeria\n4. South Korea\n5. South Africa\nWhich of the above countries are frequently mentioned in the media for their low birth rates, or ageing population or declining population?","o":["1, 2 and 4","1, 3 and 5","2 and 4 only","3 and 5 only"],"a":0,"x":"1. Italy - Correct\nItaly has one of the lowest birth rates in Europe (about 1.2 children per woman), a rapidly ageing population and a shrinking population, which is often in the news.\n\n2. Japan - Correct\nJapan has one of the oldest populations in the world, with nearly 30 percent aged 65 or above, and its population has been falling since about 2008.\n\n3. Nigeria - Incorrect\nNigeria has a high fertility rate (around 5 children per woman), a very young population and one of the fastest-growing populations in the world.\n\n4. South Korea - Correct\nSouth Korea has the world's lowest fertility rate, about 0.72 in 2023, and its population is ageing and declining.\n\n5. South Africa - Incorrect\nSouth Africa has a relatively young population and moderate fertility (about 2.3); it is not known for low birth rates or population decline.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1, 2 and 4."},{"i":"2024-68","y":2024,"n":68,"s":0,"q":"Which of the following statements are correct in respect of a Money Bill in the Parliament?\n1. Article 109 mentions special procedure in respect of Money Bills.\n2. A Money Bill shall not be introduced in the Council of States.\n3. The Rajya Sabha can either approve the Bill or suggest changes but cannot reject it.\n4. Amendments to a Money Bill suggested by the Rajya Sabha have to be accepted by the Lok Sabha.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3","1, 3 and 4"],"a":2,"x":"1. Article 109 mentions the special procedure in respect of Money Bills - Correct\nArticle 110 defines a Money Bill, and Article 109 lays down the special procedure for passing it.\n\n2. A Money Bill shall not be introduced in the Council of States - Correct\nUnder Article 109(1), a Money Bill can be introduced only in the Lok Sabha, and only on the recommendation of the President (Article 117). The Speaker's certificate decides whether a bill is a Money Bill.\n\n3. The Rajya Sabha can approve the Bill or suggest changes but cannot reject it - Correct\nThe Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend a Money Bill. It must return it within 14 days, with or without recommendations. If it does not return it within 14 days, the bill is deemed passed by both Houses.\n\n4. Amendments suggested by the Rajya Sabha have to be accepted by the Lok Sabha - Incorrect\nThe Lok Sabha may accept or reject all or any of the Rajya Sabha's recommendations. If it rejects them, the bill is deemed passed in the form passed by the Lok Sabha.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2024-69","y":2024,"n":69,"s":12,"q":"Which of the following is/are correctly matched in terms of equivalent rank in the three services of Indian Defence forces?\n| | Army | Airforce | Navy\n| 1. | Brigadier | Air Commodore | Commander\n| 2. | Major General | Air Vice Marshal | Vice Admiral\n| 3. | Major | Squadron Leader | Lieutenant Commander\n| 4. | Lieutenant Colonel | Group Captain | Captain\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 4","1 and 3","2, 3 and 4","3 only"],"a":3,"x":"The equivalent ranks in the Army, Air Force and Navy are:\nColonel = Group Captain = Captain\nLieutenant Colonel = Wing Commander = Commander\nBrigadier = Air Commodore = Commodore\nMajor General = Air Vice Marshal = Rear Admiral\nLieutenant General = Air Marshal = Vice Admiral\n\n1. Brigadier - Air Commodore - Commander - Incorrect\nThe naval equivalent of Brigadier is Commodore, not Commander.\n\n2. Major General - Air Vice Marshal - Vice Admiral - Incorrect\nThe naval equivalent of Major General is Rear Admiral. Vice Admiral equals Lieutenant General and Air Marshal.\n\n3. Major - Squadron Leader - Lieutenant Commander - Correct\nThese are equivalent ranks.\n\n4. Lieutenant Colonel - Group Captain - Captain - Incorrect\nLieutenant Colonel equals Wing Commander and Commander. Group Captain and Captain (Navy) equal Colonel.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 3 only."},{"i":"2024-70","y":2024,"n":70,"s":0,"q":"The North Eastern Council (NEC) was established by the North Eastern Council Act, 1971. Subsequent to the amendment of NEC Act in 2002, the Council comprises which of the following members?\n1. Governor of the Constituent State\n2. Chief Minister of the Constituent State\n3. Three Members to be nominated by the President of India\n4. The Home Minister of India\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2 and 3 only","1, 3 and 4 only","2 and 4 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"a":0,"x":"The North Eastern Council was set up under the North Eastern Council Act, 1971 as an advisory body. The 2002 amendment made it a regional planning body and added Sikkim.\n\n1. Governor of the constituent State - Member\nThe Governors of all eight member States are members.\n\n2. Chief Minister of the constituent State - Member\nThe Chief Ministers of all eight States are members.\n\n3. Three members nominated by the President - Member\nThe President nominates three members.\n\n4. The Home Minister of India - Not a member under the amended Act\nIn 2018 the Union Cabinet decided that the Union Home Minister would be the ex-officio Chairman and the Minister of DoNER the Vice-Chairman. This was an executive decision; the Home Minister is not part of the composition laid down by the 2002 amendment.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1, 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2024-71","y":2024,"n":71,"s":0,"q":"How many Delimitation Commissions have been constituted by the Government of India till December 2023?","o":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"a":3,"x":"A Delimitation Commission redraws the boundaries of Lok Sabha and Assembly constituencies based on the latest census, under Articles 82 and 170. Its orders cannot be challenged in court.\n\nDelimitation Commissions have been set up four times, under the Delimitation Commission Acts of:\n1952 (based on the 1951 Census),\n1962 (based on the 1961 Census),\n1972 (based on the 1971 Census), and\n2002 (based on the 2001 Census; chaired by Justice Kuldip Singh; it redrew boundaries without changing the number of seats, which remain frozen until the first census after 2026).\n\n(A separate commission for Jammu and Kashmir, 2020-22, was set up under the J&K Reorganisation Act, not a general Delimitation Commission.)\n\n(a) One, (b) Two and (c) Three - Incorrect.\n(d) Four - Correct."},{"i":"2024-72","y":2024,"n":72,"s":0,"q":"The Constitution (71st Amendment) Act, 1992 amends the Eighth Schedule to the Constitution to include which of the following languages?\n1. Konkani\n2. Manipuri\n3. Nepali\n4. Maithili\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2 and 4","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"a":0,"x":"1. Konkani - Added by the 71st Amendment\nKonkani, spoken mainly in Goa and coastal Karnataka, was added in 1992.\n\n2. Manipuri - Added by the 71st Amendment\nManipuri (Meitei) of Manipur was added in 1992.\n\n3. Nepali - Added by the 71st Amendment\nNepali, spoken in Sikkim, Darjeeling and parts of the North-east, was added in 1992.\n\n4. Maithili - Not added by the 71st Amendment\nMaithili was added by the 92nd Amendment Act, 2003, along with Bodo, Dogri and Santhali. This brought the total to 22 languages.\n\n(Sindhi was added by the 21st Amendment, 1967.)\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2024-73","y":2024,"n":73,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Party | Its Leader\n| 1. | Bharatiya Jana Sangh | Dr. Shyama Prasad Mukherjee\n| 2. | Socialist Party | C. Rajagopalachari\n| 3. | Congress for Democracy | Jagjivan Ram\n| 4. | Swatantra Party | Acharya Narendra Dev\nHow many of the above are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":1,"x":"1. Bharatiya Jana Sangh : Dr. Syama Prasad Mookerjee - Correct\nMookerjee founded the Bharatiya Jana Sangh in 1951 after resigning from Nehru's cabinet.\n\n2. Socialist Party : C. Rajagopalachari - Incorrect\nThe Socialist Party (formed after the Congress Socialist Party left the Congress in 1948) was led by Acharya Narendra Dev, Jayaprakash Narayan and Ram Manohar Lohia.\n\n3. Congress for Democracy : Jagjivan Ram - Correct\nJagjivan Ram left the Congress in 1977 and formed the Congress for Democracy with H.N. Bahuguna and Nandini Satpathy, which then joined the Janata Party alliance.\n\n4. Swatantra Party : Acharya Narendra Dev - Incorrect\nThe Swatantra Party (1959) was founded by C. Rajagopalachari, with Minoo Masani and N.G. Ranga, to promote a free-market economy.\n\n(Pairs 2 and 4 have swapped leaders.)\n\nTwo pairs are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2024-74","y":2024,"n":74,"s":0,"q":"Which of the following statements are correct about the Constitution of India?\n1. Powers of the Municipalities are given in Part IX A of the Constitution.\n2. Emergency provisions are given in Part XVIII of the Constitution.\n3. Provisions related to the amendment of the Constitution are given in Part XX of the Constitution.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"1. Powers of the Municipalities are given in Part IX A - Correct\nPart IXA (Articles 243P to 243ZG) was added by the 74th Amendment Act, 1992. Article 243W deals with the powers, authority and responsibilities of municipalities, and the Twelfth Schedule lists 18 functional items.\n\n2. Emergency provisions are given in Part XVIII - Correct\nPart XVIII (Articles 352 to 360) deals with National Emergency (352), President's Rule (356) and Financial Emergency (360).\n\n3. Provisions for amendment of the Constitution are in Part XX - Correct\nPart XX contains only Article 368, which deals with the power of Parliament to amend the Constitution and its procedure.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2024-75","y":2024,"n":75,"s":0,"q":"Which one of the following statements is correct as per the Constitution of India?","o":["Inter-State trade and commerce is a State subject under the State List.","Inter-State migration is a State subject under the State List.","Inter-State quarantine is a Union subject under the Union List.","Corporation tax is a State subject under the State List."],"a":2,"x":"(a) Inter-State trade and commerce is a State subject - Incorrect\nInter-State trade and commerce is Entry 42 of the Union List. (Trade and commerce within a State is Entry 26 of the State List.)\n\n(b) Inter-State migration is a State subject - Incorrect\nInter-State migration is in Entry 81 of the Union List.\n\n(c) Inter-State quarantine is a Union subject - Correct\nEntry 81 of the Union List reads 'Inter-State migration; inter-State quarantine'. This was relevant during the COVID-19 pandemic, when the Centre issued guidelines on movement between States.\n\n(d) Corporation tax is a State subject - Incorrect\nCorporation tax is Entry 85 of the Union List; it is levied and collected by the Centre and shared with States through the divisible pool."},{"i":"2024-76","y":2024,"n":76,"s":0,"q":"Under which of the following Articles of the Constitution of India, has the Supreme Court of India placed the Right to Privacy?","o":["Article 15","Article 16","Article 19","Article 21"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Article 15 - Incorrect\nArticle 15 prohibits discrimination on grounds of religion, race, caste, sex or place of birth.\n\n(b) Article 16 - Incorrect\nArticle 16 guarantees equality of opportunity in public employment.\n\n(c) Article 19 - Incorrect\nArticle 19 guarantees six freedoms, such as speech and movement. Privacy is linked to some of these freedoms, but the Court did not place it primarily under Article 19.\n\n(d) Article 21 - Correct\nIn Justice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.) v. Union of India (August 2017), a nine-judge bench of the Supreme Court unanimously held that the right to privacy is a fundamental right. It is intrinsic to the right to life and personal liberty under Article 21, and is also part of the freedoms guaranteed in Part III. The judgment overruled M.P. Sharma (1954) and Kharak Singh (1962), and later influenced decisions on Aadhaar, Section 377 and data protection."},{"i":"2024-77","y":2024,"n":77,"s":0,"q":"What are the duties of the Chief of Defence Staff (CDS) as Head of the Department of Military Affairs?\n1. Permanent Chairman of Chiefs of Staff Committee\n2. Exercise military command over the three Service Chiefs\n3. Principal Military Advisor to Defence Minister on all tri-service matters\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"a":3,"x":"The post of Chief of Defence Staff was created in December 2019 on the Kargil Review Committee's and Shekatkar Committee's recommendations; General Bipin Rawat was the first CDS. The CDS also heads the Department of Military Affairs in the Ministry of Defence.\n\n1. Permanent Chairman of the Chiefs of Staff Committee - Correct\nEarlier, the senior-most Service Chief chaired the committee by rotation. The CDS now permanently chairs it.\n\n2. Exercise military command over the three Service Chiefs - Incorrect\nThe CDS does not command the Service Chiefs or the forces. The Army, Navy and Air Force Chiefs continue to command their services. The CDS is a 'first among equals' and focuses on jointness and integration, such as creating theatre commands.\n\n3. Principal Military Advisor to the Defence Minister on all tri-service matters - Correct\nThe CDS advises the Defence Minister on tri-service matters, while the Service Chiefs advise on matters exclusively concerning their services. The CDS is also military adviser to the Nuclear Command Authority.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1 and 3 only."},{"i":"2024-78","y":2024,"n":78,"s":12,"q":"Operations undertaken by the Army towards upliftment of the local population in remote areas to include addressing of their basic needs is called:","o":["Operation Sankalp","Operation Maitri","Operation Sadbhavana","Operation Madad"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Operation Sankalp - Incorrect\nOperation Sankalp is the Indian Navy's deployment (since 2019) to ensure the safe passage of Indian-flagged ships in the Persian Gulf and Gulf of Oman, and later to counter piracy and attacks in the Arabian Sea.\n\n(b) Operation Maitri - Incorrect\nOperation Maitri was India's rescue and relief operation after the 2015 Nepal earthquake (an operation of the same name was also used earlier for other relief missions).\n\n(c) Operation Sadbhavana - Correct\nOperation Sadbhavana, started in 1998, is the Indian Army's civic action programme in Jammu and Kashmir, Ladakh and the North-east. The Army runs Army Goodwill Schools, medical camps, women's empowerment and vocational training centres, builds infrastructure and organises educational tours, to address basic needs of people in remote areas and win their confidence.\n\n(d) Operation Madad - Incorrect\nOperation Madad was the Indian Navy's rescue and relief operation during the 2018 Kerala floods."},{"i":"2024-79","y":2024,"n":79,"s":11,"q":"The longest border between any two countries in the world is between:","o":["Canada and the United States of America","Chile and Argentina","China and India","Kazakhstan and Russian Federation"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Canada and the United States of America - Correct\nThe Canada-USA border is about 8,891 km long, including about 2,475 km between Alaska and Canada. It is the longest international border in the world and is often called the world's longest undefended border.\n\n(b) Chile and Argentina - Incorrect\nTheir border along the Andes is about 5,300 km, the third longest in the world.\n\n(c) China and India - Incorrect\nThe India-China border is about 3,488 km long (as per India).\n\n(d) Kazakhstan and Russian Federation - Incorrect\nTheir border is about 7,600 km, the longest continuous land border and second longest overall, but shorter than the Canada-USA border."},{"i":"2024-80","y":2024,"n":80,"s":0,"q":"Which of the following statements about the Ethics Committee in the Lok Sabha are correct?\n1. Initially it was an ad-hoc Committee.\n2. Only a Member of the Lok Sabha can make a complaint relating to unethical conduct of a member of the Lok Sabha.\n3. This Committee cannot take up any matter which is sub-judice.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":2,"x":"1. Initially it was an ad-hoc committee - Correct\nThe Ethics Committee of the Lok Sabha was first set up in 2000 as an ad-hoc committee. It became a permanent part of the Rules of Procedure in 2015. (The Rajya Sabha Ethics Committee was set up in 1997.)\n\n2. Only a member of the Lok Sabha can make a complaint about unethical conduct of a member - Incorrect\nAny person can complain against a member, but the complaint must be sent through a member of the Lok Sabha and be accompanied by evidence and an affidavit. A member can complain directly. So complaints are not restricted to members alone.\n\n3. The Committee cannot take up any matter which is sub-judice - Correct\nThe Committee does not entertain complaints on matters pending before courts, nor complaints based only on unsubstantiated media reports.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1 and 3 only."},{"i":"2024-81","y":2024,"n":81,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding 'Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam':\n1. Provisions will come into effect from the 18th Lok Sabha.\n2. This will be in force for 15 years after becoming an Act.\n3. There are provisions for the reservation of seats for Scheduled Castes Women within the quota reserved for the Scheduled Castes.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only"],"a":2,"x":"The Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam is the Constitution (106th Amendment) Act, 2023. It reserves one-third of the seats in the Lok Sabha, State Legislative Assemblies and the Delhi Assembly for women.\n\n1. Provisions will come into effect from the 18th Lok Sabha - Incorrect\nThe reservation will come into effect only after the delimitation exercise based on the first census conducted after the commencement of the Act. Therefore it did not apply to the 18th Lok Sabha elected in 2024.\n\n2. It will be in force for 15 years after becoming an Act - Correct\nThe reservation will continue for 15 years from its commencement, and Parliament can extend it by law.\n\n3. There is reservation for Scheduled Caste women within the SC quota - Correct\nOne-third of the seats reserved for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes will be reserved for women of those groups. (There is no separate reservation for OBC women.)\n\nHence the answer is (c) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2024-82","y":2024,"n":82,"s":4,"q":"Which of the following statements about 'Exercise Mitra Shakti-2023' are correct?\n1. This was a joint military exercise between India and Bangladesh.\n2. It commenced in Aundh (Pune).\n3. Joint response during counter-terrorism operations was a goal of this operation.\n4. Indian Air Force was a part of this exercise.\nSelect the answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2 and 3","1, 2 and 4","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"a":3,"x":"1. It was a joint military exercise between India and Bangladesh - Incorrect\nMitra Shakti is the annual joint military exercise between India and Sri Lanka. The India-Bangladesh army exercise is called 'Sampriti'.\n\n2. It commenced in Aundh (Pune) - Correct\nThe 9th edition began at the Foreign Training Node, Aundh, Pune, in August 2023.\n\n3. Joint response during counter-terrorism operations was a goal - Correct\nThe exercise focused on joint sub-conventional operations, including counter-terrorism, under Chapter VII of the UN Charter (peacekeeping-type operations).\n\n4. The Indian Air Force was part of the exercise - Correct\nThe Indian Air Force and the Sri Lanka Air Force took part along with the armies.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 2, 3 and 4."},{"i":"2024-83","y":2024,"n":83,"s":0,"q":"A Writ of Prohibition is an order issued by the Supreme Court or High Courts to:","o":["a government officer prohibiting him from taking a particular action.","the Parliament/Legislative Assembly to pass a law on Prohibition.","the lower court prohibiting continuation of proceedings in a case.","the Government prohibiting it from following an unconstitutional policy."],"a":2,"x":"(a) A government officer prohibiting him from taking a particular action - Incorrect\nA writ of prohibition lies only against judicial and quasi-judicial authorities, not against administrative officers, legislative bodies or private persons. Directions to public officials to perform their duty are given by mandamus.\n\n(b) The Parliament or Assembly to pass a law on Prohibition - Incorrect\nCourts cannot direct legislatures to pass laws; 'prohibition' here has nothing to do with liquor prohibition.\n\n(c) The lower court prohibiting continuation of proceedings in a case - Correct\nProhibition (literally 'to forbid') is issued by a higher court (Supreme Court under Article 32 or High Court under Article 226) to a lower court or tribunal to stop it from exceeding its jurisdiction or acting without jurisdiction. It is preventive, issued while proceedings are pending. Certiorari, in contrast, is curative and quashes an order already passed.\n\n(d) The Government prohibiting it from following an unconstitutional policy - Incorrect\nUnconstitutional policies are struck down through judicial review, not through the writ of prohibition."},{"i":"2024-84","y":2024,"n":84,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. It is the Governor of the State who recognizes and declares any community of that State as a Scheduled Tribe.\n2. A community declared as a Scheduled Tribe in a State need not be so in another State.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":1,"x":"1. The Governor of the State recognises and declares any community as a Scheduled Tribe - Incorrect\nUnder Article 342(1), the President, after consultation with the Governor, specifies by public notification the communities that are Scheduled Tribes in a State or Union Territory. After the first notification, any inclusion or exclusion requires a law passed by Parliament (Article 342(2)), as with the Constitution (Scheduled Tribes) Order Amendment Acts. The Governor does not declare STs.\n\n2. A community declared as a Scheduled Tribe in one State need not be so in another - Correct\nST lists are State- and UT-specific. For example, a community may be listed as ST in one State but as SC, OBC or general in another. A person's ST status is valid only in the State where the community is notified.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2024-85","y":2024,"n":85,"s":0,"q":"With reference to Union Budget, consider the following statements:\n1. The Union Finance Minister on behalf of the Prime Minister lays the Annual Financial Statement before both the Houses of Parliament.\n2. At the Union level, no demand for a grant can be made except on the recommendation of the President of India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"1. The Union Finance Minister, on behalf of the Prime Minister, lays the Annual Financial Statement before both Houses - Incorrect (see Discrepancy)\nArticle 112 says the President 'shall cause to be laid' before both Houses the Annual Financial Statement (the Budget). The Finance Minister presents the Budget on behalf of the President, not the Prime Minister. In practice, the Finance Minister presents it in the Lok Sabha and it is laid in the Rajya Sabha, usually by the Minister of State for Finance.\n\n2. At the Union level, no demand for a grant can be made except on the recommendation of the President - Correct\nArticle 113(3) provides that no demand for a grant shall be made except on the recommendation of the President.\n\nDiscrepancy: The official key gives (c) Both 1 and 2, apparently treating the Finance Minister as acting for the government headed by the Prime Minister. However, under Article 112 the constitutional authority is the President, so statement 1 as worded is inaccurate. The correct answer should be (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2024-86","y":2024,"n":86,"s":12,"q":"Who of the following is the author of the books \"The India Way\" and \"Why Bharat Matters\"?","o":["Bhupender Yadav","Nalin Mehta","Shashi Tharoor","Subrahmanyam Jaishankar"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Bhupender Yadav - Incorrect\nThe Union Environment Minister co-authored 'The Rise of the BJP', not these books.\n\n(b) Nalin Mehta - Incorrect\nNalin Mehta wrote 'The New BJP' and books on television and sport.\n\n(c) Shashi Tharoor - Incorrect\nTharoor's books include 'An Era of Darkness', 'Why I Am a Hindu', 'Pax Indica' and 'The Great Indian Novel'.\n\n(d) Subrahmanyam Jaishankar - Correct\nExternal Affairs Minister Dr. S. Jaishankar wrote 'The India Way: Strategies for an Uncertain World' (2020), on India's foreign policy in a changing world order, and 'Why Bharat Matters' (2024), linking foreign policy with India's civilisational identity and development."},{"i":"2024-87","y":2024,"n":87,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Country | Reason for being in the news\n| 1. | Argentina | Worst economic crisis\n| 2. | Sudan | War between the country's regular army and paramilitary forces\n| 3. | Turkey | Rescinded its membership of NATO\nHow many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","o":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","All three pairs","None of the pairs"],"a":1,"x":"1. Argentina : Worst economic crisis - Correct\nIn 2023 Argentina faced its worst economic crisis in decades, with inflation above 200 percent, a collapsing peso and poverty over 40 percent. This led to the election of Javier Milei, who promised radical reforms.\n\n2. Sudan : War between the regular army and paramilitary forces - Correct\nSince April 2023, Sudan has been in a civil war between the Sudanese Armed Forces, led by General Abdel Fattah al-Burhan, and the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces, led by General Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo ('Hemedti'), causing a massive humanitarian crisis.\n\n3. Turkey : Rescinded its NATO membership - Incorrect\nTurkey remains a NATO member (since 1952). It was in the news for delaying and then approving the NATO membership of Finland (2023) and Sweden (2024).\n\nTwo pairs are correctly matched, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2024-88","y":2024,"n":88,"s":11,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Sumed pipeline is a strategic route for Persian Gulf oil and natural gas shipments to Europe.\nStatement II: Sumed pipeline connects the Red Sea with the Mediterranean Sea.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I","Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nThe SUMED (Suez-Mediterranean) pipeline in Egypt is a strategic route for moving Persian Gulf crude oil to Europe. Very large tankers that are too big to pass through the Suez Canal fully loaded unload part of their cargo into the pipeline and pick it up again at the other end. It carries about 2.5 million barrels per day and also provides an alternative if the canal is blocked.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nThe pipeline runs about 320 km from Ain Sukhna on the Gulf of Suez (Red Sea) to Sidi Kerir near Alexandria on the Mediterranean Sea. Because it connects these two seas, it serves as a route for Gulf oil to reach European markets.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both statements are correct and Statement II explains Statement I."},{"i":"2024-89","y":2024,"n":89,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The Red Sea receives very little precipitation in any form.\n2. No water enters the Red Sea from rivers.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"1. The Red Sea receives very little precipitation in any form - Correct\nThe Red Sea lies in a hot, arid belt between the deserts of Arabia and North-east Africa. It receives only a few centimetres of rain a year, while evaporation is very high (about 2 metres a year). This is one reason it is one of the saltiest seas in the world (about 40 parts per thousand).\n\n2. No water enters the Red Sea from rivers - Correct\nNo permanent river flows into the Red Sea. The water lost through evaporation is replaced by inflow from the Gulf of Aden and the Indian Ocean through the narrow strait of Bab-el-Mandeb.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2024-90","y":2024,"n":90,"s":2,"q":"According to the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), which one of the following is the largest source of sulphur dioxide emissions?","o":["Locomotives using fossil fuels","Ships using fossil fuels","Extraction of metals from ores","Power plants using fossil fuels"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Locomotives using fossil fuels - Incorrect\nDiesel locomotives emit some sulphur dioxide, but they are a small source, and low-sulphur fuels have reduced this further.\n\n(b) Ships using fossil fuels - Incorrect\nShips burning high-sulphur heavy fuel oil are a significant source, especially near ports, but they are not the largest. The IMO capped the sulphur content of marine fuel at 0.5 percent from 2020.\n\n(c) Extraction of metals from ores - Incorrect\nSmelting of sulphide ores (for example copper, zinc and lead) releases SO2 and is a notable industrial source, but it is smaller than power generation.\n\n(d) Power plants using fossil fuels - Correct\nAccording to the US Environmental Protection Agency, the largest source of sulphur dioxide in the atmosphere is the burning of fossil fuels, especially coal, by power plants and other industrial facilities. Coal contains sulphur, which forms SO2 when burnt. In India, flue gas desulphurisation units are being installed in coal plants to control this."},{"i":"2024-91","y":2024,"n":91,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: There is instability and worsening security situation in the Sahel region.\nStatement II: There have been military takeovers/coups d'état in several countries of the Sahel region in the recent past.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I","Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nThe Sahel, a semi-arid belt south of the Sahara (including Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger, Chad and Mauritania), faces worsening instability. Jihadist groups linked to al-Qaeda and the Islamic State have expanded, millions have been displaced, and there is a severe humanitarian and food crisis.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nSeveral Sahel countries have seen military coups in recent years: Mali (2020 and 2021), Burkina Faso (two in 2022), Niger (2023) and Chad (2021). The military governments expelled French forces, ended the UN mission in Mali (MINUSMA) and left the regional bloc ECOWAS, forming the Alliance of Sahel States. The coups weakened governance and security cooperation, which has worsened instability.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both statements are correct and Statement II explains Statement I."},{"i":"2024-92","y":2024,"n":92,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: India does not import apples from the United States of America.\nStatement II: In India, the law prohibits the import of Genetically Modified food without the approval of the competent authority.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I","Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"],"a":3,"x":"Statement I - Incorrect\nIndia does import apples from the United States, especially Washington apples. In 2019 India imposed an extra 20 percent retaliatory duty on US apples in response to US steel and aluminium tariffs, but it removed this duty in 2023 after the two countries settled their WTO disputes. Imports continue.\n\nStatement II - Correct\nUnder the Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 (Section 22), no genetically modified food can be manufactured, sold or imported without the approval of the Food Safety and Standards Authority of India. Approval of the Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (under the Environment Protection Act rules) is also required for GM organisms. FSSAI requires GM-free certificates for certain imported food crops.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is correct."},{"i":"2024-93","y":2024,"n":93,"s":0,"q":"With reference to the Speaker of the Lok Sabha, consider the following statements:\nWhile any resolution for the removal of the Speaker of the Lok Sabha is under consideration\n1. He/She shall not preside.\n2. He/She shall not have the right to speak.\n3. He/She shall not be entitled to vote on the resolution in the first instance.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"x":"Article 96 deals with the position of the Speaker while a resolution for his or her removal is under consideration.\n\n1. The Speaker shall not preside - Correct\nArticle 96(1) states that the Speaker (or Deputy Speaker, if the resolution is against him) shall not preside while the removal resolution is being considered, even if present.\n\n2. The Speaker shall not have the right to speak - Incorrect\nArticle 96(2) gives the Speaker the right to speak in, and otherwise take part in, the proceedings of the House during the consideration of the resolution.\n\n3. The Speaker shall not be entitled to vote on the resolution in the first instance - Incorrect\nArticle 96(2) says the Speaker is entitled to vote only in the first instance on the resolution, but not in the case of an equality of votes (no casting vote). So the Speaker can vote in the first instance.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 only."},{"i":"2024-94","y":2024,"n":94,"s":0,"q":"With reference to the Indian Parliament, consider the following statements:\n1. A bill pending in the Lok Sabha lapses on its dissolution.\n2. A bill passed by the Lok Sabha and pending in the Rajya Sabha lapses on the dissolution of the Lok Sabha.\n3. A bill in regard to which the President of India notified his/her intention to summon the Houses to a joint sitting lapses on the dissolution of the Lok Sabha.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. A bill pending in the Lok Sabha lapses on its dissolution - Correct\nAny bill pending in the Lok Sabha, whether it originated there or was transmitted by the Rajya Sabha, lapses when the Lok Sabha is dissolved.\n\n2. A bill passed by the Lok Sabha and pending in the Rajya Sabha lapses on dissolution of the Lok Sabha - Correct\nSuch bills also lapse and must be introduced afresh in the new Lok Sabha. (However, a bill that originated in the Rajya Sabha and has not yet been passed by the Lok Sabha does not lapse.)\n\n3. A bill for which the President has notified the intention to summon a joint sitting lapses on dissolution - Incorrect\nUnder Article 108(5), if the President has notified the intention to hold a joint sitting before the Lok Sabha is dissolved, the bill does not lapse, and the joint sitting can be held even after dissolution.\n\n(Bills passed by both Houses and pending for President's assent, or returned by the President for reconsideration, also do not lapse.)\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 2."},{"i":"2024-95","y":2024,"n":95,"s":0,"q":"With reference to the Parliament of India, consider the following statements:\n1. Prorogation of a House by the President of India does not require the advice of the Council of Ministers.\n2. Prorogation of a House is generally done after the House is adjourned sine die but there is no bar to the President of India prorogating the House which is in session.\n3. Dissolution of the Lok Sabha is done by the President of India who, save in exceptional circumstances, does so on the advice of the Council of Ministers.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","1 and 2","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":2,"x":"1. Prorogation by the President does not require the advice of the Council of Ministers - Incorrect\nUnder Article 85(2)(a), the President prorogues the Houses. Like other executive functions, this is done on the advice of the Council of Ministers under Article 74. Prorogation ends a session.\n\n2. Prorogation is generally done after adjournment sine die, but there is no bar to proroguing a House in session - Correct\nNormally the presiding officer adjourns the House sine die at the end of business, and prorogation follows. However, the Constitution places no bar on the President proroguing a House while it is sitting.\n\n3. The President dissolves the Lok Sabha, save in exceptional circumstances, on the advice of the Council of Ministers - Correct\nUnder Article 85(2)(b), the President dissolves the Lok Sabha on the advice of the Council of Ministers. In exceptional circumstances, for example if a government that has lost its majority advises dissolution, the President may use discretion.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 2 and 3."},{"i":"2024-96","y":2024,"n":96,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: The European Parliament approved The Net-Zero Industry Act recently.\nStatement II: The European Union intends to achieve carbon neutrality by 2040 and therefore aims to develop all of its own clean technology by that time.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I","Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"],"a":2,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nThe European Parliament approved the Net-Zero Industry Act in April 2024, and it entered into force in June 2024. It aims to boost manufacturing of clean technologies in Europe (solar panels, batteries, heat pumps, electrolysers, wind turbines) and simplify permits for such projects, partly in response to the US Inflation Reduction Act and dependence on China.\n\nStatement II - Incorrect\nThe EU's legally binding target under the European Climate Law is climate neutrality by 2050, not 2040 (with a 55 percent reduction by 2030 and a proposed 90 percent reduction by 2040). Also, the NZIA aims for the EU to manufacture at least 40 percent of its annual deployment needs of strategic clean technologies by 2030, not to develop all of its own clean technology.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Statement I is correct, but Statement II is incorrect."},{"i":"2024-97","y":2024,"n":97,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Recently, Venezuela has achieved a rapid recovery from its economic crisis and succeeded in preventing its people from fleeing/emigrating to other countries.\nStatement II: Venezuela has the world's largest oil reserves.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I","Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct"],"a":3,"x":"Statement I - Incorrect\nVenezuela has not recovered from its economic crisis. Years of mismanagement, falling oil production, hyperinflation (over 1 million percent in 2018) and US sanctions caused a collapse of the economy. More than 7.7 million Venezuelans have left the country, one of the largest displacement crises in the world. So it has not prevented emigration.\n\nStatement II - Correct\nVenezuela has the world's largest proven crude oil reserves, about 303 billion barrels, ahead of Saudi Arabia. Most of it is extra-heavy oil in the Orinoco Belt, which is costly to extract and refine.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Statement I is incorrect, but Statement II is correct."},{"i":"2024-98","y":2024,"n":98,"s":6,"q":"With reference to the Digital India Land Records Modernisation Programme, consider the following statements:\n1. To implement the scheme, the Central Government provides 100% funding.\n2. Under the Scheme, Cadastral Maps are digitised.\n3. An initiative has been undertaken to transliterate the Records of Rights from local language to any of the languages recognized by the Constitution of India.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"The Digital India Land Records Modernisation Programme (DILRMP) aims to create an integrated land information system with up-to-date records, reducing land disputes and fraud.\n\n1. The Central Government provides 100 percent funding - Correct\nSince 2016 it has been a Central Sector Scheme with full funding from the Centre (Department of Land Resources, Ministry of Rural Development).\n\n2. Cadastral maps are digitised under the scheme - Correct\nIts components include computerisation of land records (Records of Rights), digitisation and geo-referencing of cadastral maps, computerisation of registration, modern record rooms, and the Unique Land Parcel Identification Number (ULPIN, or 'Bhu-Aadhaar').\n\n3. An initiative has been taken to transliterate Records of Rights from local languages to any Constitution-recognised language - Correct\nTo overcome language barriers, the government has undertaken transliteration of land records from the local language into any of the 22 languages in the Eighth Schedule.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2024-99","y":2024,"n":99,"s":3,"q":"With reference to the 'Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan', consider the following statements:\n1. This scheme guarantees a minimum package of antenatal care services to women in their second and third trimesters of pregnancy and six months post-delivery health care service in any government health facility.\n2. Under this scheme, private sector health care providers of certain specialities can volunteer to provide services at nearby government health facilities.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":1,"x":"The Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan (PMSMA) was launched in 2016 by the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare to provide assured, comprehensive and quality antenatal care, free of cost, on the 9th of every month.\n\n1. It guarantees antenatal care in the second and third trimesters and six months of post-delivery care in any government health facility - Incorrect\nPMSMA provides a minimum package of antenatal care (check-ups, tests, identification of high-risk pregnancies) to pregnant women in their second and third trimesters, on a fixed day, at designated government facilities. It does not include six months of post-delivery (postnatal) care.\n\n2. Private sector specialists can volunteer to provide services at nearby government facilities - Correct\nObstetricians, radiologists and physicians from the private sector can register as volunteers and provide services on the 9th of each month, through the 'I pledge for 9' campaign.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2024-100","y":2024,"n":100,"s":1,"q":"With reference to the Pradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Maan-dhan (PM-SYM) Yojana, consider the following statements:\n1. The entry age group for enrolment in the scheme is 21 to 40 years.\n2. Age specific contribution shall be made by the beneficiary.\n3. Each subscriber under the scheme shall receive a minimum pension of ₹ 3,000 per month after attaining the age of 60 years.\n4. Family pension is applicable to the spouse and unmarried daughters.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1, 3 and 4","2 and 3 only","2 only","1, 2 and 4"],"a":1,"x":"The Pradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Maan-dhan (2019) is a voluntary, contributory pension scheme for unorganised workers (such as street vendors, domestic workers and rickshaw pullers) with monthly income up to 15,000 rupees.\n\n1. Entry age is 21 to 40 years - Incorrect\nThe entry age is 18 to 40 years.\n\n2. Age-specific contribution is made by the beneficiary - Correct\nThe worker pays a monthly contribution depending on age at entry (from 55 rupees at age 18 to 200 rupees at age 40), and the Central Government contributes an equal matching amount.\n\n3. Each subscriber receives a minimum pension of 3,000 rupees per month after 60 - Correct\nThe scheme assures a minimum monthly pension of 3,000 rupees after the age of 60.\n\n4. Family pension is applicable to the spouse and unmarried daughters - Incorrect\nFamily pension (50 percent of the pension) is available only to the spouse after the subscriber's death, not to unmarried daughters.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2023-1","y":2023,"n":1,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Jhelum River passes through Wular Lake.\n2. Krishna River directly feeds Kolleru Lake.\n3. Meandering of Gandak River formed Kanwar Lake.\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":0,"x":"1. Jhelum River passes through Wular Lake - Correct\nThe Jhelum flows into Wular Lake in Bandipora district, Jammu and Kashmir, and flows out of it. Wular is one of the largest freshwater lakes in Asia, formed by tectonic activity, and acts as a natural flood reservoir for the Jhelum. It is a Ramsar site.\n\n2. Krishna River directly feeds Kolleru Lake - Incorrect\nKolleru Lake in Andhra Pradesh lies between the Krishna and Godavari deltas. It is fed directly by seasonal streams such as the Budameru and Tammileru and by drains and channels from the two deltas. The Krishna river itself does not flow directly into it.\n\n3. Meandering of Gandak River formed Kanwar Lake - Incorrect\nKanwar (Kabartal) Lake in Begusarai district, Bihar, Asia's largest freshwater oxbow lake and Bihar's first Ramsar site, was formed by the meandering of the Burhi Gandak river, not the Gandak.\n\nOnly one statement is correct, so the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2023-2","y":2023,"n":2,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Port | Well known as\n| 1. | Kamarajar Port | First major port in India registered as a company\n| 2. | Mundra Port | Largest privately owned port in India\n| 3. | Visakhapatnam Port | Largest container port in India\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","All three pairs","None of the pairs"],"a":1,"x":"1. Kamarajar Port : First major port registered as a company - Correct\nKamarajar Port (formerly Ennore Port), near Chennai, was India's first major port to be corporatised, registered as a public company under the Companies Act (in 1999) rather than being run by a Port Trust.\n\n2. Mundra Port : Largest privately owned port - Correct\nMundra Port in Kutch, Gujarat, operated by Adani Ports and SEZ, is India's largest privately owned commercial port by cargo handled.\n\n3. Visakhapatnam Port : Largest container port - Incorrect\nVisakhapatnam is a large major port handling bulk cargo like iron ore, coal and crude, but it is not the largest container port. Jawaharlal Nehru Port (Nhava Sheva, Navi Mumbai) has long been the largest container port among major ports, and Mundra has handled the most containers overall in recent years.\n\nTwo pairs are correctly matched, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-3","y":2023,"n":3,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following trees:\n1. Jackfruit (Artocarpus heterophyllus)\n2. Mahua (Madhuca indica)\n3. Teak (Tectona grandis)\nHow many of the above are deciduous trees?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":1,"x":"Deciduous trees shed their leaves for a part of the year, usually in the dry season. Evergreen trees keep their leaves throughout the year.\n\n1. Jackfruit (Artocarpus heterophyllus) - Not deciduous\nJackfruit is an evergreen tree of humid tropical regions, common in the Western Ghats, Kerala and the North-east. It is the State fruit of Kerala and Tamil Nadu.\n\n2. Mahua (Madhuca indica) - Deciduous\nMahua is a tropical dry deciduous tree of central India. It sheds its leaves from February to April, when its fleshy flowers (used for food and liquor by tribal communities) appear.\n\n3. Teak (Tectona grandis) - Deciduous\nTeak is the most important tree of tropical moist and dry deciduous forests in India, shedding its leaves in the dry season.\n\nTwo are deciduous, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-4","y":2023,"n":4,"s":6,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. India has more arable area than China.\n2. The proportion of irrigated area is more in India as compared to China.\n3. The average productivity per hectare in Indian agriculture is higher than that in China.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":0,"x":"1. India has more arable area than China - Correct\nIndia has about 155-156 million hectares of arable land, the largest in the world after the USA, while China has about 119-128 million hectares.\n\n2. The proportion of irrigated area is more in India than in China - Incorrect\nChina irrigates a larger share of its cultivated land, around half or more. In India, about 40-50 percent of net sown area is irrigated; the rest depends on rainfall.\n\n3. Average productivity per hectare in India is higher than in China - Incorrect\nChina's yields are much higher. For example, rice yields in China are about 7 tonnes per hectare against about 4 in India, and wheat about 5.8 against 3.5. China uses more fertilisers, better irrigation, hybrid seeds and mechanisation.\n\nOnly one statement is correct, so the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2023-5","y":2023,"n":5,"s":5,"q":"Which one of the following is the best example of repeated falls in sea level, giving rise to present-day extensive marshland?","o":["Bhitarkanika Mangroves","Marakkanam Salt Pans","Naupada Swamp","Rann of Kutch"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Bhitarkanika Mangroves - Incorrect\nBhitarkanika in Odisha is a mangrove wetland in the delta of the Brahmani and Baitarani rivers. It was formed by river deposition and tidal action, not by repeated falls in sea level.\n\n(b) Marakkanam Salt Pans - Incorrect\nThese are salt pans along the Tamil Nadu coast, created artificially for salt production.\n\n(c) Naupada Swamp - Incorrect\nNaupada in Srikakulam district, Andhra Pradesh, is a coastal swamp with salt pans, but it is not the best example of extensive marshland formed by repeated sea-level falls.\n\n(d) Rann of Kutch - Correct\nThe Rann of Kutch in Gujarat was once a shallow arm of the Arabian Sea. Repeated falls in sea level, along with tectonic uplift (as in the 1819 earthquake), left behind a vast, flat, salty marshland. During the monsoon it is flooded, and in the dry season it becomes a white salt desert. It is the best example among the options."},{"i":"2023-6","y":2023,"n":6,"s":5,"q":"Ilmenite and rutile, abundantly available in certain coastal tracts of India, are rich sources of which one of the following?","o":["Aluminium","Copper","Iron","Titanium"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Aluminium - Incorrect\nAluminium is obtained from bauxite, found in Odisha, Jharkhand and Chhattisgarh.\n\n(b) Copper - Incorrect\nCopper comes from ores such as chalcopyrite, mined in Rajasthan (Khetri), Madhya Pradesh (Malanjkhand) and Jharkhand (Singhbhum).\n\n(c) Iron - Incorrect\nIron comes mainly from haematite and magnetite. Ilmenite does contain iron, but it is mined as a source of titanium.\n\n(d) Titanium - Correct\nIlmenite (iron titanium oxide) and rutile (titanium dioxide) are the main titanium minerals. They occur in beach sand placer deposits along the coasts of Kerala (Chavara), Tamil Nadu (Manavalakurichi), Odisha (Chhatrapur) and Andhra Pradesh, along with monazite and zircon, and are processed by Indian Rare Earths Limited. Titanium is used in aircraft, space vehicles and titanium dioxide pigment."},{"i":"2023-7","y":2023,"n":7,"s":5,"q":"About three-fourths of world's cobalt, a metal required for the manufacture of batteries for electric motor vehicles, is produced by","o":["Argentina","Botswana","the Democratic Republic of the Congo","Kazakhstan"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Argentina - Incorrect\nArgentina is known for lithium (part of the 'Lithium Triangle' with Chile and Bolivia), not cobalt.\n\n(b) Botswana - Incorrect\nBotswana is famous for diamonds.\n\n(c) The Democratic Republic of the Congo - Correct\nThe DR Congo produces about 70-75 percent of the world's cobalt, mainly from the copper-cobalt belt of Katanga province. Cobalt is essential for lithium-ion battery cathodes used in electric vehicles and phones. Concerns include child labour in artisanal mines and Chinese control over much of the mining and refining.\n\n(d) Kazakhstan - Incorrect\nKazakhstan is the world's largest producer of uranium."},{"i":"2023-8","y":2023,"n":8,"s":11,"q":"Which one of the following is a part of the Congo Basin?","o":["Cameroon","Nigeria","South Sudan","Uganda"],"a":0,"x":"The Congo Basin, drained by the Congo River, has the world's second-largest tropical rainforest. It spreads across six countries: Cameroon, the Central African Republic, the Democratic Republic of the Congo, the Republic of the Congo, Equatorial Guinea and Gabon.\n\n(a) Cameroon - Correct\nSouth-eastern Cameroon lies within the Congo Basin, with its rainforests draining into the Sangha, a tributary of the Congo.\n\n(b) Nigeria - Incorrect\nNigeria lies in the Niger basin (Niger and Benue rivers).\n\n(c) South Sudan - Incorrect\nSouth Sudan lies in the Nile basin (White Nile and the Sudd wetland).\n\n(d) Uganda - Incorrect\nUganda lies in the Nile basin, around Lake Victoria and Lake Albert."},{"i":"2023-9","y":2023,"n":9,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Amarkantak Hills are at the confluence of Vindhya and Sahyadri Ranges.\n2. Biligirirangan Hills constitute the easternmost part of Satpura Range.\n3. Seshachalam Hills constitute the southernmost part of Western Ghats.\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":3,"x":"1. Amarkantak Hills are at the confluence of the Vindhya and Sahyadri Ranges - Incorrect\nThe Amarkantak plateau, where the Narmada, Son and Johila rivers rise, lies in the Maikal range at the meeting point of the Vindhya and Satpura ranges, in Madhya Pradesh. The Sahyadri (Western Ghats) are far to the south-west.\n\n2. Biligirirangan Hills are the easternmost part of the Satpura Range - Incorrect\nThe Biligirirangan (BR) Hills, on the Karnataka-Tamil Nadu border, lie at the junction of the Eastern and Western Ghats. The BRT Tiger Reserve is located there. They have no connection with the Satpura range of central India.\n\n3. Seshachalam Hills are the southernmost part of the Western Ghats - Incorrect\nThe Seshachalam hills, around Tirupati in Andhra Pradesh, are part of the Eastern Ghats and a biosphere reserve known for red sanders. The southernmost part of the Western Ghats is the Agasthyamalai and Cardamom hills.\n\nNone of the statements is correct, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2023-10","y":2023,"n":10,"s":1,"q":"With reference to India's projects on connectivity, consider the following statements:\n1. East-West Corridor under Golden Quadrilateral Project connects Dibrugarh and Surat.\n2. Trilateral Highway connects Moreh in Manipur and Chiang Mai in Thailand via Myanmar.\n3. Bangladesh-China-India-Myanmar Economic Corridor connects Varanasi in Uttar Pradesh with Kunming in China.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":3,"x":"1. The East-West Corridor under the Golden Quadrilateral connects Dibrugarh and Surat - Incorrect\nThe East-West Corridor is part of the National Highways Development Project, separate from the Golden Quadrilateral (which links Delhi, Mumbai, Chennai and Kolkata). It connects Silchar in Assam with Porbandar in Gujarat. The North-South Corridor connects Srinagar with Kanyakumari.\n\n2. The Trilateral Highway connects Moreh in Manipur and Chiang Mai in Thailand via Myanmar - Incorrect\nThe India-Myanmar-Thailand Trilateral Highway runs from Moreh (Manipur) through Myanmar (Tamu, Mandalay) to Mae Sot in Thailand, not Chiang Mai.\n\n3. The BCIM Economic Corridor connects Varanasi with Kunming - Incorrect\nThe proposed Bangladesh-China-India-Myanmar corridor was to connect Kolkata with Kunming (Yunnan, China) via Dhaka and Mandalay. India later stepped back from it because of concerns over China's Belt and Road Initiative.\n\nNone of the statements is correct, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2023-11","y":2023,"n":11,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: India, despite having uranium deposits, depends on coal for most of its electricity production.\nStatement-II: Uranium, enriched to the extent of at least 60%, is required for the production of electricity.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":2,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nIndia has uranium deposits (for example at Jaduguda in Jharkhand and Tummalapalle in Andhra Pradesh), yet coal remains the main source of electricity, supplying about 70-75 percent. Nuclear power provides only about 3 percent, because domestic uranium is limited and low-grade and nuclear expansion has been slow.\n\nStatement II - Incorrect\nNuclear power reactors do not need uranium enriched to 60 percent. Light water reactors use low-enriched uranium with about 3-5 percent U-235, and India's Pressurised Heavy Water Reactors use natural uranium (about 0.7 percent U-235) without enrichment. Uranium enriched above 20 percent is called highly enriched uranium, and around 90 percent is weapons-grade.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."},{"i":"2023-12","y":2023,"n":12,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: Marsupials are not naturally found in India.\nStatement-II: Marsupials can thrive only in montane grasslands with no predators.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":2,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nMarsupials are mammals whose young are born at an early stage and develop in a pouch, such as kangaroos, koalas, wombats and opossums. They are found naturally only in Australia, New Guinea and nearby islands, and in the Americas. No marsupials are native to India.\n\nStatement II - Incorrect\nMarsupials live in many kinds of habitats, including deserts, tropical rainforests, eucalyptus woodlands, grasslands, mountains and even water (the water opossum). Many live alongside predators such as dingoes, eagles, snakes and foxes. Their absence from India is due to evolutionary and biogeographic history, as Australia separated early from other continents, not because they can live only in predator-free montane grasslands.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."},{"i":"2023-13","y":2023,"n":13,"s":2,"q":"'Invasive Species Specialist Group' (that develops Global Invasive Species Database) belongs to which one of the following organizations?","o":["The International Union for Conservation of Nature","The United Nations Environment Programme","The United Nations World Commission for Environment and Development","The World Wide Fund for Nature"],"a":0,"x":"(a) The International Union for Conservation of Nature - Correct\nThe Invasive Species Specialist Group is a global network of scientific and policy experts under the IUCN's Species Survival Commission. It manages the Global Invasive Species Database and published the list of '100 of the World's Worst Invasive Alien Species', which includes Lantana camara and water hyacinth found in India.\n\n(b) The United Nations Environment Programme - Incorrect\nUNEP works on invasive species under the Convention on Biological Diversity but does not host this group.\n\n(c) The UN World Commission on Environment and Development - Incorrect\nThis commission (Brundtland Commission) produced the report 'Our Common Future' (1987), which defined sustainable development. It was dissolved after that.\n\n(d) The World Wide Fund for Nature - Incorrect\nWWF is a conservation NGO; the ISSG is not part of it."},{"i":"2023-14","y":2023,"n":14,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following fauna:\n1. Lion-tailed Macaque\n2. Malabar Civet\n3. Sambar Deer\nHow many of the above are generally nocturnal or most active after sunset?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":0,"x":"1. Lion-tailed Macaque - Not nocturnal\nThe lion-tailed macaque, an endangered primate endemic to the Western Ghats, is diurnal and arboreal. It is active during the day, feeding on fruits, seeds and insects in the rainforest canopy.\n\n2. Malabar Civet - Nocturnal\nThe Malabar large-spotted civet, endemic to the Western Ghats and critically endangered, is nocturnal and extremely elusive, active mostly at night.\n\n3. Sambar Deer - Nocturnal or most active after sunset\nStandard references, including the IUCN, describe the sambar as mainly nocturnal and crepuscular. It feeds mostly at dusk and through the night and rests in dense cover during the day, especially where it is disturbed by humans or predators.\n\nOn this basis, two animals (Malabar civet and sambar) are generally nocturnal or most active after sunset.\n\nDiscrepancy: The official answer in the sheet is (a) Only one. On the facts above the correct answer should be (b) Only two, since the sambar is widely documented as nocturnal/crepuscular. Several coaching analyses also give (b), and some answer compilations list (b) as UPSC's official answer, so please re-verify this entry against the official UPSC key."},{"i":"2023-15","y":2023,"n":15,"s":2,"q":"Which of the following organisms perform waggle dance for others of their kin to indicate the direction and the distance to a source of their food?","o":["Butterflies","Dragonflies","Honeybees","Wasps"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Butterflies - Incorrect\nButterflies do not communicate food locations to others through dances.\n\n(b) Dragonflies - Incorrect\nDragonflies are solitary predators and do not share food information.\n\n(c) Honeybees - Correct\nHoneybee foragers returning to the hive perform a 'waggle dance' on the vertical comb. The angle of the waggle run relative to the vertical shows the direction of the food source relative to the Sun, and the duration of the waggle shows the distance. For food very close to the hive, they perform a simpler 'round dance'. Karl von Frisch decoded this behaviour and shared the 1973 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.\n\n(d) Wasps - Incorrect\nSocial wasps do not use a waggle dance to indicate food direction and distance."},{"i":"2023-16","y":2023,"n":16,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Some mushrooms have medicinal properties.\n2. Some mushrooms have psycho-active properties.\n3. Some mushrooms have insecticidal properties.\n4. Some mushrooms have bioluminescent properties.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":3,"x":"1. Some mushrooms have medicinal properties - Correct\nExamples include Ganoderma (reishi), used in traditional medicine; Cordyceps, valued for energy and immunity; and shiitake, which contains lentinan used in cancer therapy research. Penicillin came from a fungus (mould), showing the medicinal value of fungi.\n\n2. Some mushrooms have psychoactive properties - Correct\n'Magic mushrooms' of the genus Psilocybe contain psilocybin, which causes hallucinations, and fly agaric (Amanita muscaria) contains psychoactive compounds.\n\n3. Some mushrooms have insecticidal properties - Correct\nCertain fungi and mushrooms produce compounds toxic to insects, and entomopathogenic fungi (such as Beauveria and Metarhizium, and Cordyceps species) infect and kill insects. They are used as biopesticides.\n\n4. Some mushrooms have bioluminescent properties - Correct\nAbout 100 species, such as Mycena and Omphalotus, glow in the dark due to a chemical reaction involving luciferin. This 'foxfire' has been reported from the Western Ghats and Meghalaya forests.\n\nAll four statements are correct, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2023-17","y":2023,"n":17,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding the Indian squirrels:\n1. They build nests by making burrows in the ground.\n2. They store their food materials like nuts and seeds in the ground.\n3. They are omnivorous.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":1,"x":"1. Indian squirrels build nests by making burrows in the ground - Incorrect\nIndian squirrels, such as the common three-striped and five-striped palm squirrels and the Indian giant squirrel, are tree squirrels. They build nests (dreys) of twigs, grass, leaves and fibres in trees, holes in trees, or crevices in buildings, not burrows in the ground (burrowing is typical of ground squirrels).\n\n2. They store food like nuts and seeds in the ground - Correct\nSquirrels are known for caching food: they bury nuts and seeds in scattered spots in the ground to eat later. Forgotten seeds often grow into plants, helping seed dispersal.\n\n3. They are omnivorous - Correct\nIndian palm squirrels eat mainly seeds, nuts, fruits and flowers, but also insects, eggs and sometimes small birds or lizards.\n\nTwo statements are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-18","y":2023,"n":18,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Some microorganisms can grow in environments with temperature above the boiling point of water.\n2. Some microorganisms can grow in environments with temperature below the freezing point of water.\n3. Some microorganisms can grow in highly acidic environment with a pH below 3.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":2,"x":"Microorganisms that live in extreme conditions are called extremophiles.\n\n1. Some microorganisms can grow above the boiling point of water - Correct\nHyperthermophiles, mostly archaea found near deep-sea hydrothermal vents and hot springs, grow at temperatures above 100°C where high pressure keeps water liquid. For example, Methanopyrus kandleri can grow at about 122°C and Pyrolobus fumarii at 113°C.\n\n2. Some microorganisms can grow below the freezing point of water - Correct\nPsychrophiles grow in polar ice, permafrost, deep oceans and salty brines at temperatures below 0°C, some down to about -15 to -20°C.\n\n3. Some microorganisms can grow in highly acidic environments with pH below 3 - Correct\nAcidophiles such as Picrophilus (which grows near pH 0) and Acidithiobacillus thrive in acid mine drainage and volcanic springs. Acidithiobacillus is used in bioleaching of metals from ores.\n\nAll three statements are correct, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2023-19","y":2023,"n":19,"s":2,"q":"Which one of the following makes a tool with a stick to scrape insects from a hole in a tree or a log of wood?","o":["Fishing cat","Orangutan","Otter","Sloth bear"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Fishing cat - Incorrect\nThe fishing cat, found in wetlands of India (the State animal of West Bengal), catches fish by scooping them with its paws or diving. It does not make tools.\n\n(b) Orangutan - Correct\nOrangutans, especially Sumatran orangutans, are known to make and use tools. They strip leaves from sticks and use them to extract insects, termites, honey or seeds from tree holes and fruits. Such tool-making behaviour is shared with chimpanzees and shows high intelligence. It is also passed on culturally within groups.\n\n(c) Otter - Incorrect\nSea otters use stones to crack open shellfish, but they do not make stick tools to extract insects from holes in trees.\n\n(d) Sloth bear - Incorrect\nThe sloth bear feeds on termites and ants by breaking open mounds with its claws and sucking up insects through a gap in its front teeth and its mobile lips. It does not make tools."},{"i":"2023-20","y":2023,"n":20,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following:\n1. Aerosols\n2. Foam agents\n3. Fire retardants\n4. Lubricants\nIn the making of how many of the above are hydrofluorocarbons used?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":2,"x":"Hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs) were introduced to replace ozone-depleting CFCs and HCFCs. They do not harm the ozone layer but are powerful greenhouse gases, so their use is being phased down under the Kigali Amendment (2016) to the Montreal Protocol.\n\n1. Aerosols - HFCs used\nHFCs are used as propellants in aerosol sprays and metered-dose inhalers.\n\n2. Foam agents - HFCs used\nThey are used as blowing agents to make insulation foams (polyurethane and polystyrene) for buildings and refrigerators.\n\n3. Fire retardants - HFCs used\nHFCs such as HFC-227ea are used in fire suppression systems as replacements for halons.\n\n4. Lubricants - HFCs not used\nHFCs are not used as lubricants. (Their largest use is as refrigerants in air conditioners and refrigerators.)\n\nThree are correct, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2023-21","y":2023,"n":21,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: Interest income from the deposits in Infrastructure Investment Trusts (InvITs) distributed to their investors is exempted from tax, but the dividend is taxable.\nStatement-II: InvITs are recognized as borrowers under the 'Securitization and Reconstruction of Financial Assets and Enforcement of Security Interest Act, 2002'.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":3,"x":"Statement I - Incorrect\nIt is actually the reverse. An Infrastructure Investment Trust (InvIT) pools money from investors to invest in income-generating infrastructure (like roads and power transmission lines) and distributes income to unitholders. Interest income distributed to unitholders is taxable in their hands. Dividend income distributed by an InvIT is exempt in the hands of unitholders, provided the underlying special purpose vehicle has not opted for the concessional corporate tax regime.\n\nStatement II - Correct\nThe Finance Act, 2021 amended the SARFAESI Act, 2002 so that InvITs and REITs are treated as borrowers. This allows lenders to enforce security interest against them, which helps InvITs raise debt from banks and financial institutions.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."},{"i":"2023-22","y":2023,"n":22,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: In the post-pandemic recent past, many Central Banks worldwide had carried out interest rate hikes.\nStatement-II: Central Banks generally assume that they have the ability to counteract the rising consumer prices via monetary policy means.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nAfter the COVID-19 pandemic, inflation surged worldwide because of supply chain disruptions, pent-up demand and the rise in energy and food prices after the Russia-Ukraine war (2022). Central banks such as the US Federal Reserve, the European Central Bank, the Bank of England and the RBI raised interest rates sharply in 2022-23. The RBI raised the repo rate from 4 percent to 6.5 percent.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nCentral banks assume that monetary policy can control rising prices. Raising interest rates makes borrowing costlier, reduces spending and investment, cools demand, and brings inflation down towards the target. This belief is precisely why they carried out the rate hikes.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both statements are correct and Statement II explains Statement I."},{"i":"2023-23","y":2023,"n":23,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: Carbon markets are likely to be one of the most widespread tools in the fight against climate change.\nStatement-II: Carbon markets transfer resources from the private sector to the State.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":1,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nCarbon markets put a price on carbon emissions. They include compliance markets (cap-and-trade systems such as the EU Emissions Trading System and China's national ETS, and India's Carbon Credit Trading Scheme, 2023) and voluntary carbon markets. They are expected to be among the most widespread tools against climate change, as Article 6 of the Paris Agreement also shows.\n\nStatement II - Correct (as per key), but does not explain Statement I\nIn compliance markets, governments often auction emission allowances to companies, so money flows from the private sector to the State. The revenue can be used for climate action or compensation.\n\nHowever, this revenue transfer is not the reason carbon markets are seen as an effective and widespread tool. Their main strength is that they create a price signal that encourages emission cuts wherever they are cheapest. So Statement II does not explain Statement I.\n\nHence the answer is (b) Both statements are correct but Statement II is not the correct explanation for Statement I."},{"i":"2023-24","y":2023,"n":24,"s":1,"q":"Which one of the following activities of the Reserve Bank of India is considered to be part of 'sterilization'?","o":["Conducting 'Open Market Operations'","Oversight of settlement and payment systems","Debt and cash management for the Central and State Governments","Regulating the functions of Non-banking Financial Institutions"],"a":0,"x":"Sterilisation means offsetting the effect of the RBI's foreign exchange operations on domestic money supply. When the RBI buys dollars (for example during large capital inflows), it releases rupees into the system, increasing liquidity and possibly inflation. To neutralise this, it absorbs rupees by selling government securities.\n\n(a) Conducting Open Market Operations - Correct\nOpen Market Operations (buying and selling government securities) are the main tool of sterilisation. The RBI has also used Market Stabilisation Scheme bonds and now the Standing Deposit Facility for this purpose.\n\n(b) Oversight of settlement and payment systems - Incorrect\nThis is a regulatory function of the RBI, not related to sterilisation.\n\n(c) Debt and cash management for Central and State Governments - Incorrect\nThe RBI acts as banker and debt manager to governments, but this is a separate function.\n\n(d) Regulating Non-banking Financial Institutions - Incorrect\nThis is the RBI's supervisory role."},{"i":"2023-25","y":2023,"n":25,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following markets:\n1. Government Bond Market\n2. Call Money Market\n3. Treasury Bill Market\n4. Stock Market\nHow many of the above are included in capital markets?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":1,"x":"The capital market deals with long-term funds (more than one year), while the money market deals with short-term funds (up to one year).\n\n1. Government Bond Market - Capital market\nDated government securities have maturities of 5 to 40 years, so they belong to the capital market.\n\n2. Call Money Market - Money market\nCall money is borrowing and lending among banks for one day (call money) or up to 14 days (notice money). It is a money market segment.\n\n3. Treasury Bill Market - Money market\nTreasury bills are short-term government securities of 91, 182 and 364 days, so they are money market instruments.\n\n4. Stock Market - Capital market\nShares represent long-term ownership capital, so the stock market is part of the capital market.\n\nTwo are part of capital markets, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-26","y":2023,"n":26,"s":6,"q":"Which one of the following best describes the concept of 'Small Farmer Large Field'?","o":["Resettlement of a large number of people, uprooted from their countries due to war, by giving them a large cultivable land which they cultivate collectively and share the produce","Many marginal farmers in an area organize themselves into groups and synchronize and harmonize selected agricultural operations","Many marginal farmers in an area together make a contract with a corporate body and surrender their land to the corporate body for a fixed term for which the corporate body makes a payment of agreed amount to the farmers","A company extends loans, technical knowledge and material inputs to a number of small farmers in an area so that they produce the agricultural commodity required by the company for its manufacturing process and commercial production"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Resettlement of war refugees on large land for collective cultivation - Incorrect\nThis describes collective farming for displaced people, not the 'Small Farmer Large Field' concept.\n\n(b) Marginal farmers in an area organise into groups and synchronise and harmonise selected agricultural operations - Correct\nUnder the Small Farmer Large Field (SFLF) model, many small and marginal farmers with neighbouring plots cooperate so that their fragmented holdings function like one large field. They synchronise operations such as choice of crop variety, sowing dates, input purchase, use of machinery, pest management, harvesting and marketing. This gives them economies of scale, better bargaining power and lower costs, while each farmer keeps ownership of his land. The model was developed in Vietnam and has been tried in Kerala, Maharashtra and other States.\n\n(c) Marginal farmers surrender land to a corporate body for a fixed term for payment - Incorrect\nThis describes land leasing to a corporate entity.\n\n(d) A company gives loans, knowledge and inputs to small farmers to grow a commodity it needs - Incorrect\nThis describes contract farming."},{"i":"2023-27","y":2023,"n":27,"s":6,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The Government of India provides Minimum Support Price for niger (Guizotia abyssinica) seeds.\n2. Niger is cultivated as a Kharif crop.\n3. Some tribal people in India use niger seed oil for cooking.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":2,"x":"Niger (Guizotia abyssinica), also called ramtil, is a minor oilseed crop native to Ethiopia. In India it is grown mainly in the tribal belts of Odisha, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand and Maharashtra.\n\n1. The Government provides Minimum Support Price for niger seed - Correct\nNiger seed is one of the 22 mandated crops for which the Centre announces MSP, listed among the Kharif oilseeds.\n\n2. Niger is cultivated as a Kharif crop - Correct\nIt is mainly sown with the monsoon as a Kharif crop, often on marginal and hilly lands, though in parts of Odisha it is also grown in the Rabi season.\n\n3. Some tribal people use niger seed oil for cooking - Correct\nNiger seed oil is used as edible oil by tribal communities in the regions where it is grown. The seeds are also used as bird feed and exported.\n\nAll three statements are correct, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2023-28","y":2023,"n":28,"s":1,"q":"Consider the investments in the following assets:\n1. Brand recognition\n2. Inventory\n3. Intellectual property\n4. Mailing list of clients\nHow many of the above are considered intangible investments?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":2,"x":"Intangible assets are non-physical assets that give long-term value to a business, such as brands, patents, software and customer data.\n\n1. Brand recognition - Intangible\nA brand's reputation and recognition (brand equity or goodwill) have value without physical form.\n\n2. Inventory - Tangible\nInventory consists of raw materials, work-in-progress and finished goods, which are physical, tangible current assets.\n\n3. Intellectual property - Intangible\nPatents, copyrights, trademarks and designs are classic intangible assets.\n\n4. Mailing list of clients - Intangible\nCustomer lists and databases are intangible assets because they provide business value through relationships and information.\n\nThree are intangible, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2023-29","y":2023,"n":29,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following:\n1. Demographic performance\n2. Forest and ecology\n3. Governance reforms\n4. Stable government\n5. Tax and fiscal efforts\nFor the horizontal tax devolution, the Fifteenth Finance Commission used how many of the above as criteria other than population, area and income distance?","o":["Only two","Only three","Only four","All five"],"a":1,"x":"The Fifteenth Finance Commission (chaired by N.K. Singh) used the following criteria for horizontal devolution of taxes among States: income distance (45 percent), population as per the 2011 Census (15 percent), area (15 percent), forest and ecology (10 percent), demographic performance (12.5 percent), and tax and fiscal efforts (2.5 percent).\n\n1. Demographic performance - Used\nIntroduced to reward States that controlled population growth (measured by the inverse of total fertility rate), addressing concerns of southern States about using the 2011 population.\n\n2. Forest and ecology - Used\nRewards States with more dense forest cover.\n\n3. Governance reforms - Not used\n\n4. Stable government - Not used\n\n5. Tax and fiscal efforts - Used\nRewards States with better tax collection efficiency.\n\nThree of the listed criteria were used, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-30","y":2023,"n":30,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following infrastructure sectors:\n1. Affordable housing\n2. Mass rapid transport\n3. Health care\n4. Renewable energy\nOn how many of the above does UNOPS Sustainable Investments in Infrastructure and Innovation (S3i) initiative focus for its investments?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":2,"x":"UNOPS (United Nations Office for Project Services) launched the Sustainable Investments in Infrastructure and Innovation (S3i) initiative in 2018. It invests in projects that help achieve the Sustainable Development Goals in developing countries, and focuses on three sectors.\n\n1. Affordable housing - Focus sector\nS3i finances affordable housing projects (it has worked on housing in countries such as Kenya, Ghana and Pakistan).\n\n2. Mass rapid transport - Not a focus sector\n\n3. Health care - Focus sector\nIt invests in health infrastructure such as hospitals and clinics.\n\n4. Renewable energy - Focus sector\nIt invests in renewable energy projects such as solar and wind power.\n\nThree of the four are focus sectors, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2023-31","y":2023,"n":31,"s":0,"q":"In essence, what does 'Due Process of Law' mean?","o":["The principle of natural justice","The procedure established by law","Fair application of law","Equality before law"],"a":0,"x":"(a) The principle of natural justice - Correct\n'Due process of law', found in the Fifth and Fourteenth Amendments of the US Constitution, requires that any law depriving a person of life, liberty or property must be just, fair and reasonable, and must follow fair procedure. Courts can examine both the procedure and the substance of the law. In essence, it embodies the principles of natural justice (fair hearing and no bias). In India, the Supreme Court in Maneka Gandhi v. Union of India (1978) read this idea into Article 21, holding that the procedure must be 'fair, just and reasonable'.\n\n(b) The procedure established by law - Incorrect\nThis is the phrase used in Article 21, borrowed from Japan. It originally meant that a law duly enacted by the legislature, if followed correctly, was valid even if unfair (A.K. Gopalan, 1950). It is narrower than due process.\n\n(c) Fair application of law - Incorrect\nThis is part of the idea but does not capture its essence of testing the fairness of the law itself.\n\n(d) Equality before law - Incorrect\nThis is part of the rule of law and Article 14, a different concept."},{"i":"2023-32","y":2023,"n":32,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: In India, prisons are managed by State Governments with their own rules and regulations for the day-to-day administration of prisons.\nStatement-II: In India, prisons are governed by the Prisons Act, 1894 which expressly kept the subject of prisons in the control of Provincial Governments.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\n'Prisons, reformatories, borstal institutions and other institutions of a like nature' is Entry 4 of the State List. So prisons are managed by State Governments, which have their own jail manuals and rules for day-to-day administration.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I (as per key)\nPrison administration in India is still largely governed by the Prisons Act, 1894, a colonial law that placed prisons under the control of provincial governments. This arrangement continued after independence, which is why States manage prisons. (The Centre prepared a Model Prisons Act, 2023 to guide States in replacing the old law.)\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both statements are correct and Statement II explains Statement I."},{"i":"2023-33","y":2023,"n":33,"s":0,"q":"Which one of the following statements best reflects the chief purpose of the 'Constitution' of a country?","o":["It determines the objective for the making of necessary laws.","It enables the creation of political offices and a government.","It defines and limits the powers of government.","It secures social justice, social equality and social security."],"a":2,"x":"(a) It determines the objective for the making of necessary laws - Incorrect\nA constitution gives broad goals and a framework, but it does not determine the objective of every law.\n\n(b) It enables the creation of political offices and a government - Incorrect\nA constitution does create institutions of government, but this alone is not its chief purpose; even authoritarian systems create offices.\n\n(c) It defines and limits the powers of government - Correct\nThe central idea of constitutionalism is limited government. A constitution sets out what the government can do and, more importantly, places limits on its power through fundamental rights, separation of powers, judicial review and the rule of law, so that citizens are protected from arbitrary use of power. NCERT's 'Indian Constitution at Work' identifies limiting the powers of government as a key function.\n\n(d) It secures social justice, social equality and social security - Incorrect\nThese are important goals, reflected in the Preamble and Directive Principles, but they are not the defining purpose of a constitution in general."},{"i":"2023-35","y":2023,"n":35,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following organizations/bodies in India:\n1. The National Commission for Backward Classes\n2. The National Human Rights Commission\n3. The National Law Commission\n4. The National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission\nHow many of the above are constitutional bodies?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":0,"x":"Constitutional bodies are created directly by the Constitution; statutory bodies are created by Acts of Parliament; executive bodies are created by government orders.\n\n1. The National Commission for Backward Classes - Constitutional\nIt was originally a statutory body (1993), but the 102nd Amendment Act, 2018 gave it constitutional status under Article 338B.\n\n2. The National Human Rights Commission - Statutory\nIt was set up in 1993 under the Protection of Human Rights Act, 1993.\n\n3. The National Law Commission - Executive\nThe Law Commission is a non-statutory body constituted by a government order for a fixed term, to recommend legal reforms.\n\n4. The National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission - Statutory\nIt was set up under the Consumer Protection Act, 1986 (now the 2019 Act).\n\nOnly one is a constitutional body, so the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2023-36","y":2023,"n":36,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. If the election of the President of India is declared void by the Supreme Court of India, all acts done by him/her in the performance of duties of his/her office of President before the date of decision become invalid.\n2. Election for the post of the President of India can be postponed on the ground that some Legislative Assemblies have been dissolved and elections are yet to take place.\n3. When a Bill is presented to the President of India, the Constitution prescribes time limits within which he/she has to declare his/her assent.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":3,"x":"1. If the President's election is declared void, all acts done before the decision become invalid - Incorrect\nArticle 71(2) provides that if the election of the President is declared void by the Supreme Court, acts done by him or her in the exercise of the office before the date of the decision shall not be invalidated. This protects the continuity of government.\n\n2. The Presidential election can be postponed because some Assemblies are dissolved - Incorrect\nArticle 71(4) (as amended by the 11th Amendment, 1961) says the election cannot be challenged on the ground that the electoral college was incomplete because of vacancies. So the election must be held on time, even if some State Assemblies are dissolved.\n\n3. The Constitution prescribes time limits for the President to declare assent - Incorrect\nArticle 111 does not fix any time limit for the President to give or withhold assent to a bill. It only says that a non-money bill may be returned for reconsideration 'as soon as possible'. This absence of a time limit allows the 'pocket veto'.\n\nNone of the statements is correct, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2023-37","y":2023,"n":37,"s":0,"q":"With reference to Finance Bill and Money Bill in the Indian Parliament, consider the following statements:\n1. When the Lok Sabha transmits Finance Bill to the Rajya Sabha, it can amend or reject the Bill.\n2. When the Lok Sabha transmits Money Bill to the Rajya Sabha, it cannot amend or reject the Bill, it can only make recommendations.\n3. In the case of disagreement between the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha, there is no joint sitting for Money Bill, but a joint sitting becomes necessary for Finance Bill.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":1,"x":"A Money Bill (Article 110) deals only with taxation, borrowing, and similar matters. A Financial Bill under Article 117(1) contains money matters along with other matters; the question uses 'Finance Bill' in this sense.\n\n1. When a Finance Bill is sent to the Rajya Sabha, it can amend or reject it - Correct\nA Financial Bill under Article 117(1) can be introduced only in the Lok Sabha on the President's recommendation, but after that it is treated like an ordinary bill. The Rajya Sabha can amend or reject it.\n\n2. The Rajya Sabha cannot amend or reject a Money Bill; it can only make recommendations - Correct\nUnder Article 109, the Rajya Sabha must return a Money Bill within 14 days, with or without recommendations, which the Lok Sabha may accept or reject.\n\n3. There is no joint sitting for a Money Bill, but a joint sitting becomes necessary for a Finance Bill - Incorrect\nThe first part is right: there is no joint sitting for Money Bills (or Constitution Amendment Bills). But for a Financial Bill, a joint sitting under Article 108 is only possible if there is a deadlock; the President 'may' summon it. It is not 'necessary'.\n\nTwo statements are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-38","y":2023,"n":38,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nOnce the Central Government notifies an area as a 'Community Reserve'\n1. the Chief Wildlife Warden of the State becomes the governing authority of such forest\n2. hunting is not allowed in such area\n3. people of such area are allowed to collect non-timber forest produce\n4. people of such area are allowed traditional agricultural practices\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":1,"x":"Community Reserves were created by the Wild Life (Protection) Amendment Act, 2002 (Section 36C). A State Government may declare private or community land as a Community Reserve where the community or individuals have volunteered to conserve wildlife and habitat. (The question's premise that the Central Government notifies it is itself inaccurate.)\n\n1. The Chief Wildlife Warden becomes the governing authority - Incorrect\nA Community Reserve is managed by a Community Reserve Management Committee, consisting of at least five members nominated by the village panchayat or gram sabha and one representative of the State Forest or Wildlife Department.\n\n2. Hunting is not allowed in such an area - Correct\nThe provisions on hunting that apply to sanctuaries also apply to community reserves, so hunting of wild animals is prohibited.\n\n3. People are allowed to collect non-timber forest produce - Correct\nSince the land remains community or private land, people's customary uses, including collection of non-timber forest produce, continue as long as they are consistent with conservation.\n\n4. People are allowed traditional agricultural practices - Treated as incorrect (as per key)\nThe Act does not expressly guarantee traditional agriculture; any change in land use requires a resolution of the management committee and State approval.\n\nTwo statements are treated as correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-39","y":2023,"n":39,"s":0,"q":"With reference to 'Scheduled Areas' in India, consider the following statements:\n1. Within a State, the notification of an area as Scheduled Area takes place through an Order of the President.\n2. The largest administrative unit forming the Scheduled Area is the District and the lowest is the cluster of villages in the Block.\n3. The Chief Ministers of the concerned States are required to submit annual reports to the Union Home Ministry on the administration of Scheduled Areas in the States.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":1,"x":"1. Notification of Scheduled Areas takes place through an Order of the President - Correct\nUnder paragraph 6 of the Fifth Schedule, the President declares an area to be a Scheduled Area by order, after consultation with the Governor of the State. The President can also increase, decrease or alter such areas.\n\n2. The largest administrative unit forming a Scheduled Area is the District and the lowest is a cluster of villages in a Block - Correct\nAccording to the Ministry of Tribal Affairs, the largest unit that can be declared a Scheduled Area is a district and the smallest is a cluster of villages within a block. Scheduled Areas exist in ten States, including Jharkhand, Odisha, Chhattisgarh and Madhya Pradesh.\n\n3. The Chief Ministers submit annual reports to the Union Home Ministry on the administration of Scheduled Areas - Incorrect\nUnder paragraph 3 of the Fifth Schedule, the Governor submits a report to the President annually, or whenever required, on the administration of Scheduled Areas. The Chief Minister does not submit it.\n\nTwo statements are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-40","y":2023,"n":40,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: The Supreme Court of India has held in some judgements that the reservation policies made under Article 16(4) of the Constitution of India would be limited by Article 335 for maintenance of efficiency of administration.\nStatement-II: Article 335 of the Constitution of India defines the term 'efficiency of administration'.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":2,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nArticle 16(4) allows reservation in public appointments for backward classes not adequately represented. Article 335 says that the claims of Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes in appointments shall be considered consistently with the maintenance of efficiency of administration. In judgments such as Indra Sawhney (1992) and M. Nagaraj (2006), the Supreme Court held that reservation must be balanced against efficiency of administration under Article 335. (The 82nd Amendment, 2000, added a proviso allowing relaxation in qualifying marks for SCs and STs in promotions.)\n\nStatement II - Incorrect\nArticle 335 only refers to 'efficiency of administration'; it does not define the term. The meaning has been discussed by courts, for example in B.K. Pavitra v. Union of India (2019), which held that efficiency should be seen broadly, including inclusiveness.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."},{"i":"2023-41","y":2023,"n":41,"s":7,"q":"In which one of the following regions was Dhanyakataka, which flourished as a prominent Buddhist centre under the Mahasanghikas, located?","o":["Andhra","Gandhara","Kalinga","Magadha"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Andhra - Correct\nDhanyakataka (modern Dharanikota, near Amaravati in Guntur district, Andhra Pradesh) on the Krishna river was an important city under the Satavahanas and later the Ikshvakus. It was a major centre of the Mahasanghika schools of Buddhism (such as the Chaityaka, Purvashaila and Aparashaila sects). The great Amaravati stupa stood nearby, and tradition associates the philosopher Nagarjuna with the region.\n\n(b) Gandhara - Incorrect\nGandhara (Peshawar valley and Taxila) was a centre of the Sarvastivada school and Gandhara art.\n\n(c) Kalinga - Incorrect\nKalinga (Odisha) is associated with Ashoka's war and Jain sites like Udayagiri-Khandagiri.\n\n(d) Magadha - Incorrect\nMagadha (Bihar) contains Bodh Gaya, Rajgir and Nalanda, but Dhanyakataka was not there."},{"i":"2023-42","y":2023,"n":42,"s":10,"q":"With reference to ancient India, consider the following statements:\n1. The concept of Stupa is Buddhist in origin.\n2. Stupa was generally a repository of relics.\n3. Stupa was a votive and commemorative structure in Buddhist tradition.\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":1,"x":"1. The concept of the Stupa is Buddhist in origin - Incorrect\nThe word stupa means a heap or mound. Burial mounds (tumuli) for important persons existed in India before the Buddha, in the Vedic period. Buddhists adopted and developed this form into a monument; Jains also built stupas (e.g., at Mathura). So the concept is not purely Buddhist in origin.\n\n2. The Stupa was generally a repository of relics - Correct\nStupas typically enshrined relics: bodily remains of the Buddha or of important monks, or objects they used, placed in a casket in the central chamber. After the Buddha's death his relics were divided among eight kingdoms, and Ashoka is said to have redistributed them into many stupas.\n\n3. The Stupa was a votive and commemorative structure in Buddhist tradition - Correct\nMany stupas were built by devotees to gain merit (votive stupas) or to mark important events and places in the Buddha's life (commemorative stupas).\n\nTwo statements are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-43","y":2023,"n":43,"s":7,"q":"With reference to ancient South India, Korkai, Poompuhar and Muchiri were well known as","o":["capital cities","ports","centres of iron-and-steel making","shrines of Jain Tirthankaras"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Capital cities - Incorrect\nThe capitals of the Sangam-age kingdoms were Madurai (Pandyas), Uraiyur (Cholas) and Vanji or Karur (Cheras).\n\n(b) Ports - Correct\nKorkai, at the mouth of the Tamraparni river, was the chief port of the Pandyas and a centre of pearl fishing. Poompuhar (Kaveripattinam), at the mouth of the Kaveri, was the main Chola port, described in the Silappadikaram and the Pattinappalai. Muchiri (Muziris), on the Periyar river in Kerala, was the Chera port famous for trade in pepper with the Roman world; excavations at Pattanam are linked to it.\n\n(c) Centres of iron-and-steel making - Incorrect\nSouth India produced high-quality 'wootz' steel, but these places were known as ports.\n\n(d) Shrines of Jain Tirthankaras - Incorrect\nThese were not known as Jain shrines."},{"i":"2023-44","y":2023,"n":44,"s":7,"q":"Which one of the following explains the practice of 'Vattakirutal' as mentioned in Sangam poems?","o":["Kings employing women bodyguards","Learned persons assembling in royal courts to discuss religious and philosophical matters","Young girls keeping watch over agricultural fields and driving away birds and animals","A king defeated in a battle committing ritual suicide by starving himself to death"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Kings employing women bodyguards - Incorrect\nSome kings are described as having women guards, but this is not the meaning of vattakirutal.\n\n(b) Learned persons assembling in royal courts to discuss religious and philosophical matters - Incorrect\nThis describes the Sangam (assembly) of poets itself, not vattakirutal.\n\n(c) Young girls guarding fields and driving away birds and animals - Incorrect\nThis theme appears in Sangam love poetry (girls guarding millet fields in the kurinji landscape), but it is not vattakirutal.\n\n(d) A defeated king committing ritual suicide by starving himself to death - Correct\nVadakkiruttal (vattakirutal) means 'sitting facing north'. According to Sangam poems, a king or warrior who was defeated or dishonoured, for example wounded in the back, would sit facing north and fast unto death to preserve his honour. A famous example is the Chera king Perunjeral Adan, who did so after being wounded in the back in battle with Karikala Chola; the Chola king Kopperuncholan is also said to have done it."},{"i":"2023-45","y":2023,"n":45,"s":8,"q":"Consider the following dynasties:\n1. Hoysala\n2. Gahadavala\n3. Kakatiya\n4. Yadava\nHow many of the above dynasties established their kingdoms in early eighth century AD?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","None"],"a":3,"x":"1. Hoysala - Not early 8th century\nThe Hoysalas rose in the Malnad region of Karnataka in the early 11th century as feudatories of the Western Chalukyas and became independent under Vishnuvardhana in the 12th century. Capitals were Belur and Dwarasamudra (Halebidu).\n\n2. Gahadavala - Not early 8th century\nThe Gahadavalas established their rule over Kanauj and Varanasi in the late 11th century under Chandradeva (around 1090). Jayachandra was defeated by Muhammad Ghori in 1194.\n\n3. Kakatiya - Not early 8th century\nThe Kakatiyas were feudatories of the Chalukyas and became independent in the 12th century under Prola II and Rudradeva, with their capital at Warangal.\n\n4. Yadava - Not early 8th century\nThe Yadavas (Seunas) of Devagiri became independent in the late 12th century under Bhillama V (around 1187).\n\n(In the early 8th century, powers such as the Rashtrakutas, Gurjara-Pratiharas and Palas were rising.)\n\nNone of them was established in the early 8th century, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2023-46","y":2023,"n":46,"s":10,"q":"With reference to ancient Indian History, consider the following pairs:\n| | Literary work | Author\n| 1. | Devichandragupta | Bilhana\n| 2. | Hammira-Mahakavya | Nayachandra Suri\n| 3. | Milinda-panha | Nagarjuna\n| 4. | Nitivakyamrita | Somadeva Suri\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":1,"x":"1. Devichandragupta : Bilhana - Incorrect\nDevichandragupta, a Sanskrit play about the Gupta king Ramagupta and his brother Chandragupta II, was written by Vishakhadatta, who also wrote Mudrarakshasa. Bilhana wrote the Vikramankadevacharita, a biography of the Chalukya king Vikramaditya VI.\n\n2. Hammira-Mahakavya : Nayachandra Suri - Correct\nThe Hammira Mahakavya, by the Jain scholar Nayachandra Suri (15th century), describes the life of Hammira Deva, the Chauhan ruler of Ranthambhor, who fought Alauddin Khalji.\n\n3. Milinda-panha : Nagarjuna - Incorrect\nThe Milindapanha ('Questions of Milinda') records a dialogue between the Indo-Greek king Menander (Milinda) and the Buddhist monk Nagasena. It is not by Nagarjuna, the Madhyamaka philosopher.\n\n4. Nitivakyamrita : Somadeva Suri - Correct\nThe Nitivakyamrita is a 10th-century work on polity by the Jain scholar Somadeva Suri, who also wrote Yashastilaka.\n\nTwo pairs are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-47","y":2023,"n":47,"s":7,"q":"\"Souls are not only the property of animal and plant life, but also of rocks, running water and many other natural objects not looked on as living by other religious sects.\" The above statement reflects one of the core beliefs of which one of the following religious sects of ancient India?","o":["Buddhism","Jainism","Shaivism","Vaishnavism"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Buddhism - Incorrect\nBuddhism does not accept a permanent soul (the doctrine of anatta, 'no-self'), let alone souls in rocks or water.\n\n(b) Jainism - Correct\nJainism teaches that the universe is full of jivas (souls). Souls exist not only in humans, animals and plants but also in earth, water, fire and air, which are one-sensed beings (ekendriya). This belief is the basis of the Jain emphasis on ahimsa, which requires avoiding harm even to such beings, for example by filtering water or sweeping the path. NCERT's Themes in Indian History Part I quotes this belief.\n\n(c) Shaivism - Incorrect\nShaivism is devotion to Shiva and does not hold this belief about souls in rocks and water.\n\n(d) Vaishnavism - Incorrect\nVaishnavism is devotion to Vishnu and his avatars; it does not teach that rocks and running water have souls."},{"i":"2023-48","y":2023,"n":48,"s":8,"q":"Who among the following rulers of Vijayanagara Empire constructed a large dam across Tungabhadra River and a canal-cum-aqueduct several kilometres long from the river to the capital city?","o":["Devaraya I","Mallikarjuna","Vira Vijaya","Virupaksha"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Devaraya I - Correct\nDevaraya I (1406-1422) of the Sangama dynasty built a dam across the Tungabhadra river and a canal or aqueduct about 24 km long to carry water from the river into the capital city of Vijayanagara. This is recorded by foreign travellers such as Nuniz and mentioned in NCERT's Themes in Indian History Part II. The water supply made agriculture and the growth of the city possible.\n\n(b) Mallikarjuna - Incorrect\nMallikarjuna (1446-1465) was a later, weaker Sangama ruler during whose time the empire faced attacks.\n\n(c) Vira Vijaya - Incorrect\nVira Vijaya Bukka ruled only briefly (around 1422-1424) and is not known for this work.\n\n(d) Virupaksha - Incorrect\nVirupaksha Raya II was the last Sangama ruler, whose weak rule led to the Saluva usurpation."},{"i":"2023-49","y":2023,"n":49,"s":8,"q":"Who among the following rulers of medieval Gujarat surrendered Diu to Portuguese?","o":["Ahmad Shah","Mahmud Begarha","Bahadur Shah","Muhammad Shah"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Ahmad Shah - Incorrect\nAhmad Shah I (1411-1442) founded the city of Ahmedabad and consolidated the Gujarat Sultanate. The Portuguese had not yet arrived.\n\n(b) Mahmud Begarha - Incorrect\nMahmud Begarha (1458-1511), with the Egyptian (Mamluk) fleet, fought the Portuguese at Chaul (1508) and lost to them at the Battle of Diu (1509). He did not surrender Diu.\n\n(c) Bahadur Shah - Correct\nBahadur Shah of Gujarat (1526-1537), threatened by the Mughal emperor Humayun, sought Portuguese help. He ceded Bassein (1534) and by the treaty of 1535 allowed the Portuguese to build a fort at Diu. When he later tried to regain control, he was killed in 1537 during a meeting with the Portuguese off Diu, and Diu remained Portuguese until 1961.\n\n(d) Muhammad Shah - Incorrect\nOther Gujarat rulers named Muhammad Shah are not associated with surrendering Diu."},{"i":"2023-50","y":2023,"n":50,"s":9,"q":"By which one of the following Acts was the Governor General of Bengal designated as the Governor General of India?","o":["The Regulating Act","The Pitt's India Act","The Charter Act of 1793","The Charter Act of 1833"],"a":3,"x":"(a) The Regulating Act - Incorrect\nThe Regulating Act, 1773 designated the Governor of Bengal as the 'Governor-General of Bengal' (Warren Hastings was the first), with an Executive Council of four, and set up a Supreme Court at Calcutta.\n\n(b) Pitt's India Act - Incorrect\nPitt's India Act, 1784 created the Board of Control to supervise the Company's political affairs, creating a system of 'dual government'.\n\n(c) The Charter Act of 1793 - Incorrect\nIt renewed the Company's trade monopoly for twenty more years.\n\n(d) The Charter Act of 1833 - Correct\nThe Charter Act of 1833 made the Governor-General of Bengal the 'Governor-General of India', with full civil and military powers over all British territories in India. Lord William Bentinck was the first Governor-General of India. The Act also centralised legislative powers, ended the Company's commercial activities, and added a law member (Macaulay) to the council. (The title 'Viceroy' came with the Government of India Act, 1858.)"},{"i":"2023-51","y":2023,"n":51,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements in relation to Janani Suraksha Yojana:\n1. It is a safe motherhood intervention of the State Health Departments.\n2. Its objective is to reduce maternal and neonatal mortality among poor pregnant women.\n3. It aims to promote institutional delivery among poor pregnant women.\n4. Its objective includes providing public health facilities to sick infants up to one year of age.\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":1,"x":"Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY), launched in 2005 under the National Rural Health Mission (now National Health Mission), gives cash assistance to pregnant women for delivering in health facilities, with ASHA workers as the link between women and the health system.\n\n1. It is a safe motherhood intervention of the State Health Departments - Incorrect\nJSY is a Centrally Sponsored Scheme of the Union Ministry of Health and Family Welfare under the National Health Mission. States implement it, but it is not their own intervention.\n\n2. Its objective is to reduce maternal and neonatal mortality among poor pregnant women - Correct\nThis is the main objective of the scheme.\n\n3. It aims to promote institutional delivery among poor pregnant women - Correct\nThe cash incentive encourages women to deliver in hospitals and health centres, which has sharply increased institutional deliveries.\n\n4. It includes providing public health facilities to sick infants up to one year of age - Incorrect\nFree treatment for sick infants up to one year is provided under the Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (JSSK, 2011), not JSY.\n\nTwo statements are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-52","y":2023,"n":52,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements in the context of interventions being undertaken under Anaemia Mukt Bharat Strategy:\n1. It provides prophylactic calcium supplementation for pre-school children, adolescents and pregnant women.\n2. It runs a campaign for delayed cord clamping at the time of child-birth.\n3. It provides for periodic deworming to children and adolescents.\n4. It addresses non-nutritional causes of anaemia in endemic pockets with special focus on malaria, hemoglobinopathies and fluorosis.\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":2,"x":"Anaemia Mukt Bharat was launched in 2018 under POSHAN Abhiyaan with a '6x6x6' strategy: six target groups, six interventions and six institutional mechanisms.\n\n1. It provides prophylactic calcium supplementation - Incorrect\nThe scheme provides prophylactic iron and folic acid (IFA) supplementation to children, adolescents, women of reproductive age, and pregnant and lactating women, not calcium (calcium is given separately in antenatal care).\n\n2. It runs a campaign for delayed cord clamping at childbirth - Correct\nDelaying the clamping of the umbilical cord by at least three minutes after birth increases the baby's iron stores. It is one of the six interventions.\n\n3. It provides periodic deworming to children and adolescents - Correct\nBiannual deworming with albendazole (linked to National Deworming Day) is included, because worm infections cause anaemia.\n\n4. It addresses non-nutritional causes of anaemia in endemic pockets, focusing on malaria, haemoglobinopathies and fluorosis - Correct\nTesting and treatment of malaria, screening for haemoglobinopathies like sickle cell disease and thalassaemia, and action on fluorosis in endemic areas are part of the strategy.\n\nThree statements are correct, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2023-53","y":2023,"n":53,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Carbon fibres are used in the manufacture of components used in automobiles and aircrafts.\n2. Carbon fibres once used cannot be recycled.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":0,"x":"1. Carbon fibres are used in making components of automobiles and aircraft - Correct\nCarbon fibre reinforced polymers are very strong, stiff and light. They are used in aircraft (for example, about half of the Boeing 787 and Airbus A350 structures by weight), racing and luxury cars, wind turbine blades, sports equipment and satellites, where reducing weight saves fuel.\n\n2. Carbon fibres, once used, cannot be recycled - Incorrect\nCarbon fibre composites can be recycled. Methods include pyrolysis (heating to burn off the resin), solvolysis (dissolving the resin with chemicals) and mechanical grinding. The recovered fibres are used in new products. Recycling is costly and still developing, but it is possible.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 only."},{"i":"2023-54","y":2023,"n":54,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following actions:\n1. Detection of car crash/collision which results in the deployment of airbags almost instantaneously\n2. Detection of accidental free fall of a laptop towards the ground which results in the immediate turning off of the hard drive\n3. Detection of the tilt of the smartphone which results in the rotation of display between portrait and landscape mode\nIn how many of the above actions is the function of accelerometer required?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":2,"x":"An accelerometer measures acceleration, including sudden changes in speed, free fall and orientation relative to gravity. It is usually a tiny micro-electro-mechanical system (MEMS) sensor.\n\n1. Detection of a car crash leading to airbag deployment - Accelerometer required\nCrash sensors detect the sudden deceleration of a collision and trigger the airbag inflator within milliseconds.\n\n2. Detection of free fall of a laptop to turn off the hard drive - Accelerometer required\nWhen the sensor detects free fall, the laptop parks the hard disk's read-write head to prevent damage on impact.\n\n3. Detection of smartphone tilt to rotate the display - Accelerometer required\nThe accelerometer senses the direction of gravity and tells the phone whether it is held upright or sideways, so the screen switches between portrait and landscape.\n\nAll three need an accelerometer, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2023-55","y":2023,"n":55,"s":2,"q":"With reference to the role of biofilters in Recirculating Aquaculture System, consider the following statements:\n1. Biofilters provide waste treatment by removing uneaten fish feed.\n2. Biofilters convert ammonia present in fish waste to nitrate.\n3. Biofilters increase phosphorus as nutrient for fish in water.\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":1,"x":"In a Recirculating Aquaculture System (RAS), water from fish tanks is cleaned and reused. Mechanical filters remove solid waste, and biofilters use bacteria to treat dissolved waste.\n\n1. Biofilters provide waste treatment by removing uneaten fish feed - Correct (as per key)\nBiofilter media provide a surface for beneficial bacteria that break down organic waste, including dissolved waste from uneaten feed and fish excreta. (Strictly, solid particles of uneaten feed are removed mainly by mechanical filters, so this statement is somewhat debatable, but the key treats it as correct.)\n\n2. Biofilters convert ammonia present in fish waste to nitrate - Correct\nFish excrete ammonia, which is toxic even at low levels. Nitrifying bacteria in the biofilter convert ammonia into nitrite (Nitrosomonas) and then into much less harmful nitrate (Nitrobacter). This is the main function of biofilters.\n\n3. Biofilters increase phosphorus as a nutrient for fish in water - Incorrect\nBiofilters do not add phosphorus for fish. Fish get nutrients from their feed, and phosphorus accumulation in water is a waste problem, not a benefit.\n\nTwo statements are treated as correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-56","y":2023,"n":56,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Objects in space | Description\n| 1. | Cepheids | Giant clouds of dust and gas in space\n| 2. | Nebulae | Stars which brighten and dim periodically\n| 3. | Pulsars | Neutron stars that are formed when massive stars run out of fuel and collapse\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":0,"x":"1. Cepheids : Giant clouds of dust and gas in space - Incorrect\nCepheids are pulsating variable stars that regularly brighten and dim over a fixed period. The period is linked to their true brightness, so astronomers use them as 'standard candles' to measure distances to galaxies (as Edwin Hubble did).\n\n2. Nebulae : Stars which brighten and dim periodically - Incorrect\nNebulae are huge clouds of dust and gas in space, where new stars are born (for example the Orion Nebula). The descriptions for 1 and 2 have been swapped.\n\n3. Pulsars : Neutron stars formed when massive stars run out of fuel and collapse - Correct\nWhen a massive star runs out of fuel, its core collapses in a supernova, leaving a very dense neutron star. A rapidly spinning, highly magnetised neutron star that emits beams of radiation seen as regular pulses is called a pulsar.\n\nOnly one pair is correct, so the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2023-57","y":2023,"n":57,"s":3,"q":"Which one of the following countries has its own Satellite Navigation System?","o":["Australia","Canada","Israel","Japan"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Australia - Incorrect\nAustralia does not have its own navigation satellite system. It is developing SouthPAN, an augmentation system that improves GPS accuracy, along with New Zealand.\n\n(b) Canada - Incorrect\nCanada has no independent satellite navigation system.\n\n(c) Israel - Incorrect\nIsrael has no satellite navigation system of its own.\n\n(d) Japan - Correct\nJapan operates the Quasi-Zenith Satellite System (QZSS), called 'Michibiki'. It is a regional satellite navigation system with satellites in highly inclined orbits that stay high over Japan, improving positioning in cities with tall buildings. Other satellite navigation systems are GPS (USA), GLONASS (Russia), Galileo (European Union), BeiDou (China) and NavIC (India)."},{"i":"2023-58","y":2023,"n":58,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Ballistic missiles are jet-propelled at subsonic speeds throughout their flights, while cruise missiles are rocket-powered only in the initial phase of flight.\n2. Agni-V is a medium-range supersonic cruise missile, while BrahMos is a solid-fuelled intercontinental ballistic missile.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":3,"x":"1. Ballistic missiles are jet-propelled at subsonic speeds throughout their flight, while cruise missiles are rocket-powered only in the initial phase - Incorrect\nThe statement reverses the two. A ballistic missile is powered by rockets only during the initial boost phase; after that it follows a free, arc-shaped (ballistic) trajectory, often leaving the atmosphere, under gravity. A cruise missile is powered throughout its flight by jet engines (turbojet, turbofan or ramjet), flies within the atmosphere at a low altitude, and can be subsonic, supersonic or hypersonic.\n\n2. Agni-V is a medium-range supersonic cruise missile, while BrahMos is a solid-fuelled intercontinental ballistic missile - Incorrect\nThis is also reversed. Agni-V is a three-stage, solid-fuelled ballistic missile with a range of over 5,000 km, and in 2024 it was tested with MIRV technology (Mission Divyastra). BrahMos, developed jointly by India and Russia, is a supersonic cruise missile flying at about Mach 2.8-3.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Neither 1 nor 2."},{"i":"2023-59","y":2023,"n":59,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements regarding mercury pollution:\n1. Gold mining activity is a source of mercury pollution in the world.\n2. Coal-based thermal power plants cause mercury pollution.\n3. There is no known safe level of exposure to mercury.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":1,"x":"1. Gold mining is a source of mercury pollution in the world - Correct\nAccording to UNEP, artisanal and small-scale gold mining is the largest source of man-made mercury emissions worldwide. Miners mix mercury with ore to form an amalgam and then burn it off, releasing mercury into the air and water. The Minamata Convention on Mercury (2013) seeks to control this.\n\n2. Coal-based thermal power plants cause mercury pollution - Correct\nCoal contains traces of mercury, which is released into the air when coal is burnt. Coal combustion is the second-largest source of mercury emissions.\n\n3. There is no known safe level of exposure to mercury - Incorrect (as per key)\nThe WHO and regulators have set tolerable intake limits for mercury and methylmercury (for example a provisional tolerable weekly intake). The phrase 'no known safe level' is used by the WHO for lead, not mercury. Mercury is highly toxic, affecting the nervous system, kidneys and foetal development, but safety thresholds are defined.\n\nTwo statements are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-60","y":2023,"n":60,"s":5,"q":"With reference to green hydrogen, consider the following statements:\n1. It can be used directly as a fuel for internal combustion.\n2. It can be blended with natural gas and used as fuel for heat or power generation.\n3. It can be used in the hydrogen fuel cell to run vehicles.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":2,"x":"Green hydrogen is produced by electrolysis of water using renewable electricity.\n\n1. It can be used directly as a fuel for internal combustion - Correct\nHydrogen can be burnt in modified internal combustion engines (hydrogen ICE vehicles), producing mainly water vapour, although some nitrogen oxides may form.\n\n2. It can be blended with natural gas and used for heat or power generation - Correct\nHydrogen can be mixed with natural gas (usually up to about 5-20 percent) in pipelines and used in boilers, turbines and cooking. In India, NTPC and GAIL have run pilot projects blending hydrogen into city gas networks.\n\n3. It can be used in hydrogen fuel cells to run vehicles - Correct\nFuel cell electric vehicles generate electricity from hydrogen and oxygen, emitting only water. Examples include the Toyota Mirai and hydrogen buses tested in India (such as by Indian Oil in Delhi and in Leh).\n\nAll three statements are correct, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2023-61","y":2023,"n":61,"s":11,"q":"Consider the following countries:\n1. Bulgaria\n2. Czech Republic\n3. Hungary\n4. Latvia\n5. Lithuania\n6. Romania\nHow many of the above-mentioned countries share a land border with Ukraine?","o":["Only two","Only three","Only four","Only five"],"a":0,"x":"Ukraine shares land borders with seven countries: Belarus, Russia, Poland, Slovakia, Hungary, Romania and Moldova.\n\n1. Bulgaria - No\nBulgaria lies south of Romania, across the Danube; it faces Ukraine only across the Black Sea.\n\n2. Czech Republic - No\nSlovakia lies between the Czech Republic and Ukraine.\n\n3. Hungary - Yes\nHungary borders Ukraine's Zakarpattia region in the west.\n\n4. Latvia - No\nLatvia borders Belarus and Russia but not Ukraine.\n\n5. Lithuania - No\nLithuania borders Belarus and Russia (Kaliningrad) but not Ukraine.\n\n6. Romania - Yes\nRomania borders Ukraine in two sections, in the north-west and along the Danube delta in the south.\n\nTwo of the listed countries share a land border with Ukraine, so the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2023-62","y":2023,"n":62,"s":5,"q":"With reference to the Earth's atmosphere, which one of the following statements is correct?","o":["The total amount of insolation received at the equator is roughly about 10 times of that received at the poles.","Infrared rays constitute roughly two-thirds of insolation.","Infrared waves are largely absorbed by water vapour that is concentrated in the lower atmosphere.","Infrared waves are a part of visible spectrum of electromagnetic waves of solar radiation."],"a":2,"x":"(a) Insolation at the equator is roughly 10 times that at the poles - Incorrect\nAnnual insolation at the equator is roughly 2.5 times that received at the poles, not 10 times. The poles receive long hours of sunlight in summer, but at a low angle.\n\n(b) Infrared rays make up roughly two-thirds of insolation - Incorrect\nOf the Sun's energy reaching the Earth, about 49 percent is infrared, about 43 percent is visible light and about 7 percent is ultraviolet. So infrared is about half, not two-thirds.\n\n(c) Infrared waves are largely absorbed by water vapour concentrated in the lower atmosphere - Correct\nWater vapour, which is mostly found in the lower troposphere, is the most important absorber of infrared radiation, along with carbon dioxide. This is why humid places stay warmer at night than dry deserts.\n\n(d) Infrared waves are part of the visible spectrum - Incorrect\nInfrared radiation has longer wavelengths than red light and lies outside the visible spectrum; we feel it as heat but cannot see it."},{"i":"2023-63","y":2023,"n":63,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: The soil in tropical rain forests is rich in nutrients.\nStatement-II: The high temperature and moisture of tropical rain forests cause dead organic matter in the soil to decompose quickly.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":3,"x":"Statement I - Incorrect\nSoils of tropical rainforests are generally poor in nutrients. Heavy, year-round rainfall washes nutrients down and out of the soil (leaching), leaving acidic, red lateritic soils rich in iron and aluminium oxides. Most of the nutrients in a rainforest are stored in the living plants, not in the soil. That is why cleared rainforest land quickly loses fertility.\n\nStatement II - Correct\nHigh temperatures and humidity in rainforests support intense activity of decomposers such as bacteria, fungi and termites. Dead leaves and organic matter decompose very quickly, and the released nutrients are rapidly taken up by plants, so little humus builds up in the soil.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."},{"i":"2023-64","y":2023,"n":64,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: The temperature contrast between continents and oceans is greater during summer than in winter.\nStatement-II: The specific heat of water is more than that of land surface.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":3,"x":"Statement I - Incorrect (as per key)\nThe temperature contrast between continents and oceans is greater in winter, when continental interiors become extremely cold (for example Siberia at about -40°C), while oceans remain relatively mild. The sharper bending of isotherms in January over the Northern Hemisphere continents reflects this.\n\nStatement II - Correct\nWater has a much higher specific heat than land (about 4.18 joules per gram per °C, compared with about 0.8 for rock and soil). So water heats up and cools down slowly, while land heats and cools quickly. Water is also transparent, allows mixing and heat transfer to depth, and loses heat through evaporation. This is the basis of land-sea temperature differences and monsoons.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."},{"i":"2023-65","y":2023,"n":65,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. In a seismograph, P waves are recorded earlier than S waves.\n2. In P waves, the individual particles vibrate to and fro in the direction of wave propagation whereas in S waves, the particles vibrate up and down at right angles to the direction of wave propagation.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"1. In a seismograph, P waves are recorded earlier than S waves - Correct\nPrimary (P) waves are the fastest seismic waves, travelling at about 6-8 km per second in the crust. Secondary (S) waves travel more slowly, about 3.5-4.5 km per second. So P waves always reach a seismograph first. The time gap between their arrival is used to find the distance to the earthquake.\n\n2. In P waves particles vibrate to and fro in the direction of wave travel, while in S waves particles vibrate at right angles to the direction of travel - Correct\nP waves are longitudinal (compressional) waves, like sound waves, and can pass through solids, liquids and gases. S waves are transverse waves and can pass only through solids. The fact that S waves do not pass through the outer core showed that the outer core is liquid (creating the S-wave shadow zone).\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2023-66","y":2023,"n":66,"s":5,"q":"With reference to coal-based thermal power plants in India, consider the following statements:\n1. None of them uses seawater.\n2. None of them is set up in water-stressed district.\n3. None of them is privately owned.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":3,"x":"1. None of them uses seawater - Incorrect\nSeveral coastal coal-based plants use seawater for cooling, for example Mundra in Gujarat, Tuticorin in Tamil Nadu, Udupi in Karnataka and Krishnapatnam in Andhra Pradesh. Seawater is used after treatment in cooling towers or through once-through cooling.\n\n2. None of them is set up in a water-stressed district - Incorrect\nStudies (for example by the World Resources Institute) have found that a large share of India's thermal power capacity is located in water-stressed or highly water-stressed areas, and some plants have had to shut down due to water shortage.\n\n3. None of them is privately owned - Incorrect\nMany coal-based plants are owned by private companies such as Adani Power, Tata Power, JSW Energy, Reliance Power and Vedanta. Private companies own a substantial share of India's thermal capacity.\n\nNone of the statements is correct, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2023-67","y":2023,"n":67,"s":3,"q":"'Wolbachia method' is sometimes talked about with reference to which one of the following?","o":["Controlling the viral diseases spread by mosquitoes","Converting crop residues into packing material","Producing biodegradable plastics","Producing biochar from thermo-chemical conversion of biomass"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Controlling viral diseases spread by mosquitoes - Correct\nWolbachia is a naturally occurring bacterium found in many insects but not normally in Aedes aegypti, the mosquito that spreads dengue, Zika, chikungunya and yellow fever. When mosquitoes are infected with Wolbachia, the viruses cannot multiply well inside them, so they are less able to transmit disease. The infected mosquitoes are released and pass the bacteria to their offspring. The World Mosquito Program has used this in several countries, and ICMR's Vector Control Research Centre in Puducherry has developed Wolbachia-infected mosquito strains.\n\n(b) Converting crop residues into packing material - Incorrect\nThis is unrelated to Wolbachia.\n\n(c) Producing biodegradable plastics - Incorrect\nBioplastics are made from starch, polylactic acid and similar materials, not by this method.\n\n(d) Producing biochar from biomass - Incorrect\nBiochar is made by pyrolysis of biomass."},{"i":"2023-68","y":2023,"n":68,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following activities:\n1. Spreading finely ground basalt rock on farmlands extensively\n2. Increasing the alkalinity of oceans by adding lime\n3. Capturing carbon dioxide released by various industries and pumping it into abandoned subterranean mines in the form of carbonated waters\nHow many of the above activities are often considered and discussed for carbon capture and sequestration?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":2,"x":"1. Spreading finely ground basalt rock on farmland - Discussed for carbon sequestration\nThis is called enhanced rock weathering. Basalt contains minerals that react with carbon dioxide and rainwater to form stable carbonates or bicarbonates, which are washed into the oceans and stored for thousands of years. It may also improve soil fertility.\n\n2. Increasing the alkalinity of oceans by adding lime - Discussed for carbon sequestration\nOcean alkalinity enhancement adds alkaline substances such as lime or crushed minerals to seawater. This allows the ocean to absorb more CO2 from the atmosphere and also reduces ocean acidification.\n\n3. Capturing industrial CO2 and pumping it into abandoned mines as carbonated water - Discussed for carbon sequestration\nThis is a form of carbon capture and storage. CO2 captured from industries is dissolved in water and injected underground, where it reacts with rocks and turns into solid minerals, as in the CarbFix project in Iceland.\n\nAll three are discussed, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2023-69","y":2023,"n":69,"s":3,"q":"'Aerial metagenomics' best refers to which one of the following situations?","o":["Collecting DNA samples from air in a habitat at one go","Understanding the genetic makeup of avian species of a habitat","Using air-borne devices to collect blood samples from moving animals","Sending drones to inaccessible areas to collect plant and animal samples from land surfaces and water bodies"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Collecting DNA samples from air in a habitat at one go - Correct\nAerial or airborne metagenomics (a form of environmental DNA sampling) involves collecting air samples with filters and sequencing the tiny fragments of DNA shed by plants, animals, fungi and microbes (through skin cells, hair, pollen, spores and so on). This lets scientists identify many species present in an area at once without seeing or catching them. Studies in zoos and forests have detected dozens of animal species this way.\n\n(b) Understanding the genetic makeup of bird species of a habitat - Incorrect\nThis refers to avian genetics, not aerial metagenomics.\n\n(c) Using airborne devices to collect blood samples from moving animals - Incorrect\nThis is not how the technique works.\n\n(d) Sending drones to inaccessible areas to collect plant and animal samples - Incorrect\nDrones may be used to collect samples, but that is not the definition of aerial metagenomics."},{"i":"2023-70","y":2023,"n":70,"s":3,"q":"'Microsatellite DNA' is used in the case of which one of the following?","o":["Studying the evolutionary relationships among various species of fauna","Stimulating 'stem cells' to transform into diverse functional tissues","Promoting clonal propagation of horticultural plants","Assessing the efficacy of drugs by conducting series of drug trials in a population"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Studying the evolutionary relationships among various species of fauna - Correct\nMicrosatellites (also called short tandem repeats or simple sequence repeats) are short DNA sequences of 1-6 base pairs repeated many times. Because the number of repeats varies greatly between individuals and populations, they are highly useful genetic markers. They are used to study genetic diversity, population structure, evolutionary relationships, parentage, DNA fingerprinting and wildlife forensics, for example identifying individual tigers from scat or tracing the source of poached animals.\n\n(b) Stimulating stem cells to transform into functional tissues - Incorrect\nThis involves growth factors and signalling molecules, not microsatellite DNA.\n\n(c) Promoting clonal propagation of horticultural plants - Incorrect\nClonal propagation is done through tissue culture and grafting. (Microsatellites can be used to check the genetic purity of clones, but they do not promote propagation.)\n\n(d) Assessing drug efficacy through drug trials - Incorrect\nDrug trials do not use microsatellites for this purpose."},{"i":"2023-71","y":2023,"n":71,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements with reference to India:\n1. According to the 'Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises Development (MSMED) Act, 2006', the 'medium enterprises' are those with investments in plant and machinery between ₹15 crore and ₹25 crore.\n2. All bank loans to the Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises qualify under the priority sector.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":1,"x":"1. Under the MSMED Act, 2006, medium enterprises have investment in plant and machinery between 15 crore and 25 crore rupees - Incorrect\nUnder the original 2006 definition, medium manufacturing enterprises had investment of more than 5 crore and up to 10 crore rupees. From July 2020 a composite definition was adopted: a medium enterprise has investment up to 50 crore rupees and turnover up to 250 crore rupees. (These limits were raised again in 2025 to 125 crore and 500 crore.) The figures 15-25 crore do not match any definition.\n\n2. All bank loans to MSMEs qualify under priority sector - Correct\nUnder the RBI's Master Directions on Priority Sector Lending, all bank loans to MSMEs (as defined under the MSMED Act) qualify as priority sector lending, without any upper limit on the loan amount. Within this, banks also have a sub-target of 7.5 percent of lending to micro enterprises.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2023-72","y":2023,"n":72,"s":1,"q":"With reference to Central Bank digital currencies, consider the following statements:\n1. It is possible to make payments in a digital currency without using US dollar or SWIFT system.\n2. A digital currency can be distributed with a condition programmed into it such as a time-frame for spending it.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"1. It is possible to make payments in a digital currency without using the US dollar or SWIFT - Correct\nCentral Bank Digital Currencies can be exchanged directly between countries' central banks on shared digital platforms, bypassing the correspondent banking network, SWIFT messaging and the US dollar as an intermediary. For example, Project mBridge connects the central banks of China, Hong Kong, Thailand, the UAE and Saudi Arabia for cross-border payments in their own CBDCs. This is why some countries see CBDCs as a way to reduce dependence on the dollar and sanctions.\n\n2. A digital currency can be distributed with a programmed condition, such as a time-frame for spending - Correct\nCBDCs are programmable: conditions can be coded into them, such as an expiry date, or a restriction that the money can be spent only on certain items (like fertiliser or education). The RBI has tested programmability in e-Rupee pilots, which can help in targeted delivery of subsidies.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2023-73","y":2023,"n":73,"s":1,"q":"In the context of finance, the term 'beta' refers to","o":["the process of simultaneous buying and selling of an asset from different platforms","an investment strategy of a portfolio manager to balance risk versus reward","a type of systemic risk that arises where perfect hedging is not possible","a numeric value that measures the fluctuations of a stock to changes in the overall stock market"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Simultaneous buying and selling of an asset on different platforms - Incorrect\nThis describes arbitrage, which profits from price differences between markets.\n\n(b) An investment strategy to balance risk versus reward - Incorrect\nThis describes asset allocation or portfolio management.\n\n(c) A type of systemic risk that arises where perfect hedging is not possible - Incorrect\nThis describes basis risk (the risk that a hedge does not move exactly with the asset).\n\n(d) A numeric value that measures the fluctuations of a stock relative to the overall market - Correct\nBeta measures how much a stock's price moves compared with the market index (such as the Nifty or Sensex). A beta of 1 means the stock moves in line with the market; a beta above 1 means it is more volatile (for example, 1.5 means it tends to rise or fall 50 percent more than the market); a beta below 1 means it is less volatile. It is used in the Capital Asset Pricing Model to estimate expected returns."},{"i":"2023-74","y":2023,"n":74,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The Self-Help Group (SHG) programme was originally initiated by the State Bank of India by providing microcredit to the financially deprived.\n2. In an SHG, all members of a group take responsibility for a loan that an individual member takes.\n3. The Regional Rural Banks and Scheduled Commercial Banks support SHGs.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":1,"x":"1. The SHG programme was originally initiated by the State Bank of India - Incorrect\nThe Self-Help Group-Bank Linkage Programme was launched as a pilot by NABARD in 1992, linking 500 SHGs with banks. It grew into the world's largest microfinance programme. SHGs are also promoted under the National Rural Livelihoods Mission (DAY-NRLM).\n\n2. In an SHG, all members take responsibility for a loan taken by an individual member - Correct\nSHGs work on the principle of joint liability and peer pressure. Members save together, lend to each other and take collective responsibility for repayment, which substitutes for collateral and results in high repayment rates.\n\n3. Regional Rural Banks and Scheduled Commercial Banks support SHGs - Correct\nCommercial banks, RRBs and cooperative banks lend to SHGs under the bank linkage programme. SHG loans count as priority sector lending.\n\nTwo statements are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-75","y":2023,"n":75,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: India's public sector health care system largely focuses on curative care with limited preventive, promotive and rehabilitative care.\nStatement-II: Under India's decentralized approach to health care delivery, the States are primarily responsible for organizing health services.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":1,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nIndia's public health system focuses largely on curative care (treating illness in hospitals), with relatively weak preventive care (vaccination, sanitation), promotive care (nutrition and healthy lifestyles) and rehabilitative care. The National Health Policy 2017 and the Ayushman Bharat Health and Wellness Centres (now Ayushman Arogya Mandirs) aim to correct this imbalance.\n\nStatement II - Correct\nHealth is a State subject (Entry 6 of the State List, 'Public health and sanitation; hospitals and dispensaries'). States are primarily responsible for organising and delivering health services, while the Centre supports them through schemes like the National Health Mission.\n\nHowever, decentralisation of health care to the States does not explain why the system is focused on curative care. That bias arises from factors like demand patterns, funding priorities and the design of the system. So Statement II is not the explanation for Statement I.\n\nHence the answer is (b) Both statements are correct but Statement II is not the correct explanation for Statement I."},{"i":"2023-76","y":2023,"n":76,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: According to the United Nations' 'World Water Development Report, 2022', India extracts more than a quarter of the world's groundwater withdrawal each year.\nStatement-II: India needs to extract more than a quarter of the world's groundwater each year to satisfy the drinking water and sanitation needs of almost 18% of world's population living in its territory.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":2,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nThe UN World Water Development Report 2022, titled 'Groundwater: Making the Invisible Visible', states that India is the world's largest user of groundwater. It extracts about 251 cubic km a year, roughly a quarter of the world's total groundwater withdrawal, more than the USA and China combined.\n\nStatement II - Incorrect\nMost of India's groundwater, about 89-90 percent, is used for irrigation, especially for water-intensive crops like paddy and sugarcane, helped by free or subsidised electricity for pumps. Only a small share goes to domestic use (drinking and sanitation) and industry. So it is wrong to say India needs to extract so much groundwater to meet the drinking water and sanitation needs of its people (about 18 percent of the world's population).\n\nHence the answer is (c) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."},{"i":"2023-77","y":2023,"n":77,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. According to the Constitution of India, the Central Government has a duty to protect States from internal disturbances.\n2. The Constitution of India exempts the States from providing legal counsel to a person being held for preventive detention.\n3. According to the Prevention of Terrorism Act, 2002, confession of the accused before the police cannot be used as evidence.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":1,"x":"1. The Central Government has a duty to protect States from internal disturbances - Correct\nArticle 355 states that it is the duty of the Union to protect every State against external aggression and internal disturbance, and to ensure that the government of every State is carried on in accordance with the Constitution. This provision is the basis for deploying central forces and, in extreme cases, imposing President's Rule under Article 356.\n\n2. The Constitution exempts the States from providing legal counsel to a person held in preventive detention - Correct\nArticle 22(1) and (2) give arrested persons the right to be informed of the grounds of arrest, to consult a lawyer of their choice and to be produced before a magistrate within 24 hours. However, Article 22(3)(b) says these protections do not apply to persons arrested under a preventive detention law. Detainees have separate safeguards under Article 22(4)-(7), such as review by an Advisory Board.\n\n3. Under the Prevention of Terrorism Act, 2002, a confession before the police cannot be used as evidence - Incorrect\nUnder normal law (the Evidence Act), confessions to the police are not admissible. However, Section 32 of POTA made confessions recorded by a police officer of the rank of Superintendent of Police or above admissible as evidence, which was one of its most criticised provisions. POTA was repealed in 2004.\n\nTwo statements are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-78","y":2023,"n":78,"s":11,"q":"Which one of the following countries has been suffering from decades of civil strife and food shortages and was in news in the recent past for its very severe famine?","o":["Angola","Costa Rica","Ecuador","Somalia"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Angola - Incorrect\nAngola suffered a long civil war (1975-2002), but it ended over two decades ago, and Angola was not in the news for a severe famine.\n\n(b) Costa Rica - Incorrect\nCosta Rica is a stable democracy in Central America that abolished its army in 1948; it is known for ecotourism, not famine.\n\n(c) Ecuador - Incorrect\nEcuador has faced gang violence and political crises, but not famine.\n\n(d) Somalia - Correct\nSomalia has suffered civil strife since the collapse of its government in 1991, including clan conflicts and the al-Shabaab insurgency. In 2021-2023 it faced its worst drought in 40 years, with five failed rainy seasons, and the UN warned of famine, with millions facing acute food insecurity. It had also suffered a famine in 2011 that killed about 2.6 lakh people."},{"i":"2023-79","y":2023,"n":79,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. In India, the Biodiversity Management Committees are key to the realization of the objectives of the Nagoya Protocol.\n2. The Biodiversity Management Committees have important functions in determining access and benefit sharing, including the power to levy collection fees on the access of biological resources within its jurisdiction.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"1. In India, Biodiversity Management Committees are key to realising the objectives of the Nagoya Protocol - Correct\nThe Nagoya Protocol (2010, in force 2014) under the Convention on Biological Diversity deals with access to genetic resources and fair and equitable sharing of benefits arising from their use. India implements it through the Biological Diversity Act, 2002 and its three-tier structure: the National Biodiversity Authority, State Biodiversity Boards and BMCs at the local level. BMCs document local biodiversity and traditional knowledge in People's Biodiversity Registers and are consulted before access is granted, so they are central to benefit sharing with local communities.\n\n2. BMCs have important functions in access and benefit sharing, including the power to levy collection fees - Correct\nUnder Section 41(3) of the Biological Diversity Act, BMCs may levy charges by way of collection fees from anyone accessing or collecting biological resources for commercial purposes within their area. The NBA and SBBs must also consult BMCs when deciding on access and benefit sharing.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2023-80","y":2023,"n":80,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of election to the President of India:\n1. The members nominated to either House of the Parliament or the Legislative Assemblies of States are also eligible to be included in the Electoral College.\n2. Higher the number of elective Assembly seats, higher is the value of vote of each MLA of that State.\n3. The value of vote of each MLA of Madhya Pradesh is greater than that of Kerala.\n4. The value of vote of each MLA of Puducherry is higher than that of Arunachal Pradesh because the ratio of total population to total number of elective seats in Puducherry is greater as compared to Arunachal Pradesh.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":0,"x":"The President is elected by an Electoral College consisting of elected members of both Houses of Parliament and elected members of the Legislative Assemblies of the States and the Union Territories of Delhi, Puducherry and Jammu and Kashmir. The value of an MLA's vote = (population of the State as per 1971 Census divided by number of elected MLAs) divided by 1,000.\n\n1. Nominated members of Parliament or State Assemblies are also included in the Electoral College - Incorrect\nOnly elected members are included (Article 54). Nominated members do not vote in the Presidential election (though they do vote in the Vice-Presidential election).\n\n2. The higher the number of elective Assembly seats, the higher the value of each MLA's vote - Incorrect\nFor a given population, more seats means each MLA represents fewer people, so the value of each vote falls.\n\n3. The value of vote of each MLA of Madhya Pradesh is greater than that of Kerala - Incorrect\nThe value of an MLA's vote is 131 for Madhya Pradesh and 152 for Kerala, so Kerala's is higher.\n\n4. The value of vote of each MLA of Puducherry is higher than that of Arunachal Pradesh because its population to seats ratio is greater - Correct\nEach Puducherry MLA's vote is worth 16, while each Arunachal Pradesh MLA's vote is worth 8, because Puducherry has more population per elected seat.\n\nOnly one statement is correct, so the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2023-81","y":2023,"n":81,"s":10,"q":"With reference to the Indian History, Alexander Rea, A. H. Longhurst, Robert Sewell, James Burgess and Walter Elliot were associated with","o":["archaeological excavations","establishment of English Press in Colonial India","establishment of Churches in Princely States","construction of railways in Colonial India"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Archaeological excavations - Correct\nAll these persons were colonial-era archaeologists and scholars who worked mainly in South India:\nAlexander Rea excavated the Iron Age burial site of Adichanallur (Tamil Nadu) and Buddhist sites in Andhra.\nA.H. Longhurst worked at Nagarjunakonda and wrote on Hampi.\nRobert Sewell compiled lists of antiquities of the Madras Presidency and wrote 'A Forgotten Empire' on Vijayanagara.\nJames Burgess headed the Archaeological Survey of Western and Southern India and wrote on Amaravati and the cave temples.\nWalter Elliot excavated at Amaravati, and the 'Elliot Marbles' are now in the British Museum; he also studied South Indian coins.\n\n(b) Establishment of the English press in colonial India - Incorrect\nPioneers of the English press included James Augustus Hicky (Hicky's Bengal Gazette, 1780).\n\n(c) Establishment of churches in princely states - Incorrect\nThey were not missionaries.\n\n(d) Construction of railways - Incorrect\nThey were not railway engineers."},{"i":"2023-82","y":2023,"n":82,"s":10,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Site | Well known for\n| 1. | Besnagar | Shaivite cave shrine\n| 2. | Bhaja | Buddhist cave shrine\n| 3. | Sittanavasal | Jain cave shrine\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":1,"x":"1. Besnagar : Shaivite cave shrine - Incorrect\nBesnagar, near Vidisha in Madhya Pradesh, is famous for the Heliodorus pillar (about 113 BCE). Heliodorus, a Greek ambassador of the Indo-Greek king Antialcidas, erected it as a Garuda pillar in honour of Vasudeva, making it an early evidence of Vaishnavism (Bhagavatism). It is not a Shaivite cave shrine. (The nearby Udayagiri caves have Gupta-period Vaishnava and Shaiva sculptures.)\n\n2. Bhaja : Buddhist cave shrine - Correct\nThe Bhaja caves near Lonavala, Maharashtra, are among the earliest Buddhist rock-cut caves (2nd century BCE). They include a chaitya hall with wooden-style architecture and viharas, as well as early sculptures.\n\n3. Sittanavasal : Jain cave shrine - Correct\nSittanavasal in Pudukkottai district, Tamil Nadu, is a Jain rock-cut cave temple (Arivar Koil) with remarkable paintings of lotus ponds, dancers and animals, dated to around the 7th-9th century. There are also Jain beds on the hill.\n\nTwo pairs are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-83","y":2023,"n":83,"s":9,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: 7th August is declared as the National Handloom Day.\nStatement-II: It was in 1905 that the Swadeshi Movement was launched on the same day.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":0,"x":"Statement I - Correct\n7 August has been celebrated as National Handloom Day since 2015, when it was first observed in Chennai. It honours handloom weavers and promotes the handloom sector, which employs millions of people, many of them women, in rural areas.\n\nStatement II - Correct, and explains Statement I\nOn 7 August 1905, a meeting at the Calcutta Town Hall formally launched the Swadeshi Movement against the partition of Bengal. It called for boycott of foreign (especially British) goods and revival of domestic products, including handloom cloth. The date was chosen for National Handloom Day to commemorate this movement.\n\nHence the answer is (a) Both statements are correct and Statement II explains Statement I."},{"i":"2023-84","y":2023,"n":84,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of the National Flag of India according to the Flag Code of India, 2002:\nStatement-I: One of the standard sizes of the National Flag of India is 600 mm × 400 mm.\nStatement-II: The ratio of the length to the height (width) of the Flag shall be 3:2.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":3,"x":"Statement I - Incorrect\nThe Flag Code of India, 2002 lays down nine standard sizes of the National Flag, in millimetres: 6300 x 4200, 3600 x 2400, 2700 x 1800, 1800 x 1200, 1350 x 900, 900 x 600, 450 x 300, 225 x 150 and 150 x 100. The size 600 x 400 is not among them.\n\nStatement II - Correct\nThe Flag Code specifies that the flag shall be rectangular, and the ratio of its length to height (width) shall be 3:2. All standard sizes follow this ratio. (The Code was amended in 2021-22 to allow machine-made polyester flags and flying the flag day and night in the open.)\n\nHence the answer is (d) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."},{"i":"2023-85","y":2023,"n":85,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of the Constitution Day:\nStatement-I: The Constitution Day is celebrated on 26th November every year to promote constitutional values among citizens.\nStatement-II: On 26th November, 1949, the Constituent Assembly of India set up a Drafting Committee under the Chairmanship of Dr. B. R. Ambedkar to prepare a Draft Constitution of India.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":2,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nSince 2015 (the 125th birth anniversary of Dr. B.R. Ambedkar), 26 November has been celebrated as Constitution Day (Samvidhan Divas) to promote constitutional values among citizens. It was earlier observed as National Law Day.\n\nStatement II - Incorrect\nOn 26 November 1949, the Constituent Assembly adopted the Constitution of India; some provisions such as citizenship and elections came into force that day, and the rest on 26 January 1950. The Drafting Committee under Dr. B.R. Ambedkar was set up much earlier, on 29 August 1947.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."},{"i":"2023-86","y":2023,"n":86,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: Switzerland is one of the leading exporters of gold in terms of value.\nStatement-II: Switzerland has the second largest gold reserves in the world.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":2,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nSwitzerland is one of the world's largest exporters of gold by value. It has little gold mining of its own, but it is the world's biggest gold refining and trading hub, refining about two-thirds of the world's gold. Refineries in Switzerland import raw gold and re-export refined bars, especially to China, India and Hong Kong. Switzerland is also one of India's largest sources of gold imports.\n\nStatement II - Incorrect\nAccording to World Gold Council data, the United States has the largest official gold reserves (about 8,133 tonnes), followed by Germany (about 3,350 tonnes), Italy, France, Russia and China. Switzerland ranks around seventh, with about 1,040 tonnes. (India holds about 800-880 tonnes.)\n\nHence the answer is (c) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."},{"i":"2023-87","y":2023,"n":87,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: Recently, the United States of America (USA) and the European Union (EU) have launched the 'Trade and Technology Council'.\nStatement-II: The USA and the EU claim that through this they are trying to bring technological progress and physical productivity under their control.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":2,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nThe EU-US Trade and Technology Council was launched in June 2021, and its first meeting was held in Pittsburgh in September 2021. It coordinates approaches to trade, technology standards, artificial intelligence, semiconductor supply chains, export controls and investment screening. (India has similar Trade and Technology Councils with the EU, launched in 2022.)\n\nStatement II - Incorrect\nThe TTC's stated aim is to deepen transatlantic cooperation on trade and technology based on shared democratic values, promote innovation, and set common rules for emerging technologies. The US and EU do not claim that it seeks to bring technological progress and physical productivity 'under their control'.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."},{"i":"2023-88","y":2023,"n":88,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: India accounts for 3.2% of global export of goods.\nStatement-II: Many local companies and some foreign companies operating in India have taken advantage of India's 'Production-linked Incentive' scheme.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":3,"x":"Statement I - Incorrect\nAccording to WTO data, India's share in global merchandise (goods) exports is only about 1.8 percent (2022). India's share in global services exports is higher, at around 4 percent. So 3.2 percent of global goods exports is wrong.\n\nStatement II - Correct\nThe Production Linked Incentive schemes, launched in 2020-21 across 14 sectors (such as mobile phones, pharmaceuticals, automobiles, solar modules and textiles), give incentives based on increase in production. Many Indian companies and some foreign companies have benefited, for example Apple suppliers Foxconn and Pegatron, and Samsung, which increased mobile phone manufacturing and exports from India.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."},{"i":"2023-89","y":2023,"n":89,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nThe 'Stability and Growth Pact' of the European Union is a treaty that\n1. limits the levels of the budgetary deficit of the countries of the European Union\n2. makes the countries of the European Union to share their infrastructure facilities\n3. enables the countries of the European Union to share their technologies\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":0,"x":"The Stability and Growth Pact (1997) is a set of rules to ensure that EU member states maintain sound public finances, especially important for countries using the euro.\n\n1. It limits the levels of budget deficit of EU countries - Correct\nThe Pact requires member states to keep their government budget deficit below 3 percent of GDP and public debt below 60 percent of GDP. Countries exceeding these limits face an 'excessive deficit procedure'. The rules were suspended during COVID-19 and reformed in 2024.\n\n2. It makes EU countries share their infrastructure facilities - Incorrect\nThe Pact has nothing to do with sharing infrastructure.\n\n3. It enables EU countries to share their technologies - Incorrect\nThe Pact does not deal with technology sharing.\n\nOnly one statement is correct, so the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2023-90","y":2023,"n":90,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Recently, all the countries of the United Nations have adopted the first-ever compact for international migration, the 'Global Compact for Safe, Orderly and Regular Migration (GCM)'.\n2. The objectives and commitments stated in the GCM are binding on the UN member countries.\n3. The GCM addresses internal migration or internally displaced people also in its objectives and commitments.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":3,"x":"1. All countries of the United Nations have adopted the Global Compact for Safe, Orderly and Regular Migration - Incorrect\nThe GCM was adopted at an intergovernmental conference in Marrakech in December 2018 and endorsed by the UN General Assembly, where 152 countries voted in favour. Five countries (the USA, Hungary, Israel, the Czech Republic and Poland) voted against, and 12 abstained. So not all UN members adopted it. India voted in favour.\n\n2. Its objectives and commitments are binding on UN members - Incorrect\nThe GCM is a non-legally binding cooperative framework. It sets out 23 objectives, but it respects the sovereign right of states to determine their migration policies.\n\n3. It addresses internal migration and internally displaced people - Incorrect\nThe GCM covers international migration only. Internally displaced people are addressed by other frameworks such as the UN Guiding Principles on Internal Displacement.\n\nNone of the statements is correct, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2023-91","y":2023,"n":91,"s":0,"q":"With reference to Home Guards, consider the following statements:\n1. Home Guards are raised under the Home Guards Act and Rules of the Central Government.\n2. The role of the Home Guards is to serve as an auxiliary force to the police in maintenance of internal security.\n3. To prevent infiltration on the international border/coastal areas, the Border Wing Home Guards Battalions have been raised in some States.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":1,"x":"1. Home Guards are raised under the Home Guards Act and Rules of the Central Government - Incorrect\nHome Guards are raised under the Home Guards Acts and Rules of the respective States and Union Territories. The Ministry of Home Affairs (through the Directorate General of Fire Services, Civil Defence and Home Guards) frames policy and shares some costs, but there is no central Home Guards Act for raising them.\n\n2. The role of Home Guards is to serve as an auxiliary force to the police in maintaining internal security - Correct\nHome Guards, a voluntary force first raised in 1946 in Bombay, assist the police in maintaining law and order and internal security. They also help during emergencies such as air raids, fires, floods, epidemics and elections.\n\n3. Border Wing Home Guards Battalions have been raised in some States to prevent infiltration - Correct\nBorder Wing Home Guards battalions have been raised in border States such as Punjab, Rajasthan, Gujarat, Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and West Bengal. They act as an auxiliary to the Border Security Force in preventing infiltration and protecting vulnerable areas.\n\nTwo statements are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-92","y":2023,"n":92,"s":0,"q":"With reference to India, consider the following pairs:\n| | Action | The Act under which it is covered\n| 1. | Unauthorized wearing of police or military uniforms | The Official Secrets Act, 1923\n| 2. | Knowingly misleading or otherwise interfering with a police officer or military officer when engaged in their duties | The Indian Evidence Act, 1872\n| 3. | Celebratory gunfire which can endanger the personal safety of others | The Arms (Amendment) Act, 2019\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":1,"x":"1. Unauthorised wearing of police or military uniforms : The Official Secrets Act, 1923 - Correct\nSection 6 of the Official Secrets Act, 1923 makes it an offence to use or wear, without authority, any naval, military, air force or police uniform, for the purpose of gaining admission to a prohibited place or for purposes prejudicial to the safety of the State.\n\n2. Knowingly misleading or interfering with a police or military officer on duty : The Indian Evidence Act, 1872 - Incorrect\nThis offence is covered by Section 7 of the Official Secrets Act, 1923, which penalises interfering with or obstructing police officers or members of the armed forces on guard or patrol near prohibited places. The Indian Evidence Act (now the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam) deals with rules of evidence in courts, not such offences.\n\n3. Celebratory gunfire endangering the safety of others : The Arms (Amendment) Act, 2019 - Correct\nThe Arms (Amendment) Act, 2019 made celebratory gunfire in public gatherings, religious places, marriage parties or other functions, which endangers human life or personal safety, a punishable offence (up to two years' imprisonment, a fine of up to 1 lakh rupees, or both).\n\nTwo pairs are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-93","y":2023,"n":93,"s":11,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Regions often mentioned in news | Reason for being in news\n| 1. | North Kivu and Ituri | War between Armenia and Azerbaijan\n| 2. | Nagorno-Karabakh | Insurgency in Mozambique\n| 3. | Kherson and Zaporizhzhia | Dispute between Israel and Lebanon\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":3,"x":"1. North Kivu and Ituri : War between Armenia and Azerbaijan - Incorrect\nNorth Kivu and Ituri are provinces in the eastern Democratic Republic of Congo, in the news for violence by armed groups such as M23 and the ADF, and for Ebola outbreaks.\n\n2. Nagorno-Karabakh : Insurgency in Mozambique - Incorrect\nNagorno-Karabakh was the region disputed between Armenia and Azerbaijan. Azerbaijan took full control in September 2023, and most ethnic Armenians left. The insurgency in Mozambique is in its Cabo Delgado province.\n\n3. Kherson and Zaporizhzhia : Dispute between Israel and Lebanon - Incorrect\nKherson and Zaporizhzhia are regions of southern Ukraine that Russia claimed to annex in 2022 during its war with Ukraine. Zaporizhzhia has Europe's largest nuclear power plant. The Israel-Lebanon dispute concerns areas such as the Shebaa Farms.\n\nNone of the pairs is correct, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2023-94","y":2023,"n":94,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement-I: Israel has established diplomatic relations with some Arab States.\nStatement-II: The 'Arab Peace Initiative' mediated by Saudi Arabia was signed by Israel and Arab League.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","o":["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is the correct explanation for Statement-I","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II is not the correct explanation for Statement-I","Statement-I is correct but Statement-II is incorrect","Statement-I is incorrect but Statement-II is correct"],"a":2,"x":"Statement I - Correct\nIsrael has diplomatic relations with several Arab states: Egypt (peace treaty of 1979), Jordan (1994), and, under the US-brokered Abraham Accords of 2020, the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain and Morocco (Sudan also agreed to normalise ties).\n\nStatement II - Incorrect\nThe Arab Peace Initiative was proposed by Saudi Arabia (then Crown Prince Abdullah) and endorsed by the Arab League at its Beirut Summit in 2002. It offers normal relations with Israel in exchange for Israel's full withdrawal to the 1967 borders, a Palestinian state with East Jerusalem as its capital, and a just solution for refugees. Israel never signed or accepted it, and it was not a 'mediated' agreement signed by both sides.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect."},{"i":"2023-95","y":2023,"n":95,"s":10,"q":"Consider the following pairs with regard to sports awards:\n| | Award | Given for\n| 1. | Major Dhyan Chand Khel Ratna Award | For the most spectacular and outstanding performance by a sportsperson over period of last four years\n| 2. | Arjuna Award | For the lifetime achievement by a sportsperson\n| 3. | Dronacharya Award | To honour eminent coaches who have successfully trained sportspersons or teams\n| 4. | Rashtriya Khel Protsahan Puraskar | To recognize the contribution made by sportspersons even after their retirement\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":1,"x":"1. Major Dhyan Chand Khel Ratna : Most spectacular and outstanding performance over the last four years - Correct\nThe Khel Ratna, India's highest sporting honour (renamed from Rajiv Gandhi Khel Ratna in 2021), is given for the most outstanding performance by a sportsperson over the previous four years.\n\n2. Arjuna Award : Lifetime achievement - Incorrect\nThe Arjuna Award is given for good performance over the previous four years along with leadership, sportsmanship and discipline. Lifetime achievement in sports was recognised by the Dhyan Chand Award (now merged as the Arjuna Award for Lifetime Achievement).\n\n3. Dronacharya Award : Eminent coaches who have trained sportspersons or teams - Correct\nThe Dronacharya Award honours coaches who have trained sportspersons or teams to outstanding results in international events.\n\n4. Rashtriya Khel Protsahan Puraskar : Contribution of retired sportspersons - Incorrect\nThis award recognises corporate entities, private and public organisations and individuals who have promoted and developed sports, for example through sports infrastructure, athlete support or sports for development. It is not for retired sportspersons.\n\nTwo pairs are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-96","y":2023,"n":96,"s":12,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of the 44th Chess Olympiad, 2022:\n1. It was the first time that Chess Olympiad was held in India.\n2. The official mascot was named 'Thambi'.\n3. The trophy for the winning team in the open section is the Vera Menchik Cup.\n4. The trophy for the winning team in the women's section is the Hamilton-Russell Cup.\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?","o":["Only one","Only two","Only three","All four"],"a":1,"x":"1. It was the first time the Chess Olympiad was held in India - Correct\nThe 44th Chess Olympiad was held at Mamallapuram near Chennai, Tamil Nadu, from 28 July to 10 August 2022. It was the first time India hosted the event. It also began the tradition of a torch relay for the Chess Olympiad.\n\n2. The official mascot was named 'Thambi' - Correct\nThe mascot was 'Thambi' (Tamil for 'younger brother'), a chess knight dressed in a traditional veshti and shirt, making the vanakkam gesture.\n\n3. The trophy for the open section winner is the Vera Menchik Cup - Incorrect\nThe open section trophy is the Hamilton-Russell Cup, named after Frederick Hamilton-Russell, who donated it in 1927.\n\n4. The trophy for the women's section winner is the Hamilton-Russell Cup - Incorrect\nThe women's section trophy is the Vera Menchik Cup, named after the first women's world chess champion. (Statements 3 and 4 have swapped the trophies.)\n\nTwo statements are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2023-97","y":2023,"n":97,"s":11,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Area of conflict mentioned in news | Country where it is located\n| 1. | Donbas | Syria\n| 2. | Kachin | Ethiopia\n| 3. | Tigray | North Yemen\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":3,"x":"1. Donbas : Syria - Incorrect\nDonbas (Donetsk and Luhansk regions) is in eastern Ukraine. It has been a centre of conflict between Ukraine and Russia-backed separatists since 2014 and a major battleground since Russia's invasion in 2022.\n\n2. Kachin : Ethiopia - Incorrect\nKachin is a state in northern Myanmar, where the Kachin Independence Army has fought the Myanmar military for decades, and fighting intensified after the 2021 coup.\n\n3. Tigray : North Yemen - Incorrect\nTigray is a region in northern Ethiopia. A war between the Ethiopian federal government (with Eritrean forces) and the Tigray People's Liberation Front from 2020 to 2022 killed hundreds of thousands and caused famine-like conditions, ending with the Pretoria agreement of November 2022.\n\nNone of the pairs is correct, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2023-98","y":2023,"n":98,"s":4,"q":"In the recent years Chad, Guinea, Mali and Sudan caught the international attention for which one of the following reasons common to all of them?","o":["Discovery of rich deposits of rare earth elements","Establishment of Chinese military bases","Southward expansion of Sahara Desert","Successful coups"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Discovery of rich deposits of rare earth elements - Incorrect\nThese countries were not in the news for rare earth discoveries.\n\n(b) Establishment of Chinese military bases - Incorrect\nChina's only overseas military base is in Djibouti.\n\n(c) Southward expansion of the Sahara Desert - Incorrect\nDesertification affects the Sahel, but it is not the common reason these four countries were in the news.\n\n(d) Successful coups - Correct\nAll four countries saw successful military takeovers in recent years:\nMali had coups in August 2020 and May 2021.\nGuinea's military ousted President Alpha Condé in September 2021.\nSudan's military dissolved the transitional civilian government in October 2021.\nChad's military set up a transitional council under Mahamat Déby after President Idriss Déby's death in April 2021, which critics called an institutional coup.\nAlong with Burkina Faso and Niger, this region has been called Africa's 'coup belt'."},{"i":"2023-99","y":2023,"n":99,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following heavy industries:\n1. Fertilizer plants\n2. Oil refineries\n3. Steel plants\nGreen hydrogen is expected to play a significant role in decarbonizing how many of the above industries?","o":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"a":2,"x":"Green hydrogen is seen as key to decarbonising 'hard-to-abate' industries, where direct electrification is difficult. India's National Green Hydrogen Mission targets these sectors.\n\n1. Fertiliser plants - Green hydrogen can play a significant role\nAmmonia, the base for urea and other fertilisers, is made from hydrogen, which today comes mostly from natural gas (grey hydrogen). Using green hydrogen produces 'green ammonia' and cuts emissions.\n\n2. Oil refineries - Green hydrogen can play a significant role\nRefineries use large amounts of hydrogen for processes like hydrocracking and removing sulphur from fuels. Replacing grey hydrogen with green hydrogen reduces their emissions; Indian refiners like IOC and Reliance have announced green hydrogen projects.\n\n3. Steel plants - Green hydrogen can play a significant role\nSteel is traditionally made by reducing iron ore with coke from coal in blast furnaces, which emits a lot of CO2. Hydrogen can instead be used to reduce iron ore directly (hydrogen-based direct reduced iron), producing water instead of CO2. Pilot projects are under way worldwide and in India.\n\nAll three industries, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2023-100","y":2023,"n":100,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements about G-20:\n1. The G-20 group was originally established as a platform for the Finance Ministers and Central Bank Governors to discuss the international economic and financial issues.\n2. Digital public infrastructure is one of India's G-20 priorities.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"1. The G-20 was originally established as a platform for Finance Ministers and Central Bank Governors - Correct\nThe G20 was formed in 1999, after the Asian financial crisis of 1997-98, as a forum for finance ministers and central bank governors of major economies to discuss global economic and financial stability. After the 2008 global financial crisis, it was upgraded to the level of heads of state and government, with the first leaders' summit held in Washington in 2008.\n\n2. Digital public infrastructure is one of India's G-20 priorities - Correct\nIndia held the G20 presidency from December 2022 to November 2023 with the theme 'Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam - One Earth, One Family, One Future'. Digital Public Infrastructure (such as Aadhaar, UPI and CoWIN) was a key priority. The New Delhi Leaders' Declaration adopted a G20 Framework for DPI and India proposed a Global DPI Repository. The African Union was also made a permanent member during India's presidency.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2022-1","y":2022,"n":1,"s":1,"q":"\"Rapid Financing Instrument\" and \"Rapid Credit Facility\" are related to the provisions of lending by which one of the following?","o":["Asian Development Bank","International Monetary Fund","United Nations Environment Programme Finance Initiative","World Bank"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Asian Development Bank - Incorrect\nADB has its own lending windows (such as the COVID-19 Pandemic Response Option), but not these two instruments.\n\n(b) International Monetary Fund - Correct\nThe Rapid Financing Instrument (RFI) and Rapid Credit Facility (RCF) are IMF emergency lending tools. The RFI provides quick financial help to all member countries facing urgent balance of payments needs, for example due to natural disasters, pandemics or commodity price shocks, without a full-fledged programme. The RCF provides similar rapid help to low-income countries on concessional terms (zero interest) through the Poverty Reduction and Growth Trust. Both were widely used during the COVID-19 pandemic.\n\n(c) UNEP Finance Initiative - Incorrect\nUNEP FI is a partnership between UNEP and financial institutions to promote sustainable finance; it does not lend.\n\n(d) World Bank - Incorrect\nThe World Bank lends for development projects through the IBRD and IDA; these two facilities belong to the IMF."},{"i":"2022-2","y":2022,"n":2,"s":1,"q":"With reference to the Indian economy, consider the following statements:\n1. An increase in Nominal Effective Exchange Rate (NEER) indicates the appreciation of rupee.\n2. An increase in the Real Effective Exchange Rate (REER) indicates an improvement in trade competitiveness.\n3. An increasing trend in domestic inflation relative to inflation in other countries is likely to cause an increasing divergence between NEER and REER.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":2,"x":"The Nominal Effective Exchange Rate (NEER) is a weighted average of the rupee's exchange rate against a basket of currencies of major trading partners. The Real Effective Exchange Rate (REER) is the NEER adjusted for the difference in inflation between India and its trading partners.\n\n1. An increase in NEER indicates appreciation of the rupee - Correct\nThe RBI expresses NEER as an index where a rise means the rupee has become stronger against the basket.\n\n2. An increase in REER indicates an improvement in trade competitiveness - Incorrect\nA rising REER means the rupee is appreciating in real terms, making Indian goods relatively more expensive abroad and imports cheaper. This indicates a loss of trade competitiveness, not an improvement.\n\n3. Rising domestic inflation relative to other countries is likely to increase the divergence between NEER and REER - Correct\nREER equals NEER multiplied by the ratio of domestic to foreign prices. If Indian inflation is higher than that of trading partners, REER rises above NEER even if the nominal rate is unchanged, so the gap between them widens.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1 and 3 only."},{"i":"2022-3","y":2022,"n":3,"s":1,"q":"With reference to the Indian economy, consider the following statements:\n1. If the inflation is too high, Reserve Bank of India (RBI) is likely to buy government securities.\n2. If the rupee is rapidly depreciating, RBI is likely to sell dollars in the market.\n3. If interest rates in the USA or European Union were to fall, that is likely to induce RBI to buy dollars.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. If inflation is too high, the RBI is likely to buy government securities - Incorrect\nBuying government securities injects money into the economy and increases liquidity, which would add to inflation. To control high inflation, the RBI sells government securities through open market operations to absorb liquidity, and raises the repo rate.\n\n2. If the rupee is rapidly depreciating, the RBI is likely to sell dollars - Correct\nSelling dollars from the foreign exchange reserves increases the supply of dollars and the demand for rupees in the market, which slows the rupee's fall. The RBI did this in 2022 when the rupee weakened sharply.\n\n3. If interest rates in the USA or EU fall, the RBI is likely to buy dollars - Correct\nLower interest rates abroad make Indian assets more attractive, so foreign capital flows into India, increasing the supply of dollars and pushing the rupee up. To prevent excessive appreciation and volatility (which could hurt exports), and to build reserves, the RBI buys dollars.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2022-4","y":2022,"n":4,"s":1,"q":"With reference to the \"G20 Common Framework\", consider the following statements:\n1. It is an initiative endorsed by the G20 together with the Paris Club.\n2. It is an initiative to support Low Income Countries with unsustainable debt.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"The 'Common Framework for Debt Treatments beyond the DSSI' was agreed in November 2020 to deal with rising debt distress in poor countries during the COVID-19 pandemic.\n\n1. It is an initiative endorsed by the G20 together with the Paris Club - Correct\nIt was agreed by the G20 and the Paris Club (a group of major official creditor countries). Importantly, it brings non-Paris Club G20 creditors such as China and India into coordinated debt restructuring.\n\n2. It is an initiative to support Low Income Countries with unsustainable debt - Correct\nIt covers the 73 low-income countries that were eligible for the Debt Service Suspension Initiative (DSSI). It provides case-by-case debt restructuring for countries with unsustainable debt, as in the cases of Chad, Zambia, Ethiopia and Ghana. It has been criticised for being slow.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2022-5","y":2022,"n":5,"s":1,"q":"With reference to the Indian economy, what are the advantages of \"Inflation-Indexed Bonds (IIBs)\"?\n1. Government can reduce the coupon rates on its borrowing by way of IIBs.\n2. IIBs provide protection to the investors from uncertainty regarding inflation.\n3. The interest received as well as capital gains on IIBs are not taxable.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"x":"Inflation-Indexed Bonds (IIBs) are bonds whose principal or interest is linked to an inflation index, so that investors are protected against rising prices. In India they were issued linked to WPI in 2013 and to CPI later.\n\n1. The government can reduce the coupon rate on its borrowing through IIBs - Correct\nInvestors in normal bonds demand an extra premium to compensate for the risk of future inflation. Since IIBs already protect them against inflation, investors accept a lower real coupon rate, which reduces the government's borrowing cost. It also gives the government an incentive to keep inflation low.\n\n2. IIBs protect investors from uncertainty regarding inflation - Correct\nBecause the returns are adjusted for inflation, investors get a positive real return regardless of the inflation rate.\n\n3. The interest received as well as capital gains on IIBs are not taxable - Incorrect\nThere is no special tax exemption for IIBs. Interest and capital gains are taxable under the Income-tax Act, which was one reason for their poor popularity in India.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2022-6","y":2022,"n":6,"s":1,"q":"With reference to foreign-owned e-commerce firms operating in India, which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. They can sell their own goods in addition to offering their platforms as market-places.\n2. The degree to which they can own big sellers on their platforms is limited.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":3,"x":"India's FDI policy (Press Note 2 of 2018) allows 100 percent FDI in e-commerce only in the marketplace model, where the company provides a platform for buyers and sellers. FDI is not allowed in the inventory model, where the company owns and sells goods itself.\n\n1. They can sell their own goods in addition to offering their platforms as marketplaces - Incorrect\nForeign-owned e-commerce companies like Amazon and Flipkart (Walmart) cannot own inventory and sell goods directly to consumers on their platforms.\n\n2. The degree to which they can own big sellers on their platforms is limited - Incorrect\nThe rule is not about a limited degree of ownership. An entity in which the e-commerce company or its group companies have any equity participation, or whose inventory they control, is not permitted to sell products on that platform at all. (This rule forced changes in companies like Cloudtail and Appario, which were linked to Amazon.)\n\nHence the answer is (d) Neither 1 nor 2."},{"i":"2022-7","y":2022,"n":7,"s":1,"q":"Which of the following activities constitute real sector in the economy?\n1. Farmers harvesting their crops\n2. Textile mills converting raw cotton into fabrics\n3. A commercial bank lending money to a trading company\n4. A corporate body issuing Rupee Denominated Bonds overseas\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2 only","2, 3 and 4 only","1, 3 and 4 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"a":0,"x":"The real sector of an economy covers the production of goods and services (agriculture, industry and services), as opposed to the financial sector, which deals with money, credit and financial assets.\n\n1. Farmers harvesting their crops - Real sector\nThis is agricultural production, a primary sector activity that produces real goods.\n\n2. Textile mills converting raw cotton into fabric - Real sector\nThis is manufacturing, a secondary sector activity that produces real goods.\n\n3. A commercial bank lending money to a trading company - Financial sector\nLending is a financial activity; it provides credit but does not itself produce goods.\n\n4. A corporate body issuing rupee-denominated bonds overseas - Financial sector\nIssuing masala bonds is a way of raising money; it is a financial activity.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2022-8","y":2022,"n":8,"s":1,"q":"Which one of the following situations best reflects \"Indirect Transfers\" often talked about in media recently with reference to India?","o":["An Indian company investing in a foreign enterprise and paying taxes to the foreign country on the profits arising out of its investment","A foreign company investing in India and paying taxes to the country of its base on the profits arising out of its investment","An Indian company purchases tangible assets in a foreign country and sells such assets after their value increases and transfers the proceeds to India","A foreign company transfers shares and such shares derive their substantial value from assets located in India"],"a":3,"x":"(a) An Indian company investing abroad and paying taxes to the foreign country on its profits - Incorrect\nThis is ordinary taxation of overseas income; it is not what 'indirect transfer' means.\n\n(b) A foreign company investing in India and paying taxes to its home country on its profits - Incorrect\nThis is also not an indirect transfer.\n\n(c) An Indian company buying tangible assets abroad, selling them at a profit and bringing the proceeds to India - Incorrect\nThis is a direct transfer of foreign assets producing capital gains.\n\n(d) A foreign company transfers shares that derive their substantial value from assets in India - Correct\nAn 'indirect transfer' happens when shares of a foreign company, which holds valuable assets in India through subsidiaries, are sold abroad. The Indian assets change hands indirectly. The Vodafone-Hutchison deal (2007) was the famous example; the Supreme Court held it was not taxable (2012), after which the government amended the Income-tax Act retrospectively to tax such transfers. This led to disputes with Vodafone and Cairn Energy. The Taxation Laws (Amendment) Act, 2021 withdrew the retrospective tax demands."},{"i":"2022-9","y":2022,"n":9,"s":1,"q":"With reference to the expenditure made by an organisation or a company, which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. Acquiring new technology is capital expenditure.\n2. Debt financing is considered capital expenditure, while equity financing is considered revenue expenditure.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":0,"x":"1. Acquiring new technology is capital expenditure - Correct\nCapital expenditure is spending that creates long-term assets or benefits lasting beyond the current year, such as buying machinery, equipment, software, patents or new technology. Acquiring new technology improves the company's productive capacity for many years, so it is capital expenditure.\n\n2. Debt financing is capital expenditure, while equity financing is revenue expenditure - Incorrect\nDebt financing (taking loans or issuing bonds) and equity financing (issuing shares) are ways of raising funds; they are sources of capital, not expenditures at all. Interest paid on debt is revenue expenditure, and dividends paid to shareholders are distributions of profit, but the financing itself is neither capital nor revenue expenditure.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 only."},{"i":"2022-10","y":2022,"n":10,"s":1,"q":"With reference to the Indian economy, consider the following statements:\n1. A share of the household financial savings goes towards government borrowings.\n2. Dated securities issued at market-related rates in auctions form a large component of internal debt.\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"1. A share of household financial savings goes towards government borrowings - Correct\nHousehold savings reach the government through several routes: deposits in small savings schemes (like PPF, NSC and post office deposits) go to the National Small Savings Fund, which lends to the government; bank deposits are partly invested by banks in government securities (to meet the Statutory Liquidity Ratio); and insurance and provident funds also invest heavily in government bonds.\n\n2. Dated securities issued at market-related rates in auctions form a large component of internal debt - Correct\nDated government securities, with maturities from 5 to 40 years, are sold through auctions conducted by the RBI at market-determined interest rates. They make up the largest part of the Central Government's internal debt, along with treasury bills and other instruments.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2022-11","y":2022,"n":11,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Pursuant to the report of H.N. Sanyal Committee, the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971 was passed.\n2. The Constitution of India empowers the Supreme Court and the High Courts to punish for contempt of themselves.\n3. The Constitution of India defines Civil Contempt and Criminal Contempt.\n4. In India, the Parliament is vested with the powers to make laws on Contempt of Court.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 4","3 and 4 only","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. The Contempt of Courts Act, 1971 was passed following the report of the H.N. Sanyal Committee - Correct\nThe H.N. Sanyal Committee (1961-63) examined the law on contempt of court, and its recommendations led to the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971.\n\n2. The Constitution empowers the Supreme Court and High Courts to punish for contempt of themselves - Correct\nArticle 129 declares the Supreme Court a court of record with power to punish for contempt of itself, and Article 215 gives the same power to High Courts.\n\n3. The Constitution defines civil contempt and criminal contempt - Incorrect\nThese terms are defined in Section 2 of the Contempt of Courts Act, 1971, not in the Constitution. Civil contempt is wilful disobedience of a court order; criminal contempt includes scandalising the court, prejudicing judicial proceedings or obstructing justice.\n\n4. Parliament is vested with powers to make laws on contempt of court - Correct\nEntry 77 of the Union List covers contempt of the Supreme Court, and Entry 14 of the Concurrent List covers contempt of court (other than the Supreme Court), so Parliament can legislate on it.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1, 2 and 4."},{"i":"2022-12","y":2022,"n":12,"s":0,"q":"With reference to India, consider the following statements:\n1. Government law officers and legal firms are recognised as advocates, but corporate lawyers and patent attorneys are excluded from recognition as advocates.\n2. Bar Councils have the power to lay down the rules relating to legal education and recognition of law colleges.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":1,"x":"1. Government law officers and legal firms are recognised as advocates, but corporate lawyers and patent attorneys are excluded - Incorrect\nUnder the Advocates Act, 1961, an 'advocate' is a person enrolled on the roll of a State Bar Council. The statement's classification is not correct: recognition depends on enrolment, not on whether one is a government law officer, part of a firm, corporate lawyer or patent attorney. (Law firms as entities are not 'advocates', and the Bar Council's rules restrict enrolled advocates from full-time employment, which affects in-house lawyers, but the statement as framed is inaccurate.)\n\n2. Bar Councils have the power to lay down rules on legal education and recognition of law colleges - Correct\nUnder Section 7 of the Advocates Act, the Bar Council of India has the function of promoting legal education and laying down standards of such education in consultation with universities, and of recognising universities whose law degrees qualify a person for enrolment as an advocate. It inspects and approves law colleges.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2022-13","y":2022,"n":13,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. A bill amending the Constitution requires a prior recommendation of the President of India.\n2. When a Constitution Amendment Bill is presented to the President of India, it is obligatory for the President of India to give his/her assent.\n3. A Constitution Amendment Bill must be passed by both the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha by a special majority and there is no provision for joint sitting.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. A bill amending the Constitution requires prior recommendation of the President - Incorrect\nUnder Article 368, a Constitution Amendment Bill can be introduced in either House of Parliament by a minister or a private member, and it does not require the President's prior recommendation.\n\n2. When a Constitution Amendment Bill is presented to the President, it is obligatory for him or her to give assent - Correct\nThe 24th Amendment Act, 1971 made it mandatory for the President to give assent to a Constitution Amendment Bill. The President cannot withhold assent or return it for reconsideration.\n\n3. A Constitution Amendment Bill must be passed by both Houses by special majority, and there is no provision for a joint sitting - Correct\nEach House must pass the bill separately by a special majority (a majority of the total membership and two-thirds of members present and voting). If the Houses disagree, the bill fails, as there is no joint sitting for Constitution Amendment Bills. Some amendments also need ratification by half of the States.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2022-14","y":2022,"n":14,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The Constitution of India classifies the ministers into four ranks viz. Cabinet Minister, Minister of State with Independent Charge, Minister of State and Deputy Minister.\n2. The total number of ministers in the Union Government, including the Prime Minister, shall not exceed 15 percent of the total number of members in the Lok Sabha.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":1,"x":"1. The Constitution classifies ministers into four ranks: Cabinet Minister, Minister of State with Independent Charge, Minister of State and Deputy Minister - Incorrect\nThe Constitution only refers to a 'Council of Ministers' headed by the Prime Minister (Article 74 and 75). The classification into ranks is based on convention and practice (and mentioned in the Salaries and Allowances of Ministers Act, 1952). The word 'Cabinet' appears only once in the Constitution, in Article 352, added by the 44th Amendment.\n\n2. The total number of ministers, including the PM, shall not exceed 15 percent of the total members of the Lok Sabha - Correct\nArticle 75(1A), inserted by the 91st Amendment Act, 2003, limits the size of the Union Council of Ministers to 15 percent of the total strength of the Lok Sabha. (For States, Article 164(1A) sets the same limit relative to the Assembly, with a minimum of 12 ministers.)\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2022-15","y":2022,"n":15,"s":0,"q":"Which of the following is/are the exclusive power(s) of Lok Sabha?\n1. To ratify the declaration of Emergency\n2. To pass a motion of no-confidence against the Council of Ministers\n3. To impeach the President of India\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2","2 only","1 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. To ratify the declaration of Emergency - Not exclusive to the Lok Sabha\nA proclamation of National Emergency under Article 352 must be approved by both Houses of Parliament within one month, by a special majority. (The Lok Sabha alone can pass a resolution disapproving continuation of the emergency, under Article 352(7).)\n\n2. To pass a motion of no-confidence against the Council of Ministers - Exclusive to the Lok Sabha\nUnder Article 75(3), the Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha. Only the Lok Sabha can pass a no-confidence motion, which forces the government to resign.\n\n3. To impeach the President of India - Not exclusive to the Lok Sabha\nImpeachment under Article 61 requires both Houses: one House frames the charges and passes the resolution by a two-thirds majority of its total membership, and the other investigates and must also pass it by the same majority.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2022-16","y":2022,"n":16,"s":0,"q":"With reference to anti-defection law in India, consider the following statements:\n1. The law specifies that a nominated legislator cannot join any political party within six months of being appointed to the House.\n2. The law does not provide any time-frame within which the presiding officer has to decide a defection case.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":1,"x":"The anti-defection law is contained in the Tenth Schedule of the Constitution, added by the 52nd Amendment Act, 1985.\n\n1. The law specifies that a nominated legislator cannot join any political party within six months of being appointed - Incorrect\nThe law says the opposite: a nominated member is disqualified if he or she joins a political party after six months of taking the seat. Within the first six months, a nominated member is free to join a party.\n\n2. The law does not provide any time-frame for the presiding officer to decide a defection case - Correct\nThe Tenth Schedule gives the Speaker or Chairman the power to decide on disqualification petitions but fixes no deadline. This has led to long delays in several cases. In Keisham Meghachandra Singh v. Speaker, Manipur (2020), the Supreme Court said such petitions should ordinarily be decided within three months and suggested an independent tribunal.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2022-17","y":2022,"n":17,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Attorney General of India and Solicitor General of India are the only officers of the Government who are allowed to participate in the meetings of the Parliament of India.\n2. According to the Constitution of India, the Attorney General of India submits his resignation when the Government which appointed him resigns.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":3,"x":"1. The Attorney General and Solicitor General are the only officers of the Government allowed to participate in meetings of Parliament - Incorrect\nUnder Article 88, only the Attorney General (apart from ministers) has the right to speak in, and take part in, the proceedings of either House, joint sittings and parliamentary committees of which he is named a member, but without the right to vote. The Solicitor General has no such constitutional right.\n\n2. According to the Constitution, the Attorney General resigns when the Government that appointed him resigns - Incorrect\nArticle 76 says the Attorney General holds office during the pleasure of the President. The Constitution does not require him to resign when the government changes. It is only a convention that the Attorney General resigns when the government resigns or is replaced, since he is appointed on its advice.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Neither 1 nor 2."},{"i":"2022-18","y":2022,"n":18,"s":0,"q":"With reference to the writs issued by the Courts in India, consider the following statements:\n1. Mandamus will not lie against a private organisation unless it is entrusted with a public duty.\n2. Mandamus will not lie against a Company even though it may be a Government Company.\n3. Any public minded person can be a petitioner to move the Court to obtain the writ of Quo Warranto.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"1. Mandamus will not lie against a private organisation unless it is entrusted with a public duty - Correct\nMandamus ('we command') directs a public authority to perform a public or statutory duty it has failed to perform. It does not lie against private individuals or bodies, unless they perform a public function or duty (for example, a private school receiving government aid in certain matters).\n\n2. Mandamus will not lie against a Company even though it may be a Government Company - Incorrect (see Discrepancy)\nUnder current law, mandamus can be issued against a government company or public corporation if it is 'State' under Article 12 or is performing public or statutory duties. For example, in Sukhdev Singh v. Bhagatram (1975) and Ramana Dayaram Shetty v. International Airport Authority (1979), the Supreme Court treated such bodies as 'State', subject to writ jurisdiction.\n\n3. Any public-minded person can move the Court for a writ of Quo Warranto - Correct\nQuo warranto ('by what authority') questions a person's right to hold a public office. Unlike other writs, the petitioner need not be personally aggrieved; any interested or public-minded person can file it.\n\nDiscrepancy: The official key gives (d) 1, 2 and 3, apparently relying on an older textbook statement that mandamus does not lie against a company 'even though it may be a Government company'. On current law, statement 2 is incorrect, so the correct answer should be (c) 1 and 3 only. Several coaching analyses also treat statement 2 as incorrect."},{"i":"2022-19","y":2022,"n":19,"s":3,"q":"With reference to Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission, consider the following statements:\n1. Private and public hospitals must adopt it.\n2. As it aims to achieve universal health coverage, every citizen of India should be part of it ultimately.\n3. It has seamless portability across the country.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"The Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission, launched in September 2021 by the National Health Authority, aims to create a digital health ecosystem with a unique Ayushman Bharat Health Account (ABHA) number for citizens, registries of health professionals and facilities, and a system for sharing health records with consent.\n\n1. Private and public hospitals must adopt it - Incorrect\nParticipation by hospitals, clinics and laboratories, both public and private, is voluntary.\n\n2. As it aims at universal health coverage, every citizen should ultimately be part of it - Incorrect\nCreating an ABHA and participating in ABDM is voluntary for citizens; no one is required to join. Its aim is to support universal health coverage, but it does not require every citizen's participation.\n\n3. It has seamless portability across the country - Correct\nHealth records linked to a person's ABHA can be accessed, with the person's consent, by any registered health facility anywhere in India. This allows people to carry their medical history with them.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 3 only."},{"i":"2022-20","y":2022,"n":20,"s":0,"q":"With reference to Deputy Speaker of Lok Sabha, consider the following statements:\n1. As per the Rules of Procedure and Conduct of Business in Lok Sabha, the election of Deputy Speaker shall be held on such date as the Speaker may fix.\n2. There is a mandatory provision that the election of a candidate as Deputy Speaker of Lok Sabha shall be from either the principal opposition party or the ruling party.\n3. The Deputy Speaker has the same power as of the Speaker when presiding over the sitting of the House and no appeal lies against his rulings.\n4. The well established parliamentary practice regarding the appointment of Deputy Speaker is that the motion is moved by the Speaker and duly seconded by the Prime Minister.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3","3 and 4 only","2 and 4 only"],"a":0,"x":"1. As per the Lok Sabha Rules, the election of the Deputy Speaker is held on a date fixed by the Speaker - Correct\nRule 8 of the Rules of Procedure and Conduct of Business in Lok Sabha provides that the election of the Deputy Speaker shall be held on such date as the Speaker may fix. (The Speaker's election date is fixed by the President.)\n\n2. There is a mandatory provision that the Deputy Speaker must be from either the principal opposition party or the ruling party - Incorrect\nThere is no such provision. By convention, the post has often been given to the opposition, but this is not mandatory. (Article 93 only requires the Lok Sabha to choose a Deputy Speaker 'as soon as may be'.)\n\n3. The Deputy Speaker has the same powers as the Speaker when presiding, and no appeal lies against his rulings - Correct\nWhen presiding over the House in the Speaker's absence, the Deputy Speaker has all the powers of the Speaker, and his rulings are final, like the Speaker's.\n\n4. The motion for the Deputy Speaker's election is moved by the Speaker and seconded by the Prime Minister - Incorrect\nThe motion to elect the Deputy Speaker is moved by a member (often the Prime Minister or the Minister of Parliamentary Affairs) and seconded by another member. The Speaker presides; he does not move the motion.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 3 only."},{"i":"2022-21","y":2022,"n":21,"s":2,"q":"Among the following crops, which one is the most important anthropogenic source of both methane and nitrous oxide?","o":["Cotton","Rice","Sugarcane","Wheat"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Cotton - Incorrect\nCotton cultivation emits nitrous oxide from fertiliser use, but it is grown on dry land and does not produce significant methane.\n\n(b) Rice - Correct\nRice paddies are usually flooded. The waterlogged soil has no oxygen, so methanogenic bacteria decompose organic matter and produce methane. Rice cultivation is responsible for about 10-12 percent of global man-made methane emissions. Rice fields also release nitrous oxide from nitrogen fertilisers, especially when fields alternate between wet and dry. So rice is the most important crop source of both gases.\n\n(c) Sugarcane - Incorrect\nSugarcane emits nitrous oxide from fertilisers and some emissions from burning residues, but not significant methane.\n\n(d) Wheat - Incorrect\nWheat is grown in non-flooded fields, so methane emissions are negligible, though fertiliser use causes nitrous oxide emissions."},{"i":"2022-22","y":2022,"n":22,"s":6,"q":"\"System of Rice Intensification\" of cultivation, in which alternate wetting and drying of rice fields is practised, results in:\n1. Reduced seed requirement\n2. Reduced methane production\n3. Reduced electricity consumption\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"The System of Rice Intensification (SRI), developed in Madagascar in the 1980s, uses young single seedlings, wide spacing, organic manure, frequent weeding and alternate wetting and drying of fields instead of continuous flooding.\n\n1. Reduced seed requirement - Correct\nBecause single young seedlings (8-12 days old) are transplanted at wide spacing, SRI needs only about 5 kg of seed per hectare, compared with 20-30 kg or more in conventional methods.\n\n2. Reduced methane production - Correct\nMethane is produced by bacteria in flooded, oxygen-free soil. Since SRI fields are not kept continuously flooded, the soil gets oxygen periodically, which greatly reduces methane emissions.\n\n3. Reduced electricity consumption - Correct\nSRI uses about 30-50 percent less irrigation water. Since much irrigation in India uses electric pumps to draw groundwater, less water means less electricity consumption.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2022-23","y":2022,"n":23,"s":11,"q":"Which one of the following lakes of West Africa has become dry and turned into a desert?","o":["Lake Victoria","Lake Faguibine","Lake Oguta","Lake Volta"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Lake Victoria - Incorrect\nLake Victoria in East Africa (Uganda, Kenya and Tanzania) is Africa's largest lake and the main source of the White Nile. It has not dried up.\n\n(b) Lake Faguibine - Correct\nLake Faguibine in northern Mali, near Timbuktu, was once one of the largest lakes in West Africa, fed by the seasonal floods of the Niger River. Since the 1970s, repeated droughts and reduced flooding have left it almost completely dry, and much of its bed has turned into desert with sand dunes. UNEP has supported projects to restore it.\n\n(c) Lake Oguta - Incorrect\nLake Oguta in Nigeria is still a lake.\n\n(d) Lake Volta - Incorrect\nLake Volta in Ghana is one of the world's largest artificial reservoirs, created by the Akosombo Dam in 1965."},{"i":"2022-24","y":2022,"n":24,"s":5,"q":"Gandikota canyon of South India was created by which one of the following rivers?","o":["Cauvery","Manjira","Pennar","Tungabhadra"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Cauvery - Incorrect\nThe Cauvery forms the Hogenakkal falls and the Shivanasamudra falls, not the Gandikota canyon.\n\n(b) Manjira - Incorrect\nThe Manjira is a tributary of the Godavari flowing through Maharashtra, Karnataka and Telangana.\n\n(c) Pennar - Correct\nThe Gandikota canyon in Kadapa district, Andhra Pradesh, has been cut by the Pennar (Penna) river through the Erramala hills. With steep red granite cliffs, it is often called the 'Grand Canyon of India'. The historic Gandikota fort stands on its edge.\n\n(d) Tungabhadra - Incorrect\nThe Tungabhadra flows past Hampi and joins the Krishna; it is not linked to Gandikota."},{"i":"2022-25","y":2022,"n":25,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Peak | Mountains\n| 1. | Namcha Barwa | Garhwal Himalaya\n| 2. | Nanda Devi | Kumaon Himalaya\n| 3. | Nokrek | Sikkim Himalaya\nWhich of the pairs given above is/are correctly matched?","o":["1 and 2","2 only","1 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. Namcha Barwa : Garhwal Himalaya - Incorrect\nNamcha Barwa (7,782 m) is in south-eastern Tibet, near where the Yarlung Tsangpo (Brahmaputra) makes its great bend. It marks the eastern end of the Himalaya (the Assam or Eastern Himalaya region), far from Garhwal.\n\n2. Nanda Devi : Kumaon Himalaya - Correct\nNanda Devi (7,816 m), the highest peak entirely within India, is in the Kumaon Himalaya of Uttarakhand. The Nanda Devi National Park is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.\n\n3. Nokrek : Sikkim Himalaya - Incorrect\nNokrek is the highest peak of the Garo Hills in Meghalaya and gives its name to the Nokrek Biosphere Reserve, known for wild citrus. It is not in the Himalaya.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2022-26","y":2022,"n":26,"s":11,"q":"The term \"Levant\" often heard in the news roughly corresponds to which of the following regions?","o":["Region along the eastern Mediterranean shores","Region along North African shores stretching from Egypt to Morocco","Region along Persian Gulf and Horn of Africa","The entire coastal areas of Mediterranean Sea"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Region along the eastern Mediterranean shores - Correct\nThe Levant (from the French 'lever', to rise, referring to the direction of sunrise) is the historical name for the lands along the eastern shore of the Mediterranean Sea. Today it roughly covers Syria, Lebanon, Israel, Palestine and Jordan, and sometimes Cyprus and southern Turkey. It is often in the news in connection with the Syrian conflict and the Israel-Palestine conflict, and groups like the Islamic State of Iraq and the Levant (ISIL).\n\n(b) Region along North African shores from Egypt to Morocco - Incorrect\nThe western part of North Africa (Morocco, Algeria, Tunisia, Libya) is called the Maghreb.\n\n(c) Region along the Persian Gulf and Horn of Africa - Incorrect\nThese areas are part of the wider Middle East and East Africa, not the Levant.\n\n(d) The entire coastal area of the Mediterranean Sea - Incorrect\nThe Levant is only the eastern portion."},{"i":"2022-27","y":2022,"n":27,"s":11,"q":"Consider the following countries:\n1. Azerbaijan\n2. Kyrgyzstan\n3. Tajikistan\n4. Turkmenistan\n5. Uzbekistan\nWhich of the above have borders with Afghanistan?","o":["1, 2 and 5 only","1, 2, 3 and 4 only","3, 4 and 5 only","1, 2, 3, 4 and 5"],"a":2,"x":"Afghanistan is a landlocked country bordered by Pakistan to the east and south, Iran to the west, Turkmenistan, Uzbekistan and Tajikistan to the north, and China through the narrow Wakhan Corridor in the north-east.\n\n1. Azerbaijan - No\nAzerbaijan is in the Caucasus region on the western shore of the Caspian Sea, far from Afghanistan.\n\n2. Kyrgyzstan - No\nKyrgyzstan lies north-east of Tajikistan. It comes close to the Wakhan Corridor but does not share a border with Afghanistan.\n\n3. Tajikistan - Yes\nIt shares the longest northern border, along the Amu Darya (Panj) river.\n\n4. Turkmenistan - Yes\nIt borders Afghanistan in the north-west.\n\n5. Uzbekistan - Yes\nIt shares a short border along the Amu Darya, crossed by the Friendship Bridge at Termez.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 3, 4 and 5 only."},{"i":"2022-28","y":2022,"n":28,"s":5,"q":"With reference to India, consider the following statements:\n1. Monazite is a source of rare earths.\n2. Monazite contains thorium.\n3. Monazite occurs naturally in the entire Indian coastal sands in India.\n4. In India, Government bodies only can process or export monazite.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1, 2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 4 only","3 and 4 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"a":1,"x":"1. Monazite is a source of rare earths - Correct\nMonazite is a phosphate mineral containing rare earth elements such as cerium, lanthanum, neodymium and praseodymium. It is India's main source of rare earths.\n\n2. Monazite contains thorium - Correct\nMonazite contains about 8-10 percent thorium oxide. India has one of the world's largest thorium reserves, mainly in monazite, which is important for the third stage of India's nuclear power programme.\n\n3. Monazite occurs naturally in the entire Indian coastal sands - Incorrect\nMonazite occurs in beach sand placer deposits only in certain stretches of the coast, mainly in Kerala (Chavara), Tamil Nadu (Manavalakurichi), Odisha (Chhatrapur) and Andhra Pradesh, along with ilmenite, rutile, zircon and garnet. It is not found along the entire coast.\n\n4. In India, only government bodies can process or export monazite - Correct\nBecause it contains thorium, monazite is a 'prescribed substance' under the Atomic Energy Act, 1962. Only government entities, mainly Indian Rare Earths Limited (under the Department of Atomic Energy), are allowed to mine, process and export it.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1, 2 and 4 only."},{"i":"2022-29","y":2022,"n":29,"s":5,"q":"In the northern hemisphere, the longest day of the year normally occurs in the:","o":["First half of the month of June","Second half of the month of June","First half of the month of July","Second half of the month of July"],"a":1,"x":"(a) First half of June - Incorrect\nIn early June, the days are still getting longer; the maximum has not yet been reached.\n\n(b) Second half of June - Correct\nThe longest day of the year in the Northern Hemisphere is the summer solstice, which normally falls on 20 or 21 June. On this day the Sun's rays fall vertically on the Tropic of Cancer, and the Northern Hemisphere is tilted most towards the Sun, so it receives the longest period of daylight. Areas north of the Arctic Circle have 24 hours of daylight.\n\n(c) First half of July - Incorrect\nBy July the days are already getting shorter. (Early July is when the Earth is at aphelion, farthest from the Sun, but this does not decide day length.)\n\n(d) Second half of July - Incorrect\nDays continue to shorten in late July."},{"i":"2022-30","y":2022,"n":30,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Wetland/Lake | Location\n| 1. | Hokera Wetland | Punjab\n| 2. | Renuka Wetland | Himachal Pradesh\n| 3. | Rudrasagar Lake | Tripura\n| 4. | Sasthamkotta Lake | Tamil Nadu\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","o":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs"],"a":1,"x":"1. Hokera Wetland : Punjab - Incorrect\nHokera (Hokersar) Wetland is near Srinagar in Jammu and Kashmir. It is a Ramsar site and an important stopover for migratory birds.\n\n2. Renuka Wetland : Himachal Pradesh - Correct\nRenuka Lake in Sirmaur district, Himachal Pradesh, is the largest natural lake in the State and a Ramsar site.\n\n3. Rudrasagar Lake : Tripura - Correct\nRudrasagar Lake at Melaghar in Tripura is a Ramsar site, famous for the Neermahal water palace built in the lake.\n\n4. Sasthamkotta Lake : Tamil Nadu - Incorrect\nSasthamkotta Lake is in Kollam district of Kerala. It is the largest freshwater lake in Kerala and a Ramsar site.\n\nTwo pairs are correctly matched, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2022-31","y":2022,"n":31,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following:\n1. Aarogya Setu\n2. CoWIN\n3. DigiLocker\n4. DIKSHA\nWhich of the above are built on top of open-source digital platforms?","o":["1 and 2 only","2, 3 and 4 only","1, 3 and 4 only","1, 2, 3 and 4"],"a":3,"x":"1. Aarogya Setu - Built on open-source platform\nThe COVID-19 contact tracing app's source code was made open-source in 2020, allowing public review.\n\n2. CoWIN - Built on open-source platform\nThe CoWIN vaccination platform used the open-source DIVOC (Digital Infrastructure for Verifiable Open Credentialing) platform for vaccine certificates, and India offered CoWIN as a digital public good to other countries.\n\n3. DigiLocker - Built on open-source platform\nDigiLocker, the digital document wallet, is built on open standards and open APIs as part of India Stack.\n\n4. DIKSHA - Built on open-source platform\nDIKSHA (Digital Infrastructure for Knowledge Sharing), the national platform for school education, is built on Sunbird, an open-source platform developed by the EkStep Foundation.\n\nAll four are built on open-source digital platforms, reflecting India's approach of building digital public infrastructure. So the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2022-32","y":2022,"n":32,"s":3,"q":"With reference to Web 3.0, consider the following statements:\n1. Web 3.0 technology enables people to control their own data.\n2. In Web 3.0 world, there can be blockchain based social networks.\n3. Web 3.0 is operated by users collectively rather than a corporation.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"Web 3.0 is the idea of a new, decentralised internet built on blockchain technology. Web 1.0 was the read-only web of static pages; Web 2.0 is the interactive, social web controlled by large platforms such as Google and Facebook.\n\n1. Web 3.0 enables people to control their own data - Correct\nIn Web 3.0, users own their data and digital identity through crypto wallets and decentralised identity systems, instead of handing it over to big technology companies.\n\n2. In the Web 3.0 world there can be blockchain-based social networks - Correct\nDecentralised social networks and platforms built on blockchain, where content and connections are not controlled by a single company, are part of the Web 3.0 vision.\n\n3. Web 3.0 is operated by users collectively rather than by a corporation - Correct\nWeb 3.0 platforms are meant to be governed collectively by their users and token-holders, often through decentralised autonomous organisations (DAOs), rather than by a single company.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2022-33","y":2022,"n":33,"s":3,"q":"With reference to \"Software as a Service (SaaS)\", consider the following statements:\n1. SaaS buyers can customise the user interface and can change data fields.\n2. SaaS users can access their data through their mobile devices.\n3. Outlook, Hotmail and Yahoo! Mail are forms of SaaS.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"Software as a Service (SaaS) is a cloud computing model in which software is hosted by a provider and accessed by users over the internet, usually through a subscription, instead of being installed on their own computers.\n\n1. SaaS buyers can customise the user interface and change data fields - Correct\nMost SaaS applications allow customers to configure the look of the interface, add or modify data fields and set up workflows to suit their business (for example, Salesforce and Zoho).\n\n2. SaaS users can access their data through mobile devices - Correct\nSince SaaS is delivered over the internet, users can access it from any device with a browser or app, including smartphones and tablets.\n\n3. Outlook, Hotmail and Yahoo! Mail are forms of SaaS - Correct\nWeb-based email services are among the earliest and most common examples of SaaS. Others include Google Workspace, Microsoft 365, Dropbox and Zoom.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2022-34","y":2022,"n":34,"s":3,"q":"Which one of the following statements best reflects the idea behind the \"Fractional Orbital Bombardment System\" often talked about in media?","o":["A hypersonic missile is launched into space to counter the asteroid approaching the Earth and explode it in space.","A spacecraft lands on another planet after making several orbital motions.","A missile is put into a stable orbit around the Earth and deorbits over a target on the Earth.","A spacecraft moves along a comet with the same speed and places a probe on its surface."],"a":2,"x":"(a) A hypersonic missile launched into space to destroy an approaching asteroid - Incorrect\nThis describes planetary defence, like NASA's DART mission (2022), which deliberately crashed a spacecraft into an asteroid to change its path.\n\n(b) A spacecraft landing on another planet after several orbits - Incorrect\nThis describes a normal orbital mission and landing.\n\n(c) A missile is put into stable orbit around the Earth and deorbits over a target - Correct\nA Fractional Orbital Bombardment System (FOBS) sends a warhead into low Earth orbit and brings it down on a target before it completes a full orbit (hence 'fractional'). This allows attacks from unexpected directions, for example over the South Pole, avoiding early-warning radars that face north, and gives no limit on range. The Soviet Union deployed such a system in the 1960s. It was in the news in 2021 when China reportedly tested a hypersonic glide vehicle launched on a FOBS-type trajectory.\n\n(d) A spacecraft moving with a comet and placing a probe on it - Incorrect\nThis describes the European Space Agency's Rosetta mission and its Philae lander (2014)."},{"i":"2022-35","y":2022,"n":35,"s":3,"q":"Which one of the following is the context in which the term \"qubit\" is mentioned?","o":["Cloud Services","Quantum Computing","Visible Light Communication Technologies","Wireless Communication Technologies"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Cloud Services - Incorrect\nCloud services use ordinary computers storing data in classical bits.\n\n(b) Quantum Computing - Correct\nA qubit (quantum bit) is the basic unit of information in a quantum computer. Unlike a classical bit, which is either 0 or 1, a qubit can exist in a superposition of 0 and 1 at the same time. Qubits can also be entangled, so the state of one is linked to another. These properties allow quantum computers to solve certain problems, such as simulating molecules or breaking some types of encryption, much faster than classical computers. Qubits can be made using superconducting circuits, trapped ions, photons and other methods.\n\n(c) Visible Light Communication Technologies - Incorrect\nVisible light communication (Li-Fi) transmits data using light from LEDs; it uses classical bits.\n\n(d) Wireless Communication Technologies - Incorrect\nWireless technologies like Wi-Fi and mobile networks also use classical bits."},{"i":"2022-36","y":2022,"n":36,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following communication technologies:\n1. Closed-circuit Television\n2. Radio Frequency Identification\n3. Wireless Local Area Network\nWhich of the above are considered Short-Range devices/technologies?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"Short Range Devices (SRDs) are low-power radio devices that communicate over short distances, usually a few metres to a few hundred metres, and have low potential to cause interference. In India, many SRDs are allowed to operate in certain frequency bands without a licence (for example the 2.4 GHz band).\n\n1. Closed-circuit Television - Short-range device\nWireless CCTV cameras send video signals over short distances to a local receiver or recorder.\n\n2. Radio Frequency Identification - Short-range device\nRFID tags and readers communicate over short distances and are used in FASTag toll collection, inventory tracking and access cards.\n\n3. Wireless Local Area Network - Short-range device\nWi-Fi networks connect devices within a home, office or campus using low-power radio signals.\n\nAll three are short-range technologies, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2022-37","y":2022,"n":37,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Biofilms can form on medical implants within human tissues.\n2. Biofilms can form on food and food processing surfaces.\n3. Biofilms can exhibit antibiotic resistance.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"A biofilm is a community of microorganisms, such as bacteria or fungi, that stick to a surface and cover themselves in a slimy, self-produced protective layer (extracellular matrix). Dental plaque is a common example.\n\n1. Biofilms can form on medical implants in human tissues - Correct\nBiofilms can grow on catheters, artificial joints, heart valves, pacemakers and contact lenses, causing persistent infections that are hard to treat and often require removal of the device.\n\n2. Biofilms can form on food and food-processing surfaces - Correct\nBacteria such as Listeria, Salmonella and E. coli can form biofilms on food and on equipment like conveyor belts, pipes and cutting boards, causing contamination and food poisoning.\n\n3. Biofilms can exhibit antibiotic resistance - Correct\nBacteria in biofilms can be up to 1,000 times more resistant to antibiotics than free-floating bacteria, because the matrix blocks drugs, cells grow slowly, and genes for resistance can be exchanged within the biofilm.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2022-38","y":2022,"n":38,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements in respect of probiotics:\n1. Probiotics are made of both bacteria and yeast.\n2. The organisms in probiotics are found in foods we ingest but they do not naturally occur in our gut.\n3. Probiotics help in the digestion of milk sugars.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","1 and 3","2 and 3"],"a":2,"x":"Probiotics are live microorganisms that, when consumed in adequate amounts, give health benefits, mainly by improving the balance of gut microbes. They are found in curd, yoghurt, kefir and supplements.\n\n1. Probiotics are made of both bacteria and yeast - Correct\nMost probiotics are bacteria, especially Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium species, but some are yeasts, such as Saccharomyces boulardii, which is used to prevent diarrhoea.\n\n2. The organisms in probiotics are found in foods, but they do not naturally occur in our gut - Incorrect\nMany probiotic organisms, such as Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium, are natural residents of the human gut. Probiotics help maintain or restore these beneficial populations.\n\n3. Probiotics help in the digestion of milk sugars - Correct\nLactic acid bacteria produce the enzyme lactase, which breaks down lactose (milk sugar). This is why people with lactose intolerance often digest curd and yoghurt better than milk.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1 and 3."},{"i":"2022-39","y":2022,"n":39,"s":3,"q":"In the context of vaccines manufactured to prevent COVID-19 pandemic, consider the following statements:\n1. The Serum Institute of India produced COVID-19 vaccine named Covishield using mRNA platform.\n2. Sputnik V vaccine is manufactured using vector based platform.\n3. COVAXIN is an inactivated pathogen based vaccine.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. The Serum Institute of India produced Covishield using the mRNA platform - Incorrect\nCovishield is the Oxford-AstraZeneca vaccine produced by the Serum Institute. It uses a viral vector platform: a weakened chimpanzee adenovirus (ChAdOx1) that cannot replicate carries the gene for the coronavirus spike protein into human cells. mRNA vaccines include those of Pfizer-BioNTech and Moderna, and India's Gemcovac.\n\n2. Sputnik V is manufactured using a vector-based platform - Correct\nRussia's Sputnik V, developed by the Gamaleya Institute, uses two different human adenovirus vectors (Ad26 for the first dose and Ad5 for the second) to deliver the spike protein gene. It was also produced in India by Dr. Reddy's Laboratories and others.\n\n3. COVAXIN is an inactivated pathogen-based vaccine - Correct\nCovaxin, developed by Bharat Biotech with ICMR and the National Institute of Virology, uses the whole SARS-CoV-2 virus that has been inactivated (killed) so it cannot cause disease but still triggers an immune response. It was India's first indigenous COVID-19 vaccine.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2022-40","y":2022,"n":40,"s":5,"q":"If a major solar storm (solar flare) reaches the Earth, which of the following are the possible effects on the Earth?\n1. GPS and navigation systems could fail.\n2. Tsunamis could occur at equatorial regions.\n3. Power grids could be damaged.\n4. Intense auroras could occur over much of the Earth.\n5. Forest fires could take place over much of the planet.\n6. Orbits of the satellites could be disturbed.\n7. Shortwave radio communication of the aircraft flying over polar regions could be interrupted.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2, 4 and 5 only","2, 3, 5, 6 and 7 only","1, 3, 4, 6 and 7 only","1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 and 7"],"a":2,"x":"A major solar storm (solar flares and coronal mass ejections) sends radiation and charged particles towards the Earth, causing geomagnetic storms.\n\n1. GPS and navigation systems could fail - Correct\nDisturbances in the ionosphere distort satellite signals, causing errors or loss of GPS signals.\n\n2. Tsunamis could occur in equatorial regions - Incorrect\nTsunamis are caused by undersea earthquakes, volcanic eruptions or landslides that displace water. Solar storms cannot cause them.\n\n3. Power grids could be damaged - Correct\nGeomagnetic storms induce electric currents in long power lines, which can overload and damage transformers. For example, a storm in 1989 caused a blackout across Quebec, Canada.\n\n4. Intense auroras could occur over much of the Earth - Correct\nStrong storms push auroras (northern and southern lights) far towards the equator; in May 2024 auroras were seen even in Ladakh.\n\n5. Forest fires could take place over much of the planet - Incorrect\nSolar storms do not cause forest fires.\n\n6. Orbits of satellites could be disturbed - Correct\nSolar storms heat and expand the upper atmosphere, increasing drag on satellites in low orbits. In 2022 about 40 Starlink satellites fell back to Earth due to this.\n\n7. Shortwave radio communication of aircraft over polar regions could be interrupted - Correct\nSolar storms cause high-frequency radio blackouts, especially in polar regions, forcing airlines to reroute polar flights.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1, 3, 4, 6 and 7 only."},{"i":"2022-41","y":2022,"n":41,"s":2,"q":"\"Climate Action Tracker\" which monitors the emission reduction pledges of different countries is a:","o":["Database created by coalition of research organisations","Wing of \"International Panel of Climate Change\"","Committee under \"United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change\"","Agency promoted and financed by United Nations Environment Programme and World Bank"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Database created by a coalition of research organisations - Correct\nThe Climate Action Tracker is an independent scientific analysis produced by two research organisations, Climate Analytics and the NewClimate Institute, since 2009. It tracks the climate pledges (such as Nationally Determined Contributions) and actual policies of major countries and rates them against the Paris Agreement goal of limiting warming to 1.5°C. It also estimates the warming the world is heading towards under current policies.\n\n(b) A wing of the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change - Incorrect\nThe IPCC assesses climate science through reports; it does not run the Climate Action Tracker.\n\n(c) A committee under the UNFCCC - Incorrect\nIt is independent of the UN climate convention.\n\n(d) An agency promoted and financed by UNEP and the World Bank - Incorrect\nIt is not a UN or World Bank agency. (UNEP separately publishes the annual Emissions Gap Report.)"},{"i":"2022-42","y":2022,"n":42,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. \"The Climate Group\" is an international non-profit organization that drives climate action by building large networks and runs them.\n2. The International Energy Agency in partnership with the Climate Group launched a global initiative \"EP100\".\n3. EP100 brings together leading companies committed to driving innovation in energy efficiency and increasing competitiveness while delivering on emission reduction goals.\n4. Some Indian companies are members of EP100.\n5. The International Energy Agency is the Secretariat to the \"Under2 Coalition\".\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1, 2, 4 and 5","1, 3 and 4 only","2, 3 and 5 only","1, 2, 3, 4 and 5"],"a":1,"x":"1. The Climate Group is an international non-profit that drives climate action by building and running large networks - Correct\nThe Climate Group, founded in 2004 and based in London, works with businesses and governments through networks such as RE100 (100 percent renewable electricity), EV100 (electric vehicles), EP100 (energy productivity) and the Under2 Coalition. It also organises Climate Week NYC.\n\n2. The International Energy Agency, with the Climate Group, launched the initiative 'EP100' - Incorrect\nEP100 was launched in 2016 by the Climate Group in partnership with the Alliance to Save Energy, not the International Energy Agency.\n\n3. EP100 brings together companies committed to energy efficiency innovation and competitiveness while meeting emission goals - Correct\nMember companies commit to goals such as doubling their energy productivity, implementing energy management systems or achieving net-zero carbon buildings.\n\n4. Some Indian companies are members of EP100 - Correct\nIndian companies such as Mahindra and Mahindra, Tech Mahindra, Godrej Industries and Dalmia Cement have joined EP100.\n\n5. The International Energy Agency is the Secretariat to the 'Under2 Coalition' - Incorrect\nThe Climate Group serves as the Secretariat of the Under2 Coalition, a network of state and regional governments committed to keeping warming well below 2°C.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1, 3 and 4 only."},{"i":"2022-43","y":2022,"n":43,"s":2,"q":"\"If rainforests and tropical forests are the lungs of the Earth, then surely wetlands function as its kidneys.\"\nWhich one of the following functions of wetlands best reflects the above statement?","o":["The water cycle in wetlands involves surface runoff, subsoil percolation and evaporation.","Algae form the nutrient base upon which fish, crustaceans, molluscs, birds, reptiles and mammals thrive.","Wetlands play a vital role in maintaining sedimentation balance and soil stabilization.","Aquatic plants absorb heavy metals and excess nutrients."],"a":3,"x":"The statement compares wetlands to kidneys because, just as kidneys filter waste from the blood, wetlands filter pollutants from water.\n\n(a) The water cycle in wetlands involves runoff, percolation and evaporation - Incorrect\nThis describes the hydrology of wetlands, not their cleaning function.\n\n(b) Algae form the nutrient base for fish, crustaceans, birds and other animals - Incorrect\nThis describes wetlands as productive ecosystems supporting food chains and biodiversity, not their purifying role.\n\n(c) Wetlands maintain sedimentation balance and soil stabilisation - Incorrect\nThis is an important protective function (trapping sediment and preventing erosion), but it is not the 'kidney' function.\n\n(d) Aquatic plants absorb heavy metals and excess nutrients - Correct\nWetland plants such as reeds, water hyacinth and cattails, together with microbes in the soil, absorb and break down excess nutrients like nitrogen and phosphorus, trap sediments, and take up toxic heavy metals. This purifies the water passing through them, just as kidneys purify blood. The East Kolkata Wetlands, for example, naturally treat much of Kolkata's sewage."},{"i":"2022-44","y":2022,"n":44,"s":2,"q":"In the context of WHO Air Quality Guidelines, consider the following statements:\n1. The 24-hour mean of PM2.5 should not exceed 15 µg/m³ and annual mean of PM2.5 should not exceed 5 µg/m³.\n2. In a year, the highest levels of ozone pollution occur during the periods of inclement weather.\n3. PM10 can penetrate the lung barrier and enter the bloodstream.\n4. Excessive ozone in the air can trigger asthma.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1, 3 and 4","1 and 4 only","2, 3 and 4","1 and 2 only"],"a":1,"x":"The WHO released updated Global Air Quality Guidelines in September 2021, making them stricter than the 2005 version.\n\n1. The 24-hour mean of PM2.5 should not exceed 15 µg/m³ and the annual mean should not exceed 5 µg/m³ - Correct\nThese are the 2021 guideline values for PM2.5. For PM10, the limits are 45 µg/m³ (24-hour) and 15 µg/m³ (annual).\n\n2. The highest ozone pollution levels occur during periods of inclement weather - Incorrect\nGround-level ozone is formed when nitrogen oxides and volatile organic compounds react in the presence of sunlight. So the highest levels occur during sunny, hot and calm weather, not during cloudy or rainy (inclement) weather.\n\n3. PM10 can penetrate the lung barrier and enter the bloodstream - Incorrect\nPM10 particles are usually trapped in the nose, throat and upper airways. It is the finer PM2.5 particles (and ultrafine particles) that can go deep into the lungs, cross the lung barrier and enter the bloodstream, causing heart and lung diseases.\n\n4. Excessive ozone in the air can trigger asthma - Correct\nGround-level ozone irritates and inflames the airways, reduces lung function and can trigger asthma attacks.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 1 and 4 only."},{"i":"2022-45","y":2022,"n":45,"s":2,"q":"With reference to \"Gucchi\" sometimes mentioned in the news, consider the following statements:\n1. It is a fungus.\n2. It grows in some Himalayan forest areas.\n3. It is commercially cultivated in the Himalayan foothills of north-eastern India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","3 only","1 and 2","2 and 3"],"a":2,"x":"Gucchi is the local name of the morel mushroom (Morchella esculenta), one of the most expensive mushrooms in the world, selling for about 20,000-30,000 rupees per kg. Gucchi from Jammu and Kashmir has received a GI tag.\n\n1. It is a fungus - Correct\nGucchi is an edible mushroom, which is the fruiting body of a fungus. It has a distinctive honeycomb-like cap.\n\n2. It grows in some Himalayan forest areas - Correct\nIt grows naturally in coniferous forests of the Himalaya, especially in Jammu and Kashmir, Himachal Pradesh and Uttarakhand, usually appearing in spring after snowmelt.\n\n3. It is commercially cultivated in the Himalayan foothills of north-eastern India - Incorrect\nGucchi has not yet been successfully cultivated commercially in India. It is collected from the wild by local people, which makes it rare and costly. The north-east is not known for its cultivation.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1 and 2."},{"i":"2022-46","y":2022,"n":46,"s":3,"q":"With reference to polyethylene terephthalate, the use of which is so widespread in our daily lives, consider the following statements:\n1. Its fibres can be blended with wool and cotton fibres to reinforce their properties.\n2. Containers made of it can be used to store any alcoholic beverage.\n3. Bottles made of it can be recycled into other products.\n4. Articles made of it can be easily disposed of by incineration without causing greenhouse gas emissions.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 3","2 and 4","1 and 4","2 and 3"],"a":0,"x":"Polyethylene terephthalate (PET) is a type of polyester plastic used for bottles, food containers and textile fibres.\n\n1. Its fibres can be blended with wool and cotton to reinforce their properties - Correct\nPolyester (PET) fibres are blended with cotton (terry-cot) and wool (terry-wool) to make fabrics stronger, more durable, wrinkle-resistant and quick-drying.\n\n2. Containers made of it can be used to store any alcoholic beverage - Incorrect\nPET is not suitable for storing all alcoholic beverages. Strong spirits can interact with the plastic over time, and PET lets some oxygen and carbon dioxide through, affecting quality and shelf life. So it cannot be used for 'any' alcoholic beverage; glass is preferred for many.\n\n3. Bottles made of it can be recycled into other products - Correct\nPET is one of the most widely recycled plastics. Used bottles are recycled into polyester fibre for clothing and carpets, sheets, strapping and new bottles. (Its resin code is 1.)\n\n4. Articles made of it can be easily disposed of by incineration without greenhouse gas emissions - Incorrect\nBurning PET releases carbon dioxide, a greenhouse gas, as well as other pollutants.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 3."},{"i":"2022-47","y":2022,"n":47,"s":2,"q":"Which of the following is not a bird?","o":["Golden Mahseer","Indian Nightjar","Spoonbill","White Ibis"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Golden Mahseer - Not a bird (correct answer)\nThe golden mahseer (Tor putitora) is a large freshwater fish found in fast-flowing rivers of the Himalaya. It is prized by anglers and is listed as Endangered by the IUCN. It is the State fish of Himachal Pradesh and Uttarakhand.\n\n(b) Indian Nightjar - A bird\nThe Indian nightjar is a nocturnal bird with camouflaged plumage that feeds on insects at dusk and at night.\n\n(c) Spoonbill - A bird\nThe Eurasian spoonbill is a wading bird with a long, flat, spoon-shaped bill, found in Indian wetlands.\n\n(d) White Ibis - A bird\nThe black-headed ibis (Oriental white ibis) is a wading bird found in Indian wetlands.\n\nHence the answer is (a)."},{"i":"2022-48","y":2022,"n":48,"s":3,"q":"Which of the following are nitrogen-fixing plants?\n1. Alfalfa\n2. Amaranth\n3. Chickpea\n4. Clover\n5. Purslane (Kulfa)\n6. Spinach\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 3 and 4 only","1, 3, 5 and 6 only","2, 4, 5 and 6 only","1, 2, 4, 5 and 6"],"a":0,"x":"Nitrogen-fixing plants are mainly legumes (family Fabaceae). Their roots have nodules containing Rhizobium bacteria that convert atmospheric nitrogen into a form plants can use, improving soil fertility.\n\n1. Alfalfa - Nitrogen-fixing\nAlfalfa (lucerne) is a leguminous fodder crop.\n\n2. Amaranth - Not nitrogen-fixing\nAmaranth (chaulai) belongs to the family Amaranthaceae.\n\n3. Chickpea - Nitrogen-fixing\nChickpea (gram) is a legume pulse.\n\n4. Clover - Nitrogen-fixing\nClover is a legume often used as a green manure and fodder.\n\n5. Purslane (Kulfa) - Not nitrogen-fixing\nPurslane belongs to the family Portulacaceae.\n\n6. Spinach - Not nitrogen-fixing\nSpinach belongs to the family Amaranthaceae.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1, 3 and 4 only."},{"i":"2022-49","y":2022,"n":49,"s":2,"q":"\"Biorock technology\" is talked about in which one of the following situations?","o":["Restoration of damaged coral reefs","Development of building materials using plant residues","Identification of areas for exploration/extraction of shale gas","Providing salt licks for wild animals in forests/protected areas"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Restoration of damaged coral reefs - Correct\nBiorock technology, also called mineral accretion, involves placing a steel frame on the seabed and passing a low-voltage electric current through it. This causes minerals like calcium carbonate from seawater to deposit on the frame, forming a limestone structure. Coral fragments attached to it grow faster and are more resistant to stress such as warming. In 2022 the Zoological Survey of India used this technology to restore corals in the Gulf of Kachchh, Gujarat.\n\n(b) Development of building materials from plant residues - Incorrect\nThis is unrelated to biorock.\n\n(c) Identifying areas for shale gas exploration - Incorrect\nThis is done through geological surveys.\n\n(d) Providing salt licks for wild animals - Incorrect\nSalt licks are natural or artificial mineral deposits for animals."},{"i":"2022-50","y":2022,"n":50,"s":2,"q":"The \"Miyawaki method\" is well known for the:","o":["Promotion of commercial farming in arid and semi-arid areas","Development of gardens using genetically modified flora","Creation of mini forests in urban areas","Harvesting wind energy on coastal areas and on sea surfaces"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Promotion of commercial farming in arid and semi-arid areas - Incorrect\nThe Miyawaki method is about creating forests, not farming.\n\n(b) Development of gardens using genetically modified plants - Incorrect\nThe method uses only native species, not GM plants.\n\n(c) Creation of mini forests in urban areas - Correct\nThe Miyawaki method was developed by Japanese botanist Akira Miyawaki. It involves planting a variety of native trees, shrubs and plants very densely (3-5 saplings per square metre) in small plots, after preparing the soil with organic matter. The plants grow quickly (up to ten times faster) and form a dense, multi-layered, self-sustaining forest within a few years. It is widely used in Indian cities to increase green cover, improve air quality and support biodiversity.\n\n(d) Harvesting wind energy in coastal areas and at sea - Incorrect\nThis refers to offshore wind energy."},{"i":"2022-51","y":2022,"n":51,"s":9,"q":"In the Government of India Act 1919, the functions of Provincial Government were divided into \"Reserved\" and \"Transferred\" subjects.\nWhich of the following were treated as \"Reserved\" subjects?\n1. Administration of Justice\n2. Local Self-Government\n3. Land Revenue\n4. Police\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2 and 3","2, 3 and 4","1, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4"],"a":2,"x":"The Government of India Act, 1919 (Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms) introduced dyarchy in the provinces. Provincial subjects were divided into 'reserved' subjects, administered by the Governor with his Executive Council (not responsible to the legislature), and 'transferred' subjects, administered by Indian ministers responsible to the legislative council.\n\n1. Administration of Justice - Reserved\nJustice, along with law and order, remained under British control.\n\n2. Local Self-Government - Transferred\nLocal self-government was given to Indian ministers, along with education, public health, agriculture, industries and public works.\n\n3. Land Revenue - Reserved\nLand revenue, the main source of provincial income, was kept under the Governor's control, along with finance and irrigation.\n\n4. Police - Reserved\nPolice was a reserved subject, so law and order stayed with the British.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1, 3 and 4."},{"i":"2022-52","y":2022,"n":52,"s":10,"q":"In medieval India, the term \"Fanam\" referred to:","o":["Clothing","Coins","Ornaments","Weapons"],"a":1,"x":"(a) Clothing - Incorrect\nFanam was not a type of cloth or garment.\n\n(b) Coins - Correct\nFanam (panam) was a small coin, usually of gold or silver, widely used in medieval South India, including the Vijayanagara empire. Foreign travellers such as Abdur Razzaq described it. Later the Dutch, the English (the 'Madras fanam') and the French also minted fanams on the Coromandel coast. The Tamil word 'panam' for money comes from it.\n\n(c) Ornaments - Incorrect\nIt was not an ornament.\n\n(d) Weapons - Incorrect\nIt was not a weapon."},{"i":"2022-53","y":2022,"n":53,"s":9,"q":"Consider the following freedom fighters:\n1. Barindra Kumar Ghosh\n2. Jogesh Chandra Chatterjee\n3. Rash Behari Bose\nWho of the above was/were actively associated with the Ghadar Party?","o":["1 and 2","2 only","1 and 3","3 only"],"a":3,"x":"The Ghadar Party was founded in 1913 in San Francisco by Indian immigrants, led by Lala Har Dayal, Sohan Singh Bhakna and others, to overthrow British rule through armed revolt.\n\n1. Barindra Kumar Ghosh - Not associated with the Ghadar Party\nHe was the younger brother of Aurobindo Ghosh and a leader of the Anushilan Samiti and Jugantar group in Bengal. He was convicted in the Alipore Bomb Case (1908) and sent to the Cellular Jail.\n\n2. Jogesh Chandra Chatterjee - Not associated with the Ghadar Party\nHe was a member of the Anushilan Samiti and later one of the founders of the Hindustan Republican Association (1924), linked to the Kakori conspiracy.\n\n3. Rash Behari Bose - Associated with the Ghadar Party\nRash Behari Bose worked closely with Ghadar revolutionaries who returned to India, and with Sachindra Nath Sanyal and Kartar Singh Sarabha he planned the Ghadar uprising of February 1915 in Punjab and army cantonments. When the plan was betrayed, he escaped to Japan, where he later founded the Indian Independence League.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 3 only."},{"i":"2022-54","y":2022,"n":54,"s":9,"q":"With reference to the proposals of Cripps Mission, consider the following statements:\n1. The Constituent Assembly would have members nominated by the Provincial Assemblies as well as the Princely States.\n2. Any Province, which is not prepared to accept the new Constitution would have the right to sign a separate agreement with Britain regarding its future status.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":1,"x":"The Cripps Mission came to India in March 1942 to win Indian support for the war effort.\n\n1. The Constituent Assembly would have members nominated by the Provincial Assemblies as well as the Princely States - Incorrect\nAccording to the Cripps proposals, after the war a Constituent Assembly would be set up in which members from British India would be elected by the Provincial Assemblies through proportional representation, while the Princely States would nominate their representatives. So the provincial members were to be elected, not nominated.\n\n2. Any province not prepared to accept the new Constitution would have the right to sign a separate agreement with Britain - Correct\nProvinces unwilling to join the new Indian Union could stay out and frame their own constitution, with the same status as the Union. This implied the possibility of partition, which the Congress rejected. Gandhi reportedly called the offer a 'post-dated cheque on a crashing bank'.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2022-55","y":2022,"n":55,"s":7,"q":"With reference to Indian history, consider the following texts:\n1. Nettipakarana\n2. Parishishtaparvan\n3. Avadanashataka\n4. Trishashtilakshana Mahapurana\nWhich of the above are Jaina texts?","o":["1, 2 and 3","2 and 4 only","1, 3 and 4","2, 3 and 4"],"a":1,"x":"1. Nettipakarana - Not a Jaina text\nIt is a Pali Buddhist text that gives methods for interpreting the Buddha's teachings; it is included in some versions of the Khuddaka Nikaya.\n\n2. Parishishtaparvan - Jaina text\nAlso called Sthaviravali Charitra, it was written by the Jain scholar Hemachandra in the 12th century. It describes the lives of early Jain teachers and mentions Chandragupta Maurya's conversion to Jainism and his migration to the south with Bhadrabahu.\n\n3. Avadanashataka - Not a Jaina text\nIt is a Buddhist Sanskrit collection of one hundred avadanas (stories of good deeds and their results).\n\n4. Trishashti-lakshana Mahapurana - Jaina text\nIt describes the lives of the 63 illustrious persons of Jainism (24 Tirthankaras, 12 Chakravartins, 9 Baladevas, 9 Vasudevas and 9 Prativasudevas). It was composed by Jinasena and his disciple Gunabhadra in the 9th century.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 4 only."},{"i":"2022-56","y":2022,"n":56,"s":10,"q":"With reference to Indian history, consider the following pairs:\n| | Historical person | Known as\n| 1. | Aryadeva | Jaina scholar\n| 2. | Dignaga | Buddhist scholar\n| 3. | Nathamuni | Vaishnava scholar\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","o":["None of the pairs","Only one pair","Only two pairs","All three pairs"],"a":2,"x":"1. Aryadeva : Jaina scholar - Incorrect\nAryadeva (2nd-3rd century CE) was a Buddhist philosopher of the Madhyamaka school and a disciple of Nagarjuna. His main work is the Chatuhshataka (Four Hundred Verses).\n\n2. Dignaga : Buddhist scholar - Correct\nDignaga (5th-6th century CE) was a Buddhist logician, regarded as the founder of Buddhist logic and epistemology. His main work is the Pramanasamuccaya.\n\n3. Nathamuni : Vaishnava scholar - Correct\nNathamuni (10th century) was the first acharya of the Sri Vaishnava tradition. He collected and arranged the hymns of the Alvars into the Nalayira Divya Prabandham, and is regarded as the precursor of Ramanuja.\n\nTwo pairs are correct, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2022-57","y":2022,"n":57,"s":8,"q":"With reference to Indian history, consider the following statements:\n1. The first Mongol invasion of India happened during the reign of Jalal-ud-din Khalji.\n2. During the reign of Ala-ud-din Khalji, one Mongol assault marched up to Delhi and besieged the city.\n3. Muhammad-bin-Tughluq temporarily lost portions of north-west of his kingdom to Mongols.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2","2 only","1 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. The first Mongol invasion of India happened during the reign of Jalal-ud-din Khalji - Incorrect\nThe first Mongol threat came in 1221, during the reign of Iltutmish, when Chengiz Khan pursued Jalaluddin Mangbarni, the Khwarizm prince, up to the Indus. Iltutmish wisely refused shelter to Mangbarni and avoided conflict. Jalal-ud-din Khalji (1290-1296) did defeat a Mongol force, but it was not the first invasion.\n\n2. During Ala-ud-din Khalji's reign, a Mongol assault marched up to Delhi and besieged the city - Correct\nMongols attacked several times during Alauddin's reign. In 1299 Qutlugh Khwaja reached the outskirts of Delhi, and in 1303 Targhi besieged Delhi for about two months while Alauddin was camped at Siri. After these attacks Alauddin strengthened frontier defences, built the Siri fort and maintained a large standing army.\n\n3. Muhammad-bin-Tughluq temporarily lost portions of the north-west of his kingdom to the Mongols - Incorrect (as per key)\nMuhammad bin Tughluq faced the Mongol raid of Tarmashirin (around 1327), which he repelled or bought off. He did not lose territory in the north-west to the Mongols; he even planned expeditions to Khurasan and Qarachil.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2022-58","y":2022,"n":58,"s":8,"q":"With reference to Indian history, who of the following were known as \"Kulah-Daran\"?","o":["Arab merchants","Qalandars","Persian calligraphists","Sayyids"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Arab merchants - Incorrect\nArab merchants were active in coastal trade but were not called kulah-daran.\n\n(b) Qalandars - Incorrect\nQalandars were wandering Sufi mystics who often shaved their heads and beards and rejected social conventions.\n\n(c) Persian calligraphists - Incorrect\nCalligraphists were not known by this term.\n\n(d) Sayyids - Correct\nSayyids, who claimed descent from the Prophet Muhammad through his daughter Fatima, enjoyed high social status in the Delhi Sultanate and Mughal period. They wore a distinctive tall cap called 'kulah', so they were referred to as 'kulah-daran', meaning 'cap wearers'."},{"i":"2022-59","y":2022,"n":59,"s":9,"q":"With reference to Indian history, consider the following statements:\n1. The Dutch established their factories/warehouses on the east coast on lands granted to them by Gajapati rulers.\n2. Alfonso de Albuquerque captured Goa from the Bijapur Sultanate.\n3. The English East India Company established a factory at Madras on a plot of land leased from a representative of the Vijayanagara empire.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. The Dutch established factories on the east coast on lands granted by the Gajapati rulers - Incorrect\nThe Dutch set up their first factory on the east coast at Masulipatnam (1605) with permission from the Golconda Sultanate, and at Pulicat (1610) with a grant from the Vijayanagara ruler. The Gajapati kingdom of Odisha had declined by the early 16th century and did not grant them land.\n\n2. Alfonso de Albuquerque captured Goa from the Bijapur Sultanate - Correct\nIn 1510, Albuquerque captured Goa from the Adil Shahi ruler of Bijapur (Yusuf Adil Shah), making it the headquarters of the Portuguese in India.\n\n3. The English East India Company set up a factory at Madras on land leased from a representative of the Vijayanagara empire - Correct\nIn 1639, Francis Day obtained a grant of land at Madraspatnam from Damarla Venkatadri Nayaka, a local chief under the Vijayanagara (Aravidu) ruler Venkata III. Fort St. George was built there, which grew into the city of Madras.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2022-60","y":2022,"n":60,"s":7,"q":"According to Kautilya's Arthashastra, which of the following are correct?\n1. A person could be a slave as a result of a judicial punishment.\n2. If a female slave bore her master a son, she was legally free.\n3. If a son born to a female slave was fathered by her master, the son was entitled to the legal status of the master's son.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. A person could become a slave as a result of judicial punishment - Correct\nThe Arthashastra (Book III, chapter 13) recognises several kinds of slaves (dasas): those born in the household, bought, obtained as gifts, inherited, captured in war, self-sold, and those enslaved as a result of a judicial punishment (dandapranita), for example to pay off a fine. Upinder Singh's history of ancient India also lists judicial punishment as a way of becoming a slave in the Arthashastra.\n\n2. If a female slave bore her master a son, she was legally free - Correct\nThe Arthashastra lays down that a female slave who bears a child to her master becomes free, along with the child.\n\n3. If a son born to a female slave was fathered by her master, he was entitled to the legal status of the master's son - Correct\nSuch a son was given the status of the master's son.\n\nThe Arthashastra also provided that an Arya (free person) could not be made a slave except in certain cases, and it laid down rules protecting slaves from ill-treatment.\n\nDiscrepancy: The official answer in the sheet is (b) 2 and 3 only. However, statement 1 is also supported by the Arthashastra, so the correct answer should be (d) 1, 2 and 3. Several coaching analyses also give (d). Please re-verify this entry against the official UPSC key."},{"i":"2022-62","y":2022,"n":62,"s":6,"q":"Consider the following States:\n1. Andhra Pradesh\n2. Kerala\n3. Himachal Pradesh\n4. Tripura\nHow many of the above are generally known as tea-producing States?","o":["Only one State","Only two States","Only three States","All four States"],"a":3,"x":"India is the second-largest producer of tea in the world. Assam and West Bengal produce most of it, but several other States also grow tea.\n\n1. Andhra Pradesh - Tea-producing\nTea is grown on a small scale in the Araku Valley and other hilly tribal areas of the Visakhapatnam and Alluri Sitarama Raju districts.\n\n2. Kerala - Tea-producing\nKerala is a major tea producer in South India, especially in Munnar (Idukki), Wayanad and Peermade.\n\n3. Himachal Pradesh - Tea-producing\nKangra tea from Himachal Pradesh is known for its distinctive flavour and has a GI tag.\n\n4. Tripura - Tea-producing\nTripura has a long history of tea cultivation, with dozens of tea estates, and is among the tea-growing States of the North-east.\n\nAll four States are known as tea-producing States by the Tea Board, so the answer is (d)."},{"i":"2022-63","y":2022,"n":63,"s":1,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. In India, credit rating agencies are regulated by Reserve Bank of India.\n2. The rating agency popularly known as ICRA is a public limited company.\n3. Brickwork Ratings is an Indian credit rating agency.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"1. In India, credit rating agencies are regulated by the Reserve Bank of India - Incorrect\nCredit rating agencies are registered with and regulated by the Securities and Exchange Board of India under the SEBI (Credit Rating Agencies) Regulations, 1999. The RBI only accredits some of them as External Credit Assessment Institutions for the purpose of banks' capital calculations.\n\n2. The rating agency popularly known as ICRA is a public limited company - Correct\nICRA Limited (formerly Investment Information and Credit Rating Agency of India), set up in 1991, is a public limited company listed on the stock exchanges. Moody's is its largest shareholder.\n\n3. Brickwork Ratings is an Indian credit rating agency - Correct\nBrickwork Ratings, based in Bengaluru, was an Indian credit rating agency registered with SEBI. (SEBI cancelled its registration in 2022 for lapses.)\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2022-64","y":2022,"n":64,"s":1,"q":"With reference to the 'Banks Board Bureau (BBB)', which of the following statements are correct?\n1. The Governor of RBI is the Chairman of BBB.\n2. BBB recommends for the selection of heads for Public Sector Banks.\n3. BBB helps the Public Sector Banks in developing strategies and capital raising plans.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":1,"x":"The Banks Board Bureau was set up in 2016 on the recommendation of the P.J. Nayak Committee to improve governance of public sector banks. It was replaced by the Financial Services Institutions Bureau in 2022.\n\n1. The Governor of the RBI is the Chairman of the BBB - Incorrect\nThe BBB was chaired by an eminent person appointed by the government; the first Chairman was former Comptroller and Auditor General Vinod Rai. The RBI Governor was not its chairman.\n\n2. The BBB recommends the selection of heads of Public Sector Banks - Correct\nThe BBB recommended candidates for appointment as whole-time directors (managing directors and executive directors) and non-executive chairpersons of public sector banks and state-owned financial institutions.\n\n3. The BBB helps Public Sector Banks develop strategies and capital-raising plans - Correct\nIt advised public sector banks on developing business strategies, growth plans and ways to raise capital, and on improving governance.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 3 only."},{"i":"2022-65","y":2022,"n":65,"s":1,"q":"With reference to Convertible Bonds, consider the following statements:\n1. As there is an option to exchange the bond for equity, Convertible Bonds pay a lower rate of interest.\n2. The option to convert to equity affords the bondholder a degree of indexation to rising consumer prices.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":2,"x":"A convertible bond is a bond that gives the holder the option to convert it into a fixed number of the company's shares at a later date.\n\n1. Because there is an option to convert to equity, convertible bonds pay a lower rate of interest - Correct\nThe conversion option is valuable to investors because they can benefit if the company's share price rises. In return for this benefit, companies can pay a lower coupon (interest rate) than on ordinary bonds.\n\n2. The option to convert into equity gives the bondholder a degree of indexation to rising consumer prices - Correct\nNormal fixed-rate bonds lose real value when prices rise. When there is inflation, company revenues, profits and share prices also tend to rise over time. By converting into shares, the bondholder can share in these gains, which partly protects him against rising prices, giving a degree of indexation.\n\nHence the answer is (c) Both 1 and 2."},{"i":"2022-66","y":2022,"n":66,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following:\n1. Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank\n2. Missile Technology Control Regime\n3. Shanghai Cooperation Organisation\nIndia is a member of which of the above?","o":["1 and 2 only","3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"1. Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank - India is a member\nIndia is a founding member of the AIIB (headquartered in Beijing, started in 2016) and its second-largest shareholder after China. India is also one of the largest borrowers from the bank.\n\n2. Missile Technology Control Regime - India is a member\nIndia joined the MTCR in June 2016 as its 35th member. The MTCR is an informal group of countries that seeks to limit the spread of missiles and unmanned delivery systems that can carry weapons of mass destruction. Membership has helped India access advanced technology.\n\n3. Shanghai Cooperation Organisation - India is a member\nIndia became a full member of the SCO in June 2017 at the Astana summit, along with Pakistan. India held the SCO presidency in 2023.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2022-67","y":2022,"n":67,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Vietnam has been one of the fastest growing economies in the world in the recent years.\n2. Vietnam is led by a multi-party political system.\n3. Vietnam's economic growth is linked to its integration with global supply chains and focus on exports.\n4. For a long time Vietnam's low labour costs and stable exchange rates have attracted global manufacturers.\n5. Vietnam has the most productive e-service sector in the Indo-Pacific region.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["2 and 4","3 and 5","1, 3 and 4","1 and 2"],"a":2,"x":"1. Vietnam has been one of the fastest-growing economies in recent years - Correct\nVietnam's economy has grown at around 6-7 percent a year for many years, reaching about 8 percent in 2022, one of the highest growth rates in the world.\n\n2. Vietnam is led by a multi-party political system - Incorrect\nVietnam is a one-party socialist state ruled by the Communist Party of Vietnam; other parties are not permitted.\n\n3. Vietnam's growth is linked to integration with global supply chains and focus on exports - Correct\nVietnam's growth has been driven by exports of electronics, textiles, footwear and furniture, and its integration into global value chains through free trade agreements (such as CPTPP and RCEP). Many companies have shifted production from China to Vietnam under the 'China plus one' strategy.\n\n4. Low labour costs and stable exchange rates have long attracted global manufacturers - Correct\nLow wages, a young workforce and a managed, stable currency have attracted big investors like Samsung, Intel and Nike.\n\n5. Vietnam has the most productive e-service sector in the Indo-Pacific - Incorrect\nThere is no basis for this claim; Vietnam's strength is manufacturing, not e-services.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 1, 3 and 4."},{"i":"2022-68","y":2022,"n":68,"s":1,"q":"In India, which one of the following is responsible for maintaining price stability by controlling inflation?","o":["Department of Consumer Affairs","Expenditure Management Commission","Financial Stability and Development Council","Reserve Bank of India"],"a":3,"x":"(a) Department of Consumer Affairs - Incorrect\nThis department monitors prices of essential commodities, manages the Price Stabilisation Fund and handles consumer protection, but it is not responsible for overall price stability.\n\n(b) Expenditure Management Commission - Incorrect\nThis commission, set up in 2014 under Bimal Jalan, reviewed government expenditure; it had no role in controlling inflation.\n\n(c) Financial Stability and Development Council - Incorrect\nThe FSDC, chaired by the Finance Minister, deals with financial stability, coordination among regulators and financial sector development, not inflation targeting.\n\n(d) Reserve Bank of India - Correct\nUnder the RBI Act, 1934, as amended in 2016, the primary objective of monetary policy is to maintain price stability while keeping growth in mind. The government sets an inflation target, currently 4 percent CPI inflation with a band of 2 to 6 percent, and the RBI's six-member Monetary Policy Committee sets the repo rate to achieve it."},{"i":"2022-69","y":2022,"n":69,"s":3,"q":"With reference to Non-Fungible Tokens (NFTs), consider the following statements:\n1. They enable the digital representation of physical assets.\n2. They are unique cryptographic tokens that exist on a blockchain.\n3. They can be traded or exchanged at equivalency and therefore can be used as a medium of commercial transactions.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"x":"Non-Fungible Tokens (NFTs) are unique digital certificates of ownership recorded on a blockchain.\n\n1. They enable the digital representation of physical assets - Correct\nNFTs can represent ownership of both digital items (art, music, videos, in-game items) and physical assets such as real estate, luxury goods or artworks, allowing their ownership to be tracked and transferred digitally.\n\n2. They are unique cryptographic tokens that exist on a blockchain - Correct\nEach NFT has a unique identifier and metadata recorded on a blockchain, often using standards like Ethereum's ERC-721. This makes it impossible to duplicate the token itself and provides proof of ownership and authenticity.\n\n3. They can be traded or exchanged at equivalency and so can be used as a medium of commercial transactions - Incorrect\n'Non-fungible' means each token is unique and not interchangeable with another of equal value, unlike currency or fungible tokens like Bitcoin, where one unit equals another. So NFTs cannot be exchanged at equivalency and are not suitable as a medium of exchange.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2022-70","y":2022,"n":70,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Reservoirs | States\n| 1. | Ghataprabha | Telangana\n| 2. | Gandhi Sagar | Madhya Pradesh\n| 3. | Indira Sagar | Andhra Pradesh\n| 4. | Maithon | Chhattisgarh\nHow many pairs given above are not correctly matched?","o":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs"],"a":2,"x":"The question asks how many pairs are NOT correctly matched.\n\n1. Ghataprabha : Telangana - Not correctly matched\nThe Ghataprabha reservoir (Hidkal dam) is on the Ghataprabha river, a tributary of the Krishna, in Belagavi district, Karnataka.\n\n2. Gandhi Sagar : Madhya Pradesh - Correctly matched\nThe Gandhi Sagar dam is on the Chambal river in Mandsaur district, Madhya Pradesh. Its reservoir is one of the largest in India, and the Gandhi Sagar sanctuary is being developed as a second home for cheetahs.\n\n3. Indira Sagar : Andhra Pradesh - Not correctly matched\nThe Indira Sagar dam is on the Narmada river in Khandwa district, Madhya Pradesh; its reservoir is one of India's largest by volume. (The Polavaram project in Andhra Pradesh is officially named 'Indira Sagar' too, but the well-known Indira Sagar reservoir is in MP, which is how the key treats it.)\n\n4. Maithon : Chhattisgarh - Not correctly matched\nThe Maithon dam is on the Barakar river, built by the Damodar Valley Corporation, on the Jharkhand-West Bengal border (Dhanbad district, Jharkhand).\n\nThree pairs are not correctly matched, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2022-71","y":2022,"n":71,"s":1,"q":"In India, which one of the following compiles information on industrial disputes, closures, retrenchments and lay-offs in factories employing workers?","o":["Central Statistics Office","Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade","Labour Bureau","National Technical Manpower Information System"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Central Statistics Office - Incorrect\nThe CSO (now part of the National Statistical Office) compiles national accounts, GDP estimates and the Index of Industrial Production.\n\n(b) Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade - Incorrect\nDPIIT deals with industrial policy, FDI policy, intellectual property and start-ups. It compiles the Wholesale Price Index (through its Office of Economic Adviser), but not data on industrial disputes.\n\n(c) Labour Bureau - Correct\nThe Labour Bureau, an attached office of the Ministry of Labour and Employment (based in Shimla and Chandigarh), compiles statistics on industrial disputes, strikes and lockouts, closures, retrenchments and lay-offs, based on returns under the Industrial Disputes Act. It also compiles the Consumer Price Index for industrial workers, agricultural labourers and rural labourers.\n\n(d) National Technical Manpower Information System - Incorrect\nThis system collects information on the supply and demand of technical manpower (engineers, technicians)."},{"i":"2022-72","y":2022,"n":72,"s":5,"q":"In India, what is the role of the Coal Controller's Organization (CCO)?\n1. CCO is the major source of Coal Statistics in Government of India.\n2. It monitors progress of development of Captive Coal/Lignite blocks.\n3. It hears any objection to the Government's notification relating to acquisition of coal-bearing areas.\n4. It ensures that coal mining companies deliver the coal to end users in the prescribed time.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1, 2 and 3","3 and 4 only","1 and 2 only","1, 2 and 4"],"a":0,"x":"The Coal Controller's Organisation is an attached office of the Ministry of Coal, headquartered in Kolkata.\n\n1. It is the major source of coal statistics in the Government of India - Correct\nIt collects and publishes official coal and lignite statistics, such as the Provisional Coal Statistics and the Coal Directory of India.\n\n2. It monitors the progress of development of captive coal and lignite blocks - Correct\nIt monitors the development and production of coal and lignite blocks allotted to companies for captive use and for commercial mining.\n\n3. It hears objections to government notifications on acquisition of coal-bearing areas - Correct\nUnder the Coal Bearing Areas (Acquisition and Development) Act, 1957, the Coal Controller is the competent authority to hear objections from affected persons to notifications for acquisition of land.\n\n4. It ensures that coal companies deliver coal to end users in the prescribed time - Incorrect\nDelivery and supply of coal to consumers is handled by coal companies under fuel supply agreements, not by the Coal Controller. Its other functions include quality control and grading of coal and inspection of collieries.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2022-73","y":2022,"n":73,"s":0,"q":"If a particular area is brought under the Fifth Schedule of the Constitution of India, which one of the following statements best reflects the consequence of it?","o":["This would prevent the transfer of land of tribal people to non-tribal people.","This would create a local self-governing body in that area.","This would convert that area into a Union Territory.","The State having such areas would be declared a Special Category State."],"a":0,"x":"(a) This would prevent the transfer of land of tribal people to non-tribal people - Correct\nUnder paragraph 5(2) of the Fifth Schedule, the Governor can make regulations for the peace and good government of Scheduled Areas, in particular to prohibit or restrict the transfer of land by or among members of Scheduled Tribes, regulate allotment of land, and regulate money-lending. Laws like Andhra Pradesh's 1/70 Regulation prevent transfer of tribal land to non-tribals. In the Samatha judgment (1997), the Supreme Court held that tribal land in Scheduled Areas could not be leased to private non-tribal companies for mining. The PESA Act, 1996 also gives gram sabhas power over land alienation.\n\n(b) This would create a local self-governing body in that area - Incorrect\nAutonomous District Councils are a feature of the Sixth Schedule, applicable to Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram, not the Fifth Schedule.\n\n(c) This would convert that area into a Union Territory - Incorrect\nScheduled Areas remain part of the State.\n\n(d) The State would be declared a Special Category State - Incorrect\nSpecial Category Status was a separate concept based on factors like hilly terrain and low population density, decided by the National Development Council, and is unrelated."},{"i":"2022-74","y":2022,"n":74,"s":0,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The India Sanitation Coalition is a platform to promote sustainable sanitation and is funded by the Government of India and the World Health Organization.\n2. The National Institute of Urban Affairs is an apex body of the Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs in Government of India and provides innovative solutions to address the challenges of Urban India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":3,"x":"1. The India Sanitation Coalition is a platform to promote sustainable sanitation and is funded by the Government of India and the WHO - Incorrect\nThe India Sanitation Coalition was launched in 2015 at FICCI (Federation of Indian Chambers of Commerce and Industry) as a multi-stakeholder platform bringing together the private sector, government, civil society, financial institutions and others to promote sustainable sanitation. It is supported by corporates and foundations such as the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation, not funded by the Government of India and the WHO.\n\n2. The National Institute of Urban Affairs is an apex body of the Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs and provides innovative solutions for urban India - Incorrect (as per key)\nNIUA, established in 1976, is a premier national institute for research, capacity building and knowledge on urban issues. It is an autonomous body supported by the Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs, but it is not the 'apex body' of the Ministry.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Neither 1 nor 2."},{"i":"2022-75","y":2022,"n":75,"s":2,"q":"Which one of the following has been constituted under the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986?","o":["Central Water Commission","Central Ground Water Board","Central Ground Water Authority","National Water Development Agency"],"a":2,"x":"(a) Central Water Commission - Incorrect\nThe CWC, set up in 1945, is an attached office of the Ministry of Jal Shakti. It is the technical body for surface water resources, flood forecasting and river basin planning. It was not constituted under the Environment (Protection) Act.\n\n(b) Central Ground Water Board - Incorrect\nThe CGWB, set up in 1970, is a subordinate office of the Ministry of Jal Shakti responsible for scientific surveys, exploration, monitoring and assessment of groundwater. It was not constituted under the EPA.\n\n(c) Central Ground Water Authority - Correct\nThe CGWA was constituted in 1997 under Section 3(3) of the Environment (Protection) Act, 1986, following directions of the Supreme Court in the M.C. Mehta case. It regulates and controls groundwater development and management, for example by issuing No Objection Certificates for groundwater extraction by industries and notifying over-exploited areas.\n\n(d) National Water Development Agency - Incorrect\nThe NWDA, set up in 1982, is a registered society under the Ministry of Jal Shakti that carries out studies for interlinking of rivers."},{"i":"2022-76","y":2022,"n":76,"s":4,"q":"With reference to the \"United Nations Credentials Committee\", consider the following statements:\n1. It is a committee set up by the UN Security Council and works under its supervision.\n2. It traditionally meets in March, June and September every year.\n3. It assesses the credentials of all UN members before submitting a report to the General Assembly for approval.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["3 only","1 and 3","2 and 3","1 and 2"],"a":0,"x":"1. It is a committee set up by the UN Security Council and works under its supervision - Incorrect\nThe Credentials Committee is a committee of the UN General Assembly, not the Security Council. At the start of each regular session, the General Assembly appoints its nine members on the proposal of the President of the General Assembly.\n\n2. It traditionally meets in March, June and September every year - Incorrect\nIt normally meets once a year, usually in October or November, before the end of the year's session, to examine credentials. It does not meet three times a year.\n\n3. It assesses the credentials of all UN members before reporting to the General Assembly for approval - Correct\nThe Committee examines the credentials (official documents appointing representatives) of delegates of all member states and submits a report to the General Assembly for approval. It becomes important when rival governments claim to represent a country, as in the cases of Afghanistan (after the Taliban takeover in 2021) and Myanmar (after the 2021 coup), where decisions were deferred.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 3 only."},{"i":"2022-77","y":2022,"n":77,"s":4,"q":"Which one of the following statements best describes the 'Polar Code'?","o":["It is the international code of safety for ships operating in polar waters.","It is the agreement of the countries around the North Pole regarding the demarcation of their territories in the polar region.","It is a set of norms to be followed by the countries whose scientists undertake research studies in the North Pole and South Pole.","It is a trade and security agreement of the member countries of the Arctic Council."],"a":0,"x":"(a) It is the international code of safety for ships operating in polar waters - Correct\nThe International Code for Ships Operating in Polar Waters (Polar Code) was adopted by the International Maritime Organization and came into force on 1 January 2017. It is mandatory under the SOLAS and MARPOL conventions. It covers ship design and construction, equipment, operations, crew training, search and rescue, and protection of the fragile polar environment, for example by banning discharge of oil and restricting discharge of sewage and garbage in Arctic and Antarctic waters.\n\n(b) An agreement of countries around the North Pole on demarcating their territories - Incorrect\nTerritorial claims in the Arctic are governed by UNCLOS and bilateral agreements; there is no such 'Polar Code'.\n\n(c) Norms for countries whose scientists research at the poles - Incorrect\nAntarctic research is governed by the Antarctic Treaty System; this is not the Polar Code.\n\n(d) A trade and security agreement of Arctic Council members - Incorrect\nThe Arctic Council is a forum for cooperation on environment and sustainable development; its mandate excludes military security, and it has no such trade agreement."},{"i":"2022-78","y":2022,"n":78,"s":4,"q":"With reference to the United Nations General Assembly, consider the following statements:\n1. The UN General Assembly can grant observer status to the non-member States.\n2. Inter-governmental organisations can seek observer status in the UN General Assembly.\n3. Permanent Observers in the UN General Assembly can maintain missions at the UN headquarters.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"1. The UN General Assembly can grant observer status to non-member states - Correct\nThe General Assembly can grant observer status to states that are not UN members. Currently the Holy See (Vatican) and the State of Palestine are 'non-member observer states', which allows them to take part in General Assembly sessions without voting. Palestine was upgraded to this status in 2012.\n\n2. Inter-governmental organisations can seek observer status in the General Assembly - Correct\nIntergovernmental organisations can apply for observer status, which is granted by a General Assembly resolution. Examples include the European Union, the African Union, the International Solar Alliance and the Commonwealth Secretariat.\n\n3. Permanent Observers can maintain missions at the UN headquarters - Correct\nNon-member observer states and many intergovernmental organisations maintain permanent observer missions at the UN Headquarters in New York.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2022-79","y":2022,"n":79,"s":6,"q":"With reference to the \"Tea Board\" in India, consider the following statements:\n1. The Tea Board is a statutory body.\n2. It is a regulatory body attached to the Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare.\n3. The Tea Board's Head Office is situated in Bengaluru.\n4. The Board has overseas offices at Dubai and Moscow.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 3","2 and 4","3 and 4","1 and 4"],"a":3,"x":"1. The Tea Board is a statutory body - Correct\nThe Tea Board was constituted as a statutory body in 1954 under Section 4 of the Tea Act, 1953. It regulates and promotes the tea industry, including production, marketing, research and export promotion.\n\n2. It is a regulatory body attached to the Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare - Incorrect\nThe Tea Board functions under the Ministry of Commerce and Industry, since tea is an important export commodity. (Other commodity boards, like the Coffee, Rubber and Spices Boards, are also under the Commerce Ministry.)\n\n3. The Tea Board's head office is in Bengaluru - Incorrect\nIts head office is in Kolkata. (The Coffee Board has its head office in Bengaluru.)\n\n4. The Board has overseas offices at Dubai and Moscow - Correct\nThe Tea Board has overseas offices in Dubai and Moscow to promote Indian tea in the Middle East and Russia, which are major markets for Indian tea.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1 and 4."},{"i":"2022-80","y":2022,"n":80,"s":2,"q":"Which one of the following best describes the term \"greenwashing\"?","o":["Conveying a false impression that a company's products are eco-friendly and environmentally sound","Non-inclusion of ecological/environmental costs in the Annual Financial Statements of a country","Ignoring the disastrous ecological consequences while undertaking infrastructure development","Making mandatory provisions for environmental costs in a government project/programme"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Conveying a false impression that a company's products are eco-friendly and environmentally sound - Correct\nGreenwashing is the practice of making misleading or exaggerated claims about the environmental benefits of a product, service or company, for example labelling products as 'natural', 'green' or 'carbon neutral' without proper evidence, to attract environmentally conscious customers. In India, the Advertising Standards Council of India, SEBI (for green bonds and ESG funds) and the Central Consumer Protection Authority (Guidelines for Prevention and Regulation of Greenwashing, 2024) have acted against it.\n\n(b) Not including environmental costs in a country's annual financial statements - Incorrect\nThis relates to the absence of green accounting in national accounts.\n\n(c) Ignoring disastrous ecological consequences during infrastructure development - Incorrect\nThis describes environmentally reckless development, not greenwashing.\n\n(d) Making mandatory provisions for environmental costs in a government project - Incorrect\nThis describes responsible environmental budgeting, which is the opposite of greenwashing."},{"i":"2022-81","y":2022,"n":81,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. High clouds primarily reflect solar radiation and cool the surface of the Earth.\n2. Low clouds have a high absorption of infrared radiation emanating from the Earth's surface and thus cause warming effect.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":3,"x":"1. High clouds primarily reflect solar radiation and cool the surface of the Earth - Incorrect\nHigh, thin clouds such as cirrus are made of ice crystals and let most sunlight pass through. However, they absorb the long-wave (infrared) radiation emitted by the Earth and re-radiate part of it back downwards. Their overall effect is to warm the surface.\n\n2. Low clouds strongly absorb infrared radiation from the Earth's surface and cause warming - Incorrect\nLow, thick clouds such as stratus and stratocumulus are bright and have a high albedo; they reflect a large part of incoming sunlight back to space. Their overall effect is to cool the Earth's surface.\n\nThe two statements have reversed the roles of high and low clouds. This is important in climate science because changes in cloud cover can either amplify or reduce global warming.\n\nHence the answer is (d) Neither 1 nor 2."},{"i":"2022-82","y":2022,"n":82,"s":11,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Bidibidi is a large refugee settlement in north-western Kenya.\n2. Some people who fled from South Sudan civil war live in Bidibidi.\n3. Some people who fled from civil war in Somalia live in Dadaab refugee complex in Kenya.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2","2 only","2 and 3","3 only"],"a":2,"x":"1. Bidibidi is a large refugee settlement in north-western Kenya - Incorrect\nBidibidi is in Yumbe district of north-western Uganda, not Kenya. Opened in 2016, it became one of the largest refugee settlements in the world, with over 2 lakh residents.\n\n2. Some people who fled the South Sudan civil war live in Bidibidi - Correct\nBidibidi was established to host refugees fleeing the civil war in South Sudan, which broke out in 2013 and intensified in 2016. Uganda is known for its progressive refugee policy, giving refugees land and freedom of movement.\n\n3. Some people who fled the civil war in Somalia live in the Dadaab refugee complex in Kenya - Correct\nThe Dadaab refugee complex in north-eastern Kenya was set up in 1991 to host Somalis fleeing civil war, and later drought and famine. It is one of the world's largest refugee camps, with hundreds of thousands of residents. (Kenya also hosts the Kakuma camp in the north-west.)\n\nHence the answer is (c) 2 and 3."},{"i":"2022-83","y":2022,"n":83,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following countries:\n1. Armenia\n2. Azerbaijan\n3. Croatia\n4. Romania\n5. Uzbekistan\nWhich of the above are members of the Organization of Turkic States?","o":["1, 2 and 4","1 and 3","2 and 5","3, 4 and 5"],"a":2,"x":"The Organization of Turkic States (formerly the Cooperation Council of Turkic Speaking States, or Turkic Council, founded in 2009 and renamed in 2021) promotes cooperation among Turkic-speaking countries. Its members are Turkey, Azerbaijan, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan and Uzbekistan. Hungary, Turkmenistan and the Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus are observers.\n\n1. Armenia - Not a member\nArmenia is not a Turkic nation (Armenians speak an Indo-European language), and it has hostile relations with Azerbaijan and Turkey.\n\n2. Azerbaijan - Member\nAzerbaijan is a Turkic-speaking nation and a founding member.\n\n3. Croatia - Not a member\nCroatia is a Slavic country in the Balkans.\n\n4. Romania - Not a member\nRomania is a Romance-language country.\n\n5. Uzbekistan - Member\nUzbekistan joined as a full member in 2019.\n\nHence the answer is (c) 2 and 5."},{"i":"2022-84","y":2022,"n":84,"s":5,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Gujarat has the largest solar park in India.\n2. Kerala has a fully solar powered International Airport.\n3. Goa has the largest floating solar photovoltaic project in India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2","2 only","1 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. Gujarat has the largest solar park in India - Incorrect (as of 2022)\nAt the time of the exam, the largest solar park in India was the Bhadla Solar Park in Jodhpur district, Rajasthan, with about 2,245 MW capacity. (Gujarat's Khavda Renewable Energy Park in Kutch, under construction, is planned to be the world's largest renewable energy park at 30 GW.)\n\n2. Kerala has a fully solar-powered international airport - Correct\nCochin International Airport in Kerala became the world's first fully solar-powered airport in 2015. It generates more electricity from its solar plants than it consumes. It received the UN's 'Champion of the Earth' award in 2018.\n\n3. Goa has the largest floating solar photovoltaic project in India - Incorrect\nGoa does not have the largest floating solar plant. In 2022 the largest was NTPC's 100 MW floating solar plant at Ramagundam, Telangana; the larger Omkareshwar floating solar project in Madhya Pradesh was later developed.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2022-85","y":2022,"n":85,"s":4,"q":"With reference to the United Nations Convention on the Law of Sea, consider the following statements:\n1. A coastal state has the right to establish the breadth of its territorial sea up to a limit not exceeding 12 nautical miles, measured from baseline determined in accordance with the convention.\n2. Ships of all states, whether coastal or land-locked, enjoy the right of innocent passage through the territorial sea.\n3. The Exclusive Economic Zone shall not extend beyond 200 nautical miles from the baseline from which the breadth of the territorial sea is measured.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"The United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS), adopted in 1982, is the main international treaty governing the use of oceans. India ratified it in 1995.\n\n1. A coastal state can establish the breadth of its territorial sea up to 12 nautical miles from the baseline - Correct\nArticle 3 of UNCLOS allows a territorial sea of up to 12 nautical miles from the baseline, over which the coastal state has sovereignty, including the airspace, seabed and subsoil.\n\n2. Ships of all states, coastal or land-locked, enjoy the right of innocent passage through the territorial sea - Correct\nArticle 17 gives ships of all states the right of innocent passage, meaning continuous and fast passage that is not harmful to the peace, good order or security of the coastal state.\n\n3. The Exclusive Economic Zone shall not extend beyond 200 nautical miles from the baseline - Correct\nArticle 57 states that the EEZ shall not extend beyond 200 nautical miles from the baselines. In the EEZ, the coastal state has sovereign rights over natural resources, such as fish, oil and gas, while other states enjoy freedom of navigation and overflight. (The continental shelf can extend further, up to 350 nautical miles in some cases.)\n\nHence the answer is (d) 1, 2 and 3."},{"i":"2022-86","y":2022,"n":86,"s":11,"q":"Which one of the following statements best reflects the issue with Senkaku Islands, sometimes mentioned in the news?","o":["It is generally believed that they are artificial islands made by a country around South China Sea.","China and Japan engage in maritime disputes over these islands in East China Sea.","A permanent American military base has been set up there to help Taiwan to increase its defence capabilities.","Though International Court of Justice declared them as no man's land, some South-East Asian countries claim them."],"a":1,"x":"(a) They are artificial islands built by a country around the South China Sea - Incorrect\nThe Senkaku Islands are natural islands in the East China Sea. The artificial islands in the news are those China has built on reefs in the Spratly Islands of the South China Sea.\n\n(b) China and Japan engage in maritime disputes over these islands in the East China Sea - Correct\nThe Senkaku Islands (called Diaoyu Islands by China and Tiaoyutai by Taiwan) are a group of small, uninhabited islands north-east of Taiwan. Japan administers them and considers them its territory since 1895, but China and Taiwan claim them. The dispute intensified after Japan bought three of the islands from a private owner in 2012, and Chinese coast guard ships regularly enter the surrounding waters. The area is important for fishing, potential oil and gas reserves and strategic sea lanes.\n\n(c) A permanent American military base has been set up there to help Taiwan - Incorrect\nThere is no American base on the islands. US bases in the region are in Okinawa and elsewhere in Japan, although the US says the islands are covered by its security treaty with Japan.\n\n(d) The International Court of Justice declared them no man's land, but some South-East Asian countries claim them - Incorrect\nThe ICJ has made no such declaration, and South-East Asian countries do not claim them."},{"i":"2022-87","y":2022,"n":87,"s":4,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Country | Important reason for being in the news recently\n| 1. | Chad | Setting up of permanent military base by China\n| 2. | Guinea | Suspension of Constitution and Government by military\n| 3. | Lebanon | Severe and prolonged economic depression\n| 4. | Tunisia | Suspension of Parliament by President\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","o":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs"],"a":2,"x":"1. Chad : Setting up of a permanent Chinese military base - Incorrect\nChad was in the news because, after President Idriss Déby was killed in April 2021, a military council led by his son took power. China's only overseas military base is in Djibouti.\n\n2. Guinea : Suspension of the Constitution and Government by the military - Correct\nIn September 2021, special forces led by Colonel Mamady Doumbouya overthrew President Alpha Condé, suspended the Constitution and dissolved the government.\n\n3. Lebanon : Severe and prolonged economic depression - Correct\nLebanon has faced one of the world's worst economic crises since 2019, which the World Bank described as among the most severe since the mid-19th century. The Lebanese pound lost over 90 percent of its value, banks froze deposits, and poverty rose sharply, worsened by the 2020 Beirut port explosion.\n\n4. Tunisia : Suspension of Parliament by the President - Correct\nIn July 2021, President Kais Saied suspended Parliament and dismissed the Prime Minister, and later dissolved Parliament (2022) and introduced a new constitution giving himself greater powers.\n\nThree pairs are correct, so the answer is (c)."},{"i":"2022-88","y":2022,"n":88,"s":11,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Region often mentioned in the news | Country\n| 1. | Anatolia | Turkey\n| 2. | Amhara | Ethiopia\n| 3. | Cabo Delgado | Spain\n| 4. | Catalonia | Italy\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","o":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs"],"a":1,"x":"1. Anatolia : Turkey - Correct\nAnatolia, also called Asia Minor, is the large peninsula in western Asia that forms most of Turkey's territory. It was in the news after the massive earthquakes of February 2023 in southern Turkey.\n\n2. Amhara : Ethiopia - Correct\nAmhara is a region of Ethiopia. It was affected by the Tigray war (2020-2022) and later by conflict between the Ethiopian army and the Fano militia.\n\n3. Cabo Delgado : Spain - Incorrect\nCabo Delgado is the northernmost province of Mozambique, in the news for an Islamist insurgency since 2017 that displaced lakhs of people and disrupted large natural gas projects.\n\n4. Catalonia : Italy - Incorrect\nCatalonia is a region in north-eastern Spain, with Barcelona as its capital. It was in the news for its independence movement and the 2017 referendum declared illegal by Spain.\n\nTwo pairs are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2022-89","y":2022,"n":89,"s":2,"q":"With reference to Indian laws about wildlife protection, consider the following statements:\n1. Wild animals are the sole property of the government.\n2. When a wild animal is declared protected, such animal is entitled for equal protection whether it is found in protected areas or outside.\n3. Apprehension of a protected wild animal becoming a danger to human life is sufficient ground for its capture or killing.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2","2 only","1 and 3","3 only"],"a":1,"x":"1. Wild animals are the sole property of the government - Incorrect (as per key)\nSection 39 of the Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972 declares that wild animals which are hunted, killed, found dead or captured in violation of the Act, and their trophies and articles, become the property of the government. It does not declare all wild animals to be the 'sole property' of the government.\n\n2. When a wild animal is declared protected, it is entitled to equal protection whether found in protected areas or outside - Correct\nThe protection given to a species listed in the Schedules of the Act applies everywhere in India, not only inside national parks and sanctuaries. For example, hunting a tiger or blackbuck is an offence whether it happens inside or outside a protected area.\n\n3. Apprehension of a protected wild animal becoming a danger to human life is sufficient ground for its capture or killing - Incorrect\nUnder Section 11, the Chief Wildlife Warden may permit hunting or capture of a Schedule I animal only if it has actually become dangerous to human life or is so disabled or diseased that it cannot recover, and he must record reasons in writing. A mere apprehension or fear that it might become dangerous is not sufficient. (Killing in genuine self-defence is not an offence.)\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 only."},{"i":"2022-90","y":2022,"n":90,"s":2,"q":"Certain species of which one of the following organisms are well known as cultivators of fungi?","o":["Ant","Cockroach","Crab","Spider"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Ant - Correct\nLeaf-cutter ants (genera Atta and Acromyrmex) of Central and South America, and other attine ants, cut pieces of leaves and carry them to their nests, where they use them to grow fungus gardens. The ants feed on the fungus, and the fungus depends on the ants for food and care. This is one of the oldest forms of agriculture, practised by ants for about 50-60 million years. (Some termites and ambrosia beetles also farm fungi.)\n\n(b) Cockroach - Incorrect\nCockroaches are scavengers and do not cultivate fungi.\n\n(c) Crab - Incorrect\nCrabs do not cultivate fungi, although some farm bacteria on their bodies.\n\n(d) Spider - Incorrect\nSpiders are predators and do not cultivate fungi."},{"i":"2022-91","y":2022,"n":91,"s":7,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | Site of Ashoka's major rock edicts | Location in the State of\n| 1. | Dhauli | Odisha\n| 2. | Erragudi | Andhra Pradesh\n| 3. | Jaugada | Madhya Pradesh\n| 4. | Kalsi | Karnataka\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","o":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs"],"a":1,"x":"Ashoka's Major Rock Edicts are found at 14 or more locations across the subcontinent.\n\n1. Dhauli : Odisha - Correct\nDhauli, near Bhubaneswar, has Ashoka's Major Rock Edicts, including the Separate Kalinga Edicts, and a rock-cut elephant sculpture. It is near the site of the Kalinga war.\n\n2. Erragudi : Andhra Pradesh - Correct\nErragudi, in Kurnool district of Andhra Pradesh, has a full set of Major Rock Edicts along with a Minor Rock Edict.\n\n3. Jaugada : Madhya Pradesh - Incorrect\nJaugada is in Ganjam district of Odisha, not Madhya Pradesh. It also has the Separate Kalinga Edicts.\n\n4. Kalsi : Karnataka - Incorrect\nKalsi is in Dehradun district of Uttarakhand, near the confluence of the Yamuna and Tons. It has a complete set of Major Rock Edicts. (Karnataka has Minor Rock Edicts, such as at Maski and Brahmagiri.)\n\nTwo pairs are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2022-92","y":2022,"n":92,"s":8,"q":"Consider the following pairs:\n| | King | Dynasty\n| 1. | Nannuka | Chandela\n| 2. | Jayashakti | Paramara\n| 3. | Nagabhata II | Gurjara-Pratihara\n| 4. | Bhoja | Rashtrakuta\nHow many pairs given above are correctly matched?","o":["Only one pair","Only two pairs","Only three pairs","All four pairs"],"a":1,"x":"1. Nannuka : Chandela - Correct\nNannuka (around 831 CE) is regarded as the founder of the Chandela dynasty of Bundelkhand, which later built the Khajuraho temples.\n\n2. Jayashakti : Paramara - Incorrect\nJayashakti (also called Jeja) was an early Chandela ruler, a grandson of Nannuka. The Chandela territory came to be known as Jejakabhukti after him. He was not a Paramara.\n\n3. Nagabhata II : Gurjara-Pratihara - Correct\nNagabhata II (around 800-833 CE) was a Gurjara-Pratihara king who captured Kanauj and made it his capital. He took part in the tripartite struggle for Kanauj with the Palas and Rashtrakutas.\n\n4. Bhoja : Rashtrakuta - Incorrect\nThe famous Bhoja (around 1010-1055 CE) was a king of the Paramara dynasty of Malwa, known for his learning and for founding Bhojpur and the Bhoj Shala at Dhar. Another Bhoja, Mihira Bhoja, was a Gurjara-Pratihara king. Neither was a Rashtrakuta.\n\nTwo pairs are correct, so the answer is (b)."},{"i":"2022-93","y":2022,"n":93,"s":7,"q":"Which one of the following statements about Sangam literature in ancient South India is correct?","o":["Sangam poems are devoid of any reference to material culture.","The social classification of Varna was known to Sangam poets.","Sangam poems have no reference to warrior ethic.","Sangam literature refers to magical forces as irrational."],"a":1,"x":"(a) Sangam poems are devoid of any reference to material culture - Incorrect\nSangam literature is a rich source on material culture. It describes crops, food, clothing, houses, crafts, trade, ports like Puhar and Muziris, Roman trade, coins and urban life.\n\n(b) The social classification of varna was known to Sangam poets - Correct\nSangam texts mention terms like 'anthanar' (Brahmins), 'arasar' (kings or rulers), 'vanikar' (traders) and 'vellalar' (cultivators), showing that the poets were familiar with the varna classification of northern India, though social divisions in the Tamil region were not as rigid and were more linked to occupation and landscape. Brahmins received gifts from kings (as noted by Upinder Singh).\n\n(c) Sangam poems have no reference to warrior ethic - Incorrect\nThe 'puram' (exterior) poems celebrate war, heroism, the glory of kings and warriors, hero stones (nadukal) set up for fallen warriors, and practices like vadakkiruttal (fasting to death after defeat).\n\n(d) Sangam literature refers to magical forces as irrational - Incorrect\nSangam poems treat sacred and magical forces, such as 'ananku' (a sacred power residing in kings, women and objects), as real and powerful, not as irrational."},{"i":"2022-94","y":2022,"n":94,"s":8,"q":"\"Yogavasistha\" was translated into Persian by Nizamuddin Panipati during the reign of:","o":["Akbar","Humayun","Shahjahan","Aurangzeb"],"a":0,"x":"(a) Akbar - Correct\nThe Yogavasistha, a Sanskrit philosophical text on the teachings of sage Vasistha to Rama, was translated into Persian by Nizamuddin Panipati in 1597 during Akbar's reign, under the patronage of Prince Salim (later Jahangir). Akbar set up a translation bureau (Maktab Khana) that translated Sanskrit works like the Mahabharata (as Razmnama), Ramayana, Atharvaveda, Lilavati and Rajatarangini into Persian, reflecting his policy of sulh-i-kul (universal peace).\n\n(b) Humayun - Incorrect\nHumayun's reign (1530-1540 and 1555-1556) was disturbed by conflict with Sher Shah, and this translation was not done then.\n\n(c) Shahjahan - Incorrect\nIn Shah Jahan's time, his son Dara Shikoh translated the Upanishads into Persian (Sirr-i-Akbar) and sponsored another version of the Yogavasistha, but Nizamuddin Panipati's translation was under Akbar.\n\n(d) Aurangzeb - Incorrect\nAurangzeb did not patronise such translations."},{"i":"2022-95","y":2022,"n":95,"s":10,"q":"The world's second tallest statue in sitting pose of Ramanuja was inaugurated by the Prime Minister of India at Hyderabad recently.\nWhich one of the following statements correctly represents the teachings of Ramanuja?","o":["The best means of salvation was devotion.","Vedas are eternal, self-existent and wholly authoritative.","Logical arguments were essential means for the highest bliss.","Salvation was to be obtained through meditation."],"a":0,"x":"Ramanuja (11th-12th century) was a Tamil philosopher who propounded Vishishtadvaita (qualified non-dualism). The 216-foot 'Statue of Equality' of Ramanuja was inaugurated at Muchintal near Hyderabad in February 2022, honouring his message of equality.\n\n(a) The best means of salvation was devotion - Correct\nRamanuja taught that the individual soul and the world are real but are parts of Brahman (Vishnu), like the body is part of the soul. He held that the best way to attain salvation is intense devotion (bhakti) to Vishnu and complete surrender (prapatti) to God's grace. He opened the path of devotion to all, regardless of caste.\n\n(b) Vedas are eternal, self-existent and wholly authoritative - Incorrect\nThis is the central idea of the Mimamsa school (Jaimini, Kumarila Bhatta), which emphasised Vedic rituals.\n\n(c) Logical arguments were essential means for the highest bliss - Incorrect\nThis reflects the Nyaya school (Gautama), which held that correct knowledge through logic leads to liberation.\n\n(d) Salvation was to be obtained through meditation - Incorrect\nThis reflects the Yoga school (Patanjali), and Shankaracharya's Advaita emphasised knowledge (jnana)."},{"i":"2022-96","y":2022,"n":96,"s":10,"q":"The Prime Minister recently inaugurated the new Circuit House near Somnath Temple at Veraval.\nWhich of the following statements are correct regarding Somnath Temple?\n1. Somnath Temple is one of the Jyotirlinga shrines.\n2. A description of Somnath Temple was given by Al-Biruni.\n3. Pran Pratishtha of Somnath Temple (installation of the present day temple) was done by President S. Radhakrishnan.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 and 2 only","2 and 3 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"x":"1. Somnath Temple is one of the Jyotirlinga shrines - Correct\nThe Somnath temple at Prabhas Patan near Veraval in Gujarat is traditionally regarded as the first of the twelve Jyotirlinga shrines of Shiva.\n\n2. A description of Somnath Temple was given by Al-Biruni - Correct\nAl-Biruni, the Persian scholar who came to India with Mahmud of Ghazni, described the Somnath temple and its linga in his book Kitab-ul-Hind (Tahqiq-i-Hind), and also mentioned its destruction by Mahmud in 1025-26.\n\n3. The Pran Pratishtha of the present-day temple was done by President S. Radhakrishnan - Incorrect\nAfter independence, the temple was rebuilt at the initiative of Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel and K.M. Munshi. The Pran Pratishtha (consecration) was performed by the first President, Dr. Rajendra Prasad, on 11 May 1951, despite Nehru's reservations.\n\nHence the answer is (a) 1 and 2 only."},{"i":"2022-97","y":2022,"n":97,"s":3,"q":"Which one of the following statements best describes the role of B cells and T cells in the human body?","o":["They protect the body from environmental allergens.","They alleviate the body's pain and inflammation.","They act as immunosuppressants in the body.","They protect the body from the diseases caused by pathogens."],"a":3,"x":"(a) They protect the body from environmental allergens - Incorrect\nAllergies are actually an overreaction of the immune system to harmless substances, involving B cells producing IgE antibodies. Protecting against allergens is not the role of B and T cells.\n\n(b) They alleviate the body's pain and inflammation - Incorrect\nB and T cells are not painkillers; they can in fact contribute to inflammation during immune responses.\n\n(c) They act as immunosuppressants - Incorrect\nImmunosuppressant drugs suppress B and T cells (for example after organ transplants). B and T cells themselves drive immunity, though regulatory T cells help control excessive responses.\n\n(d) They protect the body from diseases caused by pathogens - Correct\nB cells and T cells are lymphocytes of the adaptive immune system. B cells, which mature in the bone marrow, produce antibodies that bind to and neutralise pathogens like bacteria and viruses (humoral immunity). T cells, which mature in the thymus, include helper T cells that coordinate the immune response and cytotoxic T cells that kill infected cells (cell-mediated immunity). Memory B and T cells give long-lasting immunity, which is the basis of vaccination."},{"i":"2022-98","y":2022,"n":98,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Other than those made by humans, nanoparticles do not exist in nature.\n2. Nanoparticles of some metallic oxides are used in the manufacture of some cosmetics.\n3. Nanoparticles of some commercial products which enter the environment are unsafe for humans.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","3 only","1 and 2","2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"1. Other than those made by humans, nanoparticles do not exist in nature - Incorrect\nNanoparticles (particles 1-100 nanometres in size) occur naturally in many forms: volcanic ash, sea spray, smoke from forest fires, fine mineral dust, clay particles and even viruses. Some organisms, such as certain bacteria, produce nanoparticles naturally.\n\n2. Nanoparticles of some metallic oxides are used in manufacturing some cosmetics - Correct\nNanoparticles of zinc oxide and titanium dioxide are widely used in sunscreens because they block ultraviolet rays while appearing transparent on the skin. They are also used in other cosmetics.\n\n3. Nanoparticles from some commercial products that enter the environment are unsafe for humans - Correct\nEngineered nanoparticles, such as silver nanoparticles from textiles and titanium dioxide, can enter water and soil. Because of their tiny size and high reactivity, some can enter cells, cause oxidative stress and harm humans and other organisms. Their safety is an area of active research and regulation.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 2 and 3."},{"i":"2022-99","y":2022,"n":99,"s":3,"q":"Consider the following statements:\nDNA Barcoding can be a tool to:\n1. assess the age of a plant or animal.\n2. distinguish among species that look alike.\n3. identify undesirable animal or plant materials in processed foods.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 only","3 only","1 and 2","2 and 3"],"a":3,"x":"DNA barcoding is a method of identifying species using a short, standard section of DNA, like a supermarket barcode. For animals the mitochondrial COI gene is usually used, and for plants genes like rbcL and matK.\n\n1. It can assess the age of a plant or animal - Incorrect\nDNA barcodes identify which species an organism belongs to; they do not show how old an individual is.\n\n2. It can distinguish among species that look alike - Correct\nMany species look very similar (cryptic species) or are hard to identify at certain stages, such as eggs or larvae. DNA barcoding can tell them apart, helping in biodiversity studies and conservation.\n\n3. It can identify undesirable animal or plant materials in processed foods - Correct\nDNA barcoding can detect food fraud and adulteration, such as cheaper fish sold as expensive fish, undeclared meat in processed food, or fillers in herbal products. It is also used in wildlife forensics to identify products from protected species.\n\nHence the answer is (d) 2 and 3."},{"i":"2022-100","y":2022,"n":100,"s":2,"q":"Consider the following:\n1. Carbon monoxide\n2. Nitrogen oxide\n3. Ozone\n4. Sulphur dioxide\nExcess of which of the above in the environment is/are cause(s) of acid rain?","o":["1, 2 and 3","2 and 4 only","4 only","1, 3 and 4"],"a":1,"x":"Acid rain is rain with a pH below about 5.6, caused mainly by sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides released from burning fossil fuels. These gases react with water, oxygen and other chemicals in the atmosphere to form sulphuric acid and nitric acid, which fall with rain. Acid rain damages forests, soils, lakes and buildings (for example, the marble of the Taj Mahal).\n\n1. Carbon monoxide - Not a cause\nCarbon monoxide is a poisonous gas from incomplete combustion, but it does not form strong acids in rain.\n\n2. Nitrogen oxide - Cause\nNitrogen oxides from vehicles and power plants form nitric acid.\n\n3. Ozone - Not a direct cause\nOzone is an oxidant that can help convert SO2 and NOx into acids, but by itself it is not a cause of acid rain.\n\n4. Sulphur dioxide - Cause\nSulphur dioxide, mainly from coal-fired power plants and smelters, forms sulphuric acid, the main component of acid rain.\n\nHence the answer is (b) 2 and 4 only."}];

